{"MINDMAP_V2_NOTES_N5":{"n5-001||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"core","definition":"The **NはNです** pattern means “A is B,” asserting what a thing or person is, including a job or nationality. **は** marks the topic, and **です** is the polite auxiliary verb placed at the end of the sentence.","nuance":"This is a **neutral** statement with no special emphasis. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often drop です and say “わたし田中”. In class, at work, or when meeting someone for the first time, use the full です form for politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"です","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am Tanaka.","scene":"Introducing yourself in class for the first time."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Holding up a book for a friend to see."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-001||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern follows **N + は + N + です**. The です ending has several forms for negatives, past statements, and questions.","nuance":"The negative **ではありません** is used in writing or formal speech; **じゃありません** sounds more familiar and casual. To make a question, simply add **か** at the end and raise your intonation.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"は + N + です","note":"Present affirmative"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"は + N + ではありません","note":"Present negative (じゃありません is more casual)"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"は + N + でした","note":"Past affirmative"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"は + N + ではありませんでした","note":"Past negative"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"は + N + ですか","note":"Yes/No question"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am a student.","scene":"Answering an occupation question in class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka is not a doctor.","scene":"Correcting a misunderstanding about his job."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-001||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"usage","definition":"NはNです is used to **introduce yourself**, **ask and answer about people or things**, and identify jobs or nationalities. It is a foundational sentence frame for beginner conversations.","nuance":"When pointing to something far from both speaker and listener, use **あれ**; for something near the listener, use **それ**; for something near yourself, use **これ**. Questions usually only need か; no other question particle is required.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is that person a teacher?","scene":"Asking a friend about a stranger at school."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a bag.","scene":"Pointing to your bag on the desk."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-001||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes NはNです from easily confused patterns in Lesson 1: negatives, questions, and “also”.","nuance":"To **negate**, replace です with ではありません. To **ask**, add か. To say **also**, replace は with も. Change only ONE part at a time to form each new pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Using the basic affirmative as a starting point for comparison."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNではありません","point":"NEGATIVE — “A is B” becomes “A is not B”. 「私は学生です。」→「私は学生ではありません。」"},{"vs":"NはNですか","point":"QUESTION — add か at the end. 「これは本です。」→「これは本ですか。」"},{"vs":"NもNです","point":"ALSO — replace は with も. 「私も学生です。」 means “I am also a student”."}]}},"n5-001||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with NはNです and their corrections. Learners often make three mistakes: pronouncing the particle は as 'ha' from the kana chart, treating を like an object marker before です, and forgetting か when forming a question. These errors often come from translating directly from English sentence habits. Each mistake is shown as ❌ wrong → ✅ correct for easy comparison and review.","nuance":"Most errors come from literal translation habits or pronouncing は as 'ha' based on the kana chart.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"A standard affirmative sentence with no mistake."}],"pitfalls":["❌ Watashi *ha* Tanaka desu (pronouncing は as 'ha') → ✅ は as a particle must be pronounced 'wa'.","❌ 私は学生をです。 → ✅ 私は学生です。 (do not use を with です)","❌ Forgetting か in a question (using an affirmative sentence) → ✅ これは本ですか。 (add か at the end)"],"compare":null}},"n5-001||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture **は** as an arrow pointing to the topic, and **です** as a polite “=” sign. The sentence 「私は田中です」 can be read as: arrow pointing to “I” → equals sign → “Tanaka”.","nuance":"A mental image makes this easier to remember and helps prevent mispronouncing は as 'ha'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-001||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNです","patternReading":"えぬはえぬです","coreMeaning":"N is N — polite assertion","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases in textbooks and daily conversation: introducing yourself, introducing someone else, and asking about a name or object.","nuance":"Learning a whole phrase instead of assembling isolated words helps you respond faster; remember to add か when turning it into a question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am a student.","scene":"Answering in an interview or introducing yourself."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-002||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"core","definition":"**NはNではありません** is the negative form of **NはNです**, used to say that one noun is not another noun. This is a polite negative form introduced early in self-introductions. The speaker denies the subject's label or identity, not its existence.","nuance":"The tone is **polite and gentle**, suitable for teachers, elders, and customers. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often use **じゃありません** instead of **ではありません** because it is shorter and more natural; **ではありません** is slightly formal and common in writing or careful speech.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"am not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am not a student.","scene":"Answering about your occupation in a first class meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あれ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"病院","r":"びょういん","m":"hospital"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is not a hospital.","scene":"Pointing into the distance to correct someone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-002||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connected forms","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the structure and conjugation. **N1 before the predicate** is the topic and always takes **は**. **N2 after it** is the noun being negated and connects directly to **ではありません** without another particle.","nuance":"In rapid speech, **では** often becomes **じゃ**, so **ではありません** → **じゃありません**. Both are polite. **じゃない** is casual and used with friends, not superiors. The polite past form is **ではありませんでした**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 ではありません","note":"Formal, written, explanatory"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 じゃありません","note":"Natural speech, still polite"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 じゃない","note":"Friends, casual"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 ではありませんでした","note":"Polite past"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"じゃありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is not my book.","scene":"Picking up a book and confirming it is not yours"},{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"ではありませんでした","m":"was not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My father was not a teacher before.","scene":"Talking with a friend about your father's past"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-002||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **introduce**, **correct**, or **reject** a noun. It gives a negative answer to the question **NはNですか**.","nuance":"When rejecting a statement, speakers often add **いいえ** at the beginning. Do not use **はい** for a negative answer. The tone is polite rather than harsh; for emphasis, use **ではありません** instead of **じゃありません**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本人","r":"にほんじん","m":"Japanese person"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"am not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, I am not Japanese.","scene":"Explaining you are a foreigner after being asked directions"},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is not my umbrella.","scene":"Seeing someone pick up your umbrella at a restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-002||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with positive/question forms","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with the positive sentence **NはNです**, the question **NはNですか**, and the casual form **Nじゃない**. The key point: **ではありません** negates a noun, opposite to a positive statement or question.","nuance":"In politeness level: **ではありません** > **じゃありません** > **じゃない**. In conversation, **じゃありません** is most natural with new acquaintances; **じゃない** is for close friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is not a book.","scene":"Answering これは本ですか？"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです states 'N is N'; ではありません negates it: 'N is not N.' Example: 私は学生です → 私は学生ではありません."},{"vs":"NはNですか","point":"NはNですか asks 'Is it ~?'; answer negatively with ではありません. Example: A: これは本ですか？ B: いいえ、本ではありません."},{"vs":"Nじゃない","point":"じゃない is the casual shortened form of ではありません; use it with friends, while ではありません is for polite communication."}]}},"n5-002||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes with this pattern: dropping は, omitting では, or adding an extra です. Remember: **noun negation = N + は + N + ではありません** (or じゃありません).","nuance":"These errors make the sentence grammatically wrong or unnatural. Dropping は removes the topic, omitting では breaks the noun-negation structure, and adding です duplicates the polite ending because ありません already functions as one.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is not a student.","scene":"Describing a friend and confirming he is not a student"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私学生ではありません。 → ✅ 私は学生ではありません。","❌ これは本ありません。 → ✅ これは本ではありません。","❌ 学生ではありませんです。 → ✅ 学生ではありません。"],"compare":null}},"n5-002||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember it by breaking it down: **では + ありません** → 'not being'. In fast speech, **では** becomes **じゃ**. Picture a noun card with a **cross through it**.","nuance":"This is a memory aid, not a perfectly literal grammar analysis. The simpler the image, the easier it is to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-002||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNではありません","patternReading":"わではありません","coreMeaning":"It is not ~ (polite noun negation)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in tests and daily life: **いいえ、〜ではありません** (rejection), **これは〜ではありません** (objects), **〜じゃありません** (conversation), and **〜じゃない** (friends).","nuance":"When asked **〜ですか？**, Japanese speakers often answer briefly with **いいえ、〜ではありません** before giving the correct information. Memorizing chunks improves quick response.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"is not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, it is not a restroom.","scene":"Answering それはトイレですか？ by saying it is not a restroom"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-003||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNですか","patternReading":"えぬはえぬですか","coreMeaning":"Yes/No question: Is N N?","slug":"core","definition":"**NはNですか** is a Yes/No question used to confirm information about identity, occupation, objects, and more. Answers commonly use **はい、そうです** (yes) or **いいえ、ちがいます** (no, that's not right). The formula is simply **statement NはNです + か**.","nuance":"This is a **polite** way to ask, suitable for almost anyone, including older people or someone you have just met. Raise your intonation at か, and do not use question words such as 何・誰 because this is a Yes/No question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Are you a student?","scene":"Asking someone you just met at school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this a Japanese book?","scene":"At a bookstore, asking a clerk about a book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-003||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & sentence forms","sub":"NはNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNですか","patternReading":"えぬはえぬですか","coreMeaning":"Yes/No question: Is N N?","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the **structure and form** of the pattern. At N5 level, the pattern is nearly fixed: **N1 は N2 ですか**.","nuance":"The particle は is pronounced **wa** even though it is written は. In this lesson, ですか does not change for past or negative forms; those more complex questions come later.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 ですか","note":"Ask whether N1 is N2"},{"lhs":"はい","rhs":"、そうです。","note":"Affirmative; agree with the question"},{"lhs":"いいえ","rhs":"、ちがいます。","note":"Negative; that is not the case"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん","r":"さん","m":"Mr./Ms."},{"t":"は"},{"t":"会社員","r":"かいしゃいん","m":"company employee"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is Tanaka a company employee?","scene":"Asking someone you just met about their job"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本人","r":"にほんじん","m":"Japanese person"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Are you Japanese?","scene":"Asking a foreign traveler"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-003||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NはNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNですか","patternReading":"えぬはえぬですか","coreMeaning":"Yes/No question: Is N N?","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears often when **greeting someone for the first time** and **confirming an object**. You can use it to ask about nationality or occupation, or point to an object and ask whether it is the one you need.","nuance":"In real conversation, Japanese speakers **often answer briefly** with はい／いいえ rather than repeating the whole sentence. Avoid repeatedly asking あなたは〜ですか, which can feel blunt; use the listener's name + さん instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"トイレ","r":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this the restroom?","scene":"At a restaurant, asking an employee where the restroom is"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ミラー","r":"ミラー","m":"Miller"},{"t":"さん","r":"さん","m":"Mr./Ms."},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is Miller a student?","scene":"Asking a classmate about a foreign friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-003||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from similar patterns","sub":"NはNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNですか","patternReading":"えぬはえぬですか","coreMeaning":"Yes/No question: Is N N?","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch compares NはNですか with easily confused patterns so you can **choose the right form** when expressing a question.","nuance":"A Yes/No question only changes です to ですか; a choice question requires か in both parts and is not answered with はい/いいえ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this your book?","scene":"Finding a book and asking a classmate"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"Statement: 'N is N'. Add か at the end to make it a question."},{"vs":"NはAですか、Bですか","point":"Choice question: 'Is N A or B?' Answer with one of the two, not はい/いいえ."},{"vs":"Nも","point":"Nも means 'N also'; it is not a question and does not need か."}]}},"n5-003||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNですか","patternReading":"えぬはえぬですか","coreMeaning":"Yes/No question: Is N N?","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with this pattern include **omitting か**, giving a **contradictory answer**, or using **statement intonation** instead of the question structure.","nuance":"A polite question cannot rely only on the kind of rising intonation used in English; it needs the particle か at the end. An answer beginning with はい must agree with the question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"be"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this a book?","scene":"Pointing to a book and asking a classmate"}],"pitfalls":["❌ これは本です？ → ✅ これは本ですか。","❌ はい、ちがいます。 → ✅ はい、そうです。／いいえ、ちがいます。","❌ これは本か？ → ✅ これは本ですか。"],"compare":null}},"n5-003||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNですか","patternReading":"えぬはえぬですか","coreMeaning":"Yes/No question: Is N N?","slug":"memory","definition":"A **very simple** memory tip: take any statement NはNです and **attach か** at the end to make a Yes/No question. か is like a spoken '?'","nuance":"か has a gentle, polite nuance. When speaking, say ですか with a slight rise at the end; without it, the listener may think you are making a statement.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-004||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"core","definition":"Nも means **also** and adds a person or thing to an existing context with a similar person or thing. It **replaces** は or が and never appears with them. For example, if Lee is already said to be a student, you can say 私**も**学生です.","nuance":"A neutral particle used with friends, teachers, and coworkers. It is neither formal nor casual. The focus is on “adding,” not on “stating the topic” as with は.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"リー","m":"Lee"},{"t":"さん","m":"Mr./Ms."},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Lee is also a student.","scene":"In class, the teacher introduces each student."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am also a student.","scene":"After Lee introduces themself, you speak next."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-004||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"forms","definition":"Form-wise, も comes **directly after a noun**, before the verb, adjective, or copula. It replaces は or が, so はも/がも **do not exist**. Conjugation remains normal, just as in a sentence with は.","nuance":"Adding も does not change tense, negation, or past meaning. Negative sentences use ではありません/じゃありません; questions use か at the end.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + も + Nです","rhs":"N cũng là N","note":"present affirmative"},{"lhs":"N + も + Nではありません","rhs":"N cũng không phải là N","note":"negative"},{"lhs":"N + も + Nですか","rhs":"N cũng là N à?","note":"question"},{"lhs":"N + も + Vます","rhs":"N cũng làm V","note":"action"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is also mine.","scene":"While sorting things, point to another item."},{"tokens":[{"t":"スミス","m":"Smith"},{"t":"さん","m":"Mr./Ms."},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"アメリカ人","r":"アメリカじん","m":"American"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is Mr. Smith also American?","scene":"Meet two foreigners; know one is American."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-004||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"usage","definition":"Nも appears frequently in conversation when you want to **add** a person, item, or event to the current context. It often follows someone else's mention of one item, as you introduce a second item.","nuance":"In questions, も expresses **mild surprise or confirmation**: “Oh, is this one the same too?” With negation, it means “also not,” as in 私も行きません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Are you going too?","scene":"The group is planning lunch; ask a coworker."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll have this one too.","scene":"At a restaurant, add another item."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't understand either.","scene":"Friends discuss homework; neither understands."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-004||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with は / が","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"compare","definition":"The same sentence can use は, が, or も, but the meaning changes. **は** introduces the topic, **が** emphasizes the subject, and **も** adds an item to the list. Learners most often confuse も by keeping は/が and adding it.","nuance":"「私も学生です」 implies “the other person is also a student.” 「私が学生です」 means “I am the one who is a student,” with no added-item meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is also a student.","scene":"Talking about two friends; the second also studies."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"は states the topic: 私は学生です = I am a student (just talking about me). も adds a subject: 私も学生です = I am also a student (implying someone else is too)."},{"vs":"Nが","point":"が emphasizes the subject: 私が学生です = I am the one who is a student. も does not emphasize; it adds: 私も学生です = I am also a student."}]}},"n5-004||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners may translate “also” and put it in the wrong position, or keep は and add も. Remember: も **replaces** は/が. Also use も only when you truly add an item; for a single statement, use は.","nuance":"私 はも学生です sounds very unnatural in Japanese. When correcting it, remove は or が completely. In negative sentences, も still follows the noun: 私も行きません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This one is delicious too.","scene":"While eating, taste another dish."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私はも学生です。 → ✅ 私も学生です。","❌ これはも私のです。 → ✅ これも私のです。","❌ 今日も寒いです。 (no connection to the previous day) → ✅ 今日は寒いです。","✅ 私も行きません。 (I am not going either)"],"compare":null}},"n5-004||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember も as a **plus sign (+)**: one person or thing is already present, and you add another. When you want to say “that one too,” attach も directly after the noun.","nuance":"Picture two children raising their hands for candy: the first says “Me!”, and the next says “Me **too**!” → 私も! も feels like “adding another matching item.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-004||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nも · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nも","patternReading":"も","coreMeaning":"Also; adds a person/thing, replacing は/が","slug":"phrases","definition":"Some very common expressions with も are **私もそうです** to agree, **これもください** when shopping, and **〜もいいですか** when asking permission to add something.","nuance":"These phrases are short, natural, and used daily. 私もそうです replaces the longer “I feel the same”; 〜もいいですか is often used to ask permission to add something.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"そう","r":"そう","m":"so"},{"t":"です","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Me too.","scene":"A friend says “I'm exhausted!” and you agree."},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","r":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"も","m":"also"},{"t":"お願い","r":"おねがい","m":"request"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll have that one too.","scene":"At a café, add another menu item."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-005||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"core","definition":"The particle の connects **two nouns**. N1 comes before and modifies N2, expressing **N's N** or **N belonging to N**. This is the most basic way to express possession, relationships, and categories in Japanese.","nuance":"の is a neutral particle used in both speech and writing. Its exact meaning depends on context: possession (私の本), affiliation (会社の社員), field (日本語の先生), or location (学校の前).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is my book.","scene":"Introducing your belongings to a new friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","r":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"in"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person is a Japanese teacher.","scene":"Pointing out someone at school."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-005||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"forms","definition":"There is only one form: **N1 + の + N2**. Nouns do not change. In conversation, if the listener already understands N2, it can be **omitted**, leaving **N1 + の**: これは私のです = this is mine.","nuance":"With three nouns, add の step by step: 私の会社の車 = my company's car. The order cannot be reversed, because each の makes the noun before it modify the noun after it.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"のN2","note":"N1 modifies N2"},{"lhs":"誰","rhs":"のNですか","note":"Ask 'whose?', e.g.: 誰の本ですか"},{"lhs":"N1の","rhs":"(lược N2)","note":"When N2 is understood: これは私のです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これは","r":"これは","m":"as for this"},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"かばん","r":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whose bag is this?","scene":"Finding an unfamiliar bag in class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"これは","r":"これは","m":"as for this"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is mine.","scene":"Answering 'Whose is this?'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-005||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"usage","definition":"の is used in many everyday situations: **possession** (私の本), **workplace or organization** (会社の社員), **field** (日本語の先生), **location** (学校の前), and **family relationships** (山田さんの妹).","nuance":"This usage is very natural and common. With people, の can show a relationship: 友達の田中さん = Tanaka, who is my friend. Here N1 modifies N2, translated naturally as 'my friend Tanaka'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"front"},{"t":"には"},{"t":"銀行","r":"ぎんこう","m":"bank"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"exists (thing)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a bank in front of the school.","scene":"Showing a new friend the area around school."},{"tokens":[{"t":"山田","r":"やまだ","m":"Yamada"},{"t":"さん","r":"さん","m":"Mr./Ms."},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"妹","r":"いもうと","m":"younger sister"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yamada's younger sister is a student.","scene":"Asking about a coworker's family."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-005||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions & common mix-ups","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish の from structures often confused with it at N5. **NのN** links two nouns; **NはNです** asserts 'N is N'. **このN** means 'this N' and does not express possession. **Nも** means 'N also' and adds similar information.","nuance":"の and この both appear before nouns but have completely different roles: の is a linking particle, while この is a demonstrative used with a noun. Never add の after この: この本, not このの本.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is my book.","scene":"Answering who owns something with の."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"は marks the topic and です ends the assertion; の only links nouns. 私の本 = my book, while 私は本です = I am a book (wrong meaning)."},{"vs":"このN","point":"この means 'this' and directly points to the noun after it; の shows a relationship between two nouns. この本 = this book; 私の本 = my book."},{"vs":"Nも","point":"も means 'also' and does not create possession. 私も学生です = I am also a student; 私の学生 = my student."}]}},"n5-005||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Errors & traps","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with の include **forgetting の** between two nouns, **adding an extra の** after この/その/あの, or mechanically translating every use as 'of'.","nuance":"English often uses possessives or compound nouns instead of a word like の. Japanese requires の in 私の本, but この, その, and あの are already demonstratives and do not need の.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is mine.","scene":"Claiming ownership of a book."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私本 → ✅ 私の本 (の is required between two nouns)","❌ このの本 → ✅ この本 (この does not take の)","❌ 日本語先生 → ✅ 日本語の先生 (add の to show the field)"],"compare":null}},"n5-005||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture the particle の as a **bridge** joining two nouns: **N1 の N2**. When two nouns appear next to each other, you will usually need の between them to create a relationship.","nuance":"The character の looks like a loop or hooked connector. When you see noun + noun, ask yourself: 'Does this belong to or relate to that?' and place の between them.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-005||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N / N belonging to N","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common の phrases found often in N5 textbooks and daily life include **私の家族** (my family), **学校の先生** (the school's teachers), **日本語の本** (Japanese-language book), and **会社の前** (in front of the company).","nuance":"Memorize these phrases because they appear frequently in listening, reading, and everyday conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の","r":"の","m":"of"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"四人","r":"よにん","m":"four people"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are four people in my family.","scene":"Introducing your family in the first class."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-006||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"core","definition":"These three demonstrative pronouns refer to things or events based on their distance from the speaker and listener. これ = a thing near the speaker, それ = a thing near the listener, and あれ = a thing far from both. They stand alone like nouns and can function as the subject or object.","nuance":"All three are common in both everyday speech and writing. For people, use この人・その人・あの人 (or こちら・そちら・あちら) for more natural and polite speech. Beyond physical distance, それ often refers to something the listener has just mentioned.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Pointing to the book in your hand while introducing it."},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is that?","scene":"Asking a seller about an item near them."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あれ","m":"that over there"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mt. Fuji"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That over there is Mt. Fuji.","scene":"Pointing at a distant mountain beside someone."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-006||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"forms","definition":"Because they are pronouns, これ/それ/あれ do not conjugate; they have no separate negative or past forms. They combine with particles and with the copula です or a sentence-final verb. Main patterns: これ/それ/あれ + は + N + です; これ/それ/あれ + を + Vます.","nuance":"In casual speech, Japanese speakers may omit です (for example, これは本。), but in standard writing and JLPT answers, include the complete form with です.","structure":[{"lhs":"これ・それ・あれ","rhs":"＋は＋N＋です","note":"Identification: 'this/that is ...'"},{"lhs":"これ・それ・あれ","rhs":"＋は＋N＋ですか","note":"Question: 'is this/that ...?'"},{"lhs":"これ・それ・あれ","rhs":"＋は＋N＋ではありません","note":"Negation: 'this/that is not ...'"},{"lhs":"これ・それ・あれ","rhs":"＋を＋Vます","note":"As an object: '...' or buy/take ..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いくら","m":"how much"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How much is this?","scene":"Holding an item and asking the seller."},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'll take that, please.","scene":"Pointing to an item near the seller."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-006||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"usage","definition":"The basic use is to locate a thing in relation to the people in the conversation. Something I am holding or own → これ. Something on the listener's side → それ. Something far from both → あれ. In conversation, それ can also refer to an opinion or story the listener has just mentioned.","nuance":"These words are neutral: neither formal nor overly casual. For people, use この方・その方（polite）or この人・その人（casual）to avoid sounding rude.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is my book.","scene":"Showing a friend your book."},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あなた","m":"your"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is that your pen?","scene":"Seeing a pen near you and asking if it is yours."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あれ","m":"that over there"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That over there is the station.","scene":"Pointing out a distant station while walking."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-006||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing この/その/あの","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"compare","definition":"These three pairs are easy to confuse with これ/それ/あれ. Understanding each type helps you choose the right word.","nuance":"これ/それ/あれ cannot come before a noun, while この/その/あの must be followed by one. ここ/そこ/あそこ refer to places, not objects. こちら/そちら/あちら are more polite forms, often used for people or directions.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Pointing to a book nearby to contrast it with この本."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"この／その／あの","point":"これ/それ/あれ stand alone, while この/その/あの always accompany a noun. Example: これは本です (this is a book) / この本は新しいです (this book is new)."},{"vs":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","point":"これ/それ/あれ refer to things, while ここ/そこ/あそこ refer to places. Example: これは机です (this is a desk) / ここは教室です (this is the classroom)."},{"vs":"こちら／そちら／あちら","point":"これ/それ/あれ are neutral, while こちら/そちら/あちら are polite forms used for people or directions. Example: これは田中さんです (not for introducing people) / こちらは田中さんです (this is Mr. Tanaka, politely)."}]}},"n5-006||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Here are typical mistakes learners make when using これ/それ/あれ. Pay attention to word position, particles, and physical distance.","nuance":"The most serious mistake is placing これ directly before a noun, because これ is an independent pronoun. To modify a noun, use この・その・あの. Also remember です at the end in standard writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is my book.","scene":"Using the correct sentence to avoid omitting です."}],"pitfalls":["❌ これ本です。 → ✅ この本です。（これ cannot come before a noun）","❌ これ私の本です。 → ✅ これは私の本です。（missing topic particle は）","❌ それは遠くの山。 → ✅ あれは遠くの山。（use あれ for something far from both）","❌ これは鈴木さんです（introducing someone nearby） → ✅ この方は鈴木さんです。（use この+person to introduce someone）"],"compare":null}},"n5-006||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: connect the three words to three distances. Kore resembles “here”—the thing I am holding. Sore resembles “there”—the thing near the listener. Are resembles “over there”—the thing beyond both people's reach. From near to far, the order is always これ → それ → あれ.","nuance":"This is a visual memory aid, not a grammar rule. Build quick recall by pointing to real objects.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-006||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"これ／それ／あれ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"これ／それ／あれ","patternReading":"これ／それ／あれ","coreMeaning":"Point to things by distance: near me, near you, or far from both","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are set phrases used with これ/それ/あれ in shopping, information-seeking, and requests. They appear often in textbooks and everyday conversation.","nuance":"All are common polite speech and can be used with older people or acquaintances. For highly formal situations, switch to こちら・そちら・あちら.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いくら","m":"how much"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How much is that?","scene":"Asking the price of an item the seller is holding."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-007||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"この／その／あのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"この／その／あのN","patternReading":"この・その・あの","coreMeaning":"This / that / that over there + noun","slug":"core","definition":"この／その／あの are **demonstrative adjectives** placed BEFORE a noun to identify 'which person/thing' in space or conversation. この = near the speaker, その = near the listener, あの = far from both. Unlike これ／それ／あれ, these three words MUST be followed by a noun.","nuance":"Used in both **spoken and written language**, with polite tone when paired with です/ます. They simply indicate distance, without special emphasis. Distance can be physical (within reach) or psychological (mentioned earlier).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"面白い","r":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is interesting.","scene":"Holding the book on the table"},{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いくら","m":"how much"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How much is that bag?","scene":"Asking the price of the bag the clerk is holding"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that over there"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Who is that person over there?","scene":"Seeing someone far across the street"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-007||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"この／その／あのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"この／その／あのN","patternReading":"この・その・あの","coreMeaning":"This / that / that over there + noun","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **この/その/あの ＋ noun**. This is a **noun modifier** and never stands alone. It does not change for tense or number; tense and politeness appear at the end of the sentence (です／ます...).","nuance":"In casual speech, です may be omitted: 「この本、面白い？」. Polite writing still uses です/ます. この/その/あの have no separate negative form—the predicate is negated: この本は**面白くない**です.","structure":[{"lhs":"この","rhs":"N","note":"This N — near speaker"},{"lhs":"その","rhs":"N","note":"That N — near listener"},{"lhs":"あの","rhs":"N","note":"That N over there — far from both"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"かわいい","m":"cute"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cat is cute.","scene":"Petting your cat"},{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That car is new.","scene":"Complimenting your friend's car"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that over there"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That shop over there is expensive.","scene":"Pointing to the distant shop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-007||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"この／その／あのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"この／その／あのN","patternReading":"この・その・あの","coreMeaning":"This / that / that over there + noun","slug":"usage","definition":"These three words **point to a specific person or thing** and provide information about it or ask about it. この is for something beside the speaker; その for something beside the listener; あの for something far from both. Beyond physical distance, they can refer to something 'known' such as あの日 (that day).","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often use **あのN** for something both people know: 「あの店、行った？」. このN can feel familiar or speaker-centered. Do not use この/その/あの as a whole-sentence substitute; a noun must follow.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ラーメン","m":"ramen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This ramen is delicious, isn't it?","scene":"Eating ramen with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please give me that book.","scene":"Pointing to a book on the seller's table"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that over there"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is that person over there a teacher?","scene":"Asking about someone in the distance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-007||compare":{"title":"⚖️ この／その／あの vs これ／それ／あれ","sub":"この／その／あのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"この／その／あのN","patternReading":"この・その・あの","coreMeaning":"This / that / that over there + noun","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch clarifies two commonly confused sets: **これ／それ／あれ** (pronouns that stand alone as subjects) and **この／その／あのN** (noun modifiers). They express similar distance, but have **different grammatical roles**.","nuance":"To say 'this is a book,' use **これ**: これは本です. To say 'this book is interesting,' use **この**: この本は面白いです. このは本です is wrong because この needs a following noun.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that over there"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That shop over there is cheap.","scene":"Pointing to the distant shop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"これ／それ／あれ","point":"Stand ALONE as the subject: これは本です。／ この/その/あの ALWAYS need a noun: この本は面白いです。"},{"vs":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","point":"Point to PLACES: ここは図書館です。 このN points to a person/thing: この建物は図書館です。"}]}},"n5-007||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"この／その／あのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"この／その／あのN","patternReading":"この・その・あの","coreMeaning":"This / that / that over there + noun","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: **using この/その/あの without a noun**, **misjudging distance**, and **confusing them with location words**.","nuance":"These errors often come from translating 'this/that' mechanically while forgetting the noun-modifying rule. Check: Is there a noun after この/その/あの? How far is it from the speaker and listener?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please give me that book.","scene":"Pointing to a book near the listener"}],"pitfalls":["❌ このは本です。 → ✅ これは本です。 (この needs a noun, so use これ)","❌ Pointing to something near the listener but saying: あの本はだれのですか。 → ✅ その本はだれのですか。","❌ このは図書館です。 → ✅ ここは図書館です。／ この建物は図書館です。"],"compare":null}},"n5-007||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"この／その／あのN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"この／その／あのN","patternReading":"この・その・あの","coreMeaning":"This / that / that over there + noun","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory aid based on **pointing fingers** and **initial sounds**: K (ko) means near me, S (so) means near the other person, and A (a) means far away. The の reminds you that a noun must follow.","nuance":"You do not need to memorize abstract theory; imagine **pointing to something** in a classroom. Each time, check where you and the listener are standing.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-008||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"そうです／そうではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"そうです／そうではありません","patternReading":"そうです／そうではありません","coreMeaning":"Confirming or denying a yes/no question without repeating the sentence","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern そうです／そうではありません is used to **confirm or deny** a yes/no question. Instead of repeating the entire question, the speaker uses そうです = 'that's right' or そうではありません = 'that's not right' for a brief, natural answer.","nuance":"This is a **polite, neutral** response suitable for elders, teachers, and customers. A more conversational variant is そうじゃありません. そうです agrees with the entire content of the question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はい","r":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"that's right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, that's right.","scene":"Asked 'これはあなたのペンですか' (Is this your pen?)"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","r":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうではありません","r":"そうではありません","m":"that's not right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, that's not right.","scene":"Asked 'あなたは学生ですか'; you are not a student."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-008||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and patterns","sub":"そうです／そうではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"そうです／そうではありません","patternReading":"そうです／そうではありません","coreMeaning":"Confirming or denying a yes/no question without repeating the sentence","slug":"forms","definition":"The polite affirmative is **そうです**; the negative is **そうではありません** (polite) or **そうじゃありません** (more conversational). Among friends, the plain forms **そうだ／そうじゃない** are used. **そうですか** expresses a reaction or asks for confirmation.","nuance":"ではありません is more formal and common in polite speech; じゃありません is shorter and natural in everyday conversation; じゃない is casual and used with friends and family.","structure":[{"lhs":"Câu hỏi CÓ/KHÔNG","rhs":"→ はい、そうです。／いいえ、そうではありません。"},{"lhs":"Khẳng định (lịch sự)","rhs":"そうです"},{"lhs":"Phủ định (lịch sự)","rhs":"そうではありません → そうじゃありません"},{"lhs":"Phủ định (thân mật)","rhs":"そうじゃない"},{"lhs":"Phản ứng/hỏi lại","rhs":"そうですか"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はい","r":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"that's right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, that's right.","scene":"A customer asks 'こちらは予約席ですか' (Is this a reserved seat?)"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","r":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうではありません","r":"そうではありません","m":"that's not right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, that's not right.","scene":"Politely answering a teacher: 'あなたは先生ですか'?"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","r":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうじゃありません","r":"そうじゃありません","m":"that's not right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, that's not right.","scene":"A close friend asks 'これ、あなたのかばん？'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-008||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"そうです／そうではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"そうです／そうではありません","patternReading":"そうです／そうではありません","coreMeaning":"Confirming or denying a yes/no question without repeating the sentence","slug":"usage","definition":"**そうです／そうではありません** answers yes/no questions (NはNですか). Also, **そうですか** is a natural reaction to new information: falling intonation means 'I see,' while rising intonation means 'Really?'.","nuance":"Do not use this pattern to answer questions containing question words such as だれ・いつ・どこ, because those questions do not ask for true/false confirmation. To react to new information, use そうですか instead of repeating the sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はい","r":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"that's right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, that's right.","scene":"A shop clerk asks 'こちら、お客様のですか'."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ああ","r":"ああ","m":"oh"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうですか","r":"そうですか","m":"I see"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Oh, I see.","scene":"A friend says Saturday is a day off."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あっ","r":"あっ","m":"oh"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうですか","r":"そうですか","m":"really"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Really?","scene":"Hearing that the company is closed tomorrow."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-008||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with NはNです","sub":"そうです／そうではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"そうです／そうではありません","patternReading":"そうです／そうではありません","coreMeaning":"Confirming or denying a yes/no question without repeating the sentence","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse **そうです** with **NはNです**. Both can affirm, but **NはNです** highlights a specific noun (subject plus complement), while **そうです** simply **confirms the entire question** without repeating its content.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, そうです is much more natural than repeating the entire sentence. **そうではありません** denies the whole question; **NはNではありません** directly denies a specific noun and is useful when you need to clarify what something is not.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"はい","r":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうです","r":"そうです","m":"that's right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this book yours? — Yes, that's right.","scene":"Confirming who owns a book."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです affirms a specific noun (これは本です); そうです is a shortened confirmation. A: それは本ですか。 B: はい、そうです。 — shorter and more natural than はい、それは本です。"},{"vs":"NはNではありません","point":"NはNではありません directly denies a noun (学生ではありません); そうではありません denies the whole statement just asked. For 'あなたは学生ですか', both work, but そうではありません is broader."}]}},"n5-008||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"そうです／そうではありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"そうです／そうではありません","patternReading":"そうです／そうではありません","coreMeaning":"Confirming or denying a yes/no question without repeating the sentence","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistake is **denying with そうです**: saying 'いいえ、そうです'. Another is writing 'そうですではない'. Remember the standard pair: affirmative **そうです**, negative **そうではありません／そうじゃありません**.","nuance":"Before learning ちがいます, そうじゃありません is a safe and polite choice. In speech, repeating 'はい、これは本です' sounds mechanical; 'はい、そうです' is more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","r":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そうじゃありません","r":"そうじゃありません","m":"that's not right"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, that's not right.","scene":"A standard answer when denying a question."}],"pitfalls":["❌ いいえ、そうです。 → ✅ いいえ、そうではありません。","❌ そうですではないです。 → ✅ そうではありません。","❌ A: これは本ですか。 B: はい、そうです本です。 → ✅ はい、そうです。","❌ A: これはだれの本ですか。 B: はい、そうです。 → ✅ Do not use this pattern for question-word questions."],"compare":null}},"n5-009||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"core","definition":"〜の〜 is the most basic **possession** pattern in Japanese. The noun before の is the **owner**, and the noun after it is the thing owned. For example, 私の傘 means “my umbrella,” and マリアさんの自転車 means “Maria’s bicycle.”","nuance":"This neutral structure works in any situation, from casual to formal. It does not change for tense or negation. Learners should remember that Japanese の usually must remain; unlike English, Japanese cannot freely omit it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I / me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is my umbrella.","scene":"Introducing your belongings in class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"それ","m":"that"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"マリアさん","m":"Maria"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"自転車","r":"じてんしゃ","m":"bicycle"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is that Maria’s bicycle?","scene":"Asking about a bicycle by the door"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"電話番号","r":"でんわばんごう","m":"phone number"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何番","r":"なんばん","m":"which number"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is Mr. Tanaka’s phone number?","scene":"Asking for a coworker’s phone number"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-009||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"forms","definition":"Formally, simply place の between two nouns: N1 の N2. N1 is usually a person or pronoun, while N2 is the thing or person being discussed. の **never changes form**; it remains the same in present, past, and negative sentences. The whole noun phrase can serve as a subject.","nuance":"Do not add another particle between N1 and N2. Japanese encodes possession with の, so when translating “my” or “your,” form 私の or あなたの before adding the noun.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1（người/đại từ）","rhs":"のN2","note":"N1 owns N2; N2 belongs to N1"},{"lhs":"N1のN2","rhs":"は N3 です","note":"Statement: N1’s N2 is N3"},{"lhs":"N1のN2","rhs":"は N3 ですか","note":"Question: Is N1’s N2 N3?"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I / me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother is a teacher.","scene":"Talking about your mother’s job"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Whose book is this?","scene":"Asking who owns a book found in class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-009||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"usage","definition":"の is used not only for objects but also for **familiar relationships** such as family, friends, schools, and workplaces. To ask “whose,” place the question word 誰の before the noun. An answer can be shortened to 私のです instead of repeating the noun.","nuance":"In daily conversation, phrases like 私の家 and 私の猫 sound completely natural. The particle の is required to connect nouns in a possessive relationship; it is not an optional politeness marker.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"山田","r":"やまだ","m":"Yamada"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"お兄さん","r":"おにいさん","m":"older brother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"会社員","r":"かいしゃいん","m":"company employee"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Yamada’s older brother is a company employee.","scene":"Introducing a friend’s family member"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I / me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"小さい","r":"ちいさい","m":"small"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My cat is small.","scene":"Showing a friend your cat’s photo"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-009||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish の, この, and これ","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch prevents confusion between possessive の and the **demonstratives** この/これ. この must accompany a noun and means “this N” (indicating location); possessive の means “whose.” これ is a pronoun that stands alone and cannot come before a noun.","nuance":"In English, both “this” and “my” can come before a noun, so learners may form これの本. Japanese does not say this; use この本 for “this book” or 私の本 for “my book.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I / me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is mine.","scene":"Confirming that the book is yours"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"この／その／あのN","point":"Indicates LOCATION: “this/that/that over there”; it does not identify the owner. Example: この本 = this book."},{"vs":"これ／それ／あれ","point":"A pronoun replacing the whole noun, so it stands alone; before a noun use この/その/あの, not の."}]}},"n5-009||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The three most common mistakes are **omitting の** (because English can say “my mother” without “of”), using これの instead of この, and unnecessarily repeating a noun in an answer. Follow the formula: owner + の + noun.","nuance":"The possessive particle の is grammatically required in both everyday speech and writing. Omitting の between two nouns, as in 私本, is completely ungrammatical, not merely casual.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that over there"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"山田","r":"やまだ","m":"Yamada"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That car is Mr. Yamada’s.","scene":"Pointing to a car parked outside"}],"pitfalls":["❌ これは私本です。 → ✅ これは私の本です。","❌ これの本は私のです。 → ✅ この本は私のです。","❌ これは山田さん車です。 → ✅ これは山田さんの車です。"],"compare":null}},"n5-009||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine の as a **string** connecting the owner to the thing owned. 私 comes first and 傘 comes after it: 私の傘. Read it directly as “my umbrella.”","nuance":"You do not need a complex rule; learn の phrases like vocabulary. The more you repeat “N1 の N2 = N2 belonging to N1,” the less often you will forget の.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-009||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NのN (sở hữu) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NのN (sở hữu)","patternReading":"の","coreMeaning":"N's N (possession)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These の phrases appear frequently in N5 conversation. Learning them by heart helps you answer questions, introduce yourself, and talk about family and friends.","nuance":"All of these phrases are neutral and can be used with someone you have just met. When talking about your own family, add 私の to make the relationship clear.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I / me"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"四人","r":"よにん","m":"four people"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are four people in my family.","scene":"Introducing your family to someone new"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-010||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"core","definition":"ここ/そこ/あそこ are demonstrative words for **places**, showing location relative to the speaker and listener: **ここ** means near the speaker, **そこ** means near the listener, and **あそこ** means far from both. They can function as the **subject** or **complement** of a sentence.","nuance":"Basic, neutral vocabulary used in both speech and writing. It carries no emotional nuance. For more **polite** speech, use こちら/そちら/あちら. The distance contrast is relative: ここ = near me, そこ = near you, あそこ = far from both.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a classroom.","scene":"Introducing the classroom while standing inside"},{"tokens":[{"t":"そこ","m":"there"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is the restroom.","scene":"Showing a guest the restroom in a café"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あそこ","m":"over there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a dog over there.","scene":"Spotting a dog far away in the park"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-010||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"forms","definition":"The four place words are **ここ** (near the speaker), **そこ** (near the listener), **あそこ** (far from both), and **どこ** (question word). They do not change form and combine with particles は, に, で, へ, から, and まで for different grammatical roles.","nuance":"In polite contexts such as with customers or superiors, use こちら/そちら/あちら. With friends and family, use ここ/そこ/あそこ. The following particle determines the meaning: に = location of existence, で = location of an action.","structure":[{"lhs":"ここ","rhs":"gần người nói"},{"lhs":"そこ","rhs":"gần người nghe"},{"lhs":"あそこ","rhs":"xa cả hai"},{"lhs":"どこ","rhs":"hỏi nơi chốn"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"郵便局","r":"ゆうびんきょく","m":"post office"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is the post office.","scene":"Introducing the post office nearby"},{"tokens":[{"t":"そこ","m":"there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a cat there.","scene":"The cat is near the listener"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あそこ","m":"over there"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That is a park over there.","scene":"Pointing to a distant park"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-010||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"usage","definition":"Main uses: (1) **Pointing out a location** in answer to どこ; (2) **Introducing a place**: ここは〜です; (3) **Action location**: ここで〜ます; (4) **Existence location**: ここに〜があります／います.","nuance":"Distinguish **ここは** (this is, introducing a place) from **ここに** (there is something here). **ここで** marks the place of an action, while **ここへ** marks a destination. In conversation, simply answering あそこです is enough.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あそこ","m":"over there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a convenience store over there.","scene":"Pointing to a distant convenience store"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat lunch here.","scene":"Answering a coworker who asks where you eat lunch"},{"tokens":[{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あそこ","m":"over there"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The restroom is over there.","scene":"Giving directions to a guest in a restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-010||compare":{"title":"⚖️ ここ／そこ／あそこ vs これ／それ／あれ","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **ここ/そこ/あそこ** (places) from **これ/それ/あれ** (objects, people, or things). For polite speech, use **こちら/そちら/あちら**.","nuance":"ここ means “this place,” while これ means “this thing.” In “What is this?”, use これは何ですか when pointing to an **object**, but ここはどこですか when asking about a **place**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is the station.","scene":"Introducing the station while standing in front of it"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"これ／それ／あれ","point":"これ refers to an object/person; ここ refers to a place. Compare: これは本です (this is a book) vs ここは教室です (this is a classroom)."},{"vs":"こちら／そちら／あちら","point":"こちら is more polite than ここ and is common in offices and hotels. Compare: ここに置いてください vs こちらに置いてください (please place it here)."},{"vs":"あれ","point":"あれ means “that object,” while あそこ means “that place.” Compare: あれは何ですか (what is that?) vs あそこはどこですか (where is that place?)."}]}},"n5-010||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using **ここ** for both objects and places, using **ここ** in polite situations, and omitting the particle when saying that something exists somewhere.","nuance":"The most common error is confusing ここ with これ when pointing to an object. First identify whether you mean a thing or a place. In polite settings, use こちら instead of ここ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に","m":"at"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a cat here.","scene":"A grammatically correct sentence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ここはりんごです。 → ✅ これはりんごです。 (this is an apple - an object)","❌ ここでお待ちください。 → ✅ こちらでお待ちください。 (polite language for customers)","❌ ここ本があります。 → ✅ ここに本があります。 (missing に for the existence location)","❌ あそこで犬がいます。 → ✅ あそこに犬がいます。 (existence verbs use に, not で)"],"compare":null}},"n5-010||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"memory","definition":"A sound-based memory aid: **こ** is near me, like “co” in “come here”; **そ** is near you, like “so” when addressing you; **あ** signals distance, like an “ah” of surprise when seeing something far away.","nuance":"Link these to これ/それ/あれ for objects and この/その/あの for noun modifiers. They belong to the same demonstrative system, so their distance pattern is easy to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-010||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"ここ／そこ／あそこ · N5","v2":{"pattern":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","patternReading":"ここ・そこ・あそこ","coreMeaning":"Here / there / over there (places)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ここ/そこ/あそこ that appear in tests and everyday conversation.","nuance":"These phrases illustrate key patterns for asking permission, pointing out a location, and describing something visible in the distance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"すわって","r":"すわって","m":"sit"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","m":"okay"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"}],"vi":"May I sit here?","scene":"Asking in a café"},{"tokens":[{"t":"そこ","m":"there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is there.","scene":"Answering where an object is"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あそこ","m":"over there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見えます","r":"みえます","m":"visible"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You can see a mountain over there.","scene":"Looking into the distance from a balcony"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-011||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"こちら／そちら／あちら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"こちら／そちら／あちら","patternReading":"こちら・そちら・あちら","coreMeaning":"This side / that side / over there (polite; for people and places)","slug":"core","definition":"こちら・そちら・あちら are the **polite forms** of ここ・そこ・あそこ. They refer to a **location** or a **person** in polite situations such as offices and shops. They can politely refer to both “this place” and “this person.”","nuance":"Used in **polite, formal contexts** such as shops, offices, and customer service. They sound overly formal with friends. When referring to a person, こちら shows **respect** toward the person being introduced.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お手洗い","r":"おてあらい","m":"restroom"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"this side"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The restroom is this way.","scene":"A restaurant worker gives a customer directions"},{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"this person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is Mr. Tanaka.","scene":"Introducing a business partner to a colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-011||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & forms","sub":"こちら／そちら／あちら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"こちら／そちら／あちら","patternReading":"こちら・そちら・あちら","coreMeaning":"This side / that side / over there (polite; for people and places)","slug":"forms","definition":"Main structure: こちら（そちら・あちら）＋ です／ではありません／でした. To specify the item, use Nはこちらです or こちらはNです. These are demonstrative nouns and do not change with the verb.","nuance":"This is **beginner N5 grammar**, used in affirmative, negative, and question sentences with です. The polite negative is usually ではありません; じゃありません is somewhat more casual.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"はこちらです","note":"States that N is this way"},{"lhs":"こちら","rhs":"はNです","note":"Introduces a person or location as N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"はこちらですか","note":"Asks whether N is this way"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"レジ","r":"レジ","m":"checkout counter"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あちら","r":"あちら","m":"over there"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The checkout counter is over there.","scene":"A supermarket worker points it out to a customer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"this is"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あなた","r":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is your seat.","scene":"A teacher guides a student into class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-011||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"こちら／そちら／あちら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"こちら／そちら／あちら","patternReading":"こちら・そちら・あちら","coreMeaning":"This side / that side / over there (polite; for people and places)","slug":"usage","definition":"Used in **polite communication**, such as giving customers directions, introducing people at work, and confirming locations in shops. こちら / そちら can also refer to the **speaker’s side / listener’s side** in business communication.","nuance":"こちらへどうぞ is a polite invitation. こちらこそ is a polite reply to thanks or an apology. In offices, こちら can refer to “our company,” while そちら can refer to “your company.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"this way"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"どうぞ","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please come this way.","scene":"A receptionist leads a visitor to a meeting room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"I"},{"t":"こそ","r":"こそ","m":"rather"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, thank you; I should be thanking you.","scene":"Replying when thanked by a client"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-011||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"こちら／そちら／あちら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"こちら／そちら／あちら","patternReading":"こちら・そちら・あちら","coreMeaning":"This side / that side / over there (polite; for people and places)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish these forms to avoid mistakes in polite communication. **ここ/そこ/あそこ** are casual and refer to **places**, not people. **これ/それ/あれ** refer to **objects**, while **この/その/あの** must be followed by a noun.","nuance":"This comparison is limited to N5 basics. When introducing a person, use こちら, not ここ or これ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is Tokyo Station.","scene":"Showing a map to a tourist"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"ここ／そこ／あそこ","point":"Casual; refers to PLACES, not PEOPLE. こちら/そちら/あちら are polite and can refer to both PEOPLE and places."},{"vs":"これ／それ／あれ","point":"Refers to OBJECTS, not people or places. こちら/そちら/あちら refer to a DIRECTION, PLACE, or PERSON."},{"vs":"この／その／あの","point":"Modifies the noun that follows (この本 = this book). こちら/そちら/あちら stand alone as nouns."}]}},"n5-011||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"こちら／そちら／あちら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"こちら／そちら／あちら","patternReading":"こちら・そちら・あちら","coreMeaning":"This side / that side / over there (polite; for people and places)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use **ここ** for both places and people, or use **これ** for objects. Pay attention to formality and scope: こちら/そちら/あちら are used for **people and places** in polite contexts.","nuance":"こちら sounds very formal and unnatural with friends. Use **ここ** or **こっち** in casual conversation. When introducing a person, use こちら, never ここ or これ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","r":"こちら","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"受付","r":"うけつけ","m":"reception desk"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is the reception desk.","scene":"Someone points out the reception desk"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ここは社長です → ✅ こちらは社長です (Use こちら, not ここ, to introduce a person)","❌ ここへどうぞ (to a person) → ✅ こちらへどうぞ (Use こちら when inviting someone this way)","❌ こちらは本です → ✅ これは本です (Use これ, not こちら, for an ordinary object)","❌ そこはお元気ですか → ✅ そちらはお元気ですか (Use そちら, not そこ, when asking about someone)"],"compare":null}},"n5-011||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"こちら／そちら／あちら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"こちら／そちら／あちら","patternReading":"こちら・そちら・あちら","coreMeaning":"This side / that side / over there (polite; for people and places)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **こ・そ・あ** + **ちら** (direction) creates polite words for direction and place. **ここ→こちら, そこ→そちら, あそこ→あちら**.","nuance":"The longer form こちら sounds more polite than the shorter ここ. Think of “こちら” as “this side”; when it comes before an introduced person, it can mean “this person.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-012||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **N1 は N2 に あります／います** describes the location of a person, animal, or object. Use あります for inanimate things (books, tables, stations, etc.) and います for people and animals. The particle に marks the location noun.","nuance":"Neutral and polite; suitable when speaking to older people or superiors. This objective pattern works well when answering “where?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"机","r":"つくえ","m":"desk"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ","m":"top"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The book is on the desk.","scene":"In class, the teacher asks where the book is"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"事務所","r":"じむしょ","m":"office"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka is in the office.","scene":"At work, you ask about Tanaka and reception points to the office"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-012||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure has three parts: **topic (は) + location (に) + existence verb**. あります (group I) and います (group II) both use polite ます forms. For the negative, replace ます with ません: ありません／いません. For the past: ありました／いました.","nuance":"In casual speech, Japanese speakers often use short forms (ある／いる), but at N5, use the ます forms for politeness and safety.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 (object) ＋ は","rhs":"N2 に あります","note":"Example: 本は机の上にあります。"},{"lhs":"N1 (person/animal) ＋ は","rhs":"N2 に います","note":"Example: 先生は教室にいます。"},{"lhs":"N1 は N2 に","rhs":"ありますか／いますか","note":"Location question; answer: はい／いいえ."},{"lhs":"N1 は N2 に","rhs":"ありません／ありませんでした","note":"Present / past negative for objects."},{"lhs":"N1 は N2 に","rhs":"いません／いませんでした","note":"Present / past negative for people and animals."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"2階","r":"にかい","m":"second floor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The restroom is on the second floor.","scene":"In a building, a visitor asks for the restroom"},{"tokens":[{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"庭","r":"にわ","m":"yard"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The dog is outside in the yard.","scene":"Mother calls the child to eat and says the dog is in the yard"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-012||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **ask or state the specific location** of an object, person, or animal. It is common in daily conversation: asking directions, finding things, and locating people. To ask where something is, use **〜はどこにありますか／いますか**.","nuance":"When the subject is already identified and marked with は, the question checks its location. To introduce something newly found, Japanese uses が instead (see the comparison section).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, where is the station?","scene":"A tourist asks for directions to the station"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher is in the classroom.","scene":"A student asks where the teacher is at school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ソファ","m":"sofa"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"下","r":"した","m":"under"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The cat is under the sofa.","scene":"In the living room, a child tells their mother about the cat"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-012||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"compare","definition":"**NはNにあります／います** specifies a location, while **NはNです** expresses identity or a characteristic. **NがNにあります** introduces something as existing at a location (new information, so が is used). **ここ/そこ/あそこ** are place pronouns that can replace a location noun phrase (with に).","nuance":"The は/が distinction is crucial: は marks a known topic; が introduces new information, often in answers to “what is there?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where is the library?","scene":"At a university, a student asks staff for the library"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです defines or identifies 'N is N'; NはNにあります gives a location. Example: これは本です (this is a book) ≠ 本は机の上にあります (the book is on the desk)."},{"vs":"NがNにあります","point":"NがNにあります introduces a newly noticed object; NはNにあります describes a known object’s location. Example: 机の上に本があります (there is a book on the desk), mentioned for the first time; 本は机の上にあります (the book is on the desk), when the book and desk are known."},{"vs":"ここ/そこ/あそこ","point":"These can replace 'N2' in the pattern. Example: 本はここにあります = 本は机の上にあります (if 'here' means 'on the desk')."}]}},"n5-012||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include **using the wrong existence verb**, **omitting に**, and **confusing は with が**. Remember: inanimate things → あります; people and animals → います; locations need に before the verb.","nuance":"Beginners often make these errors because English does not distinguish existence verbs for objects and living beings.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"鍵","r":"かぎ","m":"key"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The key is here.","scene":"You find the key and tell someone else"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 本は机の上にいます。 → ✅ 本は机の上にあります。","❌ 犬は庭にあります。 → ✅ 犬は庭にいます。","❌ 本は机の上あります。 → ✅ 本は机の上にあります。","❌ 部屋は机にあります。 → ✅ 机は部屋にあります。（The object and location are reversed）","❌ 駅はどこにいますか。 → ✅ 駅はどこにありますか。"],"compare":null}},"n5-012||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **あります** starts with “a,” like the “a” in “inanimate”; **います** starts with “i,” like English “I.” Or simply remember: あります is for objects, while います is for living beings—people and animals.","nuance":"No special nuance; this is just a playful memory aid.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-012||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNにあります／います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNにあります／います","patternReading":"にあります／にいます","coreMeaning":"Things/people at location N; inanimate → あります, people/animals → います","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common everyday phrases include **どこにありますか** (where is it?), **ここにあります** (it is here), and **あそこにいます** (they are over there). They appear constantly in daily conversation and on the JLPT N5.","nuance":"For extra politeness, you can add '〜', but this level is sufficient for N5.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"コピー機","r":"こぴーき","m":"copier"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"あります","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, where is the copier?","scene":"At work, a new employee asks where the copier is"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-013||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern asks the listener to choose **one of two options: A or B**. Unlike NはNですか (yes/no), it requires a clear choice. It translates as “Is N A or B?”","nuance":"A direct, neutral question suitable for anyone. It shows no strong emotion. In casual conversation, ですか may be omitted from the first clause, but standard written Japanese keeps it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"エレベーター","m":"elevator"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"1階","r":"いっかい","m":"first floor"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"2階","r":"にかい","m":"second floor"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is the elevator on the first or second floor?","scene":"Asking hotel staff which floor the elevator is on"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本人","r":"にほんじん","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"中国人","r":"ちゅうごくじん","m":"Chinese"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is Tanaka Japanese or Chinese?","scene":"Meeting someone whose nationality is unclear"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お支払い","r":"おしはらい","m":"payment"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"現金","r":"げんきん","m":"cash"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"カード","m":"card"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Will you pay with cash or a card?","scene":"A cashier asking a customer how they will pay"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-013||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **N (topic) + は + A + ですか + 、 + B + ですか**. A and B are two options, usually nouns. At N5, learn the polite question form with です; negative and past forms are not covered here.","nuance":"In speech, ですか may be omitted from the first clause: “NはA、Bですか?”. For exam safety, keep both ですか endings.","structure":[{"lhs":"Nは","rhs":"Aですか、Bですか","note":"Standard pattern: two clauses joined by a comma"},{"lhs":"A・B","rhs":"Danh từ hoặc na-adj (N5)","note":"Both must be the same type; usually nouns for people, things, or places"},{"lhs":"ですか","rhs":"、ですか","note":"Keep ですか in both clauses; the comma expresses “or”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ノート","m":"notebook"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this a book or a notebook?","scene":"Unsure what the object in front of you is"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"3時","r":"さんじ","m":"3 o’clock"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"4時","r":"よじ","m":"4 o’clock"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is the meeting at three or four?","scene":"A coworker confirming the scheduled meeting time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-013||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker limits the answer to **two specific possibilities**. It commonly appears in daily conversation when asking about floors, nationality, payment methods, dates, or times.","nuance":"The questioner usually already knows the basic context and only needs to confirm one of two options. It is polite without being overly formal or casual.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"うどん","m":"udon"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"そば","m":"soba"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is lunch udon or soba?","scene":"Asking a coworker during the lunch break"},{"tokens":[{"t":"佐藤さん","r":"さとうさん","m":"Sato"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is Sato a student or a teacher?","scene":"Meeting someone whose occupation is unclear"},{"tokens":[{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"1階","r":"いっかい","m":"first floor"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"2階","r":"にかい","m":"second floor"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is the restroom on the first or second floor?","scene":"Asking staff at a shopping mall"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-013||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with NはNですか","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish this pattern from three easily confused forms: a yes/no question, a noun listing with NかN, and a negative statement.","nuance":"Remember: this pattern has **two ですか** endings and a **comma**, requiring a choice. NはNですか has only one ですか, while NかN is not a question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"鉛筆","r":"えんぴつ","m":"pencil"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ボールペン","m":"ballpoint pen"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is this a pencil or a ballpoint pen?","scene":"Holding a pen and unsure which type it is"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNですか","point":"NはNですか confirms one option and takes はい/いいえ. Example: これは本ですか？ → はい、本です。 This pattern asks two choices, so choose one: これは本ですか、ノートですか？ → 本です。"},{"vs":"NかN","point":"NかN simply lists possibilities in a statement; it is not a question. Example: お茶かコーヒーをください。 This pattern creates a clear two-choice question."},{"vs":"NはNではありません","point":"NはNではありません is a negative statement (“N is not N”), not a question. This pattern is a choice question."}]}},"n5-013||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes learners make with choice questions, especially treating them as yes/no questions.","nuance":"The confusion comes from answering “yes” to an “A or B?” question. Remember that this pattern is not a yes/no question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"紅茶","r":"こうちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Coffee or tea?","scene":"Offering a drink and asking the guest’s preference"}],"pitfalls":["❌ これは本ですか、ノート。 → ✅ これは本ですか、ノートですか。 (both clauses need ですか)","❌ はい、本です。 → ✅ 本です。 or ノートです。 (choose one; do not use はい/いいえ)","❌ これは本やノートですか。 → ✅ これは本ですか、ノートですか。 (や is not used for a choice question)","❌ これとそれですか。 → ✅ これはこれですか、それですか。 (use this pattern to ask “this or that”)"],"compare":null}},"n5-013||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: when asking “A or B?”, imagine two small questions—“Is it A?” and “Is it B?”—joined by a comma. The comma represents “or” in Vietnamese.","nuance":"Remember that か appears in both clauses, not just one. Counting two か endings helps identify the pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-013||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはAですか、Bですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAですか、Bですか","patternReading":"はですかですか","coreMeaning":"Is N A or B? (asking for a choice)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common conversational phrases about floors, people, food, and days. This pattern is closely connected to everyday situations.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often omit information that is already clear, keeping only A and B with ですか. For example, お支払いは現金ですか、カードですか can be shortened to 現金ですか、カードですか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"月曜日","r":"げつようび","m":"Monday"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"火曜日","r":"かようび","m":"Tuesday"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is today Monday or Tuesday?","scene":"An employee confirming the work schedule"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-014||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"core","definition":"**〜時／〜分** is used to tell the **hour** and **minute** in Japanese. To state a point in time, use **number + 時(じ)** for hours and **number + 分(ふん/ぷん)** for minutes. For example, 9時15分 means “9:15.” Some hours and minutes have special readings: 4時=よじ, 9時=くじ, 1分=いっぷん.","nuance":"An hour/minute phrase is a noun indicating a **point in time**, and is neutral in both speech and writing. To answer “What time is it?” use **〜時〜分です**. When attaching it to an action, add **に** after the time: **6時に起きます**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"9時","r":"くじ","m":"9 o’clock"},{"t":"15分","r":"じゅうごふん","m":"15 minutes"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is 9:15 now.","scene":"Looking at a clock and answering 今何時ですか"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"6時","r":"ろくじ","m":"6 o’clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"起きます","r":"おきます","m":"wake up"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wake up at 6 every morning.","scene":"Talking with a friend about morning routines"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-014||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and structure","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern has **no verb conjugation or morphological changes**. The formula is simply **number + 時/分**; add **半(はん)** for half past and **午前/午後** for AM/PM. The key point is memorizing the special readings of certain hours and minutes.","nuance":"Unlike verbs, 時 and 分 never change with context. Remember these readings: 4時=よじ, 7時=しちじ, 9時=くじ; 1分=いっぷん, 6分=ろっぷん, 10分=じゅっぷん.","structure":[{"lhs":"Số","rhs":"時(じ)","note":"Hour. Example: 4時=よじ, 7時=しちじ, 9時=くじ."},{"lhs":"Số","rhs":"分(ふん/ぷん)","note":"Minute. Example: 1分=いっぷん, 5分=ごふん, 10分=じゅっぷん."},{"lhs":"Số＋時","rhs":"半(はん)","note":"30 minutes / half past. Example: 3時半=さんじはん."},{"lhs":"午前(ごぜん)／午後(ごご)","rhs":"＋giờ phút","note":"AM/PM. Example: 午前9時=くじ in the morning."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What time is it now?","scene":"Asking the time without a clock"},{"tokens":[{"t":"午後","r":"ごご","m":"afternoon"},{"t":"2時","r":"にじ","m":"2 o’clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会いましょう","r":"あいましょう","m":"let’s meet"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s meet at 2 p.m.","scene":"Making plans to get bubble tea"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-014||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"usage","definition":"In conversation, this pattern is used to **ask and answer the current time**, state **when an action occurs**, ask for a **time range** from one hour to another, or give an **approximate time** with **ごろ**.","nuance":"Ask the current time with **今何時ですか**. Ask when an action happens with **何時に〜ますか**. To say “around,” add **ごろ** after the hour: **7時ごろ** = around 7 o’clock.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"授業","r":"じゅぎょう","m":"class"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What time does class run from and to?","scene":"A student asks the teacher about the class schedule"},{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","r":"ばす","m":"bus"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"7時","r":"しちじ","m":"7 o’clock"},{"t":"ごろ","m":"around"},{"t":"来ます","r":"きます","m":"come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bus comes around 7 o’clock.","scene":"Checking the bus time at a stop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-014||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes **〜時／〜分** from the patterns most often confused with them. The key contrasts are **時** (a clock time) vs **時間** (a duration), and **まで** vs **までに** when used with a time.","nuance":"These are beginner patterns, but learners often confuse them because English uses “time” for both meanings. Remember: 時 is a clock point, 時間 is a duration; まで marks an endpoint, while までに marks a deadline.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"3時半","r":"さんじはん","m":"3:30"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please come at 3:30.","scene":"Telling a guest to arrive at the appointment time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"時間(じかん)","point":"時(じ) = a clock point; 時間(じかん) = a duration. Say 2時間, not 2時, for two hours. Example: 2時 = at 2; 2時間 = two hours."},{"vs":"まで / までに","point":"3時まで = until 3 (continuously); 3時までに = by 3 (deadline). Example: 5時まで働きます (work until 5); 5時までに帰ります (return by 5)."}]}},"n5-014||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner pitfalls","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using **時間** instead of **時** for “what time,” omitting **に** after a time point, and misreading special numbers. Pay attention to the errors below.","nuance":"These are basic errors but noticeably affect naturalness. When stating a specific time with an action, use **に**, except with words such as **今、昨日、明日**…","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"8時","r":"はちじ","m":"8 o’clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to work at 8 every day.","scene":"Talking about your commute"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 今7時時間です。 → ✅ 今7時です。","❌ 4時を「よんじ」と読む。 → ✅ 4時=よじ。","❌ 毎朝6時起きます。 → ✅ 毎朝6時に起きます。","❌ 6分を「ろくふん」と読む。 → ✅ 6分=ろっぷん。","❌ 2時半分に会いましょう。 → ✅ 2時半に会いましょう。"],"compare":null}},"n5-014||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **時** means a “time point,” while **時間** means a “duration” because it contains **間**, meaning an interval. For minutes, numbers **1,3,4,6,8,10** usually take **ぷん**. Special hours: 4時=よじ (like “yo”) and 9時=くじ (like “ku”).","nuance":"There is no special nuance here; these are purely memory aids. While reviewing, ask yourself: “Am I talking about a clock point or a duration? Does this number trigger a sound change?”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-014||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜時／〜分 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜時／〜分","patternReading":"じ／ふん・ぷん","coreMeaning":"How to tell the time (時) and minutes (分) in a day","slug":"phrases","definition":"High-frequency phrases for tests and daily life include **今何時ですか** (what time is it), **何時に〜ますか** (what time do you do something), **〜時半** (half past), **午前／午後** (AM/PM), and **〜時ごろ** (around a certain hour).","nuance":"All of these phrases are natural and normally polite. For a more polite question, use **ですか**: **何時ですか**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"appointment"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What time is tomorrow’s appointment?","scene":"Checking an appointment time with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-015||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ます and 〜ません are **polite** verb forms used with people you are not close to, superiors, or in formal situations. 〜ます expresses **doing an action** (affirmative), while 〜ません expresses **not doing an action** (negative). They are used only for the present and future, **not the past** (use 〜ました/〜ませんでした for the past).","nuance":"Polite, formal speech. Use it with strangers, older people, and coworkers. With close friends and family, the plain form is more common. 〜ません sounds more polite and gentle than the short negative form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study Japanese every day.","scene":"Answering a classmate about studying"},{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"勉強しません","r":"べんきょうしません","m":"do not study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not study on weekends.","scene":"Telling a coworker about weekend plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-015||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"forms","definition":"Conjugate verbs into the **polite ます form** according to three groups. Group 1 (五段): change the う sound to the corresponding い sound, then add ます. Group 2 (一段): drop る, then add ます. Group 3: する→します, 来る→きます. Negative: change ます to ません. Question: add か at the end.","nuance":"This is the basic polite form used in polite communication. If you forget a conjugation, check a dictionary or identify the verb group first.","structure":[{"lhs":"Nhóm 1（五段）","rhs":"う→い + ます","note":"Example: 買う→買います、飲む→飲みます"},{"lhs":"Nhóm 2（一段）","rhs":"bỏ る + ます","note":"Example: 食べる→食べます、見る→見ます"},{"lhs":"Nhóm 3（不規則）","rhs":"する→します、来る→きます","note":"来る is read as きます"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will buy a book.","scene":"Speaking to a clerk in a bookstore"},{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat breakfast.","scene":"Answering a coworker about breakfast"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-015||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch shows how to use **〜ます/〜ません** in specific situations: describing habits, asking and answering politely, and making negative statements. This is the most basic pattern for **polite communication** with people you are not close to.","nuance":"〜ます can refer to the present or future. 〜ません is a polite negative and does not sound harsh. 〜ますか asks a polite question; add か at the end.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you do every day?","scene":"Asking a classmate about daily habits"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"働きません","r":"はたらきません","m":"do not work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will not work tomorrow.","scene":"Telling a coworker about a day off"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-015||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"compare","definition":"Vます is easily confused with related patterns. **Vました** is the polite past, **Vませんでした** is the polite past negative, **Vましょう** is an invitation, and **Vてください** is a request. Vます simply states or affirms an action.","nuance":"Vます ends a statement; Vましょう changes the meaning to an invitation; Vてください expresses a request. Distinguish them clearly to avoid misunderstanding.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"働きます","r":"はたらきます","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am working today.","scene":"Answering: Are you working today?"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vました","point":"Vました is the polite past; Vます is present/future. Example: 昨日働きました (worked yesterday) vs 今日働きます (will work today)."},{"vs":"Vませんでした","point":"Vませんでした is the past negative; Vません is the present/future negative. 昨日働きませんでした vs 今日働きません."},{"vs":"Vましょう","point":"Vましょう is an invitation or suggestion: “let's”; Vます simply states a fact. 食べましょう (Let's eat) vs 食べます (I will eat)."},{"vs":"Vてください","point":"Vてください asks someone to do something; Vます states or reports an action. 待ってください (Please wait) vs 待ちます (I will wait)."}]}},"n5-015||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes: **using the plain form** with strangers, **adding ありません after Vます** to make it negative, or **adding ます after です**. Remember: make the negative by changing ます to ません; です and ます do not go together.","nuance":"These errors make a sentence sound unnatural or grammatically wrong. Correct them by following the standard patterns.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みません","r":"のみません","m":"do not drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not drink alcohol.","scene":"Declining a drink"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日勉強するます。 → ✅ 毎日勉強します。 (Do not attach ます directly to the dictionary form.)","❌ 食べありません。 → ✅ 食べません。 (Just change ます→ません; do not add ありません.)","❌ 毎日勉強しますです。 → ✅ 毎日勉強します。 (Do not add です after a ます-form verb.)","❌ 昨日勉強します。 → ✅ 昨日勉強しました。 (Use ました for the past.)"],"compare":null}},"n5-015||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"memory","definition":"These memory aids help you **remember the conjugation and meaning**. ます sounds like “mát”; being polite is “cool.” ません sounds like “mà sen”; an unbloomed lotus means NO. For Group 2, drop る like taking off shoes, then add ます. For Group 1, change う to い.","nuance":"These are informal mnemonic devices, not linguistic rules, but they are easy to remember. The “cool” and “lotus” images help connect ます and ません with politeness and negation. The shoe and sound-change images help you conjugate the verb groups.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-015||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vます／Vません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vます／Vません","patternReading":"ます・ません","coreMeaning":"Do / do not (polite present/future)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are fixed phrases and common patterns with ます/ません in conversation and JLPT N5 questions. In particular, **Vませんか** is used for a polite invitation, while some greetings and thanks contain ます/ません as fixed expressions.","nuance":"Vませんか is a gentle, polite invitation and is softer than Vましょうか. Expressions such as すみません and ありがとうございます are fixed polite phrases.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"食べませんか","r":"たべませんか","m":"shall we eat?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Would you like to eat together?","scene":"Inviting a coworker to lunch"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-016||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vました／Vませんでした · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vました／Vませんでした","patternReading":"ました／ませんでした","coreMeaning":"Did / did not do (polite past)","slug":"core","definition":"The polite past form describes an action that happened and finished in the past: ました = did, ませんでした = did not do. It is polite and suitable for teachers, older people, and coworkers. Use it when talking about yesterday, last week, or earlier.","nuance":"Neutral and safe: polite but not overly formal or casual. **ませんでした** is the past negative, unlike “do not” in the present. With close friends, Japanese speakers may use た / なかった, but at N5, prioritize ました.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ました","r":"みました","m":"watched"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I watched a movie.","scene":"Friends ask what you did over the weekend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べました","r":"たべました","m":"ate"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Have you eaten lunch yet?","scene":"Asking a coworker during lunch break"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-016||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vました／Vませんでした · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vました／Vませんでした","patternReading":"ました／ませんでした","coreMeaning":"Did / did not do (polite past)","slug":"forms","definition":"The rule is regular: from **Vます**, remove **ます** and add **ました** for the polite affirmative past. From **Vません**, add **でした** to make **ませんでした**, the polite negative past. All three verb groups follow this rule, with no polite-form exceptions.","nuance":"Unlike the plain た-form, where Group I verbs change sounds, ました always stays the same, making it easy to remember. Once you know Vます, you can conjugate immediately.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"Vました","note":"Affirmative past: 行きます → 行きました"},{"lhs":"Vません","rhs":"Vませんでした","note":"Negative past: 行きません → 行きませんでした"},{"lhs":"Nhóm I: かきます、のみます、あそびます","rhs":"かきました、のみました、あそびました","note":"ます → ました"},{"lhs":"Nhóm II: たべます、みます","rhs":"たべました、みました","note":"ます → ました"},{"lhs":"Nhóm III: きます、します","rhs":"きました、しました","note":"Irregular, but still follows ます → ました"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"9時","r":"くじ","m":"9 o’clock"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"寝ました","r":"ねました","m":"went to bed"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I went to bed at 9 o’clock.","scene":"Telling a roommate when you went to bed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べませんでした","r":"たべませんでした","m":"did not eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I did not eat breakfast.","scene":"Skipping breakfast because you were rushing to work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-016||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vました／Vませんでした · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vました／Vませんでした","patternReading":"ました／ませんでした","coreMeaning":"Did / did not do (polite past)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vました is mainly used to tell about something that happened or to ask whether something has been completed. Ask: もう〜ましたか. Short answers: はい、もう〜ました / いいえ、まだです. With ぜんぜん, it means “did not ... at all.”","nuance":"In もう〜ましたか, もう signals that the question concerns completion. If you answer いいえ、まだです, the action has not been done yet. This pattern is common at work when checking task progress.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先週","r":"せんしゅう","m":"last week"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ぜんぜん","m":"at all"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しませんでした","r":"しませんでした","m":"did not do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Last week, I did not study at all.","scene":"Explaining that you were too busy to study"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しました","r":"しました","m":"did"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Have you done your homework yet?","scene":"A mother asks her child"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-016||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vました／Vませんでした · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vました／Vませんでした","patternReading":"ました／ませんでした","coreMeaning":"Did / did not do (polite past)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse **ます** (present/future) with **ました** (past). Another common error is writing ませんでした as ませんだ. Learners also use ませんでした to answer “Have you done it yet?” when they should say まだです.","nuance":"Remember: ました goes with a completed action, while ます goes with the present or future. When someone asks もう〜ましたか, answer いいえ、まだです if you have not done it yet. ませんでした means “did not do it,” a complete negative.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"いました","r":"いました","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I was at home.","scene":"Using the past correctly"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 昨日映画を見ます。 → ✅ 昨日映画を見ました。","❌ 朝ごはんを食べませんだ。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べませんでした。","❌ もう宿題をしましたか。 - いいえ、しませんでした。 → ✅ いいえ、まだです。","❌ 昨日は忙しいでした。 (with an adjective) → ✅ 昨日は忙しかったです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-016||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vました／Vませんでした · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vました／Vませんでした","patternReading":"ました／ませんでした","coreMeaning":"Did / did not do (polite past)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aid: **ました** sounds like “ma, she did it” — the action is already done. **ませんでした** is longer because it combines the negative ません with the past でした.","nuance":"Think of ます as present; adding た, as in the た-form, makes it past. For the negative, ません combines with でした to move the meaning into the past.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-016||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"Vました／Vませんでした · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vました／Vませんでした","patternReading":"ました／ませんでした","coreMeaning":"Did / did not do (polite past)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in conversation and N5 tests: **もう〜ましたか** (have you ... yet), **はい、もう〜ました** (yes, I have), **いいえ、まだです** (not yet), and **ぜんぜん〜ませんでした** (did not ... at all).","nuance":"When もう appears in a question, the speaker wants to know whether the action is complete. A short, polite answer sounds very natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"やりました","r":"やりました","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, I’ve already finished it.","scene":"Answering a manager asking if the report is done"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-017||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern NからNまで means **from N to N**, framing a range of **time** or **space** from the starting point (から) to the endpoint (まで). から marks where something starts; まで marks where it stops. This is a core N5 pattern used constantly for schedules, routes, and time expressions.","nuance":"から and まで can each stand alone: Nから = starting from N, Nまで = up to N. Together, they clearly express a complete **range**. The pattern is neutral and natural in both speech and writing, in casual and polite contexts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"うち","r":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"歩きます","r":"あるきます","m":"walk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every day, I walk from home to school.","scene":"You tell a classmate about your commute."},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"2時","r":"にじ","m":"2 o'clock"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"3時","r":"さんじ","m":"3 o'clock"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The meeting is from 2 to 3 o'clock.","scene":"Replying to a coworker about the schedule."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-017||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connections","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern connects **two nouns of the same type** with から (the starting point) and まで (the endpoint). から and まで can also be used independently after a noun to mark only the beginning or only the end.","nuance":"The sentence often ends with です or a ます-form verb. If you say only Nから, the listener understands a starting point but not the endpoint; Nまで does the opposite.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（thời gian／nơi chốn） + から","rhs":"N（thời gian／nơi chốn） + まで","note":"Connect two points of the same type to form a range."},{"lhs":"N + から","rhs":"Vます／です","note":"Marks the starting point of an action or state."},{"lhs":"N + まで","rhs":"Vます／です","note":"Marks the endpoint, the final boundary."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"9時","r":"くじ","m":"9 o'clock"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study from 9 o'clock.","scene":"Answering when you start studying today."},{"tokens":[{"t":"5時","r":"ごじ","m":"5 o'clock"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"until"},{"t":"うち","r":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"stay"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll be home until 5 o'clock.","scene":"Texting a friend that you’ll be home until 5."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-017||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern for a **time range** (from when to when), a **space range** (from where to where), **travel time** (how long it takes from A to B), and the scope of an activity.","nuance":"To emphasize travel time, it often combines with かかります (take). For a daily time span, 〜から〜まで clearly marks the action’s boundaries. Do not use this pattern for a **deadline**—use までに instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"月曜日","r":"げつようび","m":"Monday"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"金曜日","r":"きんようび","m":"Friday"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"働きます","r":"はたらきます","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I work from Monday to Friday.","scene":"Telling a friend your work schedule."},{"tokens":[{"t":"うち","r":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"10分","r":"じゅっぷん","m":"10 minutes"},{"t":"かかります","r":"かかります","m":"take (time)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It takes 10 minutes from my house to the station.","scene":"Answering how far your home is from the station."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-017||compare":{"title":"⚖️ 〜まで vs 〜までに","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"compare","definition":"A quick comparison of three easily confused particles: **から〜まで** (a range from beginning to end), **までに** (a deadline before a point), and **に** (one specific time point).","nuance":"The key difference is this: まで marks the endpoint of an ongoing action; までに means “by/before,” without requiring the action to continue; に marks one exact point, with no beginning or ending range.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"銀行","r":"ぎんこう","m":"bank"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"9時","r":"くじ","m":"9 o'clock"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"3時","r":"さんじ","m":"3 o'clock"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The bank is open from 9 to 3.","scene":"Reading the hours posted outside a bank."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Nまでに","point":"まで = an action continues UNTIL that point; までに = something must be DONE BEFORE that point. Examples: 9時まで働く (work until 9) / 9時までに来る (come by 9)."},{"vs":"Nに","point":"に = one exact time point; から〜まで = an entire range. Examples: 2時に会う (meet at 2) / 2時から3時まで会う (meet from 2 to 3)."}]}},"n5-017||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with this pattern include confusing まで with までに, using に for a starting point, and leaving out まで when you want to state the endpoint.","nuance":"When translating “from A to B,” it is easy to leave out one end. Always ask: what is the starting point, and what is the endpoint? から goes with the start; まで goes with the end.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"store"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"10時","r":"じゅうじ","m":"10 o'clock"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"6時","r":"ろくじ","m":"6 o'clock"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"to"},{"t":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The store is open from 10 to 6.","scene":"Asking a grocery store’s opening hours."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 9時までに勉強しました。 (means “studied by 9,” not “studied until 9”) → ✅ 9時まで勉強しました。","❌ うちに駅まで歩きます。 → ✅ うちから駅まで歩きます。","❌ 朝9時から5時働きます。 (means “from 9,” but lacks “to 5”) → ✅ 朝9時から5時まで働きます。"],"compare":null}},"n5-017||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture から as **a mouth opening**—everything starts flowing out from it. Picture まで as **a closed frame**—everything stops there.","nuance":"The chant “FROM から → TO まで” helps you choose the right particle. With only one point, ask whether it is OPENING the range (から) or CLOSING it (まで).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-017||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NからNまで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NからNまで","patternReading":"からまで","coreMeaning":"From N to N (time/space range)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These から〜まで phrases are nearly fixed expressions in N5 tests and everyday Japanese: 朝から晩まで, 月曜日から金曜日まで, ここから駅まで, and so on.","nuance":"Memorizing these phrases builds quick listening and speaking reflexes. Learn them with common verbs such as 働きます, 勉強します, かかります, and あります.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"から","r":"から","m":"from"},{"t":"晩","r":"ばん","m":"night"},{"t":"まで","r":"まで","m":"until"},{"t":"働きました","r":"はたらきました","m":"worked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I worked from morning until night.","scene":"Complaining to a friend after a tiring workday."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-018||core":{"title":"📖 Core meanings","sub":"Nと · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nと","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"Together with ~ / and ~","slug":"core","definition":"と is a particle attached to a noun to express **the person acting together with someone** or **a list of nouns** (and). This lesson focuses on the meaning “together with”: the subject and that person perform an action together.","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese, at all politeness levels. It has no strong nuance; it simply connects people or things. To say “alone,” you must use 一人で, not と.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ました","r":"みました","m":"watched"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watched a movie with my friend.","scene":"Talking about last weekend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"旅行します","r":"りょこうします","m":"travel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will travel with my family.","scene":"Answering what you will do this weekend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-018||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structures & combinations","sub":"Nと · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nと","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"Together with ~ / and ~","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic formula: [Person] と [Action]. と never changes form. For listing, place と between nouns: AとB. You can list more than two: AとBとC.","nuance":"The same pattern is used in writing. Note: if the subject is not acting together with someone but is directing an action toward them, use に (e.g. 先生に聞きます—ask the teacher), not と.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1（人）は","rhs":"N2と Vます","note":"N2 is the person acting with N1"},{"lhs":"N1と N2","rhs":"（N3...）","note":"List all the nouns"},{"lhs":"誰と","rhs":"Vますか","note":"Ask who acts together"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"話します","r":"はなします","m":"talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I talk with Mr. Tanaka.","scene":"At the office after a meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"肉","r":"にく","m":"meat"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I buy vegetables and meat.","scene":"Listing groceries at a supermarket"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-018||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Nと · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nと","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"Together with ~ / and ~","slug":"usage","definition":"Use と when you want to clearly say who is acting together with someone. It often appears in answers to “who are you going with?”, “who are you eating with?”, or “who are you doing it with?” When listing nouns, と means “and” and **lists all items**, not just examples.","nuance":"In everyday conversation, 誰と? is very common. You can answer briefly: 友だちと。 (With my friend.) Note that と for “and” requires a complete list; when giving only a few examples, use や.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"か"}],"vi":"Who did you go with?","scene":"Asking a coworker after a vacation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat lunch with my friend.","scene":"Lunch break at the office"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-018||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Nと · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nと","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"Together with ~ / and ~","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare と with other particles to avoid confusion. Each pair differs in one key point.","nuance":"In general, と is used for a companion or a complete list. When unsure, ask yourself: “Are both people acting together?” or “Am I listing everything?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"紅茶","r":"こうちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought coffee and tea.","scene":"Answering what you bought at the store"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NやN","point":"と gives a complete list, while や gives examples: スーパーで野菜と肉を買いました (I bought vegetables and meat, only those two) versus 野菜や肉を買いました (I bought vegetables, meat, and so on)."},{"vs":"NかN","point":"と = “and” (both), か = “or” (choose one): コーヒーと紅茶 (coffee and tea) vs コーヒーか紅茶 (coffee or tea)"},{"vs":"一人で","point":"と = someone acts with you, 一人で = no one acts with you: 友だちと行きます vs 一人で行きます"}]}},"n5-018||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & corrections","sub":"Nと · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nと","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"Together with ~ / and ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make a few errors by translating directly from English. Correct them thoroughly.","nuance":"The third error often occurs when learners use や for every list. や means “and other things,” so it is unsuitable when you want to say exactly what you bought.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with/and"},{"t":"話しました","r":"はなしました","m":"talked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I talked with my friend.","scene":"Talking about yesterday"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 一人でと行きます。 → ✅ 一人で行きます。 (once you say “alone,” と is unnecessary)","❌ 私は友だちを話しました。 → ✅ 私は友だちと話しました。 (use と, not を, for talking “with someone”)","❌ 食べますと飲みます。 → ✅ 食べて飲みます。 (と joins nouns, not verbs)"],"compare":null}},"n5-018||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Nと · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nと","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"Together with ~ / and ~","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember that と can mean “together,” think of the English word “together,” which begins with the same “to” sound as と. For listing, picture と as a plus sign (+) joining nouns.","nuance":"This is only a memory aid, not the word’s etymology, but it helps you remember the meaning quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-019||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"core","definition":"へ is a particle marking the **direction of movement** or a **destination** with a movement verb. The pattern NへVます describes someone going, coming, or returning to a place. It is a basic pattern for talking about movement in Japanese.","nuance":"へ has a nuance of **emphasizing direction** more than a specific destination. It is used in both speech and writing and is common at all ages. With the ます form, the sentence is polite and suitable for people you are not close to. Compared with に, へ often sounds more natural when talking about trips or travel.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will go to Japan next month.","scene":"Discussing travel plans with a colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to the library over the weekend.","scene":"Recounting where you went over the weekend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-019||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern is **N + へ + Vます**, where N is a place noun and V is a movement verb (行く、来る、帰る). The particle へ is written in hiragana as へ but **pronounced 'e'**, not 'he'.","nuance":"This is a simple structure with little variation. For the negative, use ～ません; for the past, use ～ました. へ does not change form with any verb.","structure":[{"lhs":"N (địa điểm)","rhs":"へ行きます","note":"go to N (present/future)"},{"lhs":"N (địa điểm)","rhs":"へ行きました","note":"went to N (past)"},{"lhs":"N (địa điểm)","rhs":"へ帰ります","note":"return to N"},{"lhs":"N (địa điểm)","rhs":"へ来ます","note":"come to N (toward the speaker)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to school tomorrow.","scene":"Answering a mother asking about school tomorrow"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"帰りました","r":"かえりました","m":"returned"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am already home.","scene":"Calling family to say you got home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-019||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"usage","definition":"The pattern NへVます is used to **ask and answer about destinations**, describe future actions (明日、日本へ行きます), completed actions (昨日、学校へ行きました), or habits (毎日、駅へ行きます).","nuance":"When asking 'どこへ行きますか', Japanese speakers often use へ instead of に because the question focuses on direction. Answers can use either へ or に. In everyday conversation, sentence-final particles such as ね or よ can be added for a more natural, familiar tone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where are you going?","scene":"Asking a friend who is hurrying away"},{"tokens":[{"t":"デパート","m":"department store"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the department store.","scene":"Answering where you are going"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to the station every morning.","scene":"Talking about a daily habit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-019||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from に・で","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"compare","definition":"Both へ and に can mark a destination, but they differ in nuance: へ emphasizes the direction of movement, while に emphasizes a specific destination. で marks the place where an action occurs and cannot be used with movement verbs. Understanding this core contrast helps you choose the right particle.","nuance":"へ and に both mark destinations, but へ is directional and に marks a specific point. Although で also relates to place, it marks where an action happens and is not used with movement verbs such as 行く or 来る.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going home now.","scene":"Speaking to a colleague before leaving work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NにVます","point":"へ = direction of movement; に = exact destination. Example: 日本へ行く (focuses on the journey) vs 日本に行く (simply going to Japan)."},{"vs":"NでVます","point":"で = place where an action occurs; it is not used with movement verbs. Do not say ❌学校で行きます; say 学校へ行きます."}]}},"n5-019||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often struggle with へ because of its kana-chart pronunciation or because they omit the particle. Common mistakes include using へ with a non-movement verb or leaving it out when naming a destination. Remember: へ goes with movement verbs such as 行く・来る・帰る, and a destination normally needs a particle.","nuance":"These mistakes are common for beginners because English does not use particles in the same way. Check whether the verb expresses movement and whether the destination has the correct particle へ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to school.","scene":"A simple sentence describing an action"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 会社行きます。 → ✅ 会社へ行きます。 (particle omitted)","❌ ここへあります。 → ✅ ここにあります。 (へ is used only with movement verbs)","❌ 日本へ(pronounced 'he') → ✅ 日本へ(pronounced 'e')"],"compare":null}},"n5-019||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember how to use へ, imagine the character へ as an arrow pointing in a direction. This arrow goes with movement, so へ is used with verbs such as 行く, 来る, and 帰る. For pronunciation, remember that へ is pronounced 'e' as a particle, not 'he'.","nuance":"This mnemonic is visual because へ really does resemble an arrow. Also contrast it with は, the topic particle: へ is the direction particle, which helps keep the two particles separate.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-019||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NへVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへVます","patternReading":"え","coreMeaning":"Go to N (direction of movement)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are common phrases with へ in conversation and on the JLPT N5. Learn them as fixed chunks so you can respond quickly when listening or speaking.","nuance":"All of these phrases use the NへVます structure and are used every day. Once familiar, you can create new phrases with places such as スーパー、たいしかん、びょういん…","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"へ","m":"direction marker"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the station.","scene":"Talking about a destination"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-020||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"core","definition":"The particle で follows a noun for a means of transportation (car, train, plane, etc.) or a tool (pen, chopsticks, etc.) to show how an action is performed. It often translates as “by” or “with.” Example: 電車で行きます = go by train.","nuance":"This is the most familiar N5 use of で. It is neutral and works in any context. で highlights the means or tool, not a place or time.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go by train.","scene":"Answering “How do you get to work?”"},{"tokens":[{"t":"はし","m":"chopsticks"},{"t":"で","m":"with"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat with chopsticks.","scene":"Telling friends how you eat at a restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"で","m":"in"},{"t":"話します","r":"はなします","m":"speak"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I speak in Japanese.","scene":"Introducing the language you use"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-020||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: N + で + Vます. N is a noun for transportation or a tool; V is a polite-form verb. で does not change with tense; tense is shown by the verb (Vます, Vました, Vません).","nuance":"For “go on foot,” use 歩いて行きます rather than 歩きで. This exception must be memorized.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"で + Vます","note":"Present/future"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"で + Vました","note":"Past"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"で + Vません","note":"Negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came by bus.","scene":"Answering a teacher’s question about how you got to class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"行きません","r":"いきません","m":"do not go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not go by car.","scene":"Declining a ride"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-020||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use で when you want to specify what means or tool is used for an action. In conversation, it often answers どうやって行きますか (How do you get there?).","nuance":"Besides transportation, で is used for tools and languages. In this lesson, transportation is the main focus. Pay attention to context so you choose the right use.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"自転車","r":"じてんしゃ","m":"bicycle"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to school by bicycle.","scene":"A student describing their daily commute"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"で","m":"with"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I write with a pen.","scene":"Answering what you write with"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-020||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from へ/と/で","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"compare","definition":"Beginners often confuse で with へ, と, or the place-marking で. Distinguish their purposes: へ attaches to a movement verb and marks direction; と marks someone you act with; で in this lesson marks transportation or a tool.","nuance":"A sentence can contain both で and へ: バス**で**学校**へ**行きます. で answers “by what means?” and へ answers “where to?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to school by bus.","scene":"A model sentence combining means and direction"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NへVます","point":"へ marks the destination; で marks the means. Example: 学校**へ**行きます (go to school) vs バス**で**行きます (go by bus)."},{"vs":"NとVます","point":"と marks the person you act with; で marks the tool. Example: 友だち**と**行きます (go with a friend) vs 自転車**で**行きます (go by bicycle)."}]}},"n5-020||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse で with に or を. Tip: で = “by/with,” に/へ = “at/to,” and を = the object. For “in Japanese,” use で, not を.","nuance":"“Go by train” is often written with 電車に because に is confused with the place marker. Correct it to 電車で: the train is the means, not the destination.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came by bus.","scene":"A correct model replacing incorrect sentences"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 電車に会社へ行きます。 → ✅ 電車で会社へ行きます。","❌ 歩きで行きます。 → ✅ 歩いて行きます。","❌ はしを食べます。 → ✅ はしで食べます。","❌ 日本語に話します。 → ✅ 日本語で話します。"],"compare":null}},"n5-020||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: Does で look like a wheel? Or simply remember: “By what means? Use で.” Say quietly: “means + で + verb.”","nuance":"Do not forget the exception 歩いて (walk). When you see a means-of-transport noun, ask yourself “by what?” and place で after it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-020||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NでVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVます","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"Indicates the means or tool used for an action (by/with)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases are extremely common in textbooks and daily life. Memorize them to respond quickly in conversation and on the JLPT.","nuance":"In daily conversation, Japanese speakers often ask: 電車で行きますか、バスで行きますか? (Are you going by train or bus?)","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go by train.","scene":"A common answer about transportation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-021||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"core","definition":"**NとVます** describes an action done **together with another person** (N). N is always a person noun—such as a friend, family member, or teacher. For example, **家族と旅行します** means “travel with my family.” The focus is the **companion**, not the means of transport.","nuance":"Used in everyday speech and writing. It sounds friendly and is not especially formal. To stress “together,” you can add **一緒に**, but grammatically **と** alone is enough. If N is an object or vehicle, use **で**, not **と**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to Japan with my family.","scene":"Answering a friend about your next trip."},{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Who did you go to Japan with?","scene":"Asking about a recent trip."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-021||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch organizes the connected forms of **NとVます**: present, past, and negative. No matter which form is used, **Nと** comes before the verb and does not change.","nuance":"Because this is a noun-plus-particle pattern, there is no special conjugation rule. Just remember that N must be a person; the verb changes for tense and normal politeness.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（người）","rhs":"と + Vます","note":"Example: 友達と話します"},{"lhs":"N（người）","rhs":"と + Vました","note":"Past: 先生と昼ごはんを食べました"},{"lhs":"N（người）","rhs":"と + Vません","note":"Present negative: 友達と話しません"},{"lhs":"N（người）","rhs":"と + Vませんでした","note":"Past negative: 友達と話しませんでした"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"話します","r":"はなします","m":"talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I talk with my friend.","scene":"Describing a usual break-time activity."},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べました","r":"たべました","m":"ate"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I had lunch with my teacher.","scene":"Talking about lunch at work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-021||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"usage","definition":"Common uses of **NとVます**: ① ask who is joining you—**誰と**; ② say who is joining you—**友達と**; ③ say “alone”—use **一人で**, not **一人と**.","nuance":"In conversation, **誰と...か** is very common. When you are not going with anyone, use **一人で**—the contrasting expression to Nと.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Who did you come with?","scene":"Asking a classmate who came with someone."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came with a friend.","scene":"Answering the question above."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-021||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: と vs で","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"compare","definition":"Contrast **NとVます** and **NでVます**, one of the easiest pairs to confuse in Lesson 5. If N is the **person accompanying you**, use **と**; if N is a **vehicle or tool**, use **で**.","nuance":"**友達とバスで行きます** can contain both: “with a friend” (と) and “by bus” (で). When combined, **Nと** usually comes before **Nで**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going by bus with my friend.","scene":"Stating both the companion and the transport."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NでVます","point":"Nと = a PERSON acting with you; Nで = a MEANS of transport. Compare: 友達と行きます = go with a friend; バスで行きます = go by bus."},{"vs":"NとN","point":"NとN simply lists two nouns, with no shared action; NとVます describes an action with a person. Compare: 本とペン = a book and a pen; 友達と話します = talk with a friend."},{"vs":"いっしょにVます","point":"NとVます identifies the person joining you (友達と行きます = go with a friend); いっしょにVます only says “together,” without identifying who (いっしょに行きます = go together)."}]}},"n5-021||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with **NとVます** include using **と** with transport, using **で** with a person, or adding **と** when saying “alone.”","nuance":"The particle **と** follows only the **person acting with you**. Use **で** with vehicles, languages, and tools. For “alone,” use **一人で**, never **一人と**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"旅行します","r":"りょこうします","m":"travel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I travel with my family.","scene":"Correct sentence after fixing で with a person."}],"pitfalls":["❌ バスと行きます。 → ✅ バスで行きます。","❌ 友達で話します。 → ✅ 友達と話します。","❌ 一人と来ました。 → ✅ 一人で来ました。"],"compare":null}},"n5-021||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **と** can suggest two people standing side by side—“with whom.” **で** is like “by” in English—“by what means.”","nuance":"When unsure, ask: “Does another person need to be beside me for this action?” If yes → **と**; if only a tool is needed → **で**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-021||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"NとVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとVます","patternReading":"えぬとぶいます","coreMeaning":"Do something together with N (a person)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Useful **NとVます** phrases for tests and daily life: watch a movie with a friend, eat with family, go shopping with someone, and so on.","nuance":"Common verbs include movement and shared activities: **行く, 来る, 食べる, 見る, 話す, 旅行する**. Add the transport particle **で** when needed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ます","r":"みます","m":"watch"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watch a movie with my friend.","scene":"Talking about weekend plans."},{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"食事します","r":"しょくじします","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat with my family.","scene":"Talking about dinner at home."},{"tokens":[{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"と","m":"with"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Who are you going shopping with?","scene":"Asking who will go to the supermarket."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-022||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"いつVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いつVますか","patternReading":"いつ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask when an action happens: when do you do it?","slug":"core","definition":"〜いつ Vますか asks **when an action happens**, equivalent to “when…?”. いつ is a time question word and is **not followed by に**. Common answers include 明日 (tomorrow), 来週 (next week), and 来月 (next month).","nuance":"Used in **both conversation and everyday writing**. With friends, you can say いつ行く？; with superiors, use the polite ます form. It simply asks about timing, without special emphasis.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When did you come to Japan?","scene":"Asking a newly arrived student"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"うち","m":"home"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"return"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When will you go home?","scene":"Asking a coworker after work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-022||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"いつVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いつVますか","patternReading":"いつ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask when an action happens: when do you do it?","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **いつ + ます-form verb + か**. The verb can be in the **present/future** (ます) or **past** (ました). To ask for an approximate time, add **ごろ** after いつ: **いつごろ** means “about when.” Answers usually use a time expression, with に for a specific hour.","nuance":"With close friends, use the casual form: いつ行く？. The ました form asks about something that happened in the past. いつごろ sounds softer and more approachable.","structure":[{"lhs":"Chủ đề","rhs":"は いつ Vますか","note":"Ask when an action happens"},{"lhs":"Luôn luôn","rhs":"いつも Vます","note":"いつ + も = いつも"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When is the meeting?","scene":"Asking the secretary"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When did you come to Japan?","scene":"Asking when someone came"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-022||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"いつVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いつVますか","patternReading":"いつ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask when an action happens: when do you do it?","slug":"usage","definition":"〜いつVますか is common in daily life for asking when an activity **starts, ends, or takes place**. It can combine with から (from) and まで (until) to ask about a time range, though these are more advanced variants. With superiors, いつごろ can sound softer.","nuance":"This is very natural in daily conversation: not overly formal, but not blunt. The ます form suits coworkers and older people. With children or close friends, use the short form いつ...？.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"起きます","r":"おきます","m":"wake up"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When do you wake up?","scene":"Asking a roommate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"return"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When are you going home? — I’m going home next week.","scene":"Discussing plans to go home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-022||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing いつ and 何時","sub":"いつVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いつVますか","patternReading":"いつ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask when an action happens: when do you do it?","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **いつ** and **何時** (なんじ). Both can be translated as “when,” but **いつ** asks about a general time such as a day, week, or month; **何時** asks for a specific clock time. **どのくらい** asks about duration.","nuance":"Learners often confuse these. Remember: **いつ does not need に**, while **何時** commonly uses に when answering. Example: 何時に来ますか？ — 9時に来ます。","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来ます","r":"きます","m":"come"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What time will you come?","scene":"Asking for an exact arrival time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"何時","point":"いつ asks about a day or week; 何時 asks for a specific hour. いつ来ますか → 明日/来週. 何時に来ますか → 9時."},{"vs":"どのくらい","point":"いつ asks for a TIME POINT; どのくらい asks about DURATION. いつ帰りますか → 明日. どのくらい働きますか → 8時間."},{"vs":"どこ","point":"いつ means WHEN; どこ means WHERE. Their sounds may be easy to confuse, but their meanings are completely different."}]}},"n5-022||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"いつVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いつVますか","patternReading":"いつ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask when an action happens: when do you do it?","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with いつ include adding に after it, as in ❌ いつに来ますか. Since いつ is already a question word, it needs no particle. Also, do not use です before a verb because the polite verb form already uses ます.","nuance":"For an exact clock time, use 何時 instead of いつ. Answers to いつ are usually days or weeks without に; answers to 何時 generally use に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"何時","r":"なんじ","m":"what time"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"起きます","r":"おきます","m":"wake up"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What time do you wake up every day?","scene":"Use 何時 for an exact time"}],"pitfalls":["❌ いつに来ますか。 → ✅ いつ来ますか。","❌ いつ行きますですか。 → ✅ いつ行きますか。","❌ いつですか帰りますか。 → ✅ いつ帰りますか。","❌ 何時日本へ行きますか。 (when asking the day) → ✅ いつ日本へ行きますか。"],"compare":null}},"n5-022||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"いつVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いつVますか","patternReading":"いつ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask when an action happens: when do you do it?","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember いつ, think of English “when”: both appear at the beginning of questions and do not take a preposition. In Japanese, いつ also does not use に. Also remember that **いつも** is a separate word meaning “always”: いつ + も.","nuance":"If helpful, associate いつ with “its” in English. Or remember the familiar question: いつ? = “When?”.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-023||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"core","definition":"〜を is the **direct object particle**. It follows a noun referring to the person, thing, or matter affected by an action. The pattern **NをVます** expresses doing something to or with N. Example: 毎朝コーヒーを飲みます (I drink coffee every morning).","nuance":"The particle を has **no independent meaning**; it serves a grammatical role. N marked by を must be the direct object—the person or thing the action targets.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat breakfast.","scene":"Answering what you do in the morning"},{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞きます","r":"ききます","m":"listen to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I listen to music.","scene":"Telling a friend about your free time"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-023||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N + を + ます-form verb**. Use four endings: affirmative (ます), negative (ません), past (ました), and past negative (ませんでした).","nuance":"The ます-form is **standard polite speech**, used with strangers, superiors, at work, and in class. In casual speech, switch to the dictionary form (NをVる).","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をVます","note":"Present/future affirmative"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をVません","note":"Present/future negative"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をVました","note":"Past affirmative"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"をVませんでした","note":"Past negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study Japanese every day.","scene":"Talking about your daily schedule"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みません","r":"のみません","m":"do not drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not drink alcohol.","scene":"Politely declining an offer of alcohol"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ました","r":"みました","m":"watched"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watched a movie yesterday.","scene":"Telling a friend about yesterday"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → ます形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 — change the う-row → い-row","Remove the final sound, replace it with the corresponding い-row sound, and add ます","飲む（のむ） → `飲みます（のみます）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書きます（かきます）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話します（はなします）` (speak) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待ちます（まちます）` (wait)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 ending in る (godan-る)","Change る → り and add ます — do NOT remove る as with ichidan","帰る（かえる） → `帰ります（かえります）` (return home) ・ 走る（はしる） → `走ります（はしります）` (run) ・ 切る（きる） → `切ります（きります）` (cut) ・ 知る（しる） → `知ります（しります）` (know)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 (ichidan) — remove る","Remove る and add ます directly","食べる（たべる） → `食べます（たべます）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見ます（みます）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きます（おきます）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝ます（ねます）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する · 来る — irregular","Memorize: する→します · 来る→きます","する → `します` (do) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強します（べんきょうします）` (study) ・ 来る（くる） → `来ます（きます）` (come)"]]}}},"n5-023||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"usage","definition":"The pattern NをVます is commonly used to describe **an action affecting something**. To ask about the object, replace N with **何を**: 何を食べますか (What do you eat?).","nuance":"The pattern itself has no special politeness nuance; politeness comes from the verb ending. It works in nearly all everyday situations.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I buy vegetables at the supermarket.","scene":"A companion asks what you are buying"},{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮ります","r":"とります","m":"take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I take photos.","scene":"Talking to a companion on a trip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-023||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from が / に","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"compare","definition":"Three easily confused particles: **が** marks the subject, **を** marks the object, and **に** marks a destination or direction. Distinguish them by the noun's role in the sentence.","nuance":"With intransitive verbs (go, stand, sit, open by itself, etc.), there is no object, so do not use を. With transitive verbs (eat, drink, open a door, etc.), を is required.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drink water.","scene":"Showing the roles of が (subject) and を (object)"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Nが","point":"が = the subject performing the action. を = the thing receiving the action. Example: 私が水を飲みます — 私 is the drinker, and 水 is what is drunk."},{"vs":"Nに","point":"に marks the destination or direction of movement verbs (行く・来る・帰る). を marks the object of a transitive verb. Example: 駅に行きます (go to the station) ≠ 本を読みます (read a book)."},{"vs":"自動詞＋が","point":"Intransitive verbs describing a natural state use が and have no object. Example: ドアが開きます (the door opens by itself) vs ドアを開けます (I open the door)."}]}},"n5-023||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with を include **omitting を**, **confusing it with が**, and **using を with movement verbs**, which normally take に/to mark a destination.","nuance":"を may be omitted in extremely casual conversation (for example, ごはん食べる?), but it is required in writing and polite speech.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do my homework.","scene":"Talking to a classmate after class"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 朝ごはん食べます。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べます。 (missing the particle を)","❌ パンが食べます。 → ✅ パンを食べます。 (the object takes を; the subject takes が)","❌ 駅を行きます。 → ✅ 駅に行きます。 (the destination of 行く takes に, not を)","❌ 本を読みますを。 → ✅ 本を読みます。 (を follows the object noun, not the verb)"],"compare":null}},"n5-023||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **を** as a horizontal arrow **→**: the arrow points to the noun ahead, showing that the noun is targeted by the action. Read it as **'o'**, like “oh” when you recognize the target.","nuance":"This visual and sound-based mnemonic helps connect を with the role of “direct object” instead of requiring grammatical analysis.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-023||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをVます","patternReading":"を","coreMeaning":"Do something to/with N (the affected person or thing)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These essential **NをVます** phrases appear throughout Minna no Nihongo and nearly every beginner conversation lesson.","nuance":"These phrases are used daily; memorize them to respond quickly in multiple-choice exercises.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drink coffee every morning.","scene":"Answering about your morning routine"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-024||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"core","definition":"〜を〜します is a pattern that forms a **suru verb** from a Sino-Japanese or loanword noun, such as 勉強します and 買い物をします. It expresses a **complete action**: the noun carries the meaning, while します means \"do.\" Japanese uses this very often instead of a separate verb.","nuance":"This is a **standard, neutral expression**, usable with friends, colleagues, and older people in 〜ます form. Compared with a single verb, the suru form can feel like an **intentional, noun-based action**. In casual speech, を may be omitted: 買い物します, 電話します. Keep を when emphasizing the object.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I exercise every day.","scene":"Answering a friend about your morning routine"},{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go shopping on weekends.","scene":"Someone asks what you usually do on weekends"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-024||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N + を + N(suru) + します**. The suru noun can come directly before します; if there is an additional object, it comes before を. します is a Group III verb and conjugates as ます／ません／ました／ませんでした.","nuance":"This is the polite **-ます form**, used for present and future actions. In casual speech, change it to する／した／しない／しなかった. を is often dropped when the suru noun comes directly before します, but omission is not required.","structure":[{"lhs":"N(suru)","rhs":"をします","note":"Present/future: 買い物をします"},{"lhs":"N(suru)","rhs":"をしました","note":"Past: 買い物をしました"},{"lhs":"N(suru)","rhs":"をしません","note":"Present negative: 買い物をしません"},{"lhs":"N(suru)","rhs":"をしませんでした","note":"Past negative: 買い物をしませんでした"},{"lhs":"N + を + N(suru)","rhs":"します","note":"With an object: 日本語を勉強します"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study Japanese.","scene":"Telling your parents about your study plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しました","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I did my homework yesterday.","scene":"A classmate asks what you did last night"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-024||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern for **familiar daily activities** such as 勉強, 仕事, 運動, and 買い物. It also expresses **plans** and **habits**. Sentences often include time expressions such as 毎日, 週末, 明日, and 昨日.","nuance":"It is not highly formal but is **polite and natural**, fitting workplaces, classrooms, and families. With を, the object of the action feels clearer; without を, the sentence sounds shorter and more like a set verb expression.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will call my friend.","scene":"Telling a friend to wait for your call after work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reservation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will make a reservation tomorrow.","scene":"A couple discusses booking a hotel room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日曜日","r":"にちようび","m":"Sunday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"掃除","r":"そうじ","m":"cleaning"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I clean the house on Sundays.","scene":"Answering a friend about your weekend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-024||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with NはNです/NをVます","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps prevent confusion between NをNします, patterns with です, and ordinary verbs. Although all can relate to \"doing something,\" each pattern has a different focus.","nuance":"NをNします (really **noun + します**) presents an activity as a **unified whole**, such as 料理をします = \"cook\" in general. NをVます with an independent verb focuses more on a specific action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cook.","scene":"Answering your mother about tonight's plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"です states \"what something is\"; します expresses \"what someone does.\" 毎日運動です is wrong because です cannot express an action → use 毎日運動します."},{"vs":"NをVます","point":"NをVます (e.g. カレーを作ります) refers to a specific action; NをNします (e.g. 料理をします) refers to the activity in general."}]}},"n5-024||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with suru verbs include using です instead of します, using を for the recipient, adding an extra word at the end, or adding だ after ます.","nuance":"These errors make the sentence **unnatural**, even if it may still be understood. Remember: the ます ending is already polite, so do not add だ; \"call someone\" uses に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"運動","r":"うんどう","m":"exercise"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I exercise.","scene":"In the morning, Dad tells the family he is going to exercise"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 毎日運動です。 → ✅ 毎日運動します。","❌ 友だちを電話しました。 → ✅ 友だちに電話しました。","❌ 明日、予約しますを。 → ✅ 明日、予約します。","❌ 買い物をしますだ。 → ✅ 買い物をします。"],"compare":null}},"n5-024||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips & shortcuts","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **する = \"do\"**. Try mapping \"do + noun\" to the meaning: 買い物 means shopping, and 買い物する means \"do shopping\" = go shopping. Think of を as an **arrow pointing to the affected object**.","nuance":"Memorize common suru phrases as **chunks**, rather than separating every word. For example: 勉強します, 運動します, 予約します. Once they sound familiar, you can use them immediately.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-024||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases & collocations","sub":"NをNします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NをNします","patternReading":"エヌをエヌします","coreMeaning":"Do N — a noun + します forms a suru verb","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are among the most frequent suru-verb phrases in Minna no Nihongo textbooks and JLPT N5 tests. Learn them as a group: shopping, studying, work, communication, and reservations.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often drop を: 買い物します, 勉強します. In writing or careful speech, including を makes the structure clearer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going shopping for a bit.","scene":"Telling your family you are heading out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-025||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"core","definition":"何を + Vますか asks for the **object** of a transitive verb. The speaker knows the action—such as eating, drinking, or buying—but not the specific object. So it means “what do you do?”, “what do you eat?”, or “what do you buy?”, depending on the verb.","nuance":"This neutral question works in both casual and polite settings. The subject “you” is usually omitted in conversation. 何 before を must be read **なに**, not **なん**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べました","r":"たべました","m":"ate"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did you eat for lunch?","scene":"Asking after lunch"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日曜日","r":"にちようび","m":"Sunday"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you do on Sunday?","scene":"Asking about weekend plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you buy?","scene":"Friends entering a shop"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-025||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and question forms","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern changes according to the tense of the verb after 何を. To ask about the past, use **Vましたか**; to ask about an action in progress, use **Vていますか**. The **Vますか** form can also ask about the near future.","nuance":"Although it uses the same particles, **何をVませんか** is an invitation meaning “Why don’t we do ...?” For example, 何を食べませんか means “Shall we eat something?” Do not use it to ask for an object.","structure":[{"lhs":"何を","rhs":"Vますか","note":"Ask about present/future action"},{"lhs":"何を","rhs":"Vましたか","note":"Ask about past action"},{"lhs":"何を","rhs":"Vていますか","note":"Ask about an action in progress"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しました","r":"しました","m":"did"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did you do yesterday?","scene":"Asking after a day apart"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しています","r":"しています","m":"are doing"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What are you doing now?","scene":"Calling to ask what a friend is doing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you do tomorrow?","scene":"Asking about tomorrow's plan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-025||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"usage","definition":"In daily life, this pattern appears in almost every ordinary conversation: asking about meals, weekend plans, or shopping. A time or place adverb can be added before 何を without changing the structure.","nuance":"When answering, Japanese speakers often keep it short: “コーヒーを飲みます” or even just “コーヒーです”. There is no need to repeat the full subject.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you eat for breakfast?","scene":"A parent asking at breakfast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"スーパー","r":"すーぱー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did you buy at the supermarket?","scene":"Asking after someone returns"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you drink?","scene":"Asking at a café"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-025||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Patterns that are easy to confuse","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes 何を from three easily confused question patterns: **NはNですか** asks “what is it?”, **何で** asks about means, and **いつ** asks about time.","nuance":"Look at the particle after 何. を marks the object; で marks means or method. いつ is a separate question word, not 何 + つ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you drink?","scene":"Asking a customer"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNですか","point":"NはNですか asks what or who the subject is (a complement), while 何をVますか asks for the object affected by the verb. Example: これは何ですか vs 何を食べますか。"},{"vs":"何でVますか","point":"何で means “by what means/how”; 何を means “which object”. Example: 何で行きますか (How will you go?) vs 何を買いますか (What will you buy?)."},{"vs":"いつVますか","point":"いつ asks when; 何を asks what object. Example: いつ行きますか (When will you go?) vs 何を食べますか (What will you eat?)."}]}},"n5-025||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes and traps","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: reading 何 as なん before を, using が instead of を, and putting 何を with an intransitive verb. Remember that を marks the object.","nuance":"Another mistake is answering with “はい/いいえ”. A question containing 何 asks for information, not a yes-or-no response.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きました","r":"かきました","m":"wrote"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did you write?","scene":"Teacher asking after a writing exercise"}],"pitfalls":["❌ なんを食べますか → ✅ なにを食べますか","❌ 何が買いましたか → ✅ 何を買いましたか","❌ 何を行きますか → ✅ どこへ行きますか","❌ はい、食べます。 → ✅ パンを食べます。"],"compare":null}},"n5-025||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **を** as an arrow pointing at an object. 何 before the arrow means “what” the arrow is aimed at. To ask for an object, put 何 before を, then place the verb after it.","nuance":"If you keep the “arrow” image in mind, you will not write 何が instead of 何を: が marks the subject, not the object arrow.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-025||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"何をVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"何をVますか","patternReading":"なにを ～ますか","coreMeaning":"Ask what object the verb acts on","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are the most frequent questions with this pattern: 食べますか, 飲みますか, 買いますか, and しますか. Combine each with 何を as a ready-made formula.","nuance":"In conversation, simply say “何を食べますか?”; あなたは is not needed. For extra politeness, some nouns can take お, but at N5 this short pattern is the standard form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you eat?","scene":"Asking during a lunch break"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What will you do?","scene":"Asking someone's plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-026||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NかN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NかN","patternReading":"か","coreMeaning":"N or N (choose one)","slug":"core","definition":"The particle か connects two or more nouns to show **a choice between options**. With か, the speaker presents several possibilities and often wants the listener to choose one, or shows that they have not decided yet. It means **“or.”**\n\n**Key point**: か is used for **choices**, unlike や (partial listing) and と (complete listing).","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, and very common in daily life. When asking someone to choose, it often combines with どちら／どっち. When talking about your own plans, か shows **indecision or an unsettled choice**.\n\nIt has no special formal nuance, but formal writing may use または or もしくは.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"紅茶","r":"こうちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"します","m":"choose"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Coffee or tea—which would you like?","scene":"A café worker asks a customer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"土曜日","r":"どようび","m":"Saturday"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"日曜日","r":"にちようび","m":"Sunday"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll go on Saturday or Sunday.","scene":"Telling a friend your plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-026||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & combinations","sub":"NかN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NかN","patternReading":"か","coreMeaning":"N or N (choose one)","slug":"forms","definition":"**Basic structure**: N1 + か + N2. か comes directly after each noun except the final noun. When another particle (が, を, に, で, へ...) is added, it comes after the final noun: N1かN2 + particle.\n\nExample: 電車かバス**で**来る (come by train or bus)—で marks the means of transport after the whole N1かN2 phrase.\n\nYou can expand it to three choices: N1かN2かN3. In speech, choice questions often use **N1かN2、どちら…**.","nuance":"Place か **directly after the noun**; do not separate it. If another particle is needed, such as で or を, that particle is **not repeated** after each noun.\n\nIn a choice question, a comma or は/が often follows the noun phrase before どちら.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"N2","note":"Connects two nouns: “N1 or N2”"},{"lhs":"N1かN2","rhs":"は/が/を/に/で","note":"Add a particle based on the phrase’s grammatical role"},{"lhs":"N1かN2","rhs":"かN3","note":"Lists three or more choices"},{"lhs":"N1かN2","rhs":"、どちらが〜ですか","note":"Asks the listener which one to choose"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please come by train or bus.","scene":"Giving directions to your home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"鉛筆","r":"えんぴつ","m":"pencil"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write with a pencil or pen.","scene":"A teacher gives class instructions"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-026||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NかN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NかN","patternReading":"か","coreMeaning":"N or N (choose one)","slug":"usage","definition":"The pattern NかN often appears in daily life when you need to **offer or ask about a choice**.\n\n- **Ask what someone will choose**: N1かN2、どちらにしますか / どちらが好きですか.\n- **State your own choice**: N1かN2にします (I’ll choose N1 or N2). Note that once you have decided, you no longer use か.\n- **Indicate an uncertain means/place/time**: N1かN2で／に／へ…\n\nIn essence, か presents **mutually exclusive options**—only one is chosen.","nuance":"When answering a choice question, Japanese speakers usually state the option they chose, such as コーヒーにします (I’ll have coffee). Do not say コーヒーか紅茶にします after deciding.\n\nIn casual speech, どちら becomes どっち: コーヒーか紅茶、どっち？","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you prefer dogs or cats?","scene":"Asking a friend about pet preferences"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tomorrow, I’ll study in the library or classroom.","scene":"Discussing study plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-026||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing と / や","sub":"NかN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NかN","patternReading":"か","coreMeaning":"N or N (choose one)","slug":"compare","definition":"The particles と・や・か can all connect nouns, but they have **different meanings**:\n\n- **と**: lists **all** the items. AとB = both A and B.\n- **や**: lists **some representative items**, implying there are others. AやB = A, B, etc.\n- **か**: presents a **choice**, with one option selected. AかB = A or B.\n\nExample: コーヒー**と**紅茶を飲みます = drink both; コーヒー**か**紅茶を飲みます = drink one of the two.","nuance":"Learners often confuse these because English uses “and” and “or” in different ways depending on context. Remember: **か = or**, **と/や = and**. と is a complete “and”; や is “and so on.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"紅茶","r":"こうちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll drink coffee or tea.","scene":"Saying you have not decided what to drink"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NとN","point":"と gives a complete list: both A and B. Example: コーヒーと紅茶を飲みます = drink both coffee and tea. With か, you choose one: coffee or tea."},{"vs":"NやN","point":"や gives a partial list: other items may exist. Example: 野菜や果物を買いました = bought vegetables, fruit, etc. With か, the choice is limited to the stated options: vegetables or fruit."}]}},"n5-026||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NかN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NかN","patternReading":"か","coreMeaning":"N or N (choose one)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes with NかN:\n\n1. **Using や instead of か** in a choice question. や lists items; it is not used to ask “which one?”\n2. **Putting the particle after each noun**. Wrong: 電車でかバスで行く. Correct: 電車かバスで行く, because the particle follows the whole N1かN2 phrase.\n3. **Using または in ordinary conversation**—または is formal and common in writing, while か is natural in speech.\n4. **Confusing か with the final question particle**—in NかN, か appears between two nouns, not at the end.","nuance":"In speech, Japanese speakers sometimes repeat particles for emphasis: 電車でもバスでも, but that is a different structure, not NかN. In this pattern, use the particle once.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"果物","r":"くだもの","m":"fruit"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’ll buy vegetables or fruit.","scene":"Telling your mother what to buy"}],"pitfalls":["❌ コーヒーや紅茶、どちらにしますか。 → ✅ コーヒーか紅茶、どちらにしますか。","❌ 電車でかバスで行きます。 → ✅ 電車かバスで行きます。","❌ 鉛筆かで書いてください。 → ✅ 鉛筆かペンで書いてください。","❌ 私はコーヒーか紅茶にします。 (after deciding) → ✅ 私はコーヒーにします。"],"compare":null}},"n5-026||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NかN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NかN","patternReading":"か","coreMeaning":"N or N (choose one)","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory tip for か:\n\n- **か resembles a broken question mark (?)** → か is linked to **choice questions**.\n- Image: **a fork in the road**—turn left (A) or right (B)? You can take only one path. That is NかN.\n- Mnemonic: **“Coffee か tea—choose one!”**\n\nWhenever you see か between two nouns, think of **choosing one**.","nuance":"Compared with と (both) and や (some), か means **one of the stated options**. Remember: か is like a **choice window** in a vending machine—you press only one button.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-027||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"core","definition":"〜いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう is used to invite or suggest that the listener perform an action together with the speaker. Vませんか is a gentle invitation that lets the listener decide; Vましょう is more like a proposal that has already been tentatively agreed on.","nuance":"Common in everyday conversation with friends, coworkers, and acquaintances. **Vませんか is polite and gentle**; **Vましょう is decisive and upbeat**. Avoid it in highly formal writing or correspondence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いっしょに","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"ませんか","r":"ませんか","m":"shall we ~?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we go eat lunch together?","scene":"Inviting a coworker to lunch."},{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見","r":"み","m":"watch"},{"t":"ましょう","r":"ましょう","m":"let’s"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"Let’s watch a movie!","scene":"Inviting a friend to watch a movie."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-027||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & connections","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"forms","definition":"Form: remove ます from the polite verb form and replace it with **ませんか** or **ましょう**. Add いっしょに before the verb to emphasize “together.” With ましょう, adding か creates **Vましょうか**, a gentler question.","nuance":"All Group 1, 2, and 3 verbs follow the same rule: **Vます → remove ます → + ませんか / ましょう**. The short form is not involved.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"V (bỏ ます) + ませんか","note":"Polite invitation."},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"V (bỏ ます) + ましょう","note":"Proposal to do it together."},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"V (bỏ ます) + ましょうか","note":"Polite question: shall we ~? / shall I ~?"},{"lhs":"いっしょに + V","rhs":"いっしょに + (V bỏ ます) + ませんか / ましょう","note":"Emphasizes doing it together."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いっしょに","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"帰り","r":"かえり","m":"go home"},{"t":"ませんか","r":"ませんか","m":"shall we ~?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we go home together?","scene":"Inviting a coworker home after work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう一度","r":"もういちど","m":"once more"},{"t":"言い","r":"いい","m":"say"},{"t":"ましょう","r":"ましょう","m":"let’s"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s say it one more time!","scene":"Teacher asks the class to repeat it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-027||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"usage","definition":"Vませんか is often used for a **first invitation** or when respecting the listener’s decision. Vましょう is used when **both people are already willing** or when encouraging action. Put そろそろ before V to suggest that “it’s about time” to do something.","nuance":"At work, そろそろ会議を始めましょう is a polite suggestion to start the meeting. With close friends, you can say the shorter 行こう, but this pattern is polite and suitable for people you are not close to.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そろそろ","r":"そろそろ","m":"it’s about time"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"始め","r":"はじめ","m":"start"},{"t":"ましょう","r":"ましょう","m":"let’s"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s about time to start the meeting.","scene":"The manager suggests starting once everyone arrives."},{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","r":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drink"},{"t":"ませんか","r":"ませんか","m":"shall we ~?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Would you like to have coffee?","scene":"Inviting a coworker for coffee."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-027||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Vましょうか vs. Vましょう","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"compare","definition":"All three patterns can invite action, but they differ in how much they “settle” the plan. **Vましょうか** is a question that lets the listener agree; **Vませんか** is also a question, but specifically invites the listener and asks whether they want to join; **Vましょう** is a direct proposal that assumes both people will act together.","nuance":"Vませんか is common in everyday conversation: friendly but polite. Vましょう may sound slightly imposing with a superior, so use Vましょうか when speaking to a boss.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"始め","r":"はじめ","m":"start"},{"t":"ましょうか","r":"ましょうか","m":"shall we ~?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we start the meeting?","scene":"The chair asks before starting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vましょうか","point":"Vましょうか is a gentle question that lets the listener agree."},{"vs":"Vませんか","point":"Vませんka invites the listener and asks whether they want to join."},{"vs":"Vましょう","point":"Vましょう is a direct proposal that assumes both will act together."},{"vs":"Vてください","point":"Vてください is a request or soft command; it need not involve doing it together."}]}},"n5-027||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget to add **いっしょに** when inviting someone, or confuse the pattern with a negative form such as **たべないですか**. Remember: this pattern uses the **Vます** stem (remove ます), not the short form.","nuance":"With a boss or older person, use **Vましょうか** instead of **Vましょう** to sound softer. With close friends, Vましょう is possible but remains more polite than Vよう.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いっしょに","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"ませんか","r":"ませんか","m":"shall we ~?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we eat together?","scene":"Inviting a friend to lunch."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 一緒に食べないですか。 → ✅ いっしょに食べませんか。","❌ 会議を始めましょう。（with a boss） → ✅ 会議を始めましょうか。","❌ 食べるましょう。 → ✅ 食べましょう。","❌ 食べないませんか。 → ✅ 食べませんか。"],"compare":null}},"n5-027||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **ませんか** is a negative question, like “shall we eat?”, and acts as a polite invitation; **ましょう** is a decisive proposal, “let’s do it together!” Tip: か opens a choice for the listener.","nuance":"This is only a memory aid, not a grammar rule.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-027||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう · N5","v2":{"pattern":"いっしょにVませんか／Vましょう","patternReading":"いっしょに ませんか／ましょう","coreMeaning":"Shall we ~ together? / Let's ~ together","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases found in textbooks and daily life: inviting someone to eat, go home, go out, or suggesting that it is time to begin.","nuance":"When speaking to an older person, add そろそろ to make the suggestion less abrupt.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そろそろ","r":"そろそろ","m":"it’s about time"},{"t":"出かけ","r":"でかけ","m":"go out"},{"t":"ましょう","r":"ましょう","m":"let’s"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It’s about time we headed out.","scene":"Getting ready to go out and urging everyone along."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-028||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern NでNをVます describes an action performed using a tool or means. N1 before で marks the tool; N2 before を marks the affected object; Vます is the action. In はしでごはんを食べます: はし = chopsticks, ごはん = rice, 食べます = eat.","nuance":"で in this pattern marks a tool, not a location. To say where an action happens, use で with a place meaning, as in 学校で勉強します. For languages, で also marks a means: 日本語でレポートを書きます. This neutral pattern works in both speech and writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はし","r":"はし","m":"chopsticks"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ごはん","r":"ごはん","m":"rice"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat rice with chopsticks.","scene":"Asked how I eat rice, I raise my chopsticks."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ボールペン","m":"ballpoint pen"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I write my name with a ballpoint pen.","scene":"The teacher asks us to write our names."},{"tokens":[{"t":"スマホ","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮りました","r":"とりました","m":"took"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I took a photo with my smartphone.","scene":"Showing a friend a photo I just took."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-028||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"forms","definition":"Standard structure: [tool N] で [object N] を [Vます]. で follows the tool, and を follows the affected object. The verb uses ます forms: Vます for present/future, Vました for past, Vません for negative, and Vませんでした for past negative. Add か at the end for a yes/no question.","nuance":"In casual conversation, を may sometimes be omitted after the object, but in standard writing and JLPT N5 questions, keep を. ます is the polite form, suitable when speaking to elders, teachers, or people you do not know well.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（công cụ）","rhs":"で N（vật）を Vます","note":"Affirmative present/future"},{"lhs":"N（công cụ）","rhs":"で N（vật）を Vました","note":"Affirmative past"},{"lhs":"N（công cụ）","rhs":"で N（vật）を Vません","note":"Negative present/future"},{"lhs":"N（công cụ）","rhs":"で N（vật）を Vませんでした","note":"Negative past"},{"lhs":"N（công cụ）","rhs":"で N（vật）を Vますか","note":"Yes/no question"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ナイフ","m":"knife"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"パン","m":"bread"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"切ります","r":"きります","m":"cut"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cut bread with a knife.","scene":"Preparing a meal in the kitchen."},{"tokens":[{"t":"えんぴつ","m":"pencil"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きました","r":"かきました","m":"wrote"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wrote a letter with a pencil.","scene":"Telling a friend about writing a letter."},{"tokens":[{"t":"フォーク","m":"fork"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"サラダ","m":"salad"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat salad with a fork.","scene":"Eating salad with a fork at a restaurant."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-028||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when you want to say what you do and with what. Examples include eating rice with chopsticks, writing a name with a pen, taking photos with a phone, and speaking in Japanese. To ask about a tool, use 何で: 何で名前を書きますか？ (What do you write your name with?). If adding a location, put it first: レストランではしでごはんを食べます.","nuance":"In daily conversation, people often use this pattern when answering “with what?” If you only ask “what do you do?”, で is unnecessary. This structure is common in classrooms, offices, and restaurants. With ます verbs, it sounds polite and natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I write the report in Japanese.","scene":"A coworker asks what language I use."},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"切ります","r":"きります","m":"cut"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you cut the vegetables with?","scene":"Asking a roommate who is cooking."},{"tokens":[{"t":"はさみ","m":"scissors"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"紙","r":"かみ","m":"paper"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"切ります","r":"きります","m":"cut"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cut paper with scissors.","scene":"Making a craft and asking what to use."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-028||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare place で, NをV, NとV","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish で in this pattern from three other roles: NでVます with a place means where the action happens, NとVます marks someone you do it with, and NでNをVます marks the tool used. Without で, NをVます only states the action and object, not the means.","nuance":"When a sentence contains both a location and a tool, Japanese usually puts the location first: レストランで はしで ごはんを食べます. Two uses of で in one sentence are clear from context.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"えんぴつ","m":"pencil"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"絵","r":"え","m":"picture"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"描きます","r":"かきます","m":"draw"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I draw a picture with a pencil.","scene":"Explaining what I use for drawing."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NでVます","point":"で can answer “where”: 学校で勉強します; tool で answers “with what”: ペンで書きます."},{"vs":"NをVます","point":"Without で, the sentence only states the action and object: 名前を書きます does not say what you use; add で: ペンで名前を書きます."},{"vs":"NとVます","point":"と marks a person or group you act with: 友達と食べます; で marks a tool: はしで食べます."}]}},"n5-028||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include using は instead of で for a tool, using を instead of で for a tool, and confusing place で with tool で. Always check: the tool takes で, and the affected object takes を.","nuance":"ペンは名前を書きます sounds like “As for the pen, it writes the name,” which is wrong because the pen is not the subject. スマホを写真を撮ります is wrong because を replaces で for the tool.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I write my name with a pen.","scene":"Filling in a form and describing the action."}],"pitfalls":["❌ ペンは名前を書きます。 → ✅ ペンで名前を書きます。","❌ スマホを写真を撮ります。 → ✅ スマホで写真を撮ります。","❌ ここで書きます。(if you mean “with a pen”) → ✅ ペンで書きます。"],"compare":null}},"n5-028||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture the tool, such as chopsticks or a pen, marked by で; the affected object, such as rice or a name, marked by を; and the action as Vます. Mnemonic: “TOOL で, OBJECT を, ACTION ます.”","nuance":"When you see で, ask “with what?” When you see を, ask “what object?” If both answers make sense, you have the right pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-028||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NでNをVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでNをVます","patternReading":"えぬでえぬをぶいます","coreMeaning":"Use a tool or means to perform an action on an object","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in daily life and on the N5 exam. Learn them as tool + action chunks: はしで食べる, ペンで書く, 日本語で手紙を書く, スマホで撮る.","nuance":"When referring to a language, 日本語で手紙を書く uses Japanese as the means of writing. スマホで写真を撮る is a common phrase among young people in Japan.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"はし","r":"はし","m":"chopsticks"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ごはん","r":"ごはん","m":"rice"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat rice with chopsticks.","scene":"Mentioning an everyday eating habit."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-029||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜に〜をあげます describes giving an object to someone. に marks the recipient, and を marks the item given. This is the basic giving pattern, with the action moving from the speaker (or another giver) outward. Do not use it when the recipient is “me” (at N5, use くれます or もらいます instead).","nuance":"あげます sounds friendly and familiar, and is used when giving to an equal or someone below you. When giving to a superior such as a teacher or boss, Japanese uses さしあげます, but that is beyond N5. あげます often describes an action intended to make the recipient happy.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"gave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I gave a book to Mr. Tanaka.","scene":"You give a book to a colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"お菓子","r":"おかし","m":"snacks"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"give"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will give a child some candy.","scene":"In a park, you give a child a packet of candy"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-029||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"forms","definition":"Sentence structure: recipient + に + gift + を + あげます. The main forms of あげます are past あげました, negative あげません, and dictionary form あげる. The particles に and を are required.","nuance":"In casual speech, あげる is common; the polite form あげます is used in everyday polite conversation with equals or younger people. The negative あげません is less common when discussing gifts; speakers often use あげない instead.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（người）","rhs":"に","note":"Recipient"},{"lhs":"N（vật）","rhs":"を","note":"Item given"},{"lhs":"あげます","rhs":"～（lịch sự）","note":"Present/future"},{"lhs":"あげました","rhs":"～（quá khứ）","note":"Gave"},{"lhs":"あげません","rhs":"～（phủ định）","note":"Do not give"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"gave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I gave my friend the letter.","scene":"You tell someone you wrote a letter to your friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"兄","r":"あに","m":"older brother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"チケット","r":"チケット","m":"ticket"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"give"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will give my older brother a ticket.","scene":"You have two movie tickets and plan to give him one"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-029||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when giving a specific object to someone: for a birthday, as thanks, as a souvenir, or simply when handing something to another person. Clearly identify the recipient and the item given.","nuance":"The tone is familiar rather than highly formal. When giving to an older or higher-status person, Japanese often uses the honorific さしあげます, but at N5 あげます is sufficient. A common question is 何をあげましたか。","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誕生日","r":"たんじょうび","m":"birthday"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"gave"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did you give them for their birthday?","scene":"Asking friends about a birthday gift"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flowers"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"give"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will give my mother flowers.","scene":"You buy flowers for Mother’s Day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-029||compare":{"title":"⚖️ あげます vs もらいます/くれます","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"compare","definition":"These three verbs all describe giving and receiving. あげます means giving outward from me to someone else; もらいます means receiving from someone else; くれます means someone gives to me, with the giver as the subject. The key is the direction of the action.","nuance":"Use くれます when emphasizing who gave something, and もらいます when emphasizing that you received it. あげます is used when describing yourself giving to someone else.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげました","r":"あげました","m":"gave"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I gave my friend a book.","scene":"You describe giving your friend a book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"もらいます","point":"もらいます means “receive”: N1にN2をもらいます (the speaker receives from someone). Same event: 私は田中さんに本をもらいました。(I received a book from Mr. Tanaka) vs 田中さんに本をあげました。(I gave Mr. Tanaka a book)"},{"vs":"くれます","point":"くれます means “someone gives me”: the giver is the subject, and the recipient is me or someone close to me. 彼女が花をくれました。(She gave me flowers)"}]}},"n5-029||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse に and を, putting the recipient and gift in the wrong positions. Remember: に = person, を = thing. Also avoid あげます when “I” am the recipient; use くれます or もらいます instead.","nuance":"English says “give someone something” without particles, so the roles can be easy to mix up. Always remember: recipient + に + item + を + あげます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"妹","r":"いもうと","m":"younger sister"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flowers"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"give"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will give my younger sister flowers.","scene":"You give flowers on your younger sister’s birthday"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 田中さんを本にあげました。 → ✅ 田中さんに本をあげました。","❌ 花を私にあげます。 → ✅ 私は花をもらいました。（or 彼が私に花をくれました）","❌ 本が田中さんにあげました。 → ✅ 本を田中さんにあげました。"],"compare":null}},"n5-029||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine handing a gift to someone: に shows the recipient’s direction, like an arrow pointing to them; を marks the item in your hand. Remember the order: person + に + item + を + あげる.","nuance":"This tip helps prevent reversed particles. Think of に as “to” and を as marking “what” is given.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-029||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NにNをあげます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをあげます","patternReading":"に を あげます","coreMeaning":"Give an object to someone (from me outward)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common gift-giving phrases include 花をあげます (give flowers), 本をあげます (give a book), プレゼントをあげます (give a present), and お土産をあげます (give a souvenir).","nuance":"These phrases often appear on the N5 test and in everyday conversation. Remember to add に for the recipient.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"お土産","r":"おみやげ","m":"souvenir"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"give"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will give my friend a souvenir.","scene":"You have returned from a trip and want to give your friend a gift"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-030||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern describes receiving an object from someone else. に marks the giver, while を marks the item received. It is used in daily speech for gifts, things given to you, allowance, and more. The key point is the direction: the item moves from someone else to me.","nuance":"This is a neutral, polite expression using the ます ending. It works with friends, family, and older people when speaking politely. It often carries a gentle sense of gratitude toward the giver. For a more respectful expression, Japanese uses いただきます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"プレゼント","m":"gift"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received a gift from my friend.","scene":"Asking about a birthday gift just received"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received a book from my teacher.","scene":"A teacher gives me a textbook"},{"tokens":[{"t":"親","r":"おや","m":"parents"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"時計","r":"とけい","m":"watch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received a watch from my parents.","scene":"Telling someone about a gifted watch"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-030||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N(person) に + N(item) を + もらいます**. もらいます is a Group I verb, so it conjugates like other ます-verbs: past もらいました, negative もらいません, and past negative もらいませんでした. In casual conversation, use the plain forms **もらう / もらった**.","nuance":"This polite sentence works in most situations with people you are not close to. With friends, you can drop ます: 友だちにプレゼントをもらった.","structure":[{"lhs":"N(người)","rhs":"に N(vật) を もらいます","note":"Polite non-past affirmative."},{"lhs":"N(người)","rhs":"に N(vật) を もらいました","note":"Past: received."},{"lhs":"N(người)","rhs":"に N(vật) を もらいません","note":"Non-past negative: do not receive."},{"lhs":"誰","rhs":"に N(vật) を もらいましたか","note":"Asks who the giver was."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received this from my friend.","scene":"Showing an item and explaining its source"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"か"}],"vi":"What did you receive from your teacher?","scene":"Asking a child after class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-030||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when the speaker, or someone in the speaker’s group, receives something from someone. Common situations include receiving birthday gifts, souvenirs, letters, or allowance from parents.","nuance":"Use に with a specific person. With an institution or organization such as a school or company, から is more common. To ask “from whom?”, use 誰に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received money from my father.","scene":"Father gives me allowance"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flowers"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received flowers from someone at work.","scene":"Coworkers give flowers on my last day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-030||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from あげます","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"compare","definition":"The pattern most easily confused with もらいます is **あげます**. Both can use an NにNを structure, but the direction of giving and receiving is reversed.","nuance":"When the speaker receives, use もらいます. When the speaker gives, use あげます. The same event, “my friend gives me a gift,” can be described from my viewpoint (もらいます) or my friend’s viewpoint (あげます).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received a photo from my friend.","scene":"A friend gives me a keepsake photo"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NにNをあげます","point":"あげます means GIVE (me → someone else); もらいます means RECEIVE (someone else → me). Same event: 私は友だちに写真をあげました (I gave my friend a photo) / 友だちは私に写真をもらいました (my friend received a photo from me)."},{"vs":"NがNをくれます","point":"もらいます uses the RECEIVER’s viewpoint: 私は友だちに写真をもらいました. くれます uses the GIVER’s viewpoint and often highlights the favor: 友だちは私に写真をくれました. Same event, different subject."}]}},"n5-030||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner mistakes","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with もらいます include choosing the wrong particle, confusing the direction of giving and receiving, or using に for a place.","nuance":"These errors often appear in tests and conversation because English does not use Japanese-style particles. Check: who is the giver? Am I receiving or giving?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received a letter from my friend.","scene":"A friend sends me a handwritten letter"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生**を**本をもらいました。 → ✅ 先生**に**本をもらいました。 (the giver takes に; the item received takes を)","❌ 私は友だちに本をあげました。 (when the speaker received it) → ✅ 私は友だちに本をもらいました。","❌ 学校に本をもらいました。 → ✅ 学校**から**本をもらいました。 (use から for organizations; に for specific people)"],"compare":null}},"n5-030||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"memory","definition":"Use a quick image and sound cue to remember the direction of もらいます.","nuance":"You do not need to memorize it mechanically; remember “MÒ INTO” as something from another person moving into my hands. あげ means giving it out away from me.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-030||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NにNをもらいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNをもらいます","patternReading":"にをもらいます","coreMeaning":"Receive N from someone else (direction: someone else → me)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common noun + をもらう phrases include: プレゼントをもらう, お金をもらう, メールをもらう, おみやげをもらう...","nuance":"In tests, these nouns often appear with をもらいます/もらいました. To emphasize that something was received, use the past form もらいました.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"received"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I received a photo from him.","scene":"He gives me a keepsake photo"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-031||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"core","definition":"で is a particle marking a **means or tool**. After a language name, it means **“in/by …”**: use that language to speak or write. After a means such as a vehicle, email, or pen, it means **“by/via …”**. “Nで話します” means “speak using N (the N language),” not “talk about N.”","nuance":"Very common in both speech and writing; it is not especially formal. The listener immediately understands N as the tool used for the action. To say “with someone,” use と, not で.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"で","m":"by/in"},{"t":"話して","r":"はなして","m":"speak"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please speak in Japanese.","scene":"A teacher asks students to use Japanese in class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"メール","m":"email"},{"t":"で","m":"by/via"},{"t":"連絡","r":"れんらく","m":"contact"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will contact you by email.","scene":"Telling a friend how you will make contact"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-031||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic form is **N + で + V**. で does not change for tense or past; time is expressed by the verb. To ask about a means, use **何で** (nan de) before the verb.","nuance":"This is a fixed structure; で does not change. Adding も after で (でも) means “also by/in …”: 英語でも話せます = “I can also speak in English.”","structure":[{"lhs":"Language N","rhs":"で + V","note":"日本語で話す／日本語で書く"},{"lhs":"Means N","rhs":"で + V","note":"バスで行く／メールで送る"},{"lhs":"何","rhs":"で + Vますか","note":"ask “by what/how?”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go by bus.","scene":"Answering how you get to work each day"},{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"で","m":"in/by"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write in English.","scene":"A teacher tells students to do the exercise in English"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-031||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"usage","definition":"Use it to describe the **method** of performing an action: in which language, through which communication method, or by which vehicle. At beginner level, で follows a noun naming a tool or means.","nuance":"Do not use で with people such as friends or teachers. To say “do something with someone,” use **Nと**. で marks a tool, not a co-participant.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"で","m":"in/by"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きました","r":"かきました","m":"wrote"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wrote a letter in Japanese.","scene":"Telling a friend about writing to a Japanese friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How did you get to Japan?","scene":"Asking a friend who has just returned from Japan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"telephone"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"話しましょう","r":"はなしましょう","m":"let’s talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s talk on the phone.","scene":"Inviting a friend to talk by phone that evening"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-031||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish で place・を・と","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish で for means from で for place, and from を and と. Identify the noun before で: after a place, it marks where an action occurs; after a tool, it marks the means. を marks the direct object, while で marks the tool. と means “with someone,” while で means “by what means.”","nuance":"The same で can have different meanings depending on **which noun it follows**. After a place, it marks where the action occurs; after a tool, it marks the tool used.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"で","m":"in"},{"t":"話しました","r":"はなしました","m":"talked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I talked with my friend in Japanese.","scene":"Describing a conversation with a Japanese friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"で (địa điểm)","point":"After a place, で marks where an action occurs; after a means or language, it marks the tool. E.g.: 部屋で日本語を話します (speak in a room) / 日本語で話します (speak in Japanese)."},{"vs":"を","point":"日本語を話します = speak Japanese (the object); 日本語で話します = speak using Japanese (the tool)."},{"vs":"と","point":"友達と話します = talk with a friend; 日本語で話します = speak in Japanese."}]}},"n5-031||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors caused by confusing で for tools with が/を for objects and と for people you act with. Each error includes the correct version to help learners avoid traps in conversation and on tests.","nuance":"Carefully distinguish **object** from **tool**: “Japanese” can be the object of understanding, using が/を, or the communication tool, using で.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"で","m":"by"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came by train.","scene":"Answering “How did you get here?”"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語が話します。 → ✅ 日本語で話します。 (or 日本語を話します。)","❌ 日本語でわかりません。 → ✅ 日本語がわかりません。","❌ バスを学校へ行きます。 → ✅ バスで学校へ行きます。"],"compare":null}},"n5-031||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of で as **“with what you are holding”**—the tool used to do something. Japanese, email, a bus, and a pen can all function as tools.","nuance":"This trick helps you avoid confusing で with を or に.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-031||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nで (ngôn ngữ／phương tiện)","patternReading":"で","coreMeaning":"By N – use a language or means to perform an action","slug":"phrases","definition":"These N + で phrases are common in N5 textbooks and tests: 日本語で話す, メールで送る, バスで行く, ペンで書く, 電話で連絡する.","nuance":"Communication methods are especially common at work: メールで送る, 電話で連絡する, ファックスで送る.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"で","m":"with"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"write"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write your name here with a pen.","scene":"A staff member guides a visitor filling out a form"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-032||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"core","definition":"A basic pattern for saying **N is (adjective)**. An い-adjective can stand alone as the predicate, with no な needed. Use it to describe a person's or thing's features, qualities, or feelings.","nuance":"This is the standard polite style, suitable for teachers and strangers. With friends, です can be omitted. In the negative, くない + です sounds gentle rather than blunt.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is interesting.","scene":"Reading in the library and talking to a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒くない","r":"さむくない","m":"not cold"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is not cold today.","scene":"Going outside in the morning and noticing the weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-032||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"forms","definition":"An い-adjective has **four main predicate forms**: present affirmative (Aいです), present negative (Aくないです), past (Aかったです), and past negative (Aくなかったです). To conjugate: **remove い and add the ending**.","nuance":"くないです is the N5 polite negative. くありません is also more formal, but beginner textbooks usually teach くないです first. In casual speech, simply say Aい／Aくない.","structure":[{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aい + です","note":"present affirmative"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aくない + です","note":"present negative"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aかった + です","note":"past affirmative"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aくなかった + です","note":"past negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"big"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is big.","scene":"Standing in front of a supermarket"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今朝","r":"けさ","m":"this morning"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒くなかった","r":"さむくなかった","m":"was not cold"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was not cold this morning.","scene":"Recalling the morning weather with a colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-032||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **ask and answer** about features or qualities. Add か to make a question; use が to connect two ideas; combine it with とても (very) or あまり + negative (not ... much).","nuance":"ですが usually marks a mild contrast: 'delicious but expensive.' あまり must be used with a negative: あまりおいしくないです. In casual conversation, omit です and optionally add ね／よ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ラーメン","m":"ramen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This ramen is delicious but expensive.","scene":"Commenting on ramen at a restaurant with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"その","m":"that"},{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...much"},{"t":"大きくない","r":"おおきくない","m":"not big"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That bag is not very big.","scene":"Choosing a bag in a store"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-032||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with nouns / na-adjectives","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"compare","definition":"A quick comparison of three easily confused patterns. **NはNです** states identity or category. **NはAいです** describes a quality. **NはNAです** uses a な-adjective: there is no な before です. Noun negatives use ではありません, while い-adjective negatives use くないです.","nuance":"These patterns share Nは〜です, but their endings differ. Do not add な before です with an い-adjective; do not use ではありません to negate an い-adjective.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Introducing an object to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"The predicate is a noun: it says 'what something is.' Aいです describes a quality."},{"vs":"NはAなです","point":"With な-adjectives, な appears before a noun, but not before です (きれいな です? actually きれいです). Remember: い-adjectives need no な."},{"vs":"NはNではありません","point":"Noun negatives use ではありません, not くないです."}]}},"n5-032||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often apply noun or な-adjective rules to い-adjectives, producing errors such as **おいしいなです** or **おいしくありません** (not the target form at this level).","nuance":"These errors may confuse listeners or sound unnatural. Remember: an い-adjective can be the predicate itself; for the negative, change い to くない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おもしろくない","m":"not interesting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is not interesting.","scene":"Answering a friend's question about the book"}],"pitfalls":["❌ この本はおもしろいなです。 → ✅ この本はおもしろいです。","❌ 今日は寒いですではありません。 → ✅ 今日は寒くないです。","❌ このラーメンはおいしいくないです。 → ✅ このラーメンはおいしくないです。","❌ あの店はいくないです。 → ✅ あの店はよくないです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-032||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"memory","definition":"A fun memory aid: an い-adjective wears い like a hat. To make it negative, **take off the い hat and put on the くない hat**. To make it past, **take off い and put on かった**.","nuance":"This mnemonic helps you avoid adding な or using ではありません.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-032||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはAいです／Aくないです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはAいです／Aくないです","patternReading":"えぬ は いです ／ くないです","coreMeaning":"い-adjective predicates: affirmative / negative","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common い-adjectives often seen in conversation and on the N5 exam: **おいしい (delicious)**, **たかい (expensive, high)**, **あたらしい (new)**, **おおきい (big)**, **ちいさい (small)**.","nuance":"For objects, たかい usually means expensive; for people, it means tall. Use the context to determine the meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake is delicious.","scene":"Praising dessert at a restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-033||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"core","definition":"〜なN places a **na-adjective** before a noun to **describe** a person or thing. The formula is **adjective stem + な + noun**: きれい → きれいな町. When the adjective is the predicate after は, **do not use な**: この町はきれいです.","nuance":"Generally neutral and usable in speech, writing, with friends, or with older people. It simply **describes a quality** without emphasizing emotion. Common na-adjectives include: きれい、静か、有名、便利、元気、好き、嫌い、大切、安全.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"きれいな","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"町","r":"まち","m":"town"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a beautiful town, isn't it?","scene":"Seeing a tidy town while traveling"},{"tokens":[{"t":"静かな","r":"しずかな","m":"quiet"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like quiet rooms.","scene":"Choosing a rental room with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-033||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and structures","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"forms","definition":"When a na-adjective modifies a noun, there are four main forms: **affirmative** (NAなN), **negative** (NAじゃないN), **past** (NAだったN), and when it is the predicate, **NはNAです／じゃありません**.","nuance":"Only the **pre-noun position** requires な; the predicate position does not. Do not add な after じゃない before a noun (not じゃないなN).","structure":[{"lhs":"NA + な","rhs":" + N","note":"Affirmative modifier: きれいな町"},{"lhs":"NAじゃない","rhs":" + N","note":"Negative modifier: 有名じゃない店"},{"lhs":"NAだった","rhs":" + N","note":"Past modifier: 静かだった部屋"},{"lhs":"Nは + NA","rhs":"です／じゃありません","note":"Predicate: この町はきれいです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"便利な","r":"べんりな","m":"convenient"},{"t":"アプリ","m":"app"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使って","r":"つかって","m":"use"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am using a convenient app.","scene":"Telling a coworker about a newly downloaded app"},{"tokens":[{"t":"有名じゃない","r":"ゆうめいじゃない","m":"not famous"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to a little-known shop.","scene":"Talking about eating at a lesser-known place"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-033||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use NAなN to **attach a quality** to a noun when answering questions such as **どんなNですか**, or when praising, criticizing, or describing something.","nuance":"In conversation, **好きなN** (something or someone you like) and **きれいなN** are extremely common phrases. To praise something, ね is often added: きれいな町ですね.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"きれいな","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flower"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought a bouquet of beautiful flowers.","scene":"Showing a friend what you bought at a flower market"},{"tokens":[{"t":"有名な","r":"ゆうめいな","m":"famous"},{"t":"レストラン","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to a famous restaurant.","scene":"Talking about a weekend meal"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-033||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from い-adjectives","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **NAなN** from three commonly confused patterns: **い-adjective + N**, **NのN**, and **NAです (predicate)**.","nuance":"English has no equivalent linking particle, so learners may omit or confuse な and の.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"元気な","r":"げんきな","m":"energetic"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like energetic dogs.","scene":"Answering a friend who asks about having a dog"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"い形容詞 + N","point":"An い-adjective does NOT take な: 大きい犬 (not 大きいな犬). A na-adjective must take な: 元気な犬."},{"vs":"NのN","point":"Noun + の shows possession or relationship: 先生の本 (“the teacher's book”); NA + な shows a quality: 有名な先生 (“a famous teacher”)."},{"vs":"NAです（vị ngữ）","point":"After は, the modifier becomes the predicate and drops な: この町はきれいです, not きれいなです."}]}},"n5-033||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes learners make with NAなN: keeping **だ/です** when modifying a noun, adding **な** to an i-adjective, or using **の** instead of **な**.","nuance":"Ask yourself quickly: is this a na-adjective or an i-adjective? If it is an i-adjective, **never add な**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"有名な","r":"ゆうめいな","m":"famous"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person is a famous teacher.","scene":"Introducing a teacher at school"}],"pitfalls":["❌ きれいだ部屋 → ✅ きれいな部屋. だ ends a predicate; when modifying a noun, replace it with な. This is a very common mistake.","❌ 大きいな犬 → ✅ 大きい犬. 大きい is an い-adjective and never takes な. Only na-adjectives need な before a noun.","❌ 有名の先生 → ✅ 有名な先生. の connects nouns, while a na-adjective before a noun takes な, not の.","❌ 静かです部屋 → ✅ 静かな部屋. です is the polite predicate form and cannot come before a noun. Use the な-linking form instead.","❌ この町はきれいなです → ✅ この町はきれいです. As a predicate, drop な and use です. な is used only before a noun."],"compare":null}},"n5-033||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **な as a small bridge** connecting NA to a noun: whenever the two stand side by side, the bridge appears automatically. When NA stands at the end of a sentence, no bridge is needed.","nuance":"If you forget, say to yourself: “NA must **na** before a noun”—the sound reminds you of な.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-033||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NAなN · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NAなN","patternReading":"な","coreMeaning":"A na-adjective modifies a noun (add な before the noun)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Some common NAなN phrases used in daily life and on the N5 exam.","nuance":"Learning fixed phrases helps you respond faster than assembling each word from scratch.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"好きな","r":"すきな","m":"favorite"},{"t":"食べ物","r":"たべもの","m":"food"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is your favorite food?","scene":"Asking someone you just met"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-034||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"core","definition":"This structure politely describes what N is like. With な-adjectives (NA), the affirmative form is NA + です, and the negative form is NA + じゃありません／ではありません. Most importantly, when NA is the predicate at the end of a sentence, do not add な; な appears only when NA comes before a noun (NAなN).","nuance":"Polite and neutral; suitable for teachers, bosses, and people you have just met. じゃありません is friendly and natural in conversation; ではありません is more formal and suits writing or tests.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"町","r":"まち","m":"town"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"にぎやか","m":"lively"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This town is lively.","scene":"Commenting on your neighborhood while walking downtown."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not very"},{"t":"きれい","m":"clean"},{"t":"じゃありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My room is not very clean.","scene":"A classmate asks what your apartment is like."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-034||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"forms","definition":"Polite forms of a な-adjective predicate. Affirmative: Nは + NA + です. Negative: Nは + NA + じゃありません／ではありません. Question: Nは + NA + ですか. When NA modifies a noun, use NAなN, and な is required. The past forms, introduced later, are Nは + NA + でした／じゃありませんでした.","nuance":"じゃありません is a shortened form of ではありません and has the same meaning. In speech, じゃありません is common; in written tests, ではありません is the safer choice.","structure":[{"lhs":"N は","rhs":"NA です","note":"Affirmative, present/future"},{"lhs":"N は","rhs":"NA じゃありません／ではありません","note":"Negative; じゃ is conversational, では is formal"},{"lhs":"N は","rhs":"NA ですか","note":"Question"},{"lhs":"NA","rhs":"な + N","note":"Noun modifier; な is required"},{"lhs":"N は","rhs":"NA でした／じゃありませんでした","note":"Past (introduced later)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"簡単","r":"かんたん","m":"easy"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is easy.","scene":"Talking with a friend about the textbook while studying."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"きれい","m":"clean"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room is not clean.","scene":"Commenting to a friend while viewing an apartment."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-034||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"usage","definition":"Common NA words describe qualities and states of people or things: きれい (beautiful/clean), 静か (quiet), にぎやか (lively), 親切 (kind), 有名 (famous), 元気 (well). The sentence NはNAです answers “What is N like?”","nuance":"Do not use this pattern for actions; use a verb (Vます) instead. NA commonly combines with adverbs such as とても (very) and あまり〜じゃありません (not very ...).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"well"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother is well.","scene":"Someone asks how your family is doing."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is very quiet here.","scene":"Commenting on the atmosphere upon entering a library."},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"町","r":"まち","m":"town"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not very"},{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"じゃありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This town is not very famous.","scene":"Introducing your hometown to a foreign friend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-034||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish three easily confused word types when used as predicates: な-adjectives, い-adjectives, and nouns. All three can appear at the end of a sentence with です, but their negative forms and noun-modifying patterns differ. With な-adjectives, remember: keep な before a noun, but drop it when NA is the predicate. Their negative forms also differ from い-adjectives, and their noun connection differs from nouns.","nuance":"Both NA and い-adjectives use です in the affirmative, but they conjugate differently in the negative. NA and nouns both use です at the end, but NA needs な before a noun, while a noun needs の.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flower"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This flower is beautiful.","scene":"Showing off a flower in the park."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"ではありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That person is not famous.","scene":"Looking at a photo and answering “Is that person famous?”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Aいです","point":"Both use です in the affirmative, but the negative differs: NA → じゃありません (きれいじゃありません), い-adj → くないです (面白くないです)."},{"vs":"NはNです","point":"Before a noun, NA needs な (きれいな花), while a noun needs の (日本の花). At the sentence end, NA takes no extra な: 花はきれいです."},{"vs":"NAなN","point":"With the same NA: keep な before a noun; drop it as the predicate. 例: 静かな町です / 町は静かです."}]}},"n5-034||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistakes are adding な before です, omitting な before a noun, or conjugating NA like an い-adjective.","nuance":"These errors make the sentence grammatically incorrect even if the listener understands it. Repeat the correct forms: 静かです, 静かな町.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お寺","r":"おてら","m":"temple"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The temple is quiet.","scene":"Commenting to a friend while visiting a temple."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 静かなです。 → ✅ 静かです。 (No な needed when NA ends the sentence)","❌ きれい花です。 → ✅ きれいな花です。 (Add な before a noun after NA)","❌ きれいくないです。 → ✅ きれいじゃありません。 (Do not conjugate NA like an い-adjective)","❌ 私は元気なです。 → ✅ 私は元気です。 (な + です is wrong; な only goes before a noun)"],"compare":null}},"n5-034||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visualize な as a “handbag.” NA wears this bag only when dressing up a noun (NAなN). When NA reaches the end of the sentence alone, take the bag off, leaving NA + です.","nuance":"Mnemonic: “With a noun, wear な; at sentence end, drop な.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-034||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNAです／NAじゃありません","patternReading":"えぬ は な です／な じゃありません","coreMeaning":"N is NA / N is not NA (stating or denying a quality)","slug":"phrases","definition":"NA words that appear often in conversation and N5 tests include 元気, きれい, 有名, 簡単, 静か, にぎやか, and 親切.","nuance":"Remember them with adverbs: とても元気です, あまり有名じゃありません. When you meet a new NA, classify it as a な-adjective and apply this pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"歌手","r":"かしゅ","m":"singer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That singer is famous.","scene":"Talking about your favorite celebrity."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-035||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"どんなNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どんなNですか","patternReading":"どんな","coreMeaning":"What kind of N? / What is N like? (asking for a description)","slug":"core","definition":"「どんなNですか」is used to **ask for a description of a noun's features, qualities, appearance, or nature**. The listener answers with an い-adjective, な-adjective, or short description—not yes/no or the object's name.","nuance":"This is an **open-ended**, neutral question used to get acquainted, learn about preferences, or request information. どんな is friendlier and more common than どのような, and fits both conversation and ordinary writing. With older people, ですか provides the needed politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is the new teacher like?","scene":"Asking a classmate about the new teacher on the first day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of music do you like?","scene":"Two girls chat during a break."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-035||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & form","sub":"どんなNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どんなNですか","patternReading":"どんな","coreMeaning":"What kind of N? / What is N like? (asking for a description)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structurally, どんな always comes **directly before the noun** it modifies: どんな + N + ですか. It has no tense or negative form of its own (negation appears in the answer: あまり〜ない...). Add ですか for a polite question; in casual conversation, ですか may be omitted.","nuance":"Because どんな is an **interrogative prenominal modifier** (連体詞), it **must be followed by a noun** and cannot normally stand alone. So do not say どんなですか without N, unless the noun is understood. Answers commonly use adjectives: 「明るい人です」「親切な人です」.","structure":[{"lhs":"どんな","rhs":"N + ですか","note":"Ask about N's general qualities"},{"lhs":"どんな","rhs":"N + が + 好きですか／嫌いですか","note":"Ask what kind of N someone likes or dislikes"},{"lhs":"Trả lời","rhs":"Aいです／NAです／NAなNです","note":"Give a short adjective description"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of book is this?","scene":"Curious about the unusual book on a friend's desk."},{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of people do you like?","scene":"Asking a friend about their type while chatting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-035||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"どんなNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どんなNですか","patternReading":"どんな","coreMeaning":"What kind of N? / What is N like? (asking for a description)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch covers practical situations: asking about **people** (personality, appearance), **objects** (color, quality), **places**, **preferences**, and **jobs**. When you encounter an unfamiliar N, use どんなN to request a descriptive picture.","nuance":"In a sentence with a topic, どんな comes before the noun functioning as the topic or object. No particle follows どんな itself. For a **very polite** question, use どのような instead; at N5, どんな is already natural enough.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of shop is that?","scene":"Asking a friend about a newly opened restaurant."},{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is your new job like?","scene":"A close friend asks after your first day at work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-035||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with どの・何の","sub":"どんなNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どんなNですか","patternReading":"どんな","coreMeaning":"What kind of N? / What is N like? (asking for a description)","slug":"compare","definition":"These are the patterns most easily confused with どんなN. Each answers a different question: どんなN asks about **type or description**, 何のN asks about **topic or origin**, どのN asks about **selection**, どうですか asks about **impressions**, and NはNですか asks to **confirm identity**.","nuance":"Always ask yourself: \"What is it like?\" → どんな; \"What is it/about what?\" → 何の; \"Which one?\" → どの.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"色","r":"いろ","m":"color"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What color do you like?","scene":"Asking about preferences during a chat."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"何のNですか","point":"何の asks about **content, material, or origin**; どんな asks about **qualities**. 例: これは何の本ですか (what is the book about?) ≠ これはどんな本ですか (what is the book like?)"},{"vs":"どのNですか","point":"どの + N asks you to **choose one specific item** from a limited group; どんな + N asks about a **type** without a fixed limit. 例: どの本が好きですか (which book here do you like?) ≠ どんな本が好きですか (what kind of books do you like in general?)"},{"vs":"どうですか","point":"どうですか asks about **impressions after or during an experience**; どんなNですか asks for a **description before direct experience**. 例: 日本の生活はどうですか (how is life in Japan for you now?) ≠ 日本はどんな国ですか (what kind of country is Japan—general information)."},{"vs":"N1はN2ですか","point":"This question **confirms identity**: \"Is N1 N2?\"; どんなN does not confirm identity but requests a description. 例: あの人は田中さんですか ≠ あの人はどんな人ですか."}]}},"n5-035||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"どんなNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どんなNですか","patternReading":"どんな","coreMeaning":"What kind of N? / What is N like? (asking for a description)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with どんなN include confusing it with どの, omitting the noun after it, answering \"yes/no,\" and overusing the pronoun あなた.","nuance":"Learners often translate \"what is it like?\" directly as どう, but this structure requires どんな + N. Always include the noun for a natural question.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind of"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"food"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of food do you like?","scene":"Asking a friend over lunch."}],"pitfalls":["❌ どの人ですか (asking about personality) → ✅ どんな人ですか","❌ どんなですか (missing N) → ✅ どんな人ですか／どんな本ですか","❌ どんな本ですか？→ はい、本です (yes/no answer) → ✅ 面白い本です (description)","❌ どんなの人が好きですか → ✅ どんな人が好きですか (do not add の after どんな)"],"compare":null}},"n5-035||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"どんなNですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どんなNですか","patternReading":"どんな","coreMeaning":"What kind of N? / What is N like? (asking for a description)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: どんな comes before N like **a camera**—you ask the other person to \"take a picture\" of N and describe what it looks like. どの, by contrast, is like **an arrow** pointing to one specific item.","nuance":"It belongs to the same family as こんな・そんな・あんな; どんな is the interrogative member. Remember: \"See N? add どんな; choose N? use どの.\"","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-036||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"とても／あまり〜ない · N5","v2":{"pattern":"とても／あまり〜ない","patternReading":"とても/あまり〜ない","coreMeaning":"Very ~ / not ~ very much","slug":"core","definition":"Adverbs of degree. **とても** means *very* and comes before an **affirmative** predicate. In **あまり + negative**, **あまり** means *not... very much*, gently rating the degree as low.","nuance":"Both are used in everyday speech and informal writing. **あまり...ない** softens a negative statement, so it does not sound harsh or absolute. **とても** is stronger but still neutral, less casual than **すごく**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This cake is very delicious.","scene":"Praising a cake your friend made at their home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...very"},{"t":"高くない","r":"たかくない","m":"not expensive"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is not very expensive.","scene":"Looking at the price in a bookstore"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-036||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"とても／あまり〜ない · N5","v2":{"pattern":"とても／あまり〜ない","patternReading":"とても/あまり〜ない","coreMeaning":"Very ~ / not ~ very much","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern is used with **i-adjectives** and **na-adjectives**. For i-adjectives: 高い → 高くない. For na-adjectives: 静か → 静かじゃない. Add **です** for politeness. Past tense: **あまり高くなかったです / あまり静かじゃなかったです**.","nuance":"Never conjugate **とても / あまり**. After **あまり**, the predicate must be negative; after **とても**, it must be affirmative. The past of **あまり...ない** is **...くなかった / じゃなかった**.","structure":[{"lhs":"とても + Aい","rhs":"とてもおいしい","note":"affirmative"},{"lhs":"とても + NA","rhs":"とてもしずか","note":"affirmative, add です"},{"lhs":"あまり + Aくない","rhs":"あまり高くない","note":"negative i-adj"},{"lhs":"あまり + NAじゃない","rhs":"あまり元気じゃない","note":"negative na-adj"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"親切","r":"しんせつ","m":"kind"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The new teacher is very kind.","scene":"Meeting your teacher on the first day of Japanese class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...very"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"じゃない","m":"not"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This place is not very quiet.","scene":"Looking for a quiet café to study"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-036||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"とても／あまり〜ない · N5","v2":{"pattern":"とても／あまり〜ない","patternReading":"とても/あまり〜ない","coreMeaning":"Very ~ / not ~ very much","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to talk about the **degree** of a quality: very much, very expensive, not very cheap, not very busy. In conversation, **あまり...ない** is a polite way to give mild criticism or decline tactfully.","nuance":"If a friend asks whether a movie was good, saying **あまり面白くなかったです** sounds much more natural than **面白くなかったです** when it was not good. Japanese speakers often add **あまり** to soften negative judgments.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...much"},{"t":"飲みません","r":"のみません","m":"do not drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not drink much alcohol.","scene":"Declining beer at a company party"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"アパート","m":"apartment"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...very"},{"t":"きれい","m":"clean"},{"t":"じゃない","m":"not"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This apartment is not very clean.","scene":"Viewing an apartment and speaking quietly to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-036||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"とても／あまり〜ない · N5","v2":{"pattern":"とても／あまり〜ない","patternReading":"とても/あまり〜ない","coreMeaning":"Very ~ / not ~ very much","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish this pattern from easily confused degree adverbs and structures: **すごく**, **ぜんぜん...ない**, and **とても + negative**.","nuance":"At N5, remember: **あまり + negative = not...very much** (some degree remains), **ぜんぜん + negative = not at all** (0%), and **とても + affirmative = very**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...very"},{"t":"有名","r":"ゆうめい","m":"famous"},{"t":"じゃない","m":"not"},{"t":"けど","m":"but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"とても","m":"very"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is not very famous, but its food is delicious.","scene":"Introducing a small restaurant to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"ぜんぜん〜ない","point":"ぜんぜん...ない = not at all (0%); あまり...ない = not very much (about 20–30% remains). Example: あまりわかりません means 'I don't understand very well' and is milder than ぜんぜんわかりません, 'I understand nothing'."},{"vs":"すごく","point":"すごく is very strong and casual in speech; とても is more neutral and works in both speech and writing."},{"vs":"とても + 否定","point":"At N5, learn あまり + negative. とても + negative can mean 'cannot possibly...' in other structures; it is not the pattern for 'not...very much'."}]}},"n5-036||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"とても／あまり〜ない · N5","v2":{"pattern":"とても／あまり〜ない","patternReading":"とても/あまり〜ない","coreMeaning":"Very ~ / not ~ very much","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors include placing **あまり** before an affirmative predicate, using **とても** with a negative as if it meant 'very not,' and forgetting to change い → くない.","nuance":"These errors come from translating word for word. Check the sentence ending: **あまり** is allowed only when the predicate has **ない / ません**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"exercise"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...very"},{"t":"難しくない","r":"むずかしくない","m":"not difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This exercise is not very difficult.","scene":"Reassuring a classmate while doing exercises"}],"pitfalls":["❌ あまり便利です → ✅ あまり便利じゃないです (あまり must go with a negative).","❌ とても高くないです → ✅ あまり高くないです (do not use 'very not' at N5).","❌ あまり高いです → ✅ あまり高くないです (an i-adjective must change い to くない).","❌ 全然静かです → ✅ 全然静かじゃないです (at N5, 全然 must go with a negative)."],"compare":null}},"n5-036||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"とても／あまり〜ない · N5","v2":{"pattern":"とても／あまり〜ない","patternReading":"とても/あまり〜ない","coreMeaning":"Very ~ / not ~ very much","slug":"memory","definition":"Use kanji and an image to remember this pattern: **あまり** is written with **余**, whose basic meaning is *what is left over*, a small amount remaining. With a negative predicate, that small amount is negated, giving *not...very much*. Imagine **とても** as a heavy weight placed on a scale, making one side drop sharply to show a **very** high degree; **あまり** is like a feather that tilts the scale only slightly, showing a low or insignificant degree.","nuance":"Remember the grammar rule: **あまり** acts like a brake—when it appears at the start, the predicate must contain **ない / ません** to reduce the degree. Never use **あまり** alone with an affirmative sentence, or the meaning goes off track!","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-037||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern says that someone likes or dislikes something. The key point is that が marks the object of the emotion; を is not used. For example, 私は猫が好きです means “I like cats”: 私 is the subject, and 猫 is the object of liking.","nuance":"This pattern expresses an emotional state, not an action. 好き・嫌い are na-adjectives and do not conjugate like verbs. Use です for politeness and だ in casual speech. とても and あまり can show degree.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like cats.","scene":"Answering どんな動物が好きですか in class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"嫌い","r":"きらい","m":"dislike"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Children dislike vegetables.","scene":"A mother complains that her child will not eat vegetables."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-037||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern uses the na-adjectives **好き・嫌い**. The topic comes before は, and the object comes before が. Add じゃないです (common polite speech) or ではありません (formal speech) for negation, and でした for the past.","nuance":"In casual conversation, です may be omitted: 私は猫が好き. For a polite question, use 〜が好きですか. With 嫌い, 嫌いじゃないです means “do not dislike,” which does not necessarily mean “like.”","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 が 好きです／嫌いです","note":"Present affirmative, polite."},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 が 好きじゃないです／嫌いじゃないです","note":"Present negative."},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 が 好きでした／嫌いでした","note":"Past affirmative."},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 が 好きですか？","note":"Asking about preferences."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like tea.","scene":"Answering when offered a cup of tea."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"肉","r":"にく","m":"meat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好きじゃない","r":"すきじゃない","m":"do not like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not like meat.","scene":"Declining a meat dish."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-037||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears most often in everyday conversation when asking and answering about preferences. Use it for food, music, films, animals, and more. Remember to put が before 好き/嫌い.","nuance":"Ask “What kind do you like?” with どんな〜が好きですか?. To say “I do not like it much,” use あまり + a negative form: あまり好きじゃないです. Avoid あまり好きです because あまり requires negation here.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どんな","m":"what kind"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What kind of music do you like?","scene":"Finding common interests with someone new."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あまり","m":"not...much"},{"t":"好きじゃない","r":"すきじゃない","m":"do not like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not like coffee very much.","scene":"Politely declining coffee."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-037||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing を & 上手","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse “like something” with the object of an action verb and use を. However, **好き・嫌い are adjectives**, so **が** is required. Also distinguish 好き (like) from 上手 (be skilled), since both use the pattern “Nが” but have completely different meanings.","nuance":"With 食べます (an action verb), “eat sushi” uses を, while “like sushi” uses が. With 上手/下手, 私は歌が上手です describes ability, while 私は歌が好きです describes preference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"singing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like singing.","scene":"Talking about a preference when asked."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNを食べます","point":"食べる is an action verb → use を; 好き/嫌い are emotional adjectives → use が. 寿司を食べます (eat sushi) ≠ 寿司が好きです (like sushi)."},{"vs":"NはNが上手です","point":"The structure with が is the same, but 好き/嫌い express preference; 上手/下手 express ability. 歌が好きです = like singing, 歌が上手です = sing well."}]}},"n5-037||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The most common mistake is using を because English says “like + object.” Correct it immediately: **好き・嫌い always take が**. Also pay attention to noun order and degree markers.","nuance":"When using あまり, always add a negative form. The reversed order 好きです寿司 is also common when translating word for word. Do not conjugate 好き like an い-adjective: its negative is じゃない, not 好きくない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好きじゃない","r":"すきじゃない","m":"do not like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not like cats.","scene":"Replying when a friend invites you to a cat café."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は寿司を好きです。 → ✅ 私は寿司が好きです。","❌ 私は好きです寿司。 → ✅ 私は寿司が好きです。","❌ 私はあまりコーヒーが好きです。 → ✅ 私はあまりコーヒーが好きじゃないです。","❌ 私は猫が好きくないです。 → ✅ 私は猫が好きじゃないです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-037||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine your heart **pointing toward** an object and touching it with the が arrow. The mnemonic is: “Feeling — が, action — を.”","nuance":"Remember this group of words that uses the same structure: 好き, 嫌い, 上手, 下手, わかる, 欲しい. When one of these follows a noun, automatically think of が.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-037||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが好きです／嫌いです","patternReading":"エヌはエヌがすきですまたはきらいです","coreMeaning":"N likes/dislikes N — the object of emotion uses が","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in exams and everyday conversation. Memorizing each phrase as a whole helps you respond faster when listening or reading.","nuance":"大好き (だいすき) expresses a stronger preference than 好き. 一番好き means “like best” when comparing within a group. どんな〜が好きですか asks about general preferences.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寿司","r":"すし","m":"sushi"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"best"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like sushi best.","scene":"Telling a friend your favorite Japanese food."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-038||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **NはNが上手です／下手です** assesses a person’s (or organization’s) ability in a specific field. **N1** (before は) is the subject; **N2** (before が) is the skill, subject, or activity. **上手** (じょうず) means skilled or good at; **下手** (へた) means unskilled or bad at. Both are **na-adjectives**, so they are used with です.","nuance":"This is a **subjective** assessment based on general observation. For **other people**, 上手 is praise. For **yourself**, 上手 can sound **arrogant**, so use 得意 (とくい) or 好き instead. 下手 can be used for yourself, but calling someone else 下手 is **impolite**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka is good at cooking.","scene":"Discussing Mr. Tanaka’s cooking at a party"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"下手","r":"へた","m":"bad at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m bad at playing the piano.","scene":"Answering when asked if you play piano"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-038||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"forms","definition":"The basic structure is **N1 は N2 が 上手/下手 です**. The particle **が** must follow the skill; it cannot be replaced by を or は. Since **上手/下手 are na-adjectives**, their forms are: affirmative 上手です, negative **上手じゃありません**, past **上手でした**, and question **上手ですか**. With あまり, use **あまり上手じゃありません** (not very good at).","nuance":"In casual speech, you can say **上手だ** or omit です. A more formal form is **上手でございます**, but it is unnecessary at N5. とても (very) and あまり (not very) go directly before 上手/下手.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1は","rhs":"N2が上手です／下手です","note":"present affirmative"},{"lhs":"N1は","rhs":"N2が上手じゃありません／下手じゃありません","note":"present negative"},{"lhs":"N1は","rhs":"N2が上手でした／下手でした","note":"past"},{"lhs":"N1は","rhs":"N2が上手ですか／下手ですか","note":"question"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"歌","r":"うた","m":"singing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"じゃありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He isn’t good at singing.","scene":"Commenting on a friend’s singing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子供","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"下手","r":"へた","m":"bad at"},{"t":"でした"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was a child, I was bad at piano.","scene":"Talking about childhood"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-038||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **praise** someone (〜さんは〜が上手ですね), **ask about ability** (〜が上手ですか), or **modestly describe your own weakness** (〜が下手です). It can also describe teams or companies: “この会社は営業が上手です”.","nuance":"When talking about yourself, Japanese speakers avoid 上手 and use **得意** (confident at) or **好き** (like). When praising someone, add **ね** at the end for a warmer, more expressive compliment. For ability questions, **上手ですか** often sounds more natural than 得意ですか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"鈴木","r":"すずき","m":"Mr. Suzuki"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"ね"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Suzuki is good at Japanese, isn’t he?","scene":"Praising a foreign colleague’s Japanese"},{"tokens":[{"t":"マイク","m":"Mike"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"中国語","r":"ちゅうごくご","m":"Chinese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is Mike good at Chinese?","scene":"Asking a friend about Mike’s Chinese"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-038||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare similar patterns","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes **NはNが上手です／下手です** from similar patterns using the easily confused frame “NはNが～.” The key difference is: **上手/下手 = skill level**, **好き/嫌い = feelings**, and **わかります = understanding/recognition**.","nuance":"Use the context: praise a skill with 上手, express a preference with 好き, and confirm knowledge or understanding with わかります.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like cooking.","scene":"When someone asks about your interests"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNが好きです (thích/ghét)","point":"好き expresses liking, not skill level. Someone who likes cooking may not cook well: 料理が好きです ≠ 料理が上手です."},{"vs":"NはNがわかります (hiểu/biết)","point":"わかります expresses understanding or knowledge, not skill: 日本語がわかります (understand Japanese) does not mean the person speaks it well; 日本語が上手です means they speak/write it well."},{"vs":"NはNです (là N)","point":"NはNです states identity or a characteristic and has no が marking an object. 彼は料理です (meaningless) vs 彼は料理が上手です (he is good at cooking)."}]}},"n5-038||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common mistakes **learners** make with this pattern. The most important points are that **が** is required, and を cannot be used. Also remember the Japanese preference for **modesty** when discussing your own abilities.","nuance":"When saying you are good at something, Japanese speakers often use **得意** to avoid sounding boastful. When saying someone else is not good at something, soften it with **あまり上手じゃない** (not very good).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"得意","r":"とくい","m":"good at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m confident in my cooking.","scene":"Modestly responding to praise for your cooking"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は料理を上手です。 → ✅ 私は料理が上手です。 (the skill assessed by 上手 takes が, not を)","❌ 私は日本語が上手です。 (sounds boastful when talking about yourself) → ✅ 私は日本語が得意です。 or 好きです。","❌ 彼はピアノが下手だ。 (blunt and impolite about someone else) → ✅ 彼はピアノが少し苦手です。 (softened wording)","❌ ピアノを弾くが上手です。 → ✅ ピアノを弾くのが上手です。 (add の to nominalize a verb)"],"compare":null}},"n5-038||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tips: **上手** contains **上** (up)—a hand held high suggests skill. **下手** contains **下** (down)—a hand held low suggests clumsiness. Remember: **「は」 marks the person, 「が」 marks the skill**.","nuance":"This memory aid helps prevent particle confusion: は follows the subject, and が follows the assessed activity. Picture it as: he (は) is good at [skill] (が).","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-038||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNが上手です／下手です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが上手です／下手です","patternReading":"じょうずです／へたです","coreMeaning":"N is good/bad at N (ability assessment)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases from textbooks and daily life using this pattern pair it with skill nouns such as 料理, 日本語, スポーツ, ピアノ, 歌, 絵, ダンス, and 水泳.","nuance":"Japanese speakers often use **〜が上手** to praise someone: 料理が上手ですね, 日本語が上手ですね. It also works with specific activities such as swimming, drawing, and dancing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スポーツ","m":"sports"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good at"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Are you good at sports?","scene":"Asking a new friend about sports ability"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-039||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜がわかります expresses understanding or grasping content, a language, or a meaning. Key point: わかります is a cognitive/perceptual verb, so its object must take **が**, not を. あります also uses が when expressing ability or resources: 時間があります、お金があります.","nuance":"This is neutral and works with friends or elders when used in the polite form. Compared with 知っています, わかります emphasizes **understanding content**, not simply having information. In conversation, 私は is often omitted when the topic is clear.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"わかります","r":"わかります","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I understand a little Japanese.","scene":"Answering about Japanese ability"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"question"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意味","r":"いみ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't understand the meaning of this question.","scene":"Doing an exercise but unsure of the prompt"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-039||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"forms","definition":"The general structure is **topic は + object が + わかります**. Since わかります is a perceptual state verb, its object takes が. Negative, past, and question forms use polite conjugation. あります also belongs to the が pattern: 時間があります、お金が少しあります.","nuance":"は marks the person or thing being discussed, while が marks what is understood. If the topic is obvious—usually “I”—N1 は can be omitted. わかります is a group 1 verb, and its ます form is わかります.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 が わかります","note":"N1 is the person or thing able to understand; N2 is what is understood"},{"lhs":"N2 が","rhs":"わかりません","note":"Present negative: do not understand"},{"lhs":"N2 が","rhs":"わかりました","note":"Past: understood"},{"lhs":"N2 が","rhs":"わかりませんでした","note":"Past negative: did not understand"},{"lhs":"N2 が","rhs":"よく わかりません","note":"Degree negative: do not understand N2 clearly"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"話","r":"はなし","m":"words"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかります","r":"わかります","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I understand what the teacher said.","scene":"Teacher explaining in class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't understand English.","scene":"Listening to a foreigner speak English"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-039||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to ask and answer about understanding a language, speech, lesson content, or a word's meaning. It is extremely common in classrooms and everyday conversation when checking comprehension.","nuance":"Add degree adverbs for natural phrasing: よく (clearly), だいたい (roughly), 少し (a little), and 全然〜ない (not at all). When they do not understand, Japanese speakers often say すみません、...がわかりません to ask for repetition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかります","r":"わかります","m":"understand"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you understand the teacher's Japanese?","scene":"Asking a classmate after class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","r":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"word"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意味","r":"いみ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, I don't understand the meaning of this word.","scene":"Hearing a new word in a lesson"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-039||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from 知っています","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes わかります from 知っています, two verbs learners often confuse. Key point: **わかります** describes **understanding content, meaning, or speech**; **知っています** describes **having information or knowledge** about a person, thing, or event. Their particles also differ: わかります takes が, while 知っています takes を.","nuance":"For example, 日本語がわかります means understanding Japanese through listening or reading; 彼の名前を知っています means knowing his name as information. Do not use を with わかります or が with 知っています at N5 level.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"言葉","r":"ことば","m":"word"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"意味","r":"いみ","m":"meaning"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかります","r":"わかります","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I understand the meaning of this word.","scene":"Studying vocabulary"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"知っています","point":"わかります = understand content (e.g. 意味がわかります); 知っています = know information (e.g. 名前を知っています)."},{"vs":"Nをわかります","point":"Incorrect: わかります is a perceptual intransitive verb, so its object takes が. を is used with transitive verbs such as 知っています."}]}},"n5-039||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"English learners may use を because English treats “what you understand” like a direct object. In Japanese, **わかります** is a perceptual verb, so its object takes **が**. Learners also often confuse わかります with 知っています in negative forms and particle choice.","nuance":"Remember: the negative of わかります is わかりません (do not understand), while the negative of 知っています is 知りません (do not know). Do not mix them up.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"わかります","r":"わかります","m":"understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I understand Japanese.","scene":"Stating language ability"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本語をわかります。 → ✅ 日本語がわかります。","❌ この言葉にわかります。 → ✅ この言葉がわかります。","❌ 日本語をわかりました。 → ✅ 日本語が Normandy? わかりました。"],"compare":null}},"n5-039||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **わかります** belongs to a group of perceptual or state verbs, along with あります and できます. Their objects are marked with **が**. Imagine が as an arrow pointing to **what you understand or grasp**.","nuance":"When you see わかります, think of 'が'. を is used for direct actions on an object, such as eating, drinking, or reading.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-039||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNがわかります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNがわかります","patternReading":"わかります","coreMeaning":"Understand/know N (cognitive ability)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases for class and daily life: **日本語がわかります** (understand Japanese), **意味がわかります** (understand the meaning), and **すみません、わかりません** (sorry, I don't understand).","nuance":"To ask someone to repeat, Japanese speakers often say 'すみません、もう一度お願いします'. Before that, they may add 'すみません、...がわかりません'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","r":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"clearly"},{"t":"わかりません","r":"わかりません","m":"do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, I don't understand Japanese well.","scene":"Explaining limited ability to a Japanese speaker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-040||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"core","definition":"〜から follows a **reason clause**, followed by a **result clause**: Because ~, so ~. The speaker uses it to **explain the cause** of an action, state, or decision. In English, we often put “because” first, but in Japanese the **reason always comes before から**.","nuance":"This is a **natural, neutral** explanation used in both everyday speech and writing. When answering どうしてですか, people often need only the reason clause + から. Politeness depends on the clause before から: です/ます is polite, while the plain form is casual.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降って","r":"ふって","m":"falling"},{"t":"います"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"take"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is raining, so I am taking an umbrella.","scene":"It suddenly starts raining; remind your roommate to take an umbrella"},{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行きません","r":"いきません","m":"will not go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am busy, so I am not going.","scene":"A friend invites you out, but you have lots to do"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-040||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connecting forms","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"forms","definition":"Before から, use the **polite form** (Vます/です) in polite speech, or the **plain form** in casual conversation. The clause after から may be a statement, request, or invitation. から is a connecting particle and does not conjugate itself.","nuance":"With older people or customers, use 〜から with です/ます endings for politeness. With friends, から can connect to the plain form.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます / Vません + から","rhs":"Because I (do not) do V, ..."},{"lhs":"Aいです + から","rhs":"Because it is adjective, ..."},{"lhs":"NAです / Nです + から","rhs":"Because it is NA / N, ..."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"do not have"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"タクシー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not have time, so I am taking a taxi.","scene":"You are late for a meeting and take a taxi instead of walking"},{"tokens":[{"t":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is cheap, so I will buy it.","scene":"You see an item on sale at the supermarket"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-040||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch covers **three common real-life uses**: answering どうして, explaining a reason before making a decision, and giving a reason when inviting or suggesting something. In conversation, the clause after から may be omitted when the context is clear.","nuance":"〜から is often connected to the speaker’s **subjective reason**. So when making a request or invitation, adding から makes the sentence **softer and more persuasive**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"アニメ","m":"anime"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きたい","r":"いきたい","m":"want to go"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like anime, so I want to go to Japan.","scene":"Explaining your dream to friends"},{"tokens":[{"t":"おなか","m":"stomach"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"すきました","m":"became hungry"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何か","r":"なにか","m":"something"},{"t":"食べましょう","r":"たべましょう","m":"let us eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are hungry, so let us eat something.","scene":"It is late after class, so you invite a friend to eat"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-040||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Reason vs. starting point","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish reason から from other uses of から—especially starting-point から and NからNまで. If から is followed by a **result clause**, it expresses a reason; if it follows a **place or time noun**, it marks a starting point.","nuance":"In terms of politeness, ので sounds softer and more objective, but at N5, remember that から is safe and common in conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"散歩","r":"さんぽ","m":"walk"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The weather is nice, so I am going for a walk.","scene":"It is sunny, so you decide to go outside"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"から (điểm xuất phát)","point":"から follows a place or time noun: 学校から来ました (I came from school). Reason から connects two clauses: 学校ですから、来ました (I came because it is a school)."},{"vs":"NからNまで (từ N đến N)","point":"This phrase marks spatial or time limits and has no reason meaning: 駅から学校まで歩きます (I walk from the station to school)."},{"vs":"ので (vì, trang trọng)","point":"ので is more polite and objective and is often used in writing; から sounds more subjective and natural in conversation."}]}},"n5-040||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A summary of common mistakes learners make with reason から: putting it in the wrong position, forgetting it in an answer, and confusing it with the から that marks a place.","nuance":"These mistakes often come from translating “because...” directly into Japanese. Always check: **does から come immediately after the reason clause?**","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ふります","m":"falls"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけません","r":"でかけません","m":"will not go out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It is raining, so I am not going out.","scene":"It is raining heavily, so you decide to stay home"}],"pitfalls":["❌ から雨がふります、行きません。 → ✅ 雨がふりますから、行きません。 (から must come right after the reason, not at the beginning)","❌ どうしてですか。忙しいです。 → ✅ どうしてですか。忙しいですから。 (a reason question requires から / からです)","❌ 学校から、行きませんでした。 (intended: “because it is a school”) → ✅ 学校ですから、行きませんでした。 (a noun used as a reason needs Nですから/Nだから)"],"compare":null}},"n5-040||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: draw an arrow from **REASON → RESULT**, with から at the arrowhead. The reason always comes before the arrow.","nuance":"から sounds a little like “ra.” Remember: the reason “ra” leads into the result. Mnemonic: “REASON FIRST, result AFTER.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-040||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜から (lý do) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜から (lý do)","patternReading":"から","coreMeaning":"Because ~, so ~ (explaining reason and result)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases include 〜からです for short answers; the question-and-answer pair どうして...？ → 〜から; and 〜から、〜てください for a request with a reason.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often omit the result clause when it is clear: “どうして? — 時間がないから.” (Because I do not have time.)","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"do not have"},{"t":"から","m":"because"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"すぐ","m":"right away"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not have time, so please come right away.","scene":"Calling a friend and urging them to come quickly"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-041||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"core","definition":"どうしてVますか asks “why” in the standard polite form (ます-form). The speaker already knows that something happened or will happen, but does not know the reason and wants the listener to explain it. This is a basic everyday pattern.","nuance":"This question is not judgmental; it simply asks for the reason behind an intentional action. Compared with なぜ (formal, written), どうして feels familiar and natural in conversation. With friends, you can simply say どうして？.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why do you study Japanese?","scene":"A teacher asks a new student."},{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"新聞","r":"しんぶん","m":"newspaper"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読みます","r":"よみます","m":"read"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why do you read the newspaper every day?","scene":"Your roommate sees you reading early."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-041||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"forms","definition":"The reason-question structure starts with どうして (why), followed by a verb in the ます-form, and ends with か. Change the verb for tense, past, or negation: ました (did), ません (do not), ませんでした (did not). Standard answers use ～から or ～からです.","nuance":"In casual speech, you can drop か and raise your intonation: どうして行く？ (Why are you going?) – but at N5, keep か for politeness. In an answer, から can come at the end: 日本が好きですから (because I like Japan).","structure":[{"lhs":"どうして","rhs":"Vます + か","note":"polite present/future reason question"},{"lhs":"どうして","rhs":"Vました + か","note":"past reason question"},{"lhs":"どうして","rhs":"Vません + か","note":"ask why someone does not do it"},{"lhs":"どうして","rhs":"～んですか","note":"more polite, questioning nuance (N4)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why do you study at the library?","scene":"Curious about a friend who often goes there."},{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"来ませんでした","r":"きませんでした","m":"did not come"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why didn't you come to school yesterday?","scene":"A teacher asks an absent student."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-041||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern in three situations: (1) ask why an action is happening or will happen; (2) ask why something is not being done, using the negative; (3) follow up when you hear surprising news. Answers use ～から at the end to state the reason.","nuance":"～んですか adds a more curious, questioning tone, but at N5 you can use どうしてですか. With a superior, どうして + polite form + か is the minimum polite form; add すみません、 before the question if needed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why do you study Japanese? – Because I like Japan.","scene":"A conversation between two students."},{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"肉","r":"にく","m":"meat"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べません","r":"たべません","m":"do not eat"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"ベジタリアン","r":"べじたりあん","m":"vegetarian"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why don't you eat meat? – Because I'm a vegetarian.","scene":"Coworkers chat in the company cafeteria."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-041||compare":{"title":"⚖️ どうして vs. なぜ / なんで","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"compare","definition":"All mean “why,” but their formality differs. どうして is neutral and works in everyday conversation and simple writing. なぜ is formal and common in written text and news. なんで is casual and sounds natural with friends.","nuance":"A: どうして日本へ行きますか。 (natural). B: なぜ日本へ行きますか。 (somewhat stiff, polite). C: なんで日本へ行く？ (close friend). In polite settings, なぜ + the ます-form is safe.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"なぜ","r":"なぜ","m":"why"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why are you going to Japan? (formal)","scene":"A scholarship interview."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"なぜ","point":"Formal and written; どうして is neutral and conversational."},{"vs":"なんで","point":"Casual and used with friends; どうして is more polite."},{"vs":"どうしたんですか","point":"Asks “what happened?” about a state, not why an action occurred."}]}},"n5-041||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include omitting か in a polite question or placing どうして incorrectly. Learners also put から before the reason, but in Japanese, から belongs in the answer clause, never in the question.","nuance":"A polite question needs か: どうして行きますか must not become どうして行きます. In an answer, 「～からです」 is standard. Do not say 「から、どうして？」.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why do you study Japanese?","scene":"An ordinary question."}],"pitfalls":["❌ どうして来ませんでした。 → ✅ どうして来ませんでしたか。","❌ から日本が好きです。 → ✅ 日本が好きですから。 (in an answer)","❌ 勉強しますか、どうして？ → ✅ どうして勉強しますか。 (どうして does not go at the end)"],"compare":null}},"n5-041||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember the meaning by splitting the word: どうして = どう (how) + して (do) → “how did it come about?” → “why.” When asking, remember Vます + か; when answering, attach から (because) at the end.","nuance":"Make an association: どうして sounds like a question about the reason; answer with “because...”. Songs and films use どうして often to express curiosity or emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-041||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"どうしてVますか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"どうしてVますか","patternReading":"どうして ます か","coreMeaning":"Asking the reason: Why ~?","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common reason-question phrases in textbooks and daily life include どうしてですか (follow-up), どうしてそう思いますか (asking for an opinion), and どうして来なかったんですか (んですか adds a questioning nuance). At N5, learning どうしてですか is enough for practical use.","nuance":"When someone has just shared surprising information, どうしてですか? is a natural reaction and more polite than どうして without か.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"どうして","r":"どうして","m":"why"},{"t":"です","r":"です","m":"is"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Why is that?","scene":"You say, “I’m skipping school tomorrow” – they ask why."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-042||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NにNがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNがあります","patternReading":"にあります","coreMeaning":"An inanimate thing or event exists in a place.","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern announces the **existence** of an inanimate thing or an abstract event in a place. **が** marks what exists, and **に** marks the location. Do not use it for people or animals.","nuance":"Neutral in tone and usable in both speech and writing. It simply reports \"something exists somewhere\"; it does not express an action or emotion. When the thing is already known and you want to ask where it is, use **NはNにあります**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"机","r":"つくえ","m":"desk"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ","m":"top"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a book on the desk.","scene":"Seeing a book on a desk in class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"近く","r":"ちかく","m":"near"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a convenience store near the station.","scene":"Giving directions to a visitor asking about the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-042||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NにNがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNがあります","patternReading":"にあります","coreMeaning":"An inanimate thing or event exists in a place.","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: **location + に + thing + が + あります**. Change the tense and polarity: affirmative present **あります**, negative present **ありません**, affirmative past **ありました**, and negative past **ありませんでした**. Add **か** at the end for a question.","nuance":"This is the polite form commonly used in conversation. When introducing a thing for the first time, use **が**; if the thing is known and you want to ask where it is, change **が** to **は**: 本は机の上にあります。","structure":[{"lhs":"N（nơi） に N（vật） が","rhs":"あります","note":"There is … at …"},{"lhs":"N（nơi） に N（vật） が","rhs":"ありません","note":"There is no … at …"},{"lhs":"N（nơi） に N（vật） が","rhs":"ありました","note":"There was … at …"},{"lhs":"N（nơi） に N（vật） が","rhs":"ありませんでした","note":"There was no … at …"},{"lhs":"N（nơi） に N（vật） が","rhs":"ありますか","note":"Is there … at …?"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"冷蔵庫","r":"れいぞうこ","m":"refrigerator"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ビール","m":"beer"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is beer in the refrigerator.","scene":"Opening the refrigerator to check the drinks"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no TV in the room.","scene":"Looking for a TV in an empty room"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-042||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NにNがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNがあります","patternReading":"にあります","coreMeaning":"An inanimate thing or event exists in a place.","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern commonly describes **where objects are**, **events on a schedule**, and **having time or an abstract thing**. In conversation, a common question is **どこにありますか** (where is ...?).","nuance":"For events such as 会議・パーティー, the sentence means \"an event is scheduled\". With time such as 時間, it means \"have enough time\". The polite tone works naturally in both work and everyday settings.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"front"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"交番","r":"こうばん","m":"police box"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a police box in front of the station.","scene":"A passerby asks where the police box is"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a meeting tomorrow.","scene":"A coworker reminds you about the meeting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"there is no"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time.","scene":"Politely declining an extra task"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-042||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Easily confused patterns","sub":"NにNがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNがあります","patternReading":"にあります","coreMeaning":"An inanimate thing or event exists in a place.","slug":"compare","definition":"**あります** and **います** share the structure \"NがNに…\" but differ in subject: use あります for **inanimate things** and います for **people and animals**. **です** only confirms that \"N is N\"; it does not express existence.","nuance":"This is one of the most common N5 errors: 猫があります is wrong; use 猫がいます. To say \"there is a book here,\" do not use ここは本です, which sounds like \"this is a book\"; ここに本があります is natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a book here.","scene":"Pointing to a stack of books nearby"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NにNがいます","point":"Use います for people and animals; use あります for inanimate things. 猫がいます is correct, while 猫があります is wrong."},{"vs":"NはNです","point":"です identifies something; it does not express location. To say \"there is something somewhere,\" use ここに本があります, not ここは本です."}]}},"n5-042||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"NにNがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNがあります","patternReading":"にあります","coreMeaning":"An inanimate thing or event exists in a place.","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse **あります** with **います**, misuse the particle **に**, or omit **が**. Remember: **places always take に**, and **the existing subject is marked with が**.","nuance":"The particle **で** marks a place where an action occurs; it is not used for existence. The particle **を** marks an object and never goes with あります in an existence sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"自転車","r":"じてんしゃ","m":"bicycle"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a bicycle here.","scene":"Showing a guest where to park"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 部屋に猫があります。 → ✅ 部屋に猫がいます。","❌ 机の上に本をあります。 → ✅ 机の上に本があります。","❌ ここで本があります。 → ✅ ここに本があります。"],"compare":null}},"n5-042||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NにNがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NにNがあります","patternReading":"にあります","coreMeaning":"An inanimate thing or event exists in a place.","slug":"memory","definition":"Break **あり + ます** apart: あり means \"exist/have.\" Memory tip: **ari** sounds like \"ah-ree\"—objects do not move, so they use あります. Living things such as people, dogs, and cats can move, so they use います.","nuance":"This is a memory aid, not a grammar rule. When unsure, ask yourself: does the subject have **life**?","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-043||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **NがNにいます** states that a person or living creature is present in a specific place. The subject takes **が**, and the location takes **に**: 'someone/something is somewhere.' Most importantly, **います** is used only for living things.","nuance":"This is a neutral expression used in most everyday situations, from checking where someone is to workplace conversations. Unlike **です**, which defines what something 'is,' います focuses on presence at a specific location.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"台所","r":"だいどころ","m":"kitchen"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Father is in the kitchen.","scene":"Mom asks where Father is; you see him cooking in the kitchen"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あそこ","m":"over there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a cat over there.","scene":"You point to a cat lying in the garden"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-043||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"forms","definition":"The verb **います** conjugates like other ます-ending verbs: present **います**, negative **いません**, past **いました**, and past negative **いませんでした**. The particles **に** and **が** do not change.","nuance":"The order 'location + に + subject + が' highlights the location. Reversing it to 'subject + が + location + に' is also natural, especially when answering 'Who is where?'","structure":[{"lhs":"N(場所)","rhs":"に N(人/生き物) が います","note":"most common order"},{"lhs":"N(人/生き物)","rhs":"が N(場所) に います","note":"emphasizes the subject"},{"lhs":"います","rhs":"いません / いました / いませんでした","note":"tense + negative forms"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a teacher in the classroom.","scene":"You look into the classroom and see the teacher standing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is there a student at the station?","scene":"Asking whether your friend has seen a student waiting there"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-043||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"usage","definition":"In conversation, this pattern is commonly used to: (1) ask whether someone is present, (2) report where you or someone else is, and (3) describe how many people or animals are in a place. For people, the counter **人** usually comes before います: 学生が20人います.","nuance":"When asking 'どこにいますか', place に immediately after the question word. Short answers such as **ここ/そこ/あそこ + に** are very natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"さっき","m":"earlier"},{"t":"まで","m":"until"},{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"いました","r":"いました","m":"was"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I was still here a moment ago.","scene":"A coworker was looking for you, so you say you were here earlier"},{"tokens":[{"t":"会議室","r":"かいぎしつ","m":"meeting room"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka is in the meeting room.","scene":"The manager asks where Tanaka is; you saw him enter the meeting room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"京都","r":"きょうと","m":"Kyoto"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My family is in Kyoto.","scene":"Someone asks where your family is"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-043||compare":{"title":"⚖️ います／あります compared","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"compare","definition":"The two patterns most often confused are **います** and **あります**. Both can mean 'there is/are' or 'exists,' and both use the same structure: location + に + subject + が. The only difference is the subject: living things use います, while inanimate things use あります.","nuance":"Trees, flowers, hair, and similar things are treated as inanimate in Japanese grammar, so use あります.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"そこ","m":"there"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"is, exists (object)"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a teacher here; there is a book there.","scene":"Pointing out people and objects in a room"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NがNにあります","point":"Use あります when the subject is inanimate. Compare: 庭に犬がいます (living creature) / 庭にテーブルがあります (object)."},{"vs":"NはNです","point":"です defines identity or nature; it does not show location. Compare: 私は学生です (I am a student) / 私は教室にいます (I am in the classroom)."}]}},"n5-043||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often choose the wrong verb because both います and あります can translate as 'there is.' Use います for people and animals, and あります for objects. The location takes **に**, and the subject takes **が**.","nuance":"A common confusion comes from translating both verbs as 'there is.' For example, \"本があります\" is correct, but \"犬がありません\" is wrong because a dog is living. Learners also confuse the location particle \"に\" with \"で\", omit \"が\", or use \"を\" as if the subject were an object. For location questions, use \"どこにいますか\", not \"どこにですか\".","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"am, exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am at home.","scene":"Answering the phone when asked where you are"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 机の上に本がいます。 → ✅ 机の上に本があります。","❌ 私は会社にあります。 → ✅ 私は会社にいます。","❌ 猫を庭にいます。 → ✅ 猫が庭にいます。","❌ どこに田中さんですか。 → ✅ どこに田中さんがいますか。"],"compare":null}},"n5-043||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"memory","definition":"The easiest memory tip: **い** in います is related to **生きる (いきる = to live)**. So **living things** go with **います**. Lifeless objects use **あります**.","nuance":"Use this only as a beginner memory aid; at higher levels, あります can also express the existence of events such as plans. This does not affect N5 usage.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-043||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"NがNにいます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにいます","patternReading":"がにいます","coreMeaning":"A person or living creature exists somewhere (is present)","slug":"phrases","definition":"For JLPT N5 and everyday life, you will often see these phrases: **今、どこにいますか**, **会社にいます**, **庭に犬がいます**, **学生が20人います**. Learn them as chunks rather than analyzing every particle.","nuance":"These phrases usually use the present tense. To ask 'where were you earlier?', change the pattern to **どこにいましたか**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"どこ","m":"where"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"is, exists"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Where are you now?","scene":"Asking a friend by phone"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-044||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"core","definition":"や lists some nouns as typical examples, implying that other items are not mentioned. “There are books and magazines on the desk” sounds natural because listeners understand there may be other things too. Adding など after the final noun makes “etc.” explicit.","nuance":"Used in both everyday speech and writing. Unlike と, which gives a complete list, や creates an open-ended list and does not require exactness. This helps speech sound naturally Japanese.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"机","r":"つくえ","m":"desk"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ","m":"on top"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"雑誌","r":"ざっし","m":"magazine"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are books, magazines, and other things on the desk.","scene":"Mentioning a few typical items on a desk"},{"tokens":[{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"果物","r":"くだもの","m":"fruit"},{"t":"など","r":"など","m":"etc."},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought vegetables, fruit, and so on at the supermarket.","scene":"Describing a shopping trip without listing everything"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-044||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"forms","definition":"This pattern connects nouns only: two or more nouns. The resulting noun phrase functions like an ordinary noun, followed by が, を, に, で, and so on. など may be added after the final noun to emphasize “etc.”","nuance":"There is no conjugation here. Tense and negation belong to the final verb: あります／ありません／ありました... If the second noun is followed by a particle, place the particle after the whole phrase (NやN + を/で...).","structure":[{"lhs":"Danh từ 1","rhs":"や + Danh từ 2","note":"Lists two typical examples; a second noun is required after や."},{"lhs":"Danh từ 1","rhs":"や + Danh từ 2 + など","note":"など emphasizes “etc.”; a particle may still follow."},{"lhs":"N1 + や + N2（+ など）","rhs":"＋ が/を/に/で ＋ V","note":"The whole noun phrase can be a subject, object, or adverbial phrase."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"ノート","m":"notebook"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are books, notebooks, and other things in the bag.","scene":"Finding several items in a bag and naming only the main ones"},{"tokens":[{"t":"箱","r":"はこ","m":"box"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"など","r":"など","m":"etc."},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are letters, photos, and so on in the box.","scene":"Listing a few things found while clearing storage"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-044||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use や to list a few typical examples from a larger group. It often appears in answers to questions such as “What is there?”, “What do you do?”, or “What did you buy?” when there is no need to mention everything. The listener understands that similar items or activities remain.","nuance":"The tone is natural and familiar. In 映画や買い物をします, the listener understands that other weekend activities are also possible. Adding など makes “and so on” more explicit, especially with three or more items. Do not use it for a complete report.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movies"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On weekends, I watch movies, go shopping, and do other things.","scene":"Friends ask about weekend plans; name a few main activities"},{"tokens":[{"t":"京都","r":"きょうと","m":"Kyoto"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"お寺","r":"おてら","m":"temples"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"庭","r":"にわ","m":"gardens"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ました","r":"みました","m":"visited"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In Kyoto, I visited temples, gardens, and other places.","scene":"Describing a trip by naming a few destinations"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-044||compare":{"title":"⚖️ と・や・か compared","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"compare","definition":"These three particles can appear between nouns but convey different meanings. と means “this is everything,” や means “these are a few typical examples,” and か means “choose one of the options.” Confusing them changes the intended meaning.\n\nCompare: コーヒーとお茶をください → Give me both coffee and tea. コーヒーやお茶をください → Give me coffee, tea, and possibly something else. コーヒーかお茶をください → Give me one of the two.","nuance":"や sounds open and relaxed; と closes the list; か presents a choice. In formal writing, complete lists commonly use と or commas plus と. In conversation, や is natural when exact completeness is not important.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mt. Fuji"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"京都","r":"きょうと","m":"Kyoto"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In Japan, I visited Mt. Fuji, Kyoto, and other places.","scene":"Describing a Japan trip with two typical destinations"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NとN","point":"と gives a complete list: 机の上に本と雑誌があります → only books and magazines; や would imply other items."},{"vs":"NかN","point":"か means choose one: コーヒーかお茶をください → one of the two; や gives examples and does not force a single choice."}]}},"n5-044||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common learner mistakes","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse と and や or place や incorrectly. Golden rule: や connects nouns only and does not present a complete list. Check whether a second noun follows や and whether the list is complete.","nuance":"These errors may sound odd but are usually understandable. Using the correct particle from the start makes your Japanese much more natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"holiday"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountains"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"海","r":"うみ","m":"sea"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"During my vacation, I went to the mountains, the beach, and other places.","scene":"Describing a trip without listing every destination"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 机の上に本と雑誌があります。（when other items remain） → ✅ 机の上に本や雑誌があります。","❌ 果物はりんごやバナナやみかんやブドウがあります。（complete list; や is excessive） → ✅ 果物はりんごやバナナなどがあります。Or use と if the list is truly complete.","❌ 机の上に本やあります。（a second noun must follow や） → ✅ 机の上に本やノートがあります。","❌ 朝、パンを食べや牛乳を飲みます。（や does not join verbs） → ✅ 朝、パンを食べたり牛乳を飲んだりします。"],"compare":null}},"n5-044||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"memory","definition":"A simple memory aid: や sounds a little like “some,” so it names only some items. と sounds short and closed, so it gives the whole list. など means “etc.”, and か presents a choice of one.","nuance":"Remember the functions this way: “some” means not all, “the whole set” means complete, “etc.” leaves the list open, and “choose” means select one.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-044||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"NやN（など） · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NやN（など）","patternReading":"や など","coreMeaning":"Partial listing: N and N (implying more items)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in Minna no Nihongo textbooks, workplaces, and shopping situations. Memorize them for immediate use in conversation and N5 reading practice.","nuance":"All are casual to moderately polite. お茶やコーヒーは？ is a natural open-ended offer: “How about tea, coffee, or another drink?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"パン","m":"bread"},{"t":"や","r":"や","m":"and (open list)"},{"t":"牛乳","r":"ぎゅうにゅう","m":"milk"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I’m buying bread, milk, and other things.","scene":"Telling family you are buying groceries"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-045||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯","patternReading":"こ まい ほん かい はい","coreMeaning":"N5 counters: round (個), flat (枚), long (本), times (回), cups (杯)","slug":"core","definition":"A counter (助数詞) follows a number to show what kind of thing is counted: 個 for small or round items, 枚 for thin flat items, 本 for long cylindrical items, 回 for occurrences, and 杯 for cups or bowls. Change the counter and the image changes.","nuance":"Counters are essential when stating quantities; omitting one sounds unnatural. They work in all settings, from friends to shops and offices. The tone is neutral.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"りんご","m":"apple"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"3個","r":"さんこ","m":"3 apples"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please give me three apples.","scene":"Choosing apples at a grocery store."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ビール","m":"beer"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"1杯","r":"いっぱい","m":"1 glass"},{"t":"飲みました","r":"のみました","m":"drank"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drank one glass of beer.","scene":"Talking about drinking with friends."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-045||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and patterns","sub":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯","patternReading":"こ まい ほん かい はい","coreMeaning":"N5 counters: round (個), flat (枚), long (本), times (回), cups (杯)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic pattern: **N + を + Number + Counter + V**. To modify a noun, use **Number + Counter + の + N**. Each counter has its own reading; 本 and 杯 often change sound with 1, 3, 6, 8, and 10.","nuance":"In conversation, を before ください is often omitted: りんご3個ください. For frequency, 週に2回 means twice a week; 回 follows the time period. Learn these sound changes as vocabulary.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + を + Number + Counter + V","rhs":"counted object as action target","note":"Example: りんごを3個ください。 を may be omitted before ください in speech."},{"lhs":"Number + Counter + の + N","rhs":"modify a noun","note":"Example: 3個のりんご = three apples."},{"lhs":"Time + に + Number + 回 + V","rhs":"frequency","note":"Example: 週に3回映画を見ます = I watch movies three times a week."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"紙","r":"かみ","m":"paper"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"2枚","r":"にまい","m":"2 sheets"},{"t":"使いました","r":"つかいました","m":"used"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I used two sheets of paper.","scene":"Discussing photocopies used in a meeting."},{"tokens":[{"t":"先週","r":"せんしゅう","m":"last week"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"3回","r":"さんかい","m":"3 times"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movies"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ました","r":"みました","m":"watched"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I watched movies three times last week.","scene":"Talking about the weekend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-045||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯","patternReading":"こ まい ほん かい はい","coreMeaning":"N5 counters: round (個), flat (枚), long (本), times (回), cups (杯)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use these when shopping, ordering food, or talking about repeated events. Common pattern: **Nを + Number + Counter + ください** (please give me ~). For frequency: **time period + に + Number + 回 + V**.","nuance":"個/枚/本/杯 count concrete things; 回 counts repeated events. Add ください for a polite request; casually, you can say コーヒー1杯. These are natural in shops and restaurants.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"1杯","r":"いっぱい","m":"1 cup"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, please give me one coffee.","scene":"Ordering at a café."},{"tokens":[{"t":"週","r":"しゅう","m":"week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"2回","r":"にかい","m":"twice"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to the supermarket twice a week.","scene":"Talking about a shopping habit."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-045||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns contrasted","sub":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯","patternReading":"こ まい ほん かい はい","coreMeaning":"N5 counters: round (個), flat (枚), long (本), times (回), cups (杯)","slug":"compare","definition":"Beginners often confuse counters because they all follow a number. The key is the item's shape: round or solid → 個, thin → 枚, long → 本. 回 and 杯 may sound alike with some numbers, but 回 means occurrences and 杯 means containers.","nuance":"At a restaurant, ビール1杯 means a glass of beer, while ビール1本 means a bottle. 映画を1本見る means watching one film; it uses 本 for the film, not 回, which counts how many times you watch.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ペン","m":"pen"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"1本","r":"いっぽん","m":"1 pen"},{"t":"ください","m":"please give me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please give me one pen.","scene":"Buying a pen at an office-supply store."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜人 (にん)","point":"個 counts round objects; 人 counts people. Do NOT use 個 for people."},{"vs":"〜枚","point":"枚 counts thin flat items: 紙1枚; 本 counts long items: ペン1本. Same number, different shape."},{"vs":"〜杯","point":"回 = times (3回 = three times); 杯 = cups/bowls (3杯 = three cups). Not さんばい: 3回 = さんかい, 3杯 = さんばい; 1回=いっかい, 1杯=いっぱい."}]}},"n5-045||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors and traps","sub":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯","patternReading":"こ まい ほん かい はい","coreMeaning":"N5 counters: round (個), flat (枚), long (本), times (回), cups (杯)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Japanese requires an appropriate counter for countable nouns. Common errors include using 個 for everything, misreading sound changes, and confusing 回 with 杯.","nuance":"The sentence may still be understood with the wrong counter, but it sounds very unnatural. Borrowed words such as テレビ use 台, not 個. 本 counts many long objects, but テレビ uses 台.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"1台","r":"いちだい","m":"1 unit"},{"t":"買いました","r":"かいました","m":"bought"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought one TV.","scene":"Showing a friend a new purchase."}],"pitfalls":["❌ りんごを3ください。 → ✅ りんごを3個ください。","❌ ペンを2枚ください。 → ✅ ペンを2本ください。 (long pen uses 本)","❌ 映画を3回見ました。 (meaning 3 films) → ✅ 映画を3本見ました。","❌ コーヒーを1杯 (read 'いちはい') → ✅ いっぱい","❌ テレビを1個ください。 → ✅ テレビを1台ください。"],"compare":null}},"n5-045||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips and kanji clues","sub":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜個／〜枚／〜本／〜回／〜杯","patternReading":"こ まい ほん かい はい","coreMeaning":"N5 counters: round (個), flat (枚), long (本), times (回), cups (杯)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tips: **個** has the person radical (亻) and 固; **枚** can be linked to a light, thin sheet; **本** has a long vertical stroke suggesting a long object; **回** looks like a rotating loop; **杯** combines “wood” and “not,” suggesting a hollow wooden container.","nuance":"These mnemonics are useful at N5. From N4 onward, you will meet more counters such as 台, 匹, and 冊, but shape-based thinking remains useful. Do not rely on rote memorization alone.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-046||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"core","definition":"〜人 is the counter for **the number of people**; 〜歳 counts **age**. Learners often forget these are counters, so saying “one person” requires the counter: **1人（ひとり）**, **2人（ふたり）**, and **〜にん** from 3 onward. Likewise, age uses 〜歳（さい）, with **20歳 = はたち**. These are essential for asking about people and age in daily conversation.","nuance":"Both counters are **basic vocabulary** used in speech and writing. Their tone is **neutral**, neither formal nor casual. When asking an older person’s age, avoid asking directly with 何歳; use a more polite form such as おいくつ. However, for N5, 何歳 is acceptable. 歳 may also be written as **才** in casual contexts, but JLPT tests use 歳.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"姉","r":"あね","m":"older sister"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"25歳","r":"にじゅうごさい","m":"25 years old"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My older sister is 25 years old.","scene":"Asking about a sister’s age in a family conversation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"クラス","m":"class"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"students"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"12人","r":"じゅうににん","m":"12 people"},{"t":"います","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are 12 students in the class.","scene":"Counting students in a class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-046||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structures: **NUMBER + 人** and **NUMBER + 歳**. The number and counter join **directly**, with no particle between them. For 人, remember **一人=ひとり** and **二人=ふたり**; from **3人** onward, use Sino-Japanese readings: さんにん, よにん, ごにん... For 歳, most readings end in ~さい, but **1歳=いっさい**, **8歳=はっさい**, **10歳=じっさい** (or じゅっさい), and **20歳=はたち**. Questions use **何人（なんにん）** and **何歳（なんさい）**.","nuance":"This is simple **number-plus-counter formation**, with no tense changes. In a sentence, the counter appears with **います** to indicate people’s existence, or with **です** when stating age. In questions, add か at the end.","structure":[{"lhs":"数","rhs":"人","note":"1=ひとり, 2=ふたり, 3〜=〜にん"},{"lhs":"数","rhs":"歳","note":"〜さい, but 1=いっさい, 8=はっさい, 20=はたち"},{"lhs":"何人","rhs":"（ですか）","note":"Ask the number of people"},{"lhs":"何歳","rhs":"（ですか）","note":"Ask someone’s age"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"兄弟","r":"きょうだい","m":"siblings"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"二人","r":"ふたり","m":"2 people"},{"t":"います","m":"there are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have two siblings.","scene":"Talking about the number of siblings"},{"tokens":[{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"8歳","r":"はっさい","m":"8 years old"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My son is eight years old.","scene":"Introducing a child’s age"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-046||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"usage","definition":"Use these forms when **counting people** and **stating age**. They commonly appear when asking **family size**（何人）, asking **age**（何歳）, counting attendees, or introducing yourself or others. With strangers, 何人家族ですか is a polite way to ask about family size. With children, Japanese speakers often ask **いくつ** instead of 何歳.","nuance":"This usage is **common across situations and social groups**. In a clear context, an answer can simply give the number and counter without an additional verb. For example, 姉は25歳です introduces someone’s age objectively; 25歳になります means “will turn 25,” which is different and beyond N5.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何人","r":"なんにん","m":"how many people"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How many people are in your family?","scene":"Asking about family size"},{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"4人","r":"よにん","m":"4 people"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are four people in my family.","scene":"Answering about family size"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-046||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with 名 and いくつ","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse **〜人** with the polite people-counter **〜名（めい）**, and **〜歳** with the age question word **いくつ**. **〜人** is usual in everyday conversation; **〜名** is used in formal settings such as restaurants, hotels, and airports, often with honorific language. **〜歳** is the standard way to ask age, while **いくつ** is more casual and often used for children or politely with older people.","nuance":"何歳 can sound somewhat direct when asking an older person’s age; いくつ or おいくつ is more polite. In the N5 exam, the main task is choosing 何歳 or いくつ based on context.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何人","r":"なんにん","m":"how many people"},{"t":"来ます","r":"きます","m":"will come"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How many people are coming?","scene":"Asking friends how many will attend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お客様","r":"おきゃくさま","m":"guest"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何名様","r":"なんめいさま","m":"how many guests"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How many guests are in your party?","scene":"Greeting customers at a restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜名","point":"名（めい） is a FORMAL counter for people, used in services. Learners may use 人 everywhere → it sounds less natural in a hotel."},{"vs":"いくつ","point":"いくつ asks age in casual speech and can also ask the quantity of things. 何歳 is more direct and common for adults’ age."}]}},"n5-046||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with the people and age counters. They mainly come from **using Sino-Japanese readings** incorrectly: reading 一人 as いちにん or 二人 as ににん, or **forgetting the special form 20歳**. Also, do not use **〜さん** to count people; 〜さん follows a person’s name and is not a counter.","nuance":"Reading 20歳 as にじゅうさい is understandable, but **Japanese speakers usually use はたち**, especially for an adult’s age. はたち remains standard in both speech and formal writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"妹","r":"いもうと","m":"younger sister"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"10歳","r":"じっさい","m":"10 years old"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My younger sister is 10 years old.","scene":"Answering about a younger sister’s age"}],"pitfalls":["❌ Reading 一人 as いちにん → ✅ The correct reading is ひとり. Memorize this special exception; it does not follow the usual Sino-Japanese pattern.","❌ Reading 二人 as ににん → ✅ The correct reading is ふたり. This is another exception with no general rule, so memorize it.","❌ Reading 20歳 as にじゅっさい → ✅ The standard reading is はたち, used from casual speech to writing; にじゅっさい is understood but unnatural.","❌ Using 〜さん to count people, as in 三人（さんにん） → ✅ さん follows a person’s name (e.g., 田中さん); it is not a counter. Use 人 (にん) to count people.","❌ Using 五つ（いつつ） to count people, such as 子供が五ついます → ✅ Use 5人（ごにん）: つ is a general counter for things, never people."],"compare":null}},"n5-046||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aids using sound and imagery. For 人, 1 and 2 are **hitori/futari**. Remember **ri** as a special ending: one person is **hi-to-ri**, and two people are **fu-ta-ri**. From 3 onward, add **nin**, like **ninja**—many people are like a ninja team. For 歳, normal numbers add **sai**, but 20 is **hatachi**—picture a person wearing a **hat** and stepping into adulthood.","nuance":"These are memory techniques, not grammar rules. Feel free to create your own story or image.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-046||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"〜人／〜歳 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜人／〜歳","patternReading":"にん・さい","coreMeaning":"Counting people (人) and age (歳): number + counter, with exceptions","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear often in N5 textbooks and JLPT questions: **何人家族** (ask family size), **〜人います** (there are a number of people), **〜歳です** (state age), and **20歳（はたち）**. Mastering them helps you respond quickly in listening and reading.","nuance":"In conversation, 家族は4人です is a model answer; adding 私の家族 makes the meaning clearer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"4人","r":"よにん","m":"4 people"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are four people in my family.","scene":"Introducing your family at a gathering"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-047||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"core","definition":"Nに〜回Vます is a basic Japanese **frequency** pattern. It answers: “How many times do you do something within a period of time?” **N** is a time period such as 1日, 1週間, 1か月, or 1年; **回** counts the number of times; **Vます** is the action.","nuance":"The tone is **neutral** and polite, suitable for friends and older people alike. It does not express strong emotion; it simply states the rhythm of a repeated activity.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1週間","r":"いっしゅうかん","m":"1 week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"3回","r":"さんかい","m":"3 times"},{"t":"ジム","m":"gym"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to the gym three times a week.","scene":"Answering a coworker about your exercise habits."},{"tokens":[{"t":"1か月","r":"いっかげつ","m":"1 month"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"1回","r":"いっかい","m":"once"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歯医者","r":"はいしゃ","m":"dentist"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to the dentist once a month.","scene":"Telling a friend about regular dental visits."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-047||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"forms","definition":"The framework is **N（time）＋ に ＋ number ＋ 回 ＋ Vます**. The particle **に** comes directly after the time period and **cannot be omitted**. The number of times always comes before the verb, after 回.","nuance":"When conjugating the verb, keep Nに〜回 unchanged; only change Vます for tense or negation. In conversation, a short answer such as 「1週間に3回です。」 is possible when the meaning is clear.","structure":[{"lhs":"1週間 ＋ に ＋ 3 ＋ 回","rhs":"ジムに行きます","note":"Three times a week."},{"lhs":"1か月 ＋ に ＋ 1 ＋ 回","rhs":"歯医者に行きます","note":"Once a month."},{"lhs":"1日 ＋ に ＋ 何 ＋ 回","rhs":"薬を飲みますか","note":"Asking how many times a day."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1日","r":"いちにち","m":"1 day"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"2回","r":"にかい","m":"2 times"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I take medicine twice a day.","scene":"A doctor tells a patient to take medicine regularly."},{"tokens":[{"t":"1年","r":"いちねん","m":"1 year"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何回","r":"なんかい","m":"how many times"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How many times a year do you travel?","scene":"Asking a friend about yearly travel plans."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-047||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to talk about the **frequency** of an action: going to the gym, taking medicine, returning home, or going to the supermarket. It is a basic question-and-answer pattern for frequency, introduced in Minna no Nihongo Lesson 11.","nuance":"The time phrase often comes at the beginning to clarify the **time frame**. To ask about the other person’s schedule, simply replace the number with **何回**. Do not use this pattern for a single, non-repeating event.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1か月","r":"いっかげつ","m":"1 month"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"1回","r":"いっかい","m":"once"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"実家","r":"じっか","m":"parents’ home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I return to my parents’ home once a month.","scene":"A friend living away talks about visiting home."},{"tokens":[{"t":"1週間","r":"いっしゅうかん","m":"1 week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"何回","r":"なんかい","m":"how many times"},{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"How many times a week do you go to the supermarket?","scene":"Asking a roommate about grocery trips."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-047||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Nに〜回 vs Nで","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"compare","definition":"N5 learners often confuse **に** in this pattern with **で** in NでVます. **に** marks time/frequency, while **で** marks means or method. Also, **毎日** means “every day” but does not state how many times.","nuance":"When expressing both frequency and means, put Nに〜回 first, followed by Nで: 1週間に2回バスで行きます。","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1週間","r":"いっしゅうかん","m":"1 week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"2回","r":"にかい","m":"2 times"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go to school by bus twice a week.","scene":"Talking about how you get to school."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NでVます","point":"で = means (バスで行きます); に〜回 = times within a period (1週間に2回行きます)."},{"vs":"毎日Vます","point":"毎日 = every day; no count needed. 1日に1回 = once a day, emphasizing the number."}]}},"n5-047||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The three most common errors are **forgetting に**, **putting the frequency phrase in the wrong position**, and **misreading 回** after certain numbers. Fixing these three points is enough to use the pattern accurately.","nuance":"In casual speech, Japanese speakers may shorten this to “週に2回,” but in writing and textbook exercises, keep the full 1週間 + に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1週間","r":"いっしゅうかん","m":"1 week"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"2回","r":"にかい","m":"2 times"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study Japanese twice a week.","scene":"Talking about a language-school schedule."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 1週間3回ジムに行きます。 → ✅ 1週間に3回ジムに行きます。","❌ 1日で2回薬を飲みます。 → ✅ 1日に2回薬を飲みます。","❌ 1回 = いちかい → ✅ 1回 = いっかい; 3回 = さんかい."],"compare":null}},"n5-047||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture **回** as a closed circle: each time an action happens is one turn around the circle. The particle に acts like “per” or “each.” You can quickly translate Nに〜回 as **“each N, that many times.”**","nuance":"When you hear **に ＋ number ＋ 回**, recognize it immediately as a frequency statement. Picture a calendar: the time period is the large box, and the X marks show the number of times.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-047||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nに〜回Vます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nに〜回Vます","patternReading":"にかい","coreMeaning":"Do ~ times per / every N (frequency)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are useful phrases that appear often in Minna no Nihongo Lesson 11 and everyday life. Memorize them to respond quickly when talking about frequency.","nuance":"In casual conversation, people often shorten these to “週に2回” and “月に1回.” In tests and textbook-style writing, write the full forms: 1週間に and 1か月に.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"1日","r":"いちにち","m":"1 day"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"3回","r":"さんかい","m":"3 times"},{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meals"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I eat three meals a day.","scene":"Answering a doctor about your eating habits."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-048||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern asks to **compare two specific nouns** and requires the listener to choose one. It means: 'Which of A and B is more ~?' This is a **choice-comparison question**, not a yes/no question.","nuance":"Polite enough for seniors, coworkers, and friends. どちら is the polite spoken form of どっち. In replies, Japanese speakers often use 〜のほうが to make the contrast clear.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"猫","r":"ねこ","m":"cat"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which do you like better, cats or dogs?","scene":"Chatting with someone you just met"},{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"紅茶","r":"こうちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which do you like better, coffee or tea?","scene":"Choosing drinks with a friend at a café"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-048||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure has **two nouns** joined by と, followed by **どちらが** + an adjective or verb + ですか. The standard reply uses **Aのほうが〜です** to show which side was chosen.","nuance":"In the reply, のほうが can be combined with Bより for emphasis: AのほうがBより〜です. At N5, focus on Aのほうが〜です.","structure":[{"lhs":"Aと","rhs":"Bとどちらが～ですか","note":"Ask which is better: A or B"},{"lhs":"Aのほうが","rhs":"～です","note":"Reply: A is more ~"},{"lhs":"AとBとどちらが","rhs":"～ですか","note":"どっち is possible in casual speech"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"早い","r":"はやい","m":"fast"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which is faster, the train or the bus?","scene":"Deciding how to travel by the timetable"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"早い","r":"はやい","m":"fast"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train is faster.","scene":"Replying to the question above"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-048||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **ask which of two options** is preferable by a given criterion. It often appears in shopping, asking for directions, and everyday conversation.","nuance":"After どちらが, use an い-adjective, な-adjective, or a dictionary-form verb. With な-adjectives such as 好き, 便利, and 簡単, keep the form unchanged. For verbs, どちらができますか asks about ability.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which is more difficult, Japanese or English?","scene":"Asking a classmate"},{"tokens":[{"t":"リンゴ","m":"apple"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"バナナ","m":"banana"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which do you like better, apples or bananas?","scene":"Offering fruit during an office lunch break"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-048||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from other questions","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish this from **NはNですか** (a yes/no question) and **NはAですか、Bですか** (a choice question with or without どちら). This pattern specifically uses どちら to ask about **two specific nouns**.","nuance":"When asked 'どちらが好きですか', you cannot answer 'はい/いいえ'; you must choose. By contrast, 'これは本ですか' can be answered yes or no.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"冬","r":"ふゆ","m":"winter"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"夏","r":"なつ","m":"summer"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which do you like better, winter or summer?","scene":"Chatting about weather preferences"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNですか","point":"NはNですか asks for confirmation, not comparison. Example: 'これは本ですか' → 'Is this a book?' NとNとどちらが compares two things."},{"vs":"NはAですか、Bですか","point":"Both ask for a choice, but this pattern compares two nouns (AとB), while 'NはAですか、Bですか' asks about one noun's category (is N A or B?). Example: 'これは本ですか、雑誌ですか'."},{"vs":"NはNより〜です","point":"This is a comparative statement, not a question. '電車はバスより早いです' = The train is faster than the bus. The pattern here asks the question."}]}},"n5-048||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include forgetting the second と, using the wrong particle, and giving an answer that does not address the comparison.","nuance":"The reply should clearly name the choice with 〜のほうがです; do not answer merely 'はい' or 'いいえ'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"飛行機","r":"ひこうき","m":"airplane"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"船","r":"ふね","m":"ship"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"速い","r":"はやい","m":"fast"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which is faster, the plane or the ship?","scene":"Discussing transport with a traveler"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 猫か犬どちらが好きですか → ✅ 猫と犬とどちらが好きですか (use と to connect the choices in a どちら question, not か)","❌ どちらは好きですか → ✅ どちらが好きですか (the subject particle here is が, not は)","❌ 猫のほう好きです → ✅ 猫のほうが好きです (が is required after のほう when it is the subject)","❌ はい、好きです → ✅ 猫のほうが好きです (choose one; do not give a vague yes/no reply)"],"compare":null}},"n5-048||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: Imagine A and B standing side by side, each with its own と. The questioner stands between them and says 'どちらが' to ask which is better. どちら sounds a little like 'two'—there are two options.","nuance":"The と appears twice like two hooks attaching the two choices to the question. With only one と, the structure is unbalanced, as if only one side were being asked about.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-048||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","patternReading":"どちらがですか","coreMeaning":"Which of A and B is more ~? (comparing two choices)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases commonly appear in conversation and exams: asking about preferences, choices, convenience, and ability.","nuance":"どちらができますか asks which skill someone can perform, for example '日本語と英語とどちらができますか'—which language can you use?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"store"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"宅配","r":"たくはい","m":"home delivery"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"どちら","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which is more convenient, going to a store or home delivery?","scene":"Choosing how to shop when you do not want to go out"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-049||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"core","definition":"**N1 は N2 より + Adjective + です** is the basic comparative pattern. N1 is the topic, N2 is the comparison standard, and より means 'than'. Example: 東京は大阪より大きいです = Tokyo is larger than Osaka.","nuance":"This is a **neutral** comparison with no strong emotion. It works in speech and writing, with friends or older people. To stress a large difference, add **ずっと** (much).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"東京","r":"とうきょう","m":"Tokyo"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"large"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tokyo is larger than Osaka.","scene":"Comparing the areas of two cities."},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"バス","r":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"速い","r":"はやい","m":"fast"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train is faster than the bus.","scene":"Discussing transportation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-049||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"forms","definition":"For **い-adjectives**, keep the plain form, including final い, then add です. For **な-adjectives**, use the base form followed by です. Negative forms: い-adj → **〜くないです**, な-adj → **〜じゃありません**. Past forms: い-adj → **〜かったです**, な-adj → **〜でした**.","nuance":"Learners often make mistakes with the past form of い-adjectives: drop い and add かった; **do not** add でした after the regular form. Example: 寒い → 寒かったです, not 寒いでした.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 より + い-adj です","note":"例: 東京は大阪より大きいです"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 より + な-adj です","note":"例: 京都は大阪より静かです"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 より + い-adj くないです","note":"例: 今日は昨日より暑くないです"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 より + な-adj じゃありません","note":"例: この部屋はあの部屋よりきれいじゃありません"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 より + い-adj かったです","note":"例: 昨日は今日より寒かったです"},{"lhs":"N1 は","rhs":"N2 より + な-adj でした","note":"例: 昔は今より静かでした"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","r":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"面白い","r":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is more interesting than that book.","scene":"Comparing two books in a bookstore."},{"tokens":[{"t":"京都","r":"きょうと","m":"Kyoto"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Kyoto is quieter than Osaka.","scene":"Commenting on two cities while traveling."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-049||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to compare the degree of a quality between two things or situations: weather, prices, size, speed, and so on. **思ったより** (more than expected) and **いつもより** (more than usual) are also common in conversation for differences from expectations or habits.","nuance":"In response to **AとBとどちらが〜ですか**, use **AはBより〜です** to state that A is higher. To emphasize the choice, Japanese speakers often use **Nのほうが**, but this pattern remains completely natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"思った","r":"おもった","m":"thought"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This problem is harder than I thought.","scene":"The exercise exceeds expectations."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いつも","r":"いつも","m":"usual"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"energetic"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I feel more energetic today than usual.","scene":"Telling a friend you feel good."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-049||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns contrasted","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish this from **N2よりN1のほうが〜です** — both mean 'more than', but the order is reversed and N1, the chosen item, is emphasized. **N1はN2と同じです** expresses sameness rather than a comparative difference.","nuance":"When answering 'Which do you prefer?', Japanese speakers often use **Nのほうが** to emphasize the choice. However, **NはNより〜です** is also correct and common for objective comparisons.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","r":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"広い","r":"ひろい","m":"spacious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This park is larger than that park.","scene":"Comparing two parks while walking."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NよりNのほうが〜です","point":"The order is reversed: Nのほうが comes first to emphasize the chosen item. Same meaning, but more choice-oriented."},{"vs":"NはNと同じです","point":"同じ = 'the same'; there is no difference. This pattern says the two are equal, unlike the comparative pattern."}]}},"n5-049||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners may translate directly from their first language and use the wrong Japanese word order. Remember: **より always follows the noun used as the comparison standard**, and an adjective must follow it.","nuance":"Mistakes with adjective negatives and past forms are common. Master the conjugation of い-adjectives and な-adjectives to avoid them.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","r":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This shop is cheaper than that shop.","scene":"Comparing prices at two shops."}],"pitfalls":["❌ より日本はベトナム大きいです。 → ✅ 日本はベトナムより大きいです。 (より must follow the comparison noun)","❌ 今日は昨日より寒いでした。 → ✅ 今日は昨日より寒かったです。 (The past of an い-adj is かったです, not でした)","❌ この店はあの店より安いじゃありません。 → ✅ この店はあの店より安くないです。 (An い-adj negative uses くないです, not じゃありません)","❌ 私はあなたより。 → ✅ 私はあなたより背が高いです。 (An adjective is required after より to complete the comparison)"],"compare":null}},"n5-049||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine a balance scale: **N1** sits on the left pan, before は, and **N2** sits on the right pan, after より. **より** acts like an arrow pointing to N2, meaning 'compared with'. When the scale tilts toward N1, conclude that N1 is greater than N2.","nuance":"This tip helps you remember the order: N1 (topic) → は → N2 (standard) → より → Adjective. Never put より at the beginning.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-049||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNより〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNより〜です","patternReading":"はよりです","coreMeaning":"N1 is ~er than N2","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases used with this comparative pattern: **思ったより** (more than I thought), **いつもより** (more than usual), **ずっと** (much more), and **少し/ちょっと** (a little more). They make the size of the difference clearer.","nuance":"In conversation, **思ったより** expresses mild surprise. **ずっと** emphasizes a large gap, while **少し** indicates only a small difference.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"思った","r":"おもった","m":"thought"},{"t":"より","m":"than"},{"t":"難しくない","r":"むずかしくない","m":"not difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japanese is not as difficult as I thought.","scene":"Reassuring a friend studying Japanese."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-050||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern compares two things or people and clearly identifies which side stands out more. In AよりBのほうが〜, B is emphasized as “this side” more than A. It is the standard answer to どちらが〜ですか.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; it is neither overly formal nor casual. When answering comparisons, Japanese speakers often use のほうが to clearly indicate “this side,” rather than simply making a general statement.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"みかん","m":"mandarin"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"りんご","m":"apple"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like apples more than mandarins.","scene":"Friends discuss which fruit they like"},{"tokens":[{"t":"タクシー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train is more convenient than a taxi.","scene":"Choosing transport to the airport"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-050||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & usage","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: **N1より + N2のほうが + 〜です**. N1 is the comparison point, and N2 is emphasized. The following word is usually an い-adjective or な-adjective.","nuance":"With **好き・わかる・上手**, keep が before the predicate: 〜のほうが好きです. N1 and N2 must be **the same type**—both people, things, or concepts.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1より","rhs":"N2のほうが + イ形容詞 + です","note":"N2 is the emphasized side."},{"lhs":"N1より","rhs":"N2のほうが + ナ形容詞 + です","note":"Drop な before です."},{"lhs":"N1より","rhs":"N2のほうが + 動詞","note":"Use this to compare actions by two subjects."},{"lhs":"どちらが","rhs":"［形容詞］ですか","note":"Question asking which of two is preferred."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"速い","r":"はやい","m":"fast"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The train is faster than the bus.","scene":"Commenting on two morning transport options"},{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"難しい","r":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japanese is more difficult than English.","scene":"Classmates discuss foreign-language difficulty"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-050||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to compare two options and make a choice or comment. In conversation, people often ask **どちらが〜ですか** and answer with **〜のほうが〜です**.","nuance":"A choice question between two sides uses **が**, not は. In the answer, put your choice directly before のほうが. If the context is clear, より can be omitted, though the full form remains natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"京都","r":"きょうと","m":"Kyoto"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"静か","r":"しずか","m":"quiet"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Kyoto is quieter than Osaka.","scene":"Answering a tourist choosing Osaka or Kyoto"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"ベトナム","m":"Vietnam"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"暑い","r":"あつい","m":"hot"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Vietnam is hotter than Japan.","scene":"A Vietnamese student discusses home weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-050||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast: のほうが vs は〜より","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this with **N2はN1より〜です**. Both conclude that N2 is more〜 than N1, but the emphasis differs.","nuance":"**N1よりN2のほうが〜** puts N2 in the “more” position and is natural when answering どちらが. **N2はN1より〜** is a general comment and does not strongly suggest choosing a side.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"りんご","m":"apple"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"みかん","m":"mandarin"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like mandarins more than apples.","scene":"Quick reply to どちらが好きですか"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"N2はN1より〜です","point":"Same meaning, different emphasis: AはBより〜 is neutral, while AよりBのほうが〜 emphasizes B as “the more suitable side.” Example: りんごはみかんより高いです。 vs みかんよりりんごのほうが高いです。"},{"vs":"NとNとどちらが〜ですか","point":"This is a choice question, while the target pattern is an answer. When asked どちらが, answer with のほうが, not は. Example: 犬と猫とどちらが好きですか？ → 猫のほうが好きです。"},{"vs":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","point":"This pattern compares two items, while 〜の中で…一番〜 compares three or more. Example: りんごよりみかんのほうが好きです。 vs 果物の中でみかんが一番好きです。"}]}},"n5-050||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often **forget のほうが** or place “more” according to English word order. Remember: **comparison item → より → chosen item → のほうが → adjective → です**.","nuance":"This pattern is simple but easy to mistranslate word by word. In Japanese, “more” is expressed through both より and のほうが, not by one word after the adjective.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"より"},{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"tall"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Father is taller than Mother.","scene":"Talking about family members’ height"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本よりベトナムが暑いです。 → ✅ 日本よりベトナムのほうが暑いです。","❌ ベトナムはより暑いです。 → ✅ ベトナムは日本より暑いです。","❌ どちらは高いですか。 → ✅ どちらが高いですか。","❌ 日本よりベトナムは暑いです。 → ✅ 日本よりベトナムのほうが暑いです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-050||memory":{"title":"🎯 Visual memory tip","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory aid for the word order: **comparison item → より → chosen item → のほうが**. Picture より as one side and のほうが as the side you raise your hand to choose.","nuance":"方 (ほう) means “side.” In AよりBのほうが, imagine pointing to **side B** and saying “this side is more.” どちらが asks “which side?”, and the natural answer points to “this side.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-050||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NよりNのほうが〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NよりNのほうが〜です","patternReading":"よりのほうがです","coreMeaning":"B is more than A — emphasizes “side B”","slug":"phrases","definition":"These comparison phrases appear frequently in N5 conversation and test questions. Memorizing the fixed patterns helps you respond faster.","nuance":"When a salesperson says こちらのほうが…, they are **suggesting an option** to the customer. When asked どちらが, remember to answer with のほうが.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"こちら","m":"this side"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ほう","m":"side"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This one is cheaper.","scene":"A salesperson recommends one of two products"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-051||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **Nの中で〜が一番〜です** compares **three or more items** and states that one item **ranks first** for a quality. **N** is the group or comparison range; **〜が一番〜** identifies the number-one subject. Example: “クラスの中で田中さんが一番背が高いです” — Tanaka is the tallest in the class.","nuance":"It is used in both speech and writing and is not overly formal. The result may express a **personal preference** (e.g. 一番好き) or a **general evaluation** (e.g. 一番高い). In questions, the listener understands that a specific group is being compared.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"クラス","r":"くらす","m":"class"},{"t":"の中で","r":"のなかで","m":"among"},{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"背","r":"せ","m":"height"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"tall"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tanaka is the tallest in the class.","scene":"Discussing classmates’ heights"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"食べ物","r":"たべもの","m":"food"},{"t":"の中で","r":"のなかで","m":"among"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寿司","r":"すし","m":"sushi"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Of Japanese foods, I like sushi the most.","scene":"Friends asking about favorite Japanese food"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-051||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes the **structures** of the pattern. The key point is that **N + の中で** marks the range, while **subject + が + 一番 + adjective** identifies the top item. If N is a **place** such as この店 or 学校, **N + で** can replace **N + の中で**.","nuance":"To **modify a noun**, use **一番 + adjective + の/な + N**, for example **一番人気のメニュー**. In questions, interrogatives such as **何/誰/どれ** always take **が**, not **は**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N + の中で","rhs":"Subject + が + 一番 + Adjective / Noun","note":"Compare within a group; the subject always takes が."},{"lhs":"N + で","rhs":"Subject + が + 一番 + Adjective / Noun","note":"When N is a place such as この店、クラス (in the class), で can replace の中で."},{"lhs":"N + の中で + 何が + 一番 + 〜ですか","rhs":"～が + 一番 + 〜です","note":"A common question pattern for asking about rank."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"人気","r":"にんき","m":"popular"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"メニュー","r":"めにゅー","m":"menu item"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is the most popular item at this restaurant?","scene":"Looking at a menu and asking a staff member"},{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"の中で","r":"のなかで","m":"among"},{"t":"誰","r":"だれ","m":"who"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"年上","r":"としうえ","m":"oldest"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Who is the oldest in your family?","scene":"Asking a friend about family members"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-051||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears in many situations: **asking about rank** in a class or company; **asking about preferences**; **describing restaurants** and products; and **comparing over time** (一年で, 一週間で).","nuance":"When asking “〜の中で何が一番〜ですか,” the listener understands it as an **open question** with no preset choices. When someone says “この中で,” they often **point to a specific group of items** nearby. The pattern is friendly and works with friends, coworkers, and older people when used with です/ます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"these"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"どれ","r":"どれ","m":"which one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Which of these do you like the most?","scene":"Laying out several items and asking a friend to choose"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一年","r":"いちねん","m":"one year"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"寒い","r":"さむい","m":"cold"},{"t":"月","r":"つき","m":"month"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"二月","r":"にがつ","m":"February"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The coldest month of the year is February.","scene":"Talking about weather across the seasons"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-051||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from より・どちら","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"compare","definition":"Use this comparison to avoid confusion with **NはNより〜です** (comparative) and **AとBとどちらが〜ですか** (choosing between two).","nuance":"The key point: **Nの中で…が一番** requires a **group of ≥3** and states the range. **より** simply compares **two items**; **どちら** also limits the choice to **two options**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"果物","r":"くだもの","m":"fruit"},{"t":"の中で","r":"のなかで","m":"among"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"りんご","r":"りんご","m":"apple"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Of all the fruits, I like apples the most.","scene":"Talking about favorite fruits"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNより〜です","point":"Use this when **only two items** are compared and say which is greater. Use 〜の中で…が一番… for **three or more items** and select **one top item**. Example: りんごはバナナより好きです ↔ 果物の中でりんごが一番好きです."},{"vs":"AとBとどちらが〜ですか","point":"This asks for a comparison **between two options**. For a group of more than two, use Nの中で何が一番〜ですか. Example: コーヒーとお茶とどちらが好きですか ↔ 飲み物の中で何が一番好きですか."},{"vs":"Nは一番〜です","point":"Without の中で, the sentence only says “N is the most〜” and **does not specify the comparison range**, so context is usually needed. With Nの中で…が一番…, the **range is explicit**."}]}},"n5-051||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with this pattern mainly involve **using the wrong number of items** and **confusing が and は**.","nuance":"Always ask yourself: **How many items are being compared?** For two, use より/どちら; for three or more, use の中で…が一番. In an answer identifying “who or what is the most〜,” the subject must take **が**, not **は**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"these"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this one"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Of these, this one is the cheapest.","scene":"Comparing prices of items in front of you"}],"pitfalls":["❌ りんごとバナナの中で、バナナが一番好きです。 → ✅ りんごとバナナとどちらが好きですか。 (With only two, use どちら, not 一番.)","❌ クラスの中で田中さんは一番背が高いです。 → ✅ クラスの中で田中さんが一番背が高いです。 (A subject identified within a group takes が.)","❌ 果物で、りんごが一番好きです。 → ✅ 果物の中で、りんごが一番好きです。 (For a noun that is not a place or time, use の中で, not で.)","❌ 一番好きです。 (Without a range, the listener does not know what it is compared with.) → ✅ この中で一番好きです。 / スポーツの中でサッカーが一番好きです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-051||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture **N** as a **box** (中) containing several items. Look inside the box and choose item **number 1** (一番), then mark it with **が**. Memory formula: **“Inside the box (Nの中で), who is number one? → Subject + が + 一番.”**","nuance":"Remember that **一番** means not only “number one” but also “the most,” and normally comes with a range. Without a range, the sentence lacks information.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-051||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nの中で〜が一番〜です","patternReading":"のなかで 〜が いちばん です","coreMeaning":"the most〜among N (superlative within a group)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases often appear in exams and conversation: **クラスの中で一番**, **一年で一番**, **家族の中で一番**, and **この中で一番**. They let you speak quickly without rebuilding the structure.","nuance":"When combining them with adjectives, use the correct form: **一番 + Aい** (一番高い), **一番 + NAな** (一番好きな), and **一番 + Nの** (一番人気の).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"の中で","r":"のなかで","m":"among"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"一番","r":"いちばん","m":"the most"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"skilled"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother is the best cook in the family.","scene":"Praising Mom’s cooking during dinner"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-052||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"core","definition":"〜と同じです compares two nouns and states that they are the same. と is the comparison particle (same as, compared with), while 同じ means 'same' or 'the same kind'. The speaker makes an observation based on direct evidence, not personal feeling.","nuance":"This is a neutral, polite statement. It is not as strong as 'it is definitely the same'; it simply says 'same'. Use it with friends, older people, in class, or at work. In casual speech, drop です and say 同じだ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"かばん","m":"bag"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My bag is the same as yours.","scene":"In class, two students notice their bags match"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ネクタイ","m":"tie"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This tie is the same as my father's.","scene":"Getting ready for work, I see my tie matches my father's"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"山田さん","r":"やまださん","m":"Mr. Yamada"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My phone is the same as Mr. Yamada's.","scene":"Seeing a coworker's phone and noticing the same model"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-052||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & forms","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N1 は N2 と同じです**. N1 is the item being discussed, and N2 is the reference for comparison. To make it negative, change です to **じゃありません** (or ではありません). To ask a question, add **か** at the end. Use **も** instead of は to say 'also the same'.","nuance":"This is the standard polite form taught in Minna no Nihongo. With close friends, you can say 同じだ or 同じだよ. In writing, じゃありません can be replaced by ではありません, but at N5, じゃありません is enough to remember.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 と同じです","note":"N1 is the same as N2"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 と同じじゃありません","note":"N1 is not the same as N2 (negative)"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 と同じですか","note":"Is N1 the same as N2? (question)"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"も N2 と同じです","note":"N1 is also the same as N2 (add an item)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tanaka's umbrella is the same as mine.","scene":"After work, I see my coworker's umbrella matches mine"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"じゃありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is not the same as that book.","scene":"At a bookstore, comparing two books"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"自転車","r":"じてんしゃ","m":"bicycle"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is your bicycle the same as mine?","scene":"Asking a classmate outside school"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-052||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"usage","definition":"In conversation, this pattern compares objects, places, times, and more, and confirms whether they are the same. When two things match, say 〜と同じです. When they do not, say 〜と同じじゃありません. Common questions are 〜と同じですか or 〜と同じですか、違いますか, asking the listener to choose one of two answers.","nuance":"This is polite and works with teachers and customers. For a more casual question, say 〜と同じ？ with rising intonation. In a choice question, keep か at the end; separate the choices with 、 or それとも.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is today's homework the same as yesterday's?","scene":"A student asks the teacher about homework"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"違います","r":"ちがいます","m":"different"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Are this and this the same or different?","scene":"The teacher shows two pictures to students"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"じゃありません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My phone isn't the same as yours.","scene":"Friends compare their new phones"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-052||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch prevents confusion with similar-looking patterns. **NはNです** says one noun is another noun; it does not compare two objects. **Nも** adds another object with the same property, but does not explicitly say 'the same'. **Nと違います** is the opposite of と同じです and uses the same particle と.","nuance":"At N5, focus on distinguishing と同じ from ordinary です and も. In AはBと同じです, there must be **two objects**; one can be replaced by の when the noun would be repeated.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"時計","r":"とけい","m":"watch"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"},{"t":"山田","r":"やまだ","m":"Yamada"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"違います","r":"ちがいます","m":"different"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My watch is the same as Tanaka's. Yamada's is different.","scene":"Three coworkers compare watches on a table"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです only identifies what something is (e.g. これはかばんです). 〜と同じです needs two objects and says they match (e.g. このかばんはあなたのと同じです)."},{"vs":"Nも","point":"Nも adds another object with the same property; it does not say two things match. 私も学生です = I am also a student, while 私の学校はあなたのと同じです = My school is the same as yours."},{"vs":"Nと違います","point":"Nと違います is the direct opposite of と同じです: AはBと同じです (same) / AはBと違います (different). Both use と; replace 同じ with 違います."}]}},"n5-052||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"A common mistake is translating 'the same as yours' as あなたと同じ. When referring to an object, add の: あなたのと同じです (the same as **yours**). Learners also write 同じい because they mistake it for an い-adjective. Remember that 同じ never takes い. The comparison particle is と, not に.","nuance":"These are basic beginner errors. Fix them early because later patterns such as と同じ, と違います, and と比べます also use と.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"food"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The food at this shop is the same as that shop's.","scene":"Eating out and comparing two shops"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私のかばんはあなたと同じです。 → ✅ 私のかばんはあなたのと同じです。 (To compare my bag with 'your bag', use の instead of repeating かばん.)","❌ これとそれは同じいです。 → ✅ これとそれは同じです。 (同じ is irregular, not an い-adjective, so do not add い.)","❌ 私の自転車はあなたに同じです。 → ✅ 私の自転車はあなたのと同じです。 (The particle marking the comparison target is と, not に.)","❌ このペンはあなたのと同じですかどうか。 → ✅ このペンはあなたのと同じですか。 (At N5, a simple question only needs か; かどうか is an N4 pattern.)"],"compare":null}},"n5-052||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory aid: **同** in 同じ means 'same' or 'together'. **と** is the particle meaning 'with'. Together: **A は B と 同じです** = A is the same as B. Picture two identical bags side by side, then say 'おなじ, oh-na-ji — almost alike'.","nuance":"This mnemonic does not need to reflect the true etymology; it only needs to help you remember the kanji and particle. When you see 同じ, ask yourself: the same as what? Think of と.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-052||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはNと同じです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNと同じです","patternReading":"とおなじです","coreMeaning":"N is the same as N (comparing two similar things)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear frequently in Minna exercises and classroom conversation. You may also see 同じ時間 'the same time', 同じ先生 'the same teacher', and 同じ店 'the same shop'. Learn あなたのと同じ so you can compare objects without repeating the noun.","nuance":"All of these phrases are polite and work with friends or older people. With close friends, you can say みんなと同じだよ. For a short question, say あなたのと同じ？.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"答え","r":"こたえ","m":"answer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"みんな","m":"everyone"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My answer is the same as everyone's.","scene":"In class, the teacher asks and students raise their hands"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あなた","m":"you"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is it the same as yours?","scene":"Asking after seeing a friend's phone match mine"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あの","m":"that"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"同じ","r":"おなじ","m":"same"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This seat is the same as that seat.","scene":"Entering a classroom and noting two rows match"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-053||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"core","definition":"**NはNが欲しいです** expresses the desire to **own** an object or obtain something. **欲しい** is an i-adjective, so the desired item takes **が**, not を. This pattern expresses a **subjective feeling**: the speaker states what they want, though they may not have it yet.","nuance":"This is a polite pattern using です, suitable for everyday conversation. When speaking to a superior or stranger, Japanese speakers often ask **『何か欲しいものはありますか。』** instead of **『何が欲しいですか。』** for greater politeness. Do not use **欲しい** for a third person; use **ほしがっています**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"スマホ","m":"smartphone"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want a new smartphone.","scene":"After seeing a classmate show off a new phone, I want one too."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want money right now.","scene":"Standing at a vending machine without any change."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-053||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms and conjugation","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"forms","definition":"**欲しい** is an i-adjective, so it conjugates like one: negative **〜くない**, past **〜かった**, and past negative **〜くなかった**. Add **です** for polite speech.","nuance":"Both **欲しくないです** and **欲しくありません** are correct; **〜くないです** sounds friendlier. With a superior, use **〜くありません** or ask **『何か欲しいものはありますか。』**","structure":[{"lhs":"私はN","rhs":"が欲しいです"},{"lhs":"私はN","rhs":"が欲しくないです"},{"lhs":"私はN","rhs":"が欲しかったです"},{"lhs":"私はN","rhs":"が欲しくなかったです"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want a dog.","scene":"I see an adorable puppy while passing a pet shop."},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しくない","r":"ほしくない","m":"do not want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don’t want a new computer right now.","scene":"A friend asks about buying one, but I don’t need it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-053||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to ask what someone wants or to state that you yourself need an item. Common questions are **『何が欲しいですか。』** (“What do you want?”—less polite) and **『何か欲しいものはありますか。』** (“Is there anything you want?”—polite).","nuance":"Use **何が欲しいですか** with friends or younger people. With customers or superiors, use **何か欲しいものはありますか**. To say “I don’t need anything,” use **特にありません** (“nothing in particular”) politely.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you want?","scene":"A mother asks her child what gift they want for their birthday."},{"tokens":[{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What is it that you want?","scene":"A teacher asks a student what reward they want."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-053||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing similar patterns","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare the two patterns most easily confused with this one: **NはNが好きです** and **Vたいです**. **欲しい** and **好き** use **が**, while **〜たい** can take **を** or **が**.","nuance":"**欲しい** means wanting to **obtain or own an object**, **好き** means liking something, and **〜たい** means wanting to **perform an action**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want some water.","scene":"A shopkeeper asks what I need, and I only want to buy water."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNが好きです","point":"好き = like (you may not need to own it); 欲しい = want to have it (you want to own it). For example: 犬が好きです (I like dogs) / 犬が欲しいです (I want a dog)."},{"vs":"Vたいです","point":"欲しい + noun, Vたい + verb. For example: 水が欲しいです (I want water) / 水を飲みたいです (I want to drink water)."}]}},"n5-053||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use **を** because they expect a direct object, but **欲しい** is an adjective and requires **が**. Also, do not use **欲しい** for a third person; use **ほしがっています**.","nuance":"**ほしがっています** describes another person visibly showing desire from an outside perspective. **欲しい** is used for the speaker’s own feeling.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want a car.","scene":"Talking with a friend about my dream of buying a car."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 新しいスマホを欲しいです。 → ✅ 新しいスマホが欲しいです。","❌ 彼は新しい車が欲しいです。 → ✅ 彼は新しい車をほしがっています。"],"compare":null}},"n5-053||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"memory","definition":"Use the kanji **欲**, meaning desire, as a memory aid. **欲しい** /ほしい/ sounds a little like “ho-si”—you keep “ho”-ping for the thing you want.","nuance":"Remember **が** because this is a state-describing adjective, and remember the rule: '**欲しい is mine**.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-053||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"NはNが欲しいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはNが欲しいです","patternReading":"はがほしいです","coreMeaning":"The speaker wants to have or own something","slug":"phrases","definition":"For the N5 exam, the common phrase **欲しいもの** (something one wants) and the polite question **何か欲しいものはありますか** often appear.","nuance":"**欲しい人** can mean “the person who wants it” in a gift-giving situation, but do not confuse it with 好きな人, “a person you like.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"もの","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あります","m":"have"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Is there anything you want?","scene":"An adult asks a child before going to the supermarket."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-054||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"core","definition":"〜たいです attaches to the ます-stem of a verb (Vます without ます) to express the speaker's desire to do something. It is the most common beginner-level way to say “want to do V.” Unlike ほしい (want to have an object), たい always goes with an action/verb.","nuance":"Affirmative statements usually refer to the first person (わたし). Questions refer to the second person (あなた). Do not use it directly to state a third person's feelings. たい expresses a subjective, natural desire; for a more polite tone, you can say 〜たいと思います.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drink"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to drink water.","scene":"After a run, speaking to your mother."},{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"し","r":"し","m":"do"},{"t":"たくない","r":"たくない","m":"do not want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't want to do anything today.","scene":"Lazily sprawled on the sofa at the weekend."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-054||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"forms","definition":"General formula: **Vます-stem + たい**. Because たい is an **い-adjective**, all forms follow い-adjective conjugation: negative **〜たくない**, past **〜たかった**, and past negative **〜たくなかった**. Add です/か for polite questions and statements.","nuance":"This is the familiar polite form in conversation. In casual speech, Japanese speakers often drop です: 水が飲みたい。 Attach it directly to the ます-stem without changing the sound: 行きます→行きたい, 食べます→食べたい.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（ます-stem）","rhs":"たいです","note":"Present desire: 食べます → 食べたいです"},{"lhs":"V（ます-stem）","rhs":"たくないです","note":"Negative: do not want to"},{"lhs":"V（ます-stem）","rhs":"たかったです","note":"Past: wanted to"},{"lhs":"V（ます-stem）","rhs":"たくなかったです","note":"Past negative: did not want to"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drink"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to drink coffee.","scene":"Ordering at a café in the morning."},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"起き","r":"おき","m":"wake up"},{"t":"たくない","r":"たくない","m":"do not want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't want to get up early tomorrow.","scene":"Complaining late at night to your roommate."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"time"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なり","r":"なり","m":"become"},{"t":"たかった","r":"たかった","m":"wanted"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was young, I wanted to become a doctor.","scene":"Telling a friend about your childhood."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-054||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vたいです is used to **state your own desire**, **ask what the listener wants**, or, in casual speech, shorten to **〜たい？**. For the object of the desired action, **が** is often used instead of を: 水が飲みたい. For a third person, do not directly use たい; use reported speech such as 〜と言っていた.","nuance":"In daily life, the question「〜たいですか」is common with friends and acquaintances, but avoid it with superiors because it can sound like probing into private desires. To invite someone, **〜ませんか** is much more natural. In response to たいですか, say はい、〜たいです / いいえ、〜たくないです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you want to eat?","scene":"At the cafeteria, asking your tablemate."},{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見","r":"み","m":"watch"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to watch a movie.","scene":"Telling a friend about weekend plans."},{"tokens":[{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drink"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Do you want some tea?","scene":"Asking a close coworker during a break."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-054||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Vたい vs ほしい / ませんか","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch distinguishes **Vたいです** from two easily confused beginner patterns: **Nが欲しいです** and **Vませんか**. The key difference is that たい expresses a desire to perform an action, while 欲しい expresses a desire to possess a specific object. To invite someone to do something together, Japanese speakers usually use **〜ませんか** instead of asking “〜たいですか,” because the latter can sound like probing into a personal desire. For example, “映画を見ませんか” (Shall we watch a movie?) is much more natural than “映画を見たいですか” (Do you want to watch a movie?).","nuance":"Keep grammar and pragmatics separate: たい is a personal desire, while ませんか is an invitation directed at the listener. Using the wrong pattern can make the sentence sound unnatural or change its meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お茶","r":"おちゃ","m":"tea"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drink"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to drink tea.","scene":"An employee speaks to a coworker during a break."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Nが欲しいです","point":"欲しい means WANT TO HAVE an object (noun); たい means WANT TO DO an action (verb). Example: 水が欲しい (I want water) ≠ 水が飲みたい (I want to drink water)."},{"vs":"Vませんか","point":"〜ませんか is a natural invitation (Shall we do it together?); 〜たいですか asks about personal desire. Example: 映画を見ませんか (Shall we watch a movie?) ≠ 映画を見たいですか (Do you want to watch a movie?)."}]}},"n5-054||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes when using Vたい: **using the dictionary form**, **forming the negative incorrectly**, **speaking for a third person's feelings**, and **using たい as an invitation**.","nuance":"Remember that たい attaches to the ます-stem, never the dictionary form. Since it behaves like an い-adjective, its negative is 〜たくない.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行き","r":"いき","m":"go"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to go alone.","scene":"Turning down a friend's invitation to go together."}],"pitfalls":["❌ リンさんは 日本へ 行きたいです。(speaking for a third person's feelings) → ✅ リンさんは 日本へ 行きたいと 言っていました。","❌ 行くたいです。(using the dictionary form) → ✅ 行きたいです。(Vます-stem + たい)","❌ 行きたいじゃないです。(negative like a na-adjective) → ✅ 行きたくないです。(Vます-stem + たくない)","When inviting someone, don't ask「映画を見たいですか？」→ use「映画を見ませんか？」instead"],"compare":null}},"n5-054||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **たい** is the “want” ending attached to a ます-stem. Since it behaves like an い-adjective, remember three forms: **たい → たくない → たかった**.","nuance":"Imagine: “eat” = 食べ, and adding たい gives 食べたい — “eat + want.” In the negative, “want” becomes “do not want”: たくない; when talking about the past: たかった.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-054||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたいです","patternReading":"たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do V (the speaker's personal desire)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases with たい appear constantly on the N5 exam and in daily life: **会いたい** (want to meet; miss someone), **何が食べたい？** (what do you want to eat?), and **日本へ行きたい** (want to go to Japan).","nuance":"Japanese speakers often say「会いたい」when they miss friends, romantic partners, or family.「休みたい」is also a common phrase when tired.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家族","r":"かぞく","m":"family"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会い","r":"あい","m":"meet"},{"t":"たい","r":"たい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to see my family.","scene":"Texting your mother after a long day at work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-055||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern expresses the **purpose of movement**: going somewhere to do something. Formula: [destination] へ + [purpose] に + [movement verb]. The purpose is usually a verb with ます removed (Vます → V + に) or an activity noun such as 買い物.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, and very common in daily life. It **emphasizes the purpose** of moving. When the destination is clear, へ may be omitted. Do not use it with non-movement verbs such as 食べる or 見る.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the supermarket to shop.","scene":"Telling a friend about this afternoon's plans."},{"tokens":[{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"借り","r":"かり","m":"borrow"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to the library to borrow a book.","scene":"Telling your mother what you did yesterday."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強し","r":"べんきょうし","m":"study"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came to Japan to study Japanese.","scene":"Introducing yourself to classmates."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-055||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"forms","definition":"The full structure has the **destination** before へ, the **purpose** before に, and the **movement verb** at the end. The purpose may be an activity noun (買い物, 散歩) or a ます-form verb with ます removed (会います → 会いに).","nuance":"This is the usual polite pattern (ます-form). With close friends, the plain form is also possible: スーパーへ買い物に行く. The final verb must clearly express directional movement.","structure":[{"lhs":"N(場所) へ","rhs":"N(目的) に Vます","note":"Go somewhere to do something"},{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"V (bỏ ます) + に","note":"E.g.: 会います → 会いに, 勉強します → 勉強しに"},{"lhs":"V di chuyển","rhs":"行きます・来ます・帰ります・戻ります","note":"Final verb"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"迎え","r":"むかえ","m":"pick up"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the station to pick up a friend.","scene":"Replying to a friend's message while on the way."},{"tokens":[{"t":"図書館","r":"としょかん","m":"library"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"返し","r":"かえし","m":"return"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the library to return a book.","scene":"Telling your family before going out."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-055||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **ask and answer about the purpose of movement**. In daily conversation, Japanese speakers often use it when inviting someone to eat, see a movie, or go shopping.","nuance":"For questions, use どこへ + 何をしに + 行きますか. In answers, the destination can be omitted when it is already known: 映画を見に行きます. Use the ます-form with people you should speak to politely; with close friends, 行くの？ is natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見","r":"み","m":"watch"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Are you going to see a movie?","scene":"Inviting a friend to see a movie this weekend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買い","r":"かい","m":"buy"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the convenience store to buy lunch.","scene":"Speaking to a coworker during lunch break."},{"tokens":[{"t":"海","r":"うみ","m":"sea"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"泳ぎ","r":"およぎ","m":"swim"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the beach to swim.","scene":"Telling friends about summer plans."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-055||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with へ・に","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"compare","definition":"Do not confuse this with **NへVます** (direction only) or **NにVます** (destination or location). ～へ～にVます combines both: it states the direction with へ and the purpose with に.","nuance":"デパートへ行きます only tells the listener that you are going to the department store, not why. デパートに買い物に行きます is more complete: destination + purpose. に can replace へ when emphasizing the destination, but へ is especially common with movement verbs.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"デパート","m":"department store"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the department store.","scene":"Comparing it with the full purpose pattern."},{"tokens":[{"t":"デパート","m":"department store"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the department store to shop.","scene":"This sentence states the purpose clearly."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NへVます","point":"へ marks the direction of movement but does not state the purpose. With へ+に, に adds the purpose."},{"vs":"NにVます","point":"に can also mark a destination, but in this pattern it marks purpose when it follows an activity noun or verb stem."}]}},"n5-055||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse **を** (object) with **に** (purpose). To say “go shopping,” use 買い物に (purpose), not 買い物を. Also, the verb after に must be a movement verb.","nuance":"The purpose is an activity noun or a verb stem made from the ます-form. Do not use に with a non-movement final verb. 例: 食べにレストランへ行きます is correct, as is 本を読みに図書館へ行きます; do not say 本を読むに図書館へ行きます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to the supermarket to shop.","scene":"Correct pattern."}],"pitfalls":["❌ スーパーへ買い物を行きます。 → ✅ スーパーへ買い物に行きます。 (use に for purpose)","❌ 映画を見るに来ました。 → ✅ 映画を見に来ました。 (remove ます to make 見に; do not use the dictionary form)","❌ 図書館へ本を借りるに来ました。 → ✅ 図書館へ本を借りに来ました。 (use the ます-stem, not the dictionary form 借りる)","❌ 図書館へ本を借りに勉強します。 → ✅ 図書館へ本を借りに行きます。 (the final verb must be a movement verb such as 行く/来る/帰る)","❌ スーパーへ買い物行きます。 → ✅ スーパーへ買い物に行きます。 (missing に for the purpose of movement)"],"compare":null}},"n5-055||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **へ** as an arrow showing the direction of movement and **に** as a marker for the goal to be reached. When both appear, へ sets the direction and に attaches the purpose to the activity.","nuance":"Quick memory aid: “へ = direction; に = purpose.” Common movement verbs are 行く, 来る, and 帰る. To say “go eat,” use 食べに行く—just remove ます from the ます-form.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-055||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NへNにVます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NへNにVます","patternReading":"え に ます","coreMeaning":"Go to N to do N (purpose of movement)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear very often in conversation and on the JLPT N5. Memorizing them will help you respond quickly.","nuance":"The phrases ～に行く/来る/帰る are used as fixed units. With a place, the usual order is: place + へ + purpose phrase + に + movement verb.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"遊び","r":"あそび","m":"play"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please come over to my house.","scene":"Inviting a friend over."},{"tokens":[{"t":"レストラン","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am going to a restaurant to eat.","scene":"Inviting a friend to dinner."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-056||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **NがVたいです** expresses the speaker's desire to perform an action involving N. The special point is that **が** marks the object instead of を when the speaker wants to emphasize it, or when answering a question such as 'What do you want to do?'. This is natural and very common in everyday conversation.","nuance":"It works in both spoken and written Japanese and is moderately polite. が makes the object the **focus** of the sentence, showing a clear desire. Compared with を, が makes an answer sound more definite and natural when responding to '何が〜'. In negative sentences, it often changes to は (お茶は飲みたくない).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本料理","r":"にほんりょうり","m":"Japanese food"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べたい","r":"たべたい","m":"want to eat"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to eat Japanese food.","scene":"Hungry and thinking about sushi and Japanese dishes"},{"tokens":[{"t":"冷たい","r":"つめたい","m":"cold"},{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲みたい","r":"のみたい","m":"want to drink"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to drink cold water.","scene":"Thirsty after coming back from a run"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-056||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"forms","definition":"Vたい is formed by removing **ます** from the ます-form and adding **たい**. It conjugates exactly like an い-adjective. Combine it with a noun using the particle **が**.","nuance":"Negative and past forms use the い-adjective endings. In negative sentences, が often changes to **は** to create contrast, though が is still possible in speech.","structure":[{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vます (bỏ ます) + たいです","note":"Present affirmative"},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vます (bỏ ます) + たくないです","note":"Present negative"},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vます (bỏ ます) + たかったです","note":"Past"},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vます (bỏ ます) + たくなかったです","note":"Past negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見たかった","r":"みたかった","m":"wanted to watch"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I wanted to watch a movie.","scene":"Explaining why you did not go out yesterday"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"水","r":"みず","m":"water"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲みたくない","r":"のみたくない","m":"do not want to drink"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not want to drink water now.","scene":"Full after a meal and offered water"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-056||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker wants to do something with a specific object. が appears when the object is **new information and the focus** of the sentence. In a direct answer to 何が〜たい, using が is almost required.","nuance":"Do not use が with actions that have no object, such as 走る or 寝る. With する, the form したい often appears with 何が. が can be replaced by を in this pattern, but the emphasis on the object is lost.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お寿司","r":"おすし","m":"sushi"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"食べたい","r":"たべたい","m":"want to eat"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to eat sushi.","scene":"Walking past a sushi shop"},{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲みたい","r":"のみたい","m":"want to drink"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you want to drink?","scene":"Hosting a guest at home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"したい","m":"want to do"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What do you want to do this weekend?","scene":"Asking a friend about weekend plans"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-056||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguishing **NがVたい** from easily confused patterns helps you choose the right particle and nuance.","nuance":"The difference is what gets **emphasized**: the action or the object, wanting to do something or wanting to have something.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"飲みたい","r":"のみたい","m":"want to drink"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to drink coffee.","scene":"Sleepy and needing coffee"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NをVたいです","point":"Both express 'want to do something'. を is neutral and common in speech. が **emphasizes the object**, especially in questions and answers. Example: 水を飲みたいです (in general) vs 水が飲みたいです (I specifically crave water)."},{"vs":"Nが欲しいです","point":"Vたい means **want to do an action** and uses a verb, while 欲しい means **want to have something** and uses a noun. Example: パンが食べたい (want to eat bread) / パンが欲しい (want bread, not necessarily to eat it)."}]}},"n5-056||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with Vたい involve **particles** and choosing the correct pattern.","nuance":"Remember: が acts like a 'spotlight' on the object and appears with special predicates such as たい, 欲しい, 好き, わかる, 上手, and 下手.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"勉強したい","r":"べんきょうしたい","m":"want to study"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to study Japanese.","scene":"Telling a friend about your plans"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 田中さんはラーメンが食べたいです。 → ✅ 田中さんはラーメンを食べたがっています。 (たい is used only for the speaker's or first-person desire)","❌ 私はご飯が食べます。 → ✅ 私はご飯を食べます。 (が is used with たい/欲しい/好き/わかる, not regular verbs)","❌ 私はケーキを欲しいです。 → ✅ 私はケーキが欲しいです。 (欲しい always takes が)"],"compare":null}},"n5-056||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember が with Vたい, imagine a **finger pointing at the item** you crave. が is that finger.","nuance":"In Vietnamese, たい sounds close to **thèm**, meaning 'crave' or 'want'. Combine it with が, the pointing finger, to create a memorable story.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-056||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NがVたいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVたいです","patternReading":"が たいです","coreMeaning":"Want to do ~ N (が marks the object for emphasis)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are **very common** fixed phrases in conversation and on the JLPT N5.","nuance":"Memorizing these phrases helps you respond quickly when expressing a desire.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"見たい","r":"みたい","m":"want to watch"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to watch a movie.","scene":"Free on the weekend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-057||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"core","definition":"〜てください is a polite, gentle way to ask someone to do something. Form: the verb's te-form + ください. Japanese speakers use it daily in classrooms, offices, and shops, from 'please wait a moment' to 'please write your name here.' Unlike the harsh command 〜なさい, てください sounds like an invitation or request, though it is still a request.","nuance":"This is moderately polite: politer than the direct command 〜て, but less gentle than 〜てもらえますか or 〜ていただけませんか. Superiors use it with subordinates, teachers with students, and acquaintances with one another. With strangers or customers, speakers often choose a more polite question because てください can sound slightly imposing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"待ってください","r":"まってください","m":"please wait"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait a moment.","scene":"A cashier asks a customer to wait"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いてください","r":"かいてください","m":"please write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write your name here.","scene":"A receptionist gives a customer a form"},{"tokens":[{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開いてください","r":"ひらいてください","m":"please open"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please open your book.","scene":"A teacher speaks to students"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-057||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"forms","definition":"Conjugate the verb into the te-form, then add ください. Group 1 (u-verbs) changes according to the final sound: 書く→書いて, 聞く→聞いて, 飲む→飲んで, 買う→買って, 話す→話して. Group 2 drops る and adds て: 食べる→食べて. Group 3: 来る→来て, する→して. The negative uses Vない + でください, meaning 'please don't.'","nuance":"In conversation, speakers may omit ください for a casual tone, but the full form still requires て. In Vてください, the focus is on the action to perform, not on a consequence as in Vてはいけません. Writing ください as 下さい is also correct, but hiragana is preferred when it functions as an auxiliary request expression.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（て形）","rhs":"ください","note":"Example: 待ってください、書いてください。"},{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"でください","note":"Negative: 吸わないでください、撮らないでください。"},{"lhs":"V group 2（drop ます）","rhs":"+ てください","note":"食べます→食べてください、見ます→見てください。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"待ってください","r":"まってください","m":"please wait"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait a moment.","scene":"Asking someone to wait on the phone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いてください","r":"かいてください","m":"please write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write your name here.","scene":"A clerk reminds a customer to fill it in"},{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮らないでください","r":"とらないでください","m":"please don't take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't take photos.","scene":"A museum guide reminds visitors"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → て形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — う · つ · る → って","Remove the final sound and add って","買う（かう） → `買って（かって）` (buy) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待って（まって）` (wait) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰って（かえって）` (return) ・ 会う（あう） → `会って（あって）` (meet)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — く → いて","Remove く and add いて","書く（かく） → `書いて（かいて）` (write) ・ 聞く（きく） → `聞いて（きいて）` (listen) ・ 歩く（あるく） → `歩いて（あるいて）` (walk) ・ 働く（はたらく） → `働いて（はたらいて）` (work)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — ぐ → いで","Remove ぐ and add いで (with ゛)","泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳いで（およいで）` (swim) ・ 急ぐ（いそぐ） → `急いで（いそいで）` (hurry) ・ 脱ぐ（ぬぐ） → `脱いで（ぬいで）` (take off)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — す → して","Remove す and add して","話す（はなす） → `話して（はなして）` (speak) ・ 貸す（かす） → `貸して（かして）` (lend) ・ 押す（おす） → `押して（おして）` (press)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — ぬ · ぶ · む → んで","Remove the final sound and add んで","死ぬ（しぬ） → `死んで（しんで）` (die) ・ 遊ぶ（あそぶ） → `遊んで（あそんで）` (play) ・ 飲む（のむ） → `飲んで（のんで）` (drink) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読んで（よんで）` (read)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — ichidan: remove る + て","Remove る and add て","食べる（たべる） → `食べて（たべて）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見て（みて）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きて（おきて）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝て（ねて）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — Irregular","Memorize — follows no rule","する → `して` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来て（きて）` (come) ・ 行く（いく） → `行って（いって）` (go)"]]}}},"n5-057||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜てください is used for requests, instructions, directions, and suggestions in many settings: classrooms (open your books, listen), offices (send an email, write a report), public places (keep quiet, do not smoke), and directions. It also appears in notices and user instructions with the meaning 'please.'","nuance":"This neutral pattern works with subordinates and peers; with a superior, it can sound too casual unless you are close. In a restaurant, a customer saying すみません、水を持ってきてください is natural. On signs, 〜てください is gentler than 〜なさい and clearer than 〜てはだめ.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"まっすぐ","r":"まっすぐ","m":"straight"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"右","r":"みぎ","m":"right"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"曲がってください","r":"まがってください","m":"please turn"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Go straight, then turn right.","scene":"Giving directions to a tourist"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住所","r":"じゅうしょ","m":"address"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いてください","r":"かいてください","m":"please write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please write your address here.","scene":"A bank clerk guides a customer"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"閉めてください","r":"しめてください","m":"please close"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please close the door.","scene":"A teacher asks a student near the door"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-057||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish てください from these easily confused N5 patterns: Vましょうか, Vてはいけません, and Vたいです. The key points are who performs the action and how obligatory the expression is.","nuance":"てください = the listener acts; it is a gentle request. Vましょうか = the speaker offers to act or suggests acting together. Vてはいけません = an absolute prohibition. Vたい = the speaker's desire. For greater politeness with a superior, use Vてもらえますか instead of Vてください.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"待ってください","r":"まってください","m":"please wait"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait here.","scene":"Asking someone to stay here"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vましょうか","point":"Vましょうか offers for the SPEAKER to act or suggests acting together; てください asks the LISTENER to act. Example: 手伝いましょうか (Shall I help?) vs 手伝ってください (Please help me)."},{"vs":"Vてはいけません","point":"Vてはいけません = PROHIBITED, must not; てください = REQUEST, please do. ここで泳いではいけません (You must not swim here) vs ここで待ってください (Please wait here)."},{"vs":"Vたいです","point":"Vたい = want to do (the speaker); てください = ask the listener to do it. 水を飲みたい (I want to drink water) vs 水を飲んでください (Please drink the water)."}]}},"n5-057||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often add ます before ください or forget で in the negative. Remember: before ください, use the te-form or ない + で, never the ます-form. Also, avoid using Vてください too casually with a superior; choose a more polite expression.","nuance":"The error Vませんください is common because of confusion with Vましょう or English phrasing. Remember: ください does not stand alone here; it needs a te-form or naide-form before it.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しないでください","r":"しないでください","m":"please don't call"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please do not make phone calls on the train.","scene":"A train notice reminding passengers"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ちょっと待ちますください → ✅ ちょっと待ってください","❌ 写真を撮りませんください → ✅ 写真を撮らないでください","❌ ここに名前を書くください → ✅ ここに名前を書いてください"],"compare":null}},"n5-057||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine て as an open hand, and ください (下さい) as meaning 'please give.' Together, picture lowering your open hand to offer a request to the other person. When you see ください, ask yourself: 'Is the verb before it already in the te-form?' For negatives, ない + で is like a barrier saying 'don't do it.'","nuance":"This is a visual mnemonic, not a linguistic rule, but it can help the pattern stick. Learning 〜てください through songs, advertisements, and signs also helps make your response automatic.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-057||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common N5 phrases","sub":"Vてください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてください","patternReading":"てください","coreMeaning":"Please do (polite request)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These high-frequency Vてください phrases appear in Minna no Nihongo, N5 exams, and daily conversation: wait a moment, write your name, take a photo, close the door, and say it again. Memorize them for faster listening and speaking.","nuance":"These phrases are used in everyday polite situations. When asking a stranger, adding すみません at the beginning sounds more natural, for example: すみません、ちょっと待ってください.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"待ってください","r":"まってください","m":"please wait"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please wait a moment.","scene":"A familiar phrase when asking someone to wait"},{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮ってください","r":"とってください","m":"please take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please take a photo for me.","scene":"Asking someone nearby to take a picture"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","r":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"閉めてください","r":"しめてください","m":"please close"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please close the door.","scene":"Asking someone to close the door"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-058||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"core","definition":"Vています describes an **action in progress** at the moment the speaker mentions it. Imagine filming the action halfway through: eating, studying, or playing.","nuance":"This is very common in daily speech and writing. The politeness is in the ます ending. In casual conversation, Japanese speakers may shorten it to 〜てる, but use the full 〜ています in tests and writing.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べています","r":"たべています","m":"am eating"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am eating now.","scene":"Mother calls and asks what I am doing"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子どもたち","r":"こどもたち","m":"children"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"遊んでいます","r":"あそんでいます","m":"are playing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The children are playing in the park.","scene":"Seeing the children playing from a balcony"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-058||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"forms","definition":"To form Vています, first create the **て-form** from the ます-form: remove ます and change the ending according to the rules. Then add **います**. Example: 食べます → 食べて → 食べています.","nuance":"Negation, past, and past negation change the います part: ていません, ていました, ていませんでした. At N5, remember all four forms.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます","rhs":"Vて + います","note":"Remove ます, change to て-form, then add います."},{"lhs":"買います","rhs":"買っています","note":"Group 1: い → って."},{"lhs":"遊びます","rhs":"遊んでいます","note":"Group 1: び → んで."},{"lhs":"書きます","rhs":"書いています","note":"Group 1: き → いて."},{"lhs":"します／来ます","rhs":"しています／来ています","note":"Irregular verbs."},{"lhs":"Vています","rhs":"Vていません / Vていました / Vていませんでした","note":"Negation, past, and past negation."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強しています","r":"べんきょうしています","m":"am studying"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am studying Japanese.","scene":"Replying to a friend by text"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いています","r":"かいています","m":"is writing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is writing a letter.","scene":"Seeing a roommate writing a letter"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-058||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use Vています when asking or answering what someone is **doing right now**. The most common conversational question is **今、何をしていますか**.","nuance":"何をしていますか is polite and suitable for elders, colleagues, and customers. With close friends, Japanese speakers often shorten it to 何してる？. In tests, write the full しています.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もしもし","m":"hello"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しています","r":"しています","m":"are doing"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hello, what are you doing now?","scene":"Calling a friend on Saturday afternoon"},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"台所","r":"だいどころ","m":"kitchen"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しています","r":"しています","m":"is doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mother is cooking in the kitchen.","scene":"Seeing mother busy in the kitchen"},{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"待っています","r":"まっています","m":"am waiting"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am waiting for the train now.","scene":"Answering the phone at the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-058||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Vます vs state Vています","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"compare","definition":"Progressive Vています is easily confused with **Vます** and **state Vています**. The key point: use Vています for an action happening now; use Vます for schedules and habits; state Vています also uses the same form but means 'has already...'.","nuance":"Ask yourself: 'Can I see or film this action right now?' If yes, use progressive Vています. If it is only general information, use Vます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"走っています","r":"はしっています","m":"am running"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am running now.","scene":"A friend calls while I am jogging"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vます","point":"Vます expresses habits or future schedules; Vています expresses an action happening now. Example: 毎日走ります (I run every day) vs 今走っています (I am running now)."},{"vs":"Vています (trạng thái)","point":"Same form, different meaning: state Vています with change-of-state verbs such as 結婚しています means 'is married', not 'is getting married'. Progressive Vています with ongoing actions such as 食べています means 'is eating'."}]}},"n5-058||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often use **Vます** for every action because they think Vます simply means 'do'. In fact, an action happening now requires **Vています**.","nuance":"This mistake occurs because English and Japanese do not map present-tense meanings in exactly the same way. Check the time: an action happening now → Vています.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読んでいます","r":"よんでいます","m":"am reading"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am reading a book now.","scene":"Friends ask what I am doing in the library"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 今、ご飯を食べます。 → ✅ 今、ご飯を食べています。","❌ 私は今、勉強です。 → ✅ 私は今、勉強しています。"],"compare":null}},"n5-058||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **て** as a camera attached to the verb, and **います** as 'be present'. Together: the action is 'present' right before your eyes → Vています.","nuance":"This trick helps you avoid using Vます for an ongoing action. When you see someone doing something, silently think: 'present now' → Vています.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-058||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"Vています (進行) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (進行)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Currently doing V (an action in progress now)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear often in N5 tests and daily conversation: **今、〜ています**, **何をしていますか**, and **〜ていますか**.","nuance":"In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often shorten 〜ています to 〜てる (食べてる), but use the full 〜ています in tests.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ています","r":"みています","m":"am watching"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am watching TV now.","scene":"Mother asks what I am doing at home in the evening"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しています","r":"しています","m":"am doing"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am doing my homework.","scene":"Texting a friend while studying"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-059||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"core","definition":"〜ましょうか is the question form of 〜ましょう, used to make a suggestion before taking action. It often means “Shall I...?” when the speaker wants to help the listener, or “Shall we...?” when inviting the listener to join. The か turns the sentence into a question, so the listener can agree or refuse.","nuance":"This is a polite ます/です-level pattern, natural with friends, coworkers, and acquaintances. Compared with 〜ましょう without か, 〜ましょうか shows more respect for the listener’s opinion. When offering help, the speaker volunteers; when inviting someone, the feeling is friendly and gentle. It is not used for past or negative meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"手伝い","r":"てつだい","m":"help"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I help you?","scene":"A coworker is carrying many things."},{"tokens":[{"t":"一緒に","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"帰り","r":"かえり","m":"go home"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall we?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we go home together?","scene":"Coworkers invite each other home after work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-059||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"forms","definition":"The formation is regular: **remove ます from Vます + ましょうか**. For example: 行きます → 行きましょうか, 食べます → 食べましょうか, します → しましょうか. All three verb groups only require finding the ます-stem; no further conjugation is needed. The ending ます already makes the sentence polite.","nuance":"It has no past or negative form; it is a suggestion made **at the time of speaking**. It is as polite as 〜ましょう, but the か makes it gentler.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"ましょうか","note":"For example: 行きます→行きましょうか; 食べます→食べましょうか"},{"lhs":"Vます-stem","rhs":"ましょう","note":"Invitation/suggestion without か, e.g. 帰りましょう “Let’s go home”"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開け","r":"あけ","m":"open"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I open the window?","scene":"The meeting room feels stuffy."},{"tokens":[{"t":"タクシー","m":"taxi"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"呼び","r":"よび","m":"call"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I call a taxi?","scene":"A friend seems a little drunk."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-059||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"usage","definition":"〜ましょうか has two main uses: **offering to help** and **inviting someone to do something together**. In English, both can sometimes be translated as “Shall we...?” or “Shall I...?”, but context shows whether the speaker is helping the listener or joining the action.","nuance":"When offering help, the speaker notices a specific need, such as heavy luggage, confusion about directions, or needing a taxi. When inviting someone, it often appears with 一緒に or shared activities such as 帰る、食べる、休む. It works at work, with family, and among friends. With superiors, お＋Vます-stem＋しましょうか is more polite, but it is beyond basic N5.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"rest"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall we?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we take a short break?","scene":"The group looks tired during a long hike."},{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮り","r":"とり","m":"take"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I take a photo for you?","scene":"A friend is trying to take a selfie."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-059||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Quick comparison","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch helps distinguish 〜ましょうか from three related patterns: **Vましょう**, **Vませんか**, and **Vてください**. All can express an invitation or request, but the roles of the speaker and listener differ.","nuance":"Vましょうか is a suggestion that asks for agreement; it can mean offering help or doing something together. Vませんか is more of an invitation, leaving the decision to the listener. Vてください is a request or command for the listener to act.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮り","r":"とり","m":"take"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I take a photo for you?","scene":"A friend is standing before a beautiful view."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vましょう","point":"Vましょう gives an immediate suggestion without asking again; Vましょうか has か and asks first: 帰りましょう “Let’s go home” ↔ 帰りましょうか “Shall we go home?”"},{"vs":"Vませんか","point":"Vませんか means “Would you like to ~?”, leaving the decision to the listener; Vましょうか means “Shall I ~/Shall we ~?”, with the speaker proposing a plan."},{"vs":"Vてください","point":"Vてください asks or commands the listener to act; Vましょうか means the speaker volunteers or invites the listener to act together."}]}},"n5-059||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners commonly make three mistakes: **keeping ます before ましょうか**, **forgetting か when asking for an opinion**, and **mistaking Vましょうか for a command**. Remember: Vましょうか is always a gentle question, not a command.","nuance":"The first mistake comes from thinking “Vます + ましょうか”; actually, ます must be removed. Forgetting か removes the question and makes the sentence sound self-decided. With a boss or older person, お＋Vます-stem＋しましょうか can be more polite, but correct ましょうか is enough for N5.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ち","r":"もち","m":"carry"},{"t":"ましょうか","m":"shall I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I carry your luggage?","scene":"Speaking to a friend carrying a very heavy suitcase."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 手伝いますましょうか。 → ✅ 手伝いましょうか。","❌ タクシーを呼びましょう。 (when asking whether the listener agrees) → ✅ タクシーを呼びましょうか。","❌ Using 窓を開けましょうか to ask the listener to open the window → ✅ 窓を開けてください。"],"compare":null}},"n5-059||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember ましょうか with this image: **ましょう** is like “let’s” in English, while **か** is a question hook added at the end. When you see someone who needs help, add the “question hook” to your suggestion and ask permission.","nuance":"You do not need to memorize a complicated rule; hearing the sound “mashō-ka” can remind you: “Shall I mashō for you?”—that is, volunteering to help. The same sound can invite someone to join, depending on the context.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-059||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vましょうか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vましょうか","patternReading":"ましょうか","coreMeaning":"Suggestion: “Shall I...?” or invitation: “Shall we...?”","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases appear very often in textbooks and everyday conversation. You can learn them as ready-made chunks without analyzing every word.","nuance":"These phrases usually have a friendly, rising question intonation. When asked with ましょうか, natural replies include **はい、そうしましょう** or **いいえ、大丈夫です**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう一度","r":"もういちど","m":"one more time"},{"t":"しましょうか","m":"shall we?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we do it one more time?","scene":"After a failed game, inviting another attempt."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-060||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen)","patternReading":"で ています","coreMeaning":"Do V at N (job/habit) – an ongoing state","slug":"core","definition":"〜でVています describes an action maintained as a **job** (working somewhere) or a stable **habit**. Unlike Vています meaning an action in progress (doing it right now), this pattern emphasizes a **lasting state**. Examples: 銀行で働いています = 'I work at a bank' (job), 毎朝ジョギングをしています = 'I jog every morning' (habit).","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese. This is the polite form, suitable for introducing yourself, speaking with someone you just met, or talking to a superior. The nuance is **objective and descriptive**—it describes something ongoing in your life, not something temporary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"銀行","r":"ぎんこう","m":"bank"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いています","r":"はたらいています","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I work at a bank.","scene":"Answering: What do you do?"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"走っています","r":"はしっています","m":"jog"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I jog in the park every morning.","scene":"Talking about a daily exercise habit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-060||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen)","patternReading":"で ています","coreMeaning":"Do V at N (job/habit) – an ongoing state","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **N（place/organization）+ で + Vて + います**. で marks the place where the action occurs. **Vて** is the verb's te-form, and **います** is a polite auxiliary verb that changes for tense and negation.","nuance":"This is the polite form, used in most communication settings. In casual speech, Japanese speakers often shorten it to **Vてます** or **Vてる**. For questions, simply add か at the end while keeping the same word order.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（場所・組織）","rhs":"で","note":"N is the workplace or place where the habit occurs"},{"lhs":"V（て形）","rhs":"います","note":"Change V to the te-form, then connect it to います"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いません","note":"Present negative: not in that state"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いました","note":"Past: was in that state"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"勉強しています","r":"べんきょうしています","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study here every day.","scene":"Talking about a familiar study place"},{"tokens":[{"t":"去年","r":"きょねん","m":"last year"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"大阪","r":"おおさか","m":"Osaka"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いていました","r":"はたらいていました","m":"worked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I worked in Osaka until last year.","scene":"Talking about a former job"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-060||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen)","patternReading":"で ています","coreMeaning":"Do V at N (job/habit) – an ongoing state","slug":"usage","definition":"Vています has **three main meanings**, depending on the verb and context. With **action verbs** (働く, 勉強する, 走る), NでVています describes a **job** or **ongoing habit**. With **change-of-state verbs** (結婚する, 住む, 知る), it describes the **result** of that change.","nuance":"Use **time adverbs** and **context** to identify the meaning. If 今 appears with a specific action, it means an action in progress. If 毎日, よく, or いつも appears, it indicates a habit. If the topic is a workplace, it indicates a job. In a job interview, people often ask: どこで働いていますか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"銀行","r":"ぎんこう","m":"bank"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いています","r":"はたらいています","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My father works at a bank.","scene":"Introducing your father's job"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んでいます","r":"のんでいます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drink coffee every day.","scene":"Talking about a daily habit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-060||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing Vています","sub":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen)","patternReading":"で ています","coreMeaning":"Do V at N (job/habit) – an ongoing state","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare **NでVています** with forms that look the same but have different meanings. This pattern differs from **Vています (進行)** because it describes a **job or habit**, not an action happening at the moment of speaking. Compared with **Vます** (present/future), Vています emphasizes a **maintained state**.","nuance":"When you say 銀行で働いています, the listener understands it as a long-term job. 銀行で働きます sounds like a statement that you will work there (future). With 今, 今走っています means running now, while 毎朝走っています means jogging every morning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今","r":"いま","m":"now"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いています","r":"はたらいています","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I currently work at a school.","scene":"Emphasizing your current job, not a temporary action"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vています (進行)","point":"Doing it now vs a job/habit. 今、走っています = running now, while 毎朝走っています = jogging every morning."},{"vs":"Vます","point":"Vます simply states an action (present/future) without emphasizing a state; Vています emphasizes an ongoing state. 学校で教えます = will teach at school; 学校で教えています = work as a teacher."}]}},"n5-060||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen)","patternReading":"で ています","coreMeaning":"Do V at N (job/habit) – an ongoing state","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often **use に** with action verbs such as working or studying because they think 'where.' But **actions require で for the location**. Verbs of existence or residence, such as 住む, use に instead. Also, Vます can politely express a habit, but it does not emphasize a stable habit as clearly as Vています.","nuance":"Using に with 働く is a common error. Distinguish **場所 + で + action** (where an action occurs) from **場所 + に + existence** (where someone exists or lives). Since 働く is an action, it requires で.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病院","r":"びょういん","m":"hospital"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働いています","r":"はたらいています","m":"work"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I work at a hospital.","scene":"Answering 'Where do you work?'—use で"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 銀行に働いています。 → ✅ 銀行で働いています。","❌ 病院に働いています。 → ✅ 病院で働いています。","❌ 毎朝公園に走っています。 → ✅ 毎朝公園で走っています。"],"compare":null}},"n5-060||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NでVています (nghề nghiệp/thói quen)","patternReading":"で ています","coreMeaning":"Do V at N (job/habit) – an ongoing state","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: split the phrase into **Nで** = 'at a place,' **Vて** = 'having done the action,' and **います** = 'exist/be present.' Together: 'I exist in the state of doing that job.'","nuance":"Quick reminder: a job is a lasting state, not a temporary action. When 毎日, よく, or いつも appears with Vています, think of a habit.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-061||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"core","definition":"**Vてもいいですか** asks the listener for permission to do something. It means: “Is it okay if I do ~?” This is the most common polite way to ask permission in Japanese.","nuance":"Neutral and polite; usable with friends, coworkers, and older people. **〜よろしいですか** is more formal. The speaker expects agreement, so the tone is often gentle and slightly hesitant. Do not use it to ask about ability; use **Vることができます** for that.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I take a photo here?","scene":"Asking a teacher before photographing the board"},{"tokens":[{"t":"トイレ","r":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使って","r":"つかって","m":"use"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I use the restroom?","scene":"Asking a host during a visit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-061||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"forms","definition":"Main formula: **Vて + も + いい + ですか**. Put the verb in the **て-form**, then attach **もいいですか**. A more polite form is **Vてもよろしいですか**. In casual conversation, use **Vてもいい？**.","nuance":"The permission question itself does not change for past or future time. To ask about permission for something already done, use a different pattern. **Vてもよろしいですか** is used with superiors, customers, and in business. **Vてもいい？** is for close friends and family.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もいいですか","note":"Standard permission question"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もよろしいですか","note":"Formal and highly polite"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"もいい？","note":"Casual, rising intonation"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"エアコン","r":"エアコン","m":"air conditioner"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つけて","r":"つけて","m":"turn on"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I turn on the air conditioner?","scene":"Asking a roommate first"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ちょっと","r":"ちょっと","m":"a little"},{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"帰って","r":"かえって","m":"go home"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I leave a little early?","scene":"An employee asking a supervisor"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-061||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when the speaker wants to do something and needs the listener’s permission. It commonly appears in classrooms, offices, homes, and restaurants: entering a room, opening a window, using something, taking leave, or taking photos.","nuance":"For extra politeness, Japanese speakers often use **〜てもよろしいですか** or **〜させていただいてもよろしいでしょうか**. **Vてもいいですか** is still natural and polite in the right context. For important requests to a supervisor, use a more humble form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","r":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"座って","r":"すわって","m":"sit"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I sit in this seat?","scene":"Asking a stranger at a cinema"},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"質問","r":"しつもん","m":"question"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しても","r":"しても","m":"ask"},{"t":"いいですか","m":"may I?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Teacher, may I ask a question?","scene":"A student raising a hand"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-061||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare: 〜てはいけません・Vましょうか・Vることができます","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick comparison of three easily confused patterns: **Vてもいいですか** asks permission; **Vてはいけません** prohibits an action; **Vましょうか** offers to do something together or for someone; **Vることができます** expresses ability.","nuance":"Vてもいいですか asks the listener to allow the speaker to act. Vましょうか proposes an action for both people or an action the speaker will do for the listener. Vることができます expresses ability, not permission.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"May I eat here?","scene":"Asking park staff about eating lunch"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てはいけません","point":"Vてもいいですか asks whether an action is allowed. Vてはいけません says it is not allowed. Same action: 吸ってもいいですか → 吸ってはいけません."},{"vs":"〜ましょうか","point":"Vましょうか offers to help or do something together; it does not ask permission. 手伝いましょうか = “Shall I help?”, while 手伝ってもいいですか = “May I help?”."},{"vs":"〜ことができます","point":"Vることができます expresses ability. 泳ぐことができます = “I can swim.” 泳いでもいいですか = “May I swim?”."}]}},"n5-061||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Three common mistakes: (1) attaching **もいいですか** to Vます instead of Vて; (2) using it to ask about ability—use **Vることができます**; (3) replying “はい、いいです” to agree, which sounds unnatural.","nuance":"Common replies are **はい、どうぞ** (“yes, go ahead”) or **ええ、いいですよ**. **はい、いいです** can sound cold and is uncommon for permission requests. To refuse, say **いいえ、〜てはいけません**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"トイレ","r":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Teacher, may I go to the restroom?","scene":"A student asking during class"}],"pitfalls":["❌ トイレを使いますもいいですか。 → ✅ トイレを使ってもいいですか。","❌ 日本語を話せてもいいですか。 → ✅ 日本語を話すことができますか。","❌ はい、いいです。（when replying to a permission request） → ✅ はい、どうぞ。"],"compare":null}},"n5-061||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **Vて** is the linking form, **も** = also, and **いい** = good/okay. Together: “Is it also okay if I do ~?” → asking permission. In longer words: “If I do ~, is that okay?”","nuance":"Picture yourself raising your hand in class: your raised hand is **Vても**, and the teacher’s nod is **いいですか**.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-061||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"Vてもいいですか · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてもいいですか","patternReading":"てもいいですか","coreMeaning":"Asking permission: May I ~?","slug":"phrases","definition":"Very common permission phrases include **写真を撮ってもいいですか** (May I take a photo?), **帰ってもいいですか** (May I leave?), **ここに座ってもいいですか** (May I sit here?), and **電話を使ってもいいですか** (May I use the phone?). Memorizing them improves response speed.","nuance":"In restaurants, hotels, and other public places, add **すみません** at the beginning when addressing a stranger politely: “すみません、写真を撮ってもいいですか”.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","r":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"もいいですか","m":"is it okay?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Excuse me, may I take a photo?","scene":"A tourist asking a local at a market"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-062||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"core","definition":"～てはいけません expresses a **prohibition: something must not be done**. It is rule-based language, often used by an authority such as a teacher, parent, company, or government. The speaker conveys a rule, not a personal feeling.","nuance":"The tone is **rigid and impersonal**, with little emotion. In casual speech, Japanese speakers often replace ～てはいけません with ～てはだめ or ～ちゃだめ. ～てはいけません can also be used as a personal reminder.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"タバコ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸って","r":"すって","m":"smoke"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not smoke here.","scene":"A staff member points out a no-smoking sign in a hospital."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んで","r":"のんで","m":"drink"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Children must not drink alcohol.","scene":"A shop sign or a reminder at home."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-062||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **verb te-form + はいけません**. For example, 飲む → 飲んではいけません. Here, は is read **wa** because it is a particle. In casual conversation, speakers use **〜てはだめ** or shorten it to **〜ちゃだめ**.","nuance":"いけません is polite and is used for rules. いけない is often used for oneself or with close people. だめ sounds more casual and advisory.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"はいけません","note":"Standard, polite form"},{"lhs":"Vては","rhs":"だめです","note":"More casual, less rigid"},{"lhs":"Vては","rhs":"いけない","note":"Used with close people"},{"lhs":"Vちゃ / じゃ","rhs":"だめ","note":"Colloquial contraction"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入って","r":"はいって","m":"enter"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not enter here.","scene":"A no-entry sign stands outside the room."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not eat.","scene":"A doctor instructs a patient before a test."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-062||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern frequently appears in **school, company, hospital, public-place rules**, and in reminders from someone senior to someone junior. The speaker has the status or responsibility to communicate the rule.","nuance":"Do not use it with an older person or superior unless you have the authority to set the rule. For a gentler request, use Vないでください. For self-talk: お酒を飲みすぎてはいけない。","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"泳いで","r":"およいで","m":"swim"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not swim here.","scene":"A no-swimming sign stands by the river."},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"忘れて","r":"わすれて","m":"forget"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not forget your homework.","scene":"A teacher reminds the class before dismissal."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-062||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Key contrasts","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most easily confused with 〜てはいけません are 〜てもいいです (permission), 〜ないでください (a request not to do something), and 〜てはだめ (casual prohibition).","nuance":"Keep the distinctions clear: Vてはいけません = prohibited by a rule; Vてもいいですか asks for permission, and a negative reply may use てはいけません; Vないでください is a gentle request, not a rule.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not take photos.","scene":"A museum has a no-photography sign."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てもいいです","point":"Complete opposites: てはいけません = PROHIBITED, while てもいい = ALLOWED. Example: ここでタバコを吸ってはいけません。 vs ここでタバコを吸ってもいいです。"},{"vs":"〜ないでください","point":"てはいけません imposes a rule, while ないでください is a polite request. Example: ここに入ってはいけません。 vs ここに入らないでください。"},{"vs":"〜てはだめ","point":"てはだめ is casual, used with family or friends; てはいけません is formal and used for rules."}]}},"n5-062||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes include incorrect te-form conjugation and misreading は as 'ha'. Watch for sound changes: 飲む→飲んで, 泳ぐ→泳いで, 行く→行って.","nuance":"These are basic beginner errors. With Group 2 verbs, simply drop る and add て (食べる→食べて). With Group 3: する→して, 来る→きて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入って","r":"はいって","m":"enter"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not enter here.","scene":"A sign prohibits entry to a dangerous area."}],"pitfalls":["❌ タバコを吸てはいけません。 → ✅ タバコを吸ってはいけません。","❌ ここに入らはいけません。 → ✅ ここに入ってはいけません。","❌ は read as 'ha' → ✅ read as 'wa' in てはいけません."],"compare":null}},"n5-062||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: imagine **て** as a hand reaching toward something, but **いけません** means 'not okay' or 'cannot proceed'. Together, they express **forbidden to touch or do**.","nuance":"You can picture て as a hand and はいけません as 'not allowed', like a prohibition sign with a hand held out to block you.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-062||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vてはいけません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてはいけません","patternReading":"てはいけません","coreMeaning":"Prohibition: must not do (by rule)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in rules and signs include **入ってはいけません** (must not enter), **吸ってはいけません** (must not smoke), and **食べてはいけません** (must not eat).","nuance":"Signs often use shorter forms: 入るな, 吸うな, but polite written language uses てはいけません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"食べて","r":"たべて","m":"eat"},{"t":"はいけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must not eat here.","scene":"A library sign prohibits eating and drinking."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-063||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"core","definition":"This is the **second** meaning of Vています, different from 'be doing'. It describes a **continuing state** after an action has **finished**. For example, when you see an open window, say '窓が開いています' — this does not mean someone is opening it; the window was opened and **is still open**.","nuance":"Moderately formal, used in both speech and writing. The speaker gives an **observable comment**. Compared with '〜てあります' (N4), 'Vています' simply states the condition and does not stress who caused it; it is used with intransitive verbs.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The window is open (it was opened and is still open).","scene":"Seeing an open window in the classroom"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"結婚して","r":"けっこんして","m":"married"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tanaka is married (he got married and still has a family).","scene":"Introducing a married coworker"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-063||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **Vて-form + います**. With the state meaning, it is usually used with **intransitive verbs** (自動詞): 開く, 閉まる, つく, 消える, 入る, 結婚する, 知る... The negative uses **Vていません**, and the past uses **Vていました**.","nuance":"In casual conversation, Japanese speakers often shorten it to **〜てる**: 開いてる, 結婚してる. The polite form still uses います.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"います","note":"present: the state still remains"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いません","note":"negative: the state does not exist"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"いました","note":"past: the state existed"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついて","m":"on"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light is on.","scene":"Entering a room and seeing the light on"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ついて","m":"on"},{"t":"いません","m":"not in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The light is not on.","scene":"Looking up and seeing the light off"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-063||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vています (state) appears in **three main situations**: the state of objects (an open window, a light on, a TV off), people's conditions (being married, knowing something, being fat), and local phenomena (a congested road, a crowded market). The shared idea is: **the action is finished, and its result is still visible or evident**.","nuance":"Do not use it for an action happening right now (that is meaning 1). If you say '走っています', it means 'is running', not a resulting state.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"道","r":"みち","m":"road"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"込んで","r":"こんで","m":"crowded/congested"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The road is congested.","scene":"Running late and seeing heavy traffic"},{"tokens":[{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まって","r":"とまって","m":"stopped/parked"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The car is parked.","scene":"Pointing out a car parked outside the office"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-063||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Comparisons","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"compare","definition":"These comparisons help prevent common mix-ups.\n\nCompared with **Vています (progressive)**: the state meaning uses **change/result verbs** such as 開く, つく, and 結婚する, while the progressive uses **activity verbs** such as 読む, 書く, and 走る.\n\nCompared with **Vました**: Vました simply reports that something happened and says nothing about the present; Vています stresses that the result still remains.\n\nCompared with **Nがあります**: あります states existence; Vています describes an object's state resulting from an action.","nuance":"Quick test: if the verb answers 'What is someone doing?' → progressive; if it answers 'What state is it in?' → state.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"窓","r":"まど","m":"window"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開いて","r":"あいて","m":"open"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The window is open (a state).","scene":"Contrasting with 'opening the window' (開けています)"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vています (進行)","point":"If the verb is an action (読む/書く/走る) → 'be doing'; if it is a change verb (開く/つく/結婚する) → 'done, and still remains'. 窓が開いています = the window is open (not opening)."},{"vs":"Vました","point":"Vました only says the event is finished and does not comment on now. 結婚しました = got married; 結婚しています = is currently married."},{"vs":"Nがあります","point":"あります only means 'exists/is there'. 車があります = there is a car; 車が止まっています = the car is parked (in a stopped state)."}]}},"n5-063||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate every Vています as 'be doing', which leads to errors. Distinguish **intransitive/transitive** verbs and **state vs action**.","nuance":"For the state meaning, do not use transitive verbs (他動詞), because they express an ongoing action or a different meaning. The mistakes below are very common.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"閉まって","r":"しまって","m":"closed"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"That shop is closed.","scene":"Arriving and finding the shop closed"}],"pitfalls":["❌ ドアが閉めています。 → ✅ ドアが閉まっています。 (閉める is transitive; 閉まる is intransitive.)","❌ 結婚しました。 (when introducing current marital status) → ✅ 結婚しています。","❌ 知っていません。 → ✅ 知りません。 (知る is exceptional and does not use ～ていません.)"],"compare":null}},"n5-063||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual memory trick: imagine a window. Someone **opens it and finishes** (て), then leaves; another person looks in and still sees the **window open** (います). Think of 'て' as **done** and 'います' as **still present** → **finished, but still remaining**.","nuance":"Remember the chant: '**Done + remains = state**'.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-063||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vています (trạng thái) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vています (trạng thái)","patternReading":"ています","coreMeaning":"Being in a state (the result of a completed action)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These Vています (state) phrases are extremely common in N5 and everyday conversation:","nuance":"When you hear '結婚しています', do not understand it as 'is getting married'; it means 'is married and has a family'. '知っています' means 'know', and its negative is '知りません'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"この"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"います","m":"in a state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know this kanji.","scene":"Answering the teacher's question in class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-064||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern describes the **state of knowing** a person, thing, place, or piece of information. In Japanese, 知る (to know) is special: to say 'know' in the present, you **must** use 知っています (te-form + います), not 知ります. It is like receiving information and continuing to hold it in your mind.","nuance":"This is polite and neutral, suitable for most everyday situations. With family or friends, you can say the shorter form 知ってる. In the negative, use 知りません (do not use 知っていません). The certainty is 100%: the speaker confirms having or not having the information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know Mr. Tanaka.","scene":"Introducing yourself in class and saying you know Mr. Tanaka"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know this book?","scene":"At a bookstore, asking whether someone knows the book you are holding"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-064||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **noun + を + 知っています / 知りません**. Because 知る expresses a state, use the ています form in the present. For the past, use 知っていました (knew) / 知りませんでした (did not know). In casual speech, を may be dropped and 知っています shortened to 知ってる.","nuance":"知っています is polite and suitable for older people or strangers. Use 知ってる with friends. を is usually kept in writing, but can be omitted in natural speech: あの人知ってる？","structure":[{"lhs":"N + を","rhs":"知っています","note":"Affirmative: know N (present)"},{"lhs":"N + を","rhs":"知りません","note":"Negative: do not know N"},{"lhs":"N + を","rhs":"知っていますか","note":"Question: Do you know N?"},{"lhs":"N + を","rhs":"知っていました／知りませんでした","note":"Past: knew / did not know"},{"lhs":"N (casual speech)","rhs":"知ってる","note":"Short form of 知っています"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know that shop.","scene":"Pointing toward the shop and confirming this to a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"電話番号","r":"でんわばんごう","m":"phone number"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知りません","r":"しりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not know the phone number.","scene":"Answering that you do not know someone's phone number"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-064||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **ask or confirm** whether someone knows a thing, place, person, or piece of information, and to **answer** that you do or do not know. It is very common in daily conversation, especially when introducing something or asking for directions.","nuance":"When asked 知っていますか, answer はい、知っています if you know and いいえ、知りません if you do not. Never say はい、知ります. For directions or locations, わかりません is usually more natural and polite than 知りません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"その"},{"t":"ニュース","m":"news"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知りません","r":"しりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I do not know about that news.","scene":"Answering when someone asks whether you know the latest news"},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you know that movie?","scene":"Talking with a friend about a popular movie"},{"tokens":[{"t":"はい","m":"yes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yes, I know.","scene":"Giving an affirmative answer to 'Do you know...?'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-064||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 知っています from easily confused patterns: **わかります**, **Vています** (an ongoing action), and **NはNです**.","nuance":"知っています means 'know' as in having information or knowledge; わかります means 'understand' as in grasping content or a reason. 知っています is a static state, not an ongoing action, so it does not mean 'gradually coming to know.' NはNです only confirms identity; it does not express 'know.'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"名前","r":"なまえ","m":"name"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know that person's name.","scene":"You know someone's name, but they only recognize their face"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"わかります","point":"知っています = 'know' (have information), while わかります = 'understand' (process information). Example: あの人の名前を知っています (know the name) / 日本語の意味がわかります (understand the meaning of Japanese)."},{"vs":"Vています","point":"Vています usually describes an ongoing action, while 知っています describes the resulting state of having learned something. Do not use 知っています to mean 'gradually coming to know.'"},{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです identifies someone or something as 'A is B,' while 知っています says the speaker has information about B. Example: あの人は田中さんです (That is Mr. Tanaka) / 田中さんを知っています (I know Mr. Tanaka)."}]}},"n5-064||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes and traps","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes with 知る because they conjugate it like a regular verb. Remember: **'know' in the present is always 知っています**, the negative is 知りません, and answers use 知っています.","nuance":"The most common mistake is using 知ります for present 'know.' In reality, 知ります is used only for 'will come to know' and is rare. The standard negative is 知りません, not 知っていません. This is a classic beginner exception.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いいえ","m":"no"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"知りません","r":"しりません","m":"do not know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No, I do not know.","scene":"Giving a negative answer when asked whether you know"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私はその人を知ります。 → ✅ 私はその人を知っています。","❌ はい、知ります。 → ✅ はい、知っています。","❌ いいえ、知っていません。 → ✅ いいえ、知りません。"],"compare":null}},"n5-064||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine 'knowing' as a **file fully downloaded** into your brain. The verb 知る is the act of downloading; after the download finishes, you are in the state of 'knowing' = 知っている. So to say 'I know,' use 知っています, like 'I have finished downloading and still have it.'","nuance":"Think of knowing as a state, not an action. If someone asks 'Do you know...?' and you answer 'I know,' you are describing a state, so use -て います.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-064||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nを知っています／知りません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nを知っています／知りません","patternReading":"しっています／しりません","coreMeaning":"Know / do not know N — a state of having information","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions used with 知っています in daily life and on tests, making your speech more natural.","nuance":"よく知っています = know well; だいたい知っています = know roughly; 知りませんでした = 'I did not know' (past). These expressions often appear when describing how well you know something.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"よく","m":"well"},{"t":"知っています","r":"しっています","m":"know"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I know it well.","scene":"Someone asks whether you know the place or restaurant well"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-065||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"core","definition":"V1てV2 **joins two actions** in one sentence, showing that **V1 happens first and V2 happens afterward**. For example: 起きて、シャワーを浴びます = get up and then take a shower. This pattern **lists consecutive actions**, usually performed by the same subject.","nuance":"This is a **neutral structure**, common in everyday speech and simple writing. It **does not stress the time gap** between actions; it only states their order. Note: the て-form here is not a request (Vてください) or the progressive form (Vています).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝","r":"あさ","m":"morning"},{"t":"起きて","r":"おきて","m":"get up"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"シャワー","m":"shower"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"浴びて","r":"あびて","m":"shower"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the morning, I get up, take a shower, and eat breakfast.","scene":"Describing my morning routine"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝ました","r":"ねました","m":"sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I did my homework and then went to bed.","scene":"Talking about last night after studying"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-065||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"forms","definition":"To link V1 with V2, **change V1 into the て-form**. The change depends on the **verb group**: Group I has sound changes (音便), Group II only removes ます and adds て, and Group III includes します and 来ます. The final verb keeps its ます form or takes past/negative form.","nuance":"In a long sentence with several Vて forms, **only the final verb carries tense and politeness**: 朝起きて、食べて、行きました. Earlier verbs always stay in the て-form and must not be changed to ます.","structure":[{"lhs":"V1（ます形）","rhs":"V1（て形）","note":"Remove ます; change by verb group"},{"lhs":"V1て、","rhs":"V2ます／V2ました","note":"Link two actions; V2 determines tense"},{"lhs":"V1て、V2て、","rhs":"V3ます","note":"Link several actions; only V3 takes tense"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗って","r":"のって","m":"ride"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I take the train to work.","scene":"Describing my daily commute"},{"tokens":[{"t":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んで","r":"のんで","m":"drink"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drink coffee and then study.","scene":"Drinking coffee to stay alert while studying"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-065||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"usage","definition":"V1てV2 appears in many situations: describing a morning routine, talking about transportation (バスに乗って行きます = go by bus), explaining how to make something, or listing tasks. The key point is that **V1 modifies V2**, forming one sentence.","nuance":"This pattern is common when **one subject** performs several actions. Instead of two separate, repetitive sentences—朝起きました。それから、シャワーを浴びました—Japanese uses Vて to connect them naturally. To stress that V2 happens only after V1 is complete, use てから (N4); at N5, Vて is enough.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"スーパー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"寄って","r":"よって","m":"stop by"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I stop by the supermarket and then go home.","scene":"Stopping to shop on the way home from work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"listen"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ごはん","m":"meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作ります","r":"つくります","m":"cook"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I listen to music and then cook dinner.","scene":"Describing listening to music before cooking"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-065||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse V1てV2 with Vてから, VたりVたり, and Vています. The key differences are: V1てV2 simply links actions in order; Vてから stresses **only after V1 is completed** does V2 happen; VたりVたり lists representative actions with **no required order**.","nuance":"V1てV2 is a frequent **N5 grammar point**, neither formal nor rude. Vてから is similar, but makes the separation and sequence feel clearer. VたりVたり does not say which action comes first and has a “sometimes this, sometimes that” nuance.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んで","r":"のんで","m":"take"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝ます","r":"ねます","m":"sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I take my medicine and then go to bed.","scene":"Simple daily order, without stressing the time gap"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてから","point":"Vてから stresses that V1 must be COMPLETED before V2; V1てV2 only links the actions. Example: 薬を飲んで、寝ます (take medicine, then sleep; simple order) ↔ 薬を飲んでから、寝ます (after taking medicine, then sleep; a clear time gap)."},{"vs":"VたりVたり","point":"VたりVたり lists representative actions with NO before-after order. Example: 週末は映画を見たり、本を読んだりします (on weekends I watch movies and read books—no order stated) ↔ V1てV2 requires chronological order."},{"vs":"Vています","point":"Vています describes an action IN PROGRESS or a state; V1てV2 describes one action happening and then another. Example: 本を読んでいます (I am reading) ↔ 本を読んで、寝ます (I read and then go to bed)."}]}},"n5-065||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors include **using ますて instead of the て-form**, **marking tense on both V1 and V2**, and **forming Group I て-forms incorrectly**. This branch shows typical errors and their corrections.","nuance":"飲みて instead of 飲んで is common because learners remove ます and add て automatically. Memorize the sound-change patterns: んで / って / いて. Also, do not use V1て to connect actions with **different subjects**; to say “I go, and he comes,” use separate sentences.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いて","r":"きいて","m":"listen"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I listen to music and then study.","scene":"Correct sentence replacing the ❌ sentence above"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 朝起きまして、ジョギングします。 → ✅ 朝起きて、ジョギングします。 (Do not add ます to V1 when linking with て in ordinary conversation.)","❌ 昨日、ごはんを食べた、お風呂に入りました。 → ✅ 昨日、ごはんを食べて、お風呂に入りました。 (Tense belongs on V2; V1 stays in て-form, not 食べた.)","❌ 音楽を聞きて、勉強します。 → ✅ 音楽を聞いて、勉強します。 (A Group I verb ending in き changes to いて, not きて.)","❌ 電車に乗りて、学校へ行きます。 → ✅ 電車に乗って、学校へ行きます。 (A Group I verb ending in り changes to って, not りて.)"],"compare":null}},"n5-065||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"memory","definition":"Simple memory tip: **て is a chain link**—it links one action to the next. Imagine V1 as the first brick and V2 as the second, with て between them holding them together.","nuance":"When て appears mid-sentence, it is usually pronounced **lightly and quickly**, but its written form remains て. Do not forget the comma (、) after V1 to mark the pause.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-065||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V1てV2 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1てV2","patternReading":"どうしいちてどうしに","coreMeaning":"Do V1, then continue with V2 — actions in sequence","slug":"phrases","definition":"Below are **familiar phrases** used every day in N5-level communication. Memorizing them helps you speak smoothly without stopping to think about verb conjugation.","nuance":"These phrases are commonly used when describing routines, schedules, or how to do something. They appear frequently in JLPT N5 listening sections.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行って","r":"いって","m":"go"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I go shopping and then return home.","scene":"Describing today's plans after work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-066||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ)","patternReading":"くて / で","coreMeaning":"Linking adjectives: both ~ and ~ / ~ and ~ (same subject)","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern links two or more adjectives to describe the same thing, person, or situation. An い-adjective changes to くて; a な-adjective adds で. The meaning is 'both...and...' or '...and...'. Both traits must describe the same subject; do not use it for two different subjects.","nuance":"This is a natural, concise way to speak, common in everyday speech and writing. The tone is neutral—not formal or casual. Compared with two separate sentences, linking adjectives makes the information flow smoothly and highlights the combination of traits.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広くて","r":"ひろくて","m":"spacious and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"きれい","m":"clean"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room is spacious and clean.","scene":"Looking around a newly rented room"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"親切で","r":"しんせつで","m":"kind and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is a kind and interesting person.","scene":"Introducing a new friend to your family"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-066||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & linking forms","sub":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ)","patternReading":"くて / で","coreMeaning":"Linking adjectives: both ~ and ~ / ~ and ~ (same subject)","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: [Adjective 1] + くて/で + [Adjective 2] + ... + final clause. For い-adjectives, drop い and add くて (高い → 高くて). For な-adjectives, add で directly after the adjective (きれい → きれいで). Every clause except the final one uses the linking form; the final clause is the sentence-ending predicate.","nuance":"In speech, the comma may be omitted, but there is usually a slight pause. This pattern works with polite ~です at the end. The linked clauses are not negative or past; if needed, put the negative or past form in the final clause.","structure":[{"lhs":"い-adj","rhs":"〜くて (高い→高くて)","note":"Drop い, add くて"},{"lhs":"な-adj","rhs":"〜で (きれい→きれいで)","note":"Add で after the adjective"},{"lhs":"Câu 2 vế","rhs":"Aくて、Bです。 / Aで、Bです。","note":"Final clause keeps the polite form"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"難しくて","r":"むずかしくて","m":"difficult but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japanese is difficult but interesting.","scene":"Answering friends who ask about studying Japanese"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安くて","r":"やすくて","m":"cheap and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This place is cheap and delicious.","scene":"Inviting a friend to your usual lunch spot"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-066||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ)","patternReading":"くて / で","coreMeaning":"Linking adjectives: both ~ and ~ / ~ and ~ (same subject)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to describe two traits of one object in a single complex sentence. It is common in conversation when praising, commenting, or introducing something. With positive adjectives, it means 'both ... and ...'. With mild contrast, it may be translated as 'but' (この本は安くて、おもしろいです).","nuance":"The tone is moderately polite and suits friends or colleagues. Do not use it to link relative clauses or actions. This pattern focuses on static qualities, not actions. To link verbs, use Vて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"安くて","r":"やすくて","m":"cheap but"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This book is cheap but interesting.","scene":"Recommending a book just bought at a used bookstore"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"元気で","r":"げんきで","m":"energetic and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"明るい","r":"あかるい","m":"cheerful"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She is energetic and cheerful.","scene":"Commenting on a classmate in front of everyone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"近くて","r":"ちかくて","m":"nearby and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"便利","r":"べんり","m":"convenient"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The station is nearby and convenient.","scene":"Advertising an apartment to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-066||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with confusing patterns","sub":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ)","patternReading":"くて / で","coreMeaning":"Linking adjectives: both ~ and ~ / ~ and ~ (same subject)","slug":"compare","definition":"A quick comparison with commonly confused patterns. **Aくて/NAで** links adjectives. **Vて** links verbs (食べて、寝ます). **NはAです** uses one adjective as the predicate and does not link. **〜から** expresses a reason, while this pattern simply lists parallel traits.","nuance":"Each pattern has a different function. Confusing them can seriously change the meaning. Aくて/NAで never narrates actions or expresses purpose.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"広くて","r":"ひろくて","m":"spacious and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"きれい","m":"clean"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This room is spacious and clean (not 'spacious, not clean').","scene":"Distinguishing it from negative 広くない"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vて","point":"Vて links ACTIONS (go, eat, drink...), while Aくて/NAで links ADJECTIVES. 部屋は広くて、きれいです。"},{"vs":"NはAです","point":"NはAです states only ONE trait. To state two traits, use Aくて/NAで."},{"vs":"NはAくないです","point":"Aくない is the NEGATIVE form (not ...), not a linking form. Compare: 高くない = not expensive, 高くて = expensive and."}]}},"n5-066||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ)","patternReading":"くて / で","coreMeaning":"Linking adjectives: both ~ and ~ / ~ and ~ (same subject)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse the linking form of な-adjectives by adding くて as if they were い-adjectives. In fact, な-adjectives only add で. Also, do not link opposing adjectives (high and low, expensive and cheap) with this pattern because they create a contradiction.","nuance":"These mistakes make the sentence sound unnatural and incorrect in textbook Japanese. Always check: (1) Is it an い- or な-adjective? (2) Are the adjectives opposites? (3) Do they describe the same subject?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"新しくて","r":"あたらしくて","m":"new and"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"安全","r":"あんぜん","m":"safe"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This car is new and safe.","scene":"Introducing a newly purchased car"}],"pitfalls":["❌ きれいくて → ✅ きれいで. な-adjectives (きれい、親切、元気) do not use くて; simply add で to the stem.","❌ この部屋は広くて、狭いです. Do not link opposing adjectives in one sentence because it creates a contradiction; split it into two sentences or use が.","❌ いいくて → ✅ よくて. When いい changes to the くて form, it becomes よくて; never use いいくて."],"compare":null}},"n5-066||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくて / NAで (nối tính từ)","patternReading":"くて / で","coreMeaning":"Linking adjectives: both ~ and ~ / ~ and ~ (same subject)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember: **い → くて** (高い → 高くて), **な → で** (きれい → きれいで). If unsure, say it aloud: 'きれいで' sounds natural, while 'きれいくて' immediately sounds wrong. Imagine two traits joined by a **+** sign: 'くて' for い and 'で' for な.","nuance":"This tip helps you respond quickly in speech. Practice making sentences with familiar adjectives: 広い、高い、新しい, きれい、便利、元気. Eventually, you will not need to think about it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-067||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"core","definition":"〜てから describes the second action happening only after the first action has finished. It emphasizes the order and completion of the first clause. This is a basic pattern for saying 'after doing something' in Japanese.","nuance":"It is common in everyday speech and ordinary writing, with no especially formal or casual nuance. Compared with V1てV2, which simply connects actions, てから clearly shows that the first action must be completed.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"してから","r":"してから","m":"after finishing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ゲーム","r":"げーむ","m":"game"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After finishing my homework, I play games.","scene":"A student tells a friend about their evening plan."},{"tokens":[{"t":"シャワー","r":"しゃわー","m":"shower"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"浴びてから","r":"あびてから","m":"after showering"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝ます","r":"ねます","m":"sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I take a shower and then go to bed.","scene":"Answering, 'What do you do before bed?'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-067||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form & conjugation","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"forms","definition":"Structure: **V (て-form) + から**. To form the て-form: Group I (五段): 買う→買って, 書く→書いて; Group II (一段): 食べる→食べて; irregular: する→して, 来る→きて. **から** attaches directly; no other particle is needed. The first clause does not change for tense, past, or negation.","nuance":"This pattern has **no negative form** before から. To express negation, put it in the second clause or use another pattern such as Vないで.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"から","note":"V is a verb in the て-form"},{"lhs":"Vてから","rhs":"V2","note":"V2 is the later action"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んでから","r":"のんでから","m":"after taking"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After taking my medicine, I went to school.","scene":"Recounting this morning after being sick."},{"tokens":[{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"carefully"},{"t":"考えてから","r":"かんがえてから","m":"after thinking"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"答えて","r":"こたえて","m":"answer"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Think carefully before answering.","scene":"A teacher reminds students during a test."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-067||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜てから when the speaker wants to **emphasize the order** of two actions. It commonly appears with **habits**, **plans**, **procedures**, **advice**, and **requests**.","nuance":"The first clause can describe either an intentional action or a natural event, such as 雨がやんでから. The pattern focuses on time order and completion of the first clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"終わってから","r":"おわってから","m":"after work ends"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"飲み","r":"のみ","m":"drinking"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行きませんか","r":"いきませんか","m":"shall we go?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall we go for a drink after work?","scene":"Coworkers invite each other out after work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hands"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗ってから","r":"あらってから","m":"after washing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"食べなさい","r":"たべなさい","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Wash your hands before eating.","scene":"A mother reminds her child before a meal."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-067||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"compare","definition":"〜てから is often confused with other patterns that express sequence. The key difference is that **Vてから emphasizes that the first clause must be completed** before the later action begins.","nuance":"Choose the pattern according to the degree of completion and the style of the sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"病院","r":"びょういん","m":"hospital"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行ってから","r":"いってから","m":"after going"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After going to the hospital, I go shopping.","scene":"Talking about the day's plan."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V1てV2","point":"V1てV2 simply lists actions in ordinary time order; Vてから clearly emphasizes that the first action must be completed before the second begins."},{"vs":"Vるまえに","point":"Vるまえに = 'before V' (the later event has not happened yet), while Vてから = 'after finishing V' (the later event happens afterward). Same situation: 寝るまえに、歯を磨く (brush your teeth before bed) vs 歯を磨いてから、寝る (brush your teeth, then go to bed)."},{"vs":"Vたあとで","point":"Vたあとで also means 'after,' but is more formal and can emphasize a time gap; Vてから is conversational and presents a continuous sequence."}]}},"n5-067||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"When using 〜てから, learners often make these errors because of the English equivalent 'after.' Remember: **use it only with verbs**, **there is no negative form**, and do not confuse it with reason から.","nuance":"These errors can make a sentence ungrammatical or unclear. Learners may mechanically translate 'after' as てから, but remember: use it only with verbs, not adjectives, and write てから as one unit. Also do not confuse it with reason から (Vから).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入ってから","r":"はいってから","m":"after bathing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寝ます","r":"ねます","m":"sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After taking a bath, I go to bed.","scene":"A standard example of correct usage."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 安くてから、買いました。 → ✅ 安かったから、買いました。 (Use reason から with an adjective, not てから.)","❌ ご飯を食べて、から行きます。 → ✅ ご飯を食べてから、行きます。 (てから must be written together.)","❌ 行かないてから、電話します。 → ✅ 行ってから、電話します。 (There is no Vないてから; use Vてから or Vないで + V2.)"],"compare":null}},"n5-067||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine **Vて** as a **hook** at the end of the first action and **から** as a **starting line**: only after 'hooking' the action into place can you 'run' to the next action.","nuance":"This tip helps you remember that から means 'from there,' so てから expresses 'from after V.'","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-067||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vてから · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vてから","patternReading":"てから","coreMeaning":"After doing V (before-and-after order)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These familiar 〜てから phrases appear very often in conversation and on the N5 exam. Memorizing them will help you **respond quickly**.","nuance":"All of these phrases use common N5 verbs and sound natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"終わってから","r":"おわってから","m":"after finishing"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ビール","r":"びーる","m":"beer"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After work, I drink beer.","scene":"A worker describes an evening routine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-068||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **N1はN2が〜** describes a feature of **a part or attribute** belonging to N1. N1 is the topic, while N2 is a specific part of N1, and the rest of the sentence describes that part. For example, 象は鼻が長いです means “As for the elephant, its trunk is long,” not that the whole elephant is long.","nuance":"Common in both speech and writing. It has an **objective, descriptive** tone and does not express emotion. Unlike NはNが好き, which expresses a subjective preference, this pattern describes an observable or measurable quality.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mount Fuji"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"景色","r":"けしき","m":"scenery"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mount Fuji has beautiful scenery.","scene":"Admiring Mount Fuji with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"目","r":"め","m":"eyes"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"large"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have large eyes.","scene":"Introducing your appearance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-068||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure has two nouns: **N1** (the larger whole) and **N2** (a part of N1). **は** marks the topic, and **が** marks the subject of the part. The final element is usually an i-adjective, na-adjective, or the stative verb あります.","nuance":"Do not use it with action verbs, except stative verbs such as あります. To say that a part exists, use があります. Negatives and past forms follow the conjugation of the final adjective or verb.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 が イAいです","note":"イA: 高い・長い・多い"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 が ナAです","note":"ナA: きれい・にぎやか"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"は N2 が あります","note":"A part exists: 机は足があります"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"野菜","r":"やさい","m":"vegetables"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This dish has lots of vegetables.","scene":"Commenting on a dish"},{"tokens":[{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"耳","r":"みみ","m":"ears"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"長い","r":"ながい","m":"long"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The dog has long ears.","scene":"Talking about a dog breed"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-068||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **describe a feature** of a part of the topic. It is often used to **praise or criticize** a specific part, or to state a notable characteristic of a place, dish, or person.","nuance":"Common adjectives include 高い・低い・大きい・小さい・多い・少ない・きれい・おいしい・痛い… Do not use it for the whole subject's psychological feelings, such as 悲しい. In questions, use どう to ask about a feature.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"人口","r":"じんこう","m":"population"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"large"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Japan has a large population.","scene":"Talking about Japan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"パソコン","m":"computer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"値段","r":"ねだん","m":"price"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"expensive"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This computer is expensive.","scene":"Comparing computer prices"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"髪","r":"かみ","m":"hair"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"長い","r":"ながい","m":"long"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She has long hair.","scene":"Complimenting a woman"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-068||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns contrasted","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"compare","definition":"Patterns often confused with N1はN2が〜 include **NはNが好きです** and **NはNです**. The key difference is what N2 represents: in this pattern, N2 is a part, while in the others it is the object of a preference or an identifying noun.","nuance":"N1はN2が〜 is **objective and descriptive**; NはNが好き expresses a **personal preference**; NはNです **identifies someone or something**. Confusing them changes the meaning of the whole sentence.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"目","r":"め","m":"eyes"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"large"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have large eyes.","scene":"Comparing with 私は猫が好きです"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNが好きです","point":"好き expresses the speaker's **subjective preference**, while N2 in this pattern is a **part** of N1. For example, 私は猫が好きです (I like cats) is completely different from 猫は目がきれいです (Cats have beautiful eyes)."},{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです identifies **what N1 is** (identity); it does not describe a part. For example, 私は学生です (I am a student) differs from 私は目が大きいです (I have large eyes), which describes a specific part."}]}},"n5-068||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often misuse the particles **を** and **の**, or place the adjective before the noun incorrectly. Remember: when describing a part, **always use が**, and keep the structure unchanged.","nuance":"These errors make the sentence unnatural or ungrammatical. In particular, の can sound like simple possession and may fail to highlight the part's feature.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"頭","r":"あたま","m":"head"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","r":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is smart (has a good head).","scene":"Complimenting someone's intelligence"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 象は鼻を長いです。 → ✅ 象は鼻が長いです。 (Use が for a part; を does not go with adjectives)","❌ 私の頭は痛いです。(to say 'I have a headache') → ✅ 私は頭が痛いです。 (は follows the whole subject, not the part)","❌ 東京は高い物価です。 → ✅ 東京は物価が高いです。 (Do not put the adjective before the noun in this pattern)"],"compare":null}},"n5-068||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture N1 as a scene and N2 as the detail you want to praise in it. **は** brings the scene before the listener, while **が** points to that specific detail.","nuance":"This image helps you remember why は follows N1 and が follows N2. When you see two nouns and an adjective, ask yourself: “What is the whole, and what is its part?” Then place は and が accordingly.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-068||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"N1はN2が〜 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"N1はN2が〜","patternReading":"わ が","coreMeaning":"Describes a feature of a part of N1 (what N2 of N1 is like)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Several fixed phrases appear frequently in N5 exams and everyday life. Learn them as vocabulary chunks: when you have any topic, simply place it before は and use the pattern correctly.","nuance":"These phrases often have idiomatic meanings. For example, 頭がいい means “smart,” not simply “the head is good.” Memorizing chunks is more natural than translating each word.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼女","r":"かのじょ","m":"she"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"目","r":"め","m":"eyes"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"She has beautiful eyes.","scene":"Complimenting a friend's eyes"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-069||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"core","definition":"Vないでください is used to **ask the listener not to do something**. It is the negative form of Vてください and has a polite tone, similar to “please don't...” in English. Unlike てはいけません (prohibition), this pattern is only a reminder or request.","nuance":"It is used in both speech and writing. The politeness level is moderate; with superiors or in formal work settings, use Vないでいただけますか for a softer request. The sentence usually expresses a wish rather than sounding harsh.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮らないでください","r":"とらないでください","m":"don't take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't take photos here.","scene":"A guard reminds visitors at a museum."},{"tokens":[{"t":"心配","r":"しんぱい","m":"worry"},{"t":"しないでください","r":"しないでください","m":"don't worry"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't worry.","scene":"A mother sends encouragement before an exam."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"閉めないでください","r":"しめないでください","m":"don't close"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't close the door.","scene":"Someone says this before entering an elevator."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-069||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"forms","definition":"Formula: **Change the verb to its ない form, then add でください**. Do not use the て form before ください, unlike Vてください. Example: 吸う → 吸わ → 吸わないでください.","nuance":"This is the standard polite request form for everyday communication. Adding くださいませんか or いただけますか raises the level of respect and suits requests to superiors.","structure":[{"lhs":"V group 1 (う→わ)","rhs":"ない + でください","note":"吸う→吸わない→吸わないでください"},{"lhs":"V group 2 (drop る)","rhs":"ない + でください","note":"食べる→食べない→食べないでください"},{"lhs":"する","rhs":"しない + でください","note":"勉強する→勉強しないでください"},{"lhs":"来る","rhs":"こない + でください","note":"来ない is read こない, not きない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"タバコ","m":"cigarettes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"吸わないでください","r":"すわないでください","m":"don't smoke"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't smoke.","scene":"A café has a no-smoking sign."},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来ないでください","r":"こないでください","m":"don't come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't come here.","scene":"Someone busy tells a friend not to visit."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → ない形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — う → わない","Remove う and add わない — NOT あない!","買う（かう） → `買わない（かわない）` (buy) ・ 言う（いう） → `言わない（いわない）` (say) ・ 会う（あう） → `会わない（あわない）` (meet) ・ 歌う（うたう） → `歌わない（うたわない）` (sing)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — く → かない · ぐ → がない","Change く → か and ぐ → が, then add ない","書く（かく） → `書かない（かかない）` (write) ・ 聞く（きく） → `聞かない（きかない）` (listen) ・ 泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳がない（およがない）` (swim) ・ 急ぐ（いそぐ） → `急がない（いそがない）` (hurry)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — す → さない · つ → たない","Change to the あ-row: す→さ, つ→た, then add ない","話す（はなす） → `話さない（はなさない）` (speak) ・ 出す（だす） → `出さない（ださない）` (take out) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待たない（またない）` (wait) ・ 立つ（たつ） → `立たない（たたない）` (stand)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — ぬ · ぶ · む → なない / ばない / まない","Change to the あ-row: ぬ→な, ぶ→ば, む→ま, then add ない","死ぬ（しぬ） → `死なない（しなない）` (die) ・ 遊ぶ（あそぶ） → `遊ばない（あそばない）` (play) ・ 飲む（のむ） → `飲まない（のまない）` (drink) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読まない（よまない）` (read)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — る (godan) → らない","Remove る and add らない","帰る（かえる） → `帰らない（かえらない）` (return) ・ 走る（はしる） → `走らない（はしらない）` (run) ・ 切る（きる） → `切らない（きらない）` (cut) ・ 知る（しる） → `知らない（しらない）` (know)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — ichidan: remove る + ない","Remove る and add ない","食べる（たべる） → `食べない（たべない）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見ない（みない）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きない（おきない）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝ない（ねない）` (sleep)"],["**Special case** — Important exceptions","Memorize — no rule applies","ある → `ない` (exist (objects)) ・ いい → `よくない` (good) ・ する → `しない` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来ない（こない）` (come)"]]}}},"n5-069||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears in **three main situations**: (1) asking someone to stop what they are doing; (2) giving a gentle reminder on a sign; and (3) reassuring someone not to worry or overexert themselves.","nuance":"On signs, this is more polite than 〜てはいけません. For casual reassurance, ください can be dropped: 心配しないで, 無理しないで.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"教室","r":"きょうしつ","m":"classroom"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"走らないでください","r":"はしらないでください","m":"don't run"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't run in the classroom.","scene":"A teacher reminds students after class."},{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"しないでください","r":"しないでください","m":"don't call"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't make phone calls on the train.","scene":"A reminder to passengers in a train car."},{"tokens":[{"t":"無理","r":"むり","m":"too hard"},{"t":"しないでください","r":"しないでください","m":"don't overdo it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't overdo it.","scene":"A colleague speaks to someone who is ill."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-069||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Similar patterns compared","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"compare","definition":"These three patterns often confuse N5 learners. Remember: **Vてください = please do**, **Vないでください = please don't**, **Vてはいけません = must not (prohibited)**, while sentence-final **Vないで** is a casual request.","nuance":"When comparing them, ask: Is the speaker making a request or stating a prohibition? Use 〜ください for a request and 〜てはいけません for a prohibition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","r":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ごみ","m":"trash"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"捨てないでください","r":"すてないでください","m":"don't throw away"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't throw trash here.","scene":"A reminder sign stands outside a house."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてください","point":"Vてください tells someone TO DO something, while Vないでください tells them NOT TO. Direct pair: 食べてください (please eat) / 食べないでください (please don't eat)."},{"vs":"Vてはいけません","point":"Vてはいけません states a rule or prohibition, not a request; Vないでください is a much gentler polite request."},{"vs":"Vないで","point":"Sentence-final Vないで is a casual request used with friends. It still means “don't,” but without ください it is not polite with strangers. Before another verb, Vないで can also mean “without doing...”."}]}},"n5-069||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Here are the mistakes N5 learners most often make with Vないでください. They mostly come from translating directly from English or mis-conjugating an irregular verb. Without care, you may produce a sentence with the wrong meaning or an impolite tone in everyday conversation.","nuance":"These mistakes mainly come from translating directly or mis-conjugating irregular verbs.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お菓子","r":"おかし","m":"snacks"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べないでください","r":"たべないでください","m":"don't eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't eat snacks.","scene":"A mother reminds her child before a meal."}],"pitfalls":["❌ ここにきないでください（wrongly formed as きない） → ✅ ここにこないでください（来る becomes こない）.","❌ To say “don't eat,” saying 食べてください → ✅ 食べないでください.","❌ ここでタバコを吸わないで。（with someone unfamiliar） → ✅ 吸わないでください.","❌ 先生、ここで写真を撮らないで。（short form with a teacher） → ✅ 先生、ここで写真を撮らないでいただけますか。"],"compare":null}},"n5-069||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"memory","definition":"The simplest memory aid: ない means “not” and ください means “please” → **please do not do it**.","nuance":"This is a way to remember the order: always **Vない + でください**, never insert て.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-069||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vないでください · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないでください","patternReading":"ないでください","coreMeaning":"Please don't do ~ (polite negative request)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases found in textbooks and daily life include: **ここでタバコを吸わないでください** (please don't smoke here), **写真を撮らないでください** (please don't take photos), and **心配しないでください** (please don't worry).","nuance":"These are polite sentences commonly heard in public places, at home, or at work. They are high-frequency N5 expressions that are easy to learn.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photos"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮らないでください","r":"とらないでください","m":"don't take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't take photos.","scene":"A museum attendant reminds visitors."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-070||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vなければなりません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなければなりません","patternReading":"なければなりません","coreMeaning":"Must do V (obligation)","slug":"core","definition":"〜なければなりません expresses a **strong obligation**: “must do something.” Use it when a **rule, schedule, health condition, or objective reason** makes the action unavoidable. This is a key N5 pattern common in notices, regulations, and everyday conversation.","nuance":"The tone is **objective** and somewhat **heavy**: “it is not acceptable not to do it.” To gently tell someone what they should do, Japanese uses Vたほうがいい (should do) instead. In casual speech, this long structure is often shortened to **Vなきゃ** or **Vないと**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲まなければなりません","r":"のまなければなりません","m":"must take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must take my medicine every day.","scene":"A doctor tells a patient to take medicine regularly"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出さなければなりません","r":"ださなければなりません","m":"must submit"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must submit the report by tomorrow.","scene":"An employee reminds themselves of the deadline"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しなきゃ","m":"have to do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to do my homework.","scene":"A student reminds themselves to do homework"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-070||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vなければなりません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなければなりません","patternReading":"なければなりません","coreMeaning":"Must do V (obligation)","slug":"forms","definition":"To form なければなりません, take the verb’s **ない-form**, **remove ない**, and add **なければなりません**. For example: 飲まない → 飲まなければなりません; 食べない → 食べなければなりません.","nuance":"Conjugation depends on the **verb group**. Casual shortenings are **Vなきゃ** (short for なければなりません) and **Vないと** (with いけない/だめ omitted). Do not use these shortened forms in formal writing.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"Vなければなりません","note":"Remove ない, then add なければなりません"},{"lhs":"飲む → 飲まない","rhs":"飲まなければなりません","note":"Group 1"},{"lhs":"食べる → 食べない","rhs":"食べなければなりません","note":"Group 2"},{"lhs":"来る → 来(こ)ない / する → しない","rhs":"来なければなりません / しなければなりません","note":"Group 3"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"九時","r":"くじ","m":"9 o’clock"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"来なければなりません","r":"こなければなりません","m":"must come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must arrive before nine.","scene":"A manager announces the meeting time"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"靴","r":"くつ","m":"shoes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"脱がなければなりません","r":"ぬがなければなりません","m":"must remove"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You must take off your shoes here.","scene":"Shoes must be removed at a traditional restaurant"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-070||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vなければなりません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなければなりません","patternReading":"なければなりません","coreMeaning":"Must do V (obligation)","slug":"usage","definition":"なければなりません is commonly used in four situations: 1) **external rules** (school or company regulations), 2) **deadlines** (homework or reports), 3) **health requirements** (taking medicine or resting), and 4) **necessary tasks** the speaker recognizes personally.","nuance":"It expresses a much stronger obligation than Vてください (please do) and Vたほうがいい (should do). When speaking about yourself, it shows that you cannot avoid the task. When politely addressing someone else, it can sound almost like a **social command**, so consider the context carefully.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"制服","r":"せいふく","m":"uniform"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"着なければなりません","r":"きなければなりません","m":"must wear"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must wear a uniform every day.","scene":"Company rules require employees to wear uniforms"},{"tokens":[{"t":"十時","r":"じゅうじ","m":"10 o’clock"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"帰らなければなりません","r":"かえらなければなりません","m":"must return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must be home by ten.","scene":"A mother sets a curfew for her child"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"a meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べなきゃ","r":"たべなきゃ","m":"have to eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to eat first.","scene":"Reminding yourself to eat before school"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-070||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"Vなければなりません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなければなりません","patternReading":"なければなりません","coreMeaning":"Must do V (obligation)","slug":"compare","definition":"This section distinguishes 〜なければなりません from three N5 patterns learners often confuse: 〜てはいけません (forbidden), 〜なくてもいいです (not necessary), and 〜たいです (want to). Each has a different nuance of obligation and speaker intention; understanding the core contrast helps you use them correctly in conversation and on the JLPT.","nuance":"The difference lies in **permission and intention**: なければなりません = required; てはいけません = forbidden; なくてもいい = not necessary; たい = want to. Changing the structure completely changes the meaning of the same action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮らなければなりません","r":"とらなければなりません","m":"must take"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いけません","m":"must not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must take photos, but taking them is forbidden here.","scene":"A photographer must take photos, but this area bans photography"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてはいけません","point":"Vなければなりません means “must do,” while Vてはいけません means “must not / may not.” Example: 写真を撮らなければなりません (must take a photo) vs 写真を撮ってはいけません (must not take a photo)."},{"vs":"Vなくてもいいです","point":"Vなければなりません means “must do,” while Vなくてもいいです means “do not have to.” Example: 日曜日は働かなければなりません (must work on Sunday) vs 日曜日は働かなくてもいいです (do not have to work on Sunday)."},{"vs":"Vたいです","point":"Vなければなりません means “must do” (objective), while Vたいです means “want to do” (personal desire). Example: 行かなければなりません (must go) vs 行きたいです (want to go)."}]}},"n5-070||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vなければなりません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなければなりません","patternReading":"なければなりません","coreMeaning":"Must do V (obligation)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This section covers classic mistakes with 〜なければなりません: using the wrong ない-form, adding an unnecessary element, and confusing casual speech with formal writing. Knowing these traps helps you avoid errors on the JLPT and when speaking with Japanese people.","nuance":"Most errors come from forgetting to **change the verb to the ない-form** or adding an **extra element**. Confusing formal writing with casual shortened speech is also common.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強しなければなりません","r":"べんきょうしなければなりません","m":"must study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I must study Japanese every day.","scene":"A student reminds themselves to study Japanese"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べないなければなりません → ✅ 食べなければなりません (remove ない before adding なければ)","❌ 行きなきゃ → ✅ 行かなきゃ (use the ない-form: 行か + なきゃ)","❌ しなければなりませんです → ✅ しなければなりません (ます-form; do not add です)","❌ 毎日薬を飲みなければなりません → ✅ 毎日薬を飲まなければなりません (do not use the ます-form before なければ)"],"compare":null}},"n5-070||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"Vなければなりません · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなければなりません","patternReading":"なければなりません","coreMeaning":"Must do V (obligation)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember 〜なければなりません, imagine that when you must do something, you cannot say “no.” The sounds な-け-れ-ば are like four fingers holding tight, while なりません means “will not work.” Together: “if you do not do it, it will not work”—so you must do it! Remember the phrase “NO ACTION, NO RESULT.”","nuance":"This memory trick is based on the parts of the expression: なければ (if not) + なりません (will not work). Focus on the sense of **obligation** so you do not confuse it with patterns expressing desire or plans.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-071||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vなくてもいいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなくてもいいです","patternReading":"なくてもいいです","coreMeaning":"It is not necessary to do (you may skip it)","slug":"core","definition":"Vなくてもいいです expresses that it is **not necessary to do** something; the speaker **removes the obligation** or allows the listener to skip it. It is the negative counterpart of 〜なければなりません (must do). The shorter the sentence, the closer the meaning is to “no need / you can skip it.”","nuance":"It can be used with **friends, family, and subordinates**, as well as politely at work. It does not prohibit the action; it **relaxes the requirement**. Compared with 〜なければなりません (mandatory), 〜なくてもいいです sounds much **gentler and more reassuring**. In casual speech, it is often shortened to **〜なくていい**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来なくてもいいです","r":"こなくてもいいです","m":"no need to come"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't need to come tomorrow.","scene":"A teacher texts a student that class is optional tomorrow"},{"tokens":[{"t":"全部","r":"ぜんぶ","m":"everything"},{"t":"食べなくてもいいです","r":"たべなくてもいいです","m":"no need to eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't have to eat it all.","scene":"A mother speaks to a child reluctant to eat vegetables"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-071||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vなくてもいいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなくてもいいです","patternReading":"なくてもいいです","coreMeaning":"It is not necessary to do (you may skip it)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic formula: remove い from **Vない** → **Vなく** + **てもいいです**. It means “there is no need to V.” With i-adjectives: **Aくなくてもいいです**; with nouns / na-adjectives: **Nじゃなくてもいいです** / **ではなくてもいいです**.","nuance":"The **なく** part carries the negation; **てもいいです** remains unchanged. A more formal form is **〜なくてもよいです / 〜なくてもよい**. The past form **〜なくてもよかったです** says that something did not need to be done or, in hindsight, need not have been done. A common short form is **〜なくていい**.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"Vなくてもいいです","note":"V is a verb in ない-form; remove い and add くて. Example: 行かない → 行かなくてもいいです"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aくなくてもいいです","note":"i-adjective: 高くない → 高くなくてもいいです"},{"lhs":"N / ナA","rhs":"Nじゃなくてもいいです","note":"Noun / na-adjective: 先生じゃなくてもいいです"},{"lhs":"Polite","rhs":"〜なくてもよいです","note":"Used in written or formal speech"},{"lhs":"Past","rhs":"〜なくてもよかったです","note":"Says something did not need to be done in the past"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行かなくてもいいです","r":"いかなくてもいいです","m":"no need to go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't need to go today.","scene":"An employee is allowed to work from home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行かなくてもよかったです","r":"いかなくてもよかったです","m":"needn't have gone"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I needn't have gone yesterday.","scene":"You realize the meeting had already been canceled"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行かなくてもいい","r":"いかなくてもいい","m":"no need to go"},{"t":"ですか"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Do I not need to go?","scene":"A student asks a teacher about class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-071||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vなくてもいいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなくてもいいです","patternReading":"なくてもいいです","coreMeaning":"It is not necessary to do (you may skip it)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vなくてもいいです is most common in three situations: (1) **asking permission** not to do something, (2) **allowing or reassuring** someone that they need not do it, and (3) **answering a question** about whether it is required. With **〜なくてもいいですか**, the speaker wants the obligation waived.","nuance":"In daily conversation, **急がなくてもいいですよ** is a common reassurance (“no need to hurry”). At work, **明日来なくてもいいです** gently tells a subordinate they need not come in; it is not an order. For a more formal expression, use **〜なくてかまいません**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そんなに","r":"そんなに","m":"that much"},{"t":"急がなくてもいいです","r":"いそがなくてもいいです","m":"no need to hurry"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't need to hurry like that.","scene":"You arrive out of breath, and the other person smiles"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"出さなくてもいい","r":"ださなくてもいい","m":"no need to submit"},{"t":"ですか"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Do I not need to submit the homework today?","scene":"A student asks a teacher after not finishing the homework"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-071||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"Vなくてもいいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなくてもいいです","patternReading":"なくてもいいです","coreMeaning":"It is not necessary to do (you may skip it)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most often confused with 〜なくてもいいです are **〜なければなりません**, **〜てもいいです**, and **〜ないでください**. Focus on the scale of **obligation – permission – prohibition**.","nuance":"〜なければなりません = must (obligation), 〜てもいいです = may do (permission), 〜ないでください = please don't (prohibition). 〜なくてもいいです means “not required”—not prohibited, and not “must not do.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行かなくてもいいです","r":"いかなくてもいいです","m":"no need to go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't need to go.","scene":"Answering “Do I have to go?”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vなければなりません","point":"Vなければなりません = MUST do; Vなくてもいいです = NO NEED to do. 行かなければなりません (must go) ≠ 行かなくてもいいです (need not go)."},{"vs":"Vてもいいです","point":"Vてもいいです = permission TO do; Vなくてもいいです = permission NOT TO do. 食べてもいいです (may eat) ≠ 食べなくてもいいです (need not eat)."},{"vs":"Vないでください","point":"Vないでください = please DON'T do it (prohibition); Vなくてもいいです = NO NEED to do it (requirement relaxed). 来ないでください (don't come) ≠ 来なくてもいいです (need not come)."}]}},"n5-071||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vなくてもいいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなくてもいいです","patternReading":"なくてもいいです","coreMeaning":"It is not necessary to do (you may skip it)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often form an incorrect pattern by directly combining “Vない + てもいい,” such as “行かないてもいい.” In fact, change **ない** to **なくて** before adding **もいいです**, giving **Vなくてもいいです**.","nuance":"The same applies to i-adjectives and nouns: 高くない → **高くなくて**; じゃない → **じゃなくて**. To say that an action is unnecessary, use 〜なくてもいい; do not add じゃない afterward.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"今日","r":"きょう","m":"today"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行かなくてもいいです","r":"いかなくてもいいです","m":"no need to go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You don't need to go today.","scene":"Answering whether you have to attend the meeting"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 行かないてもいいです。 → ✅ 行かなくてもいいです。","❌ 高ないでもいいです。 → ✅ 高くなくてもいいです。","❌ 先生じゃないでもいいです。 → ✅ 先生じゃなくてもいいです。","❌ 今日は行かなくてもいいじゃないです。 → ✅ 今日は行かなくてもいいです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-071||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vなくてもいいです · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vなくてもいいです","patternReading":"なくてもいいです","coreMeaning":"It is not necessary to do (you may skip it)","slug":"memory","definition":"Break the pattern down: **なく** (the negative form of V) + **てもいい** (it is also okay). Together: “not doing it is okay” = **there is no need to do it**.","nuance":"Contrast it with 〜なければなりません: なければ means “if not...” in the obligation pattern, while なくても means “even if not...” Think of a **balance scale**: one side is MUST, the other is NO NEED.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-072||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"core","definition":"〜までに marks a time or event N, and the following action must happen or be completed BEFORE N, no later. The speaker sets a final deadline for a specific task. Unlike 〜まで, which marks a continuous period lasting until N, 〜までに marks an action completed by N.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing, at all politeness levels. Often paired with completion verbs such as 出す, 帰る, 着く, and 返す. It generally does not pair with ongoing actions such as 勉強する or 働く because までに sets a completion point. It often sounds like a clear request, promise, or instruction.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"5時","r":"ごじ","m":"5 o'clock"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"レポート","r":"れぽーと","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出して","r":"だして","m":"submit"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please submit the report by 5 o'clock.","scene":"A boss reminds an employee of the report deadline."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出して","r":"だして","m":"submit"},{"t":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please submit the homework by tomorrow.","scene":"A teacher tells students when to submit homework."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-072||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"forms","definition":"までに attaches directly to a time noun (時間, 曜日, 月日) or a dictionary-form verb (Vる) that marks an event. The pattern does not change according to the politeness or tense of the following clause.","nuance":"The same form is used in formal writing. Do not use までに with て-form or ない-form verbs.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（時間）","rhs":"までに","note":"5時までに, あしたまでに, 来週までに"},{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"までに","note":"試験が始まるまでに, 寝るまでに"},{"lhs":"N（sự kiện）","rhs":"までに","note":"会議までに, 出発までに"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"5時","r":"ごじ","m":"5 o'clock"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will go home by 5 o'clock.","scene":"Telling a coworker when you will leave work."},{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"までに","m":"before"},{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"teeth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"磨きます","r":"みがきます","m":"brush"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I brush my teeth before going to bed.","scene":"Talking about an evening routine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-072||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when you want to say clearly that something must be finished before N. It commonly appears in commands, requests, schedules, and questions about deadlines.","nuance":"It simply states a limiting point and does not carry strong emotion. The level of familiarity or formality depends on the sentence ending, such as ください, ます, or ?.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"10時","r":"じゅうじ","m":"10 o'clock"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"着きます","r":"つきます","m":"arrive"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will arrive at the station by 10 o'clock.","scene":"Texting a friend about meeting at the station."},{"tokens":[{"t":"金曜日","r":"きんようび","m":"Friday"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"返します","r":"かえします","m":"return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will pay back the money by Friday.","scene":"Promising a friend to repay borrowed money."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-072||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"compare","definition":"まで and までに are an easy pair to confuse: まで marks a continuous period, while までに marks a deadline. Vる前に expresses the order of two actions, while までに usually pairs with a time or event that serves as a deadline. All three concern time, but their focus differs: continuity, completion by a deadline, or action sequence.","nuance":"To say “before doing something,” use Vる前に; to emphasize that something must be finished by a deadline, use までに. With まで, the associated action must continue over a period.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"5時","r":"ごじ","m":"5 o'clock"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will go home by 5 o'clock.","scene":"Emphasizing the deadline, not an ongoing action."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"まで","point":"まで pairs with an action that CONTINUES until N (5時まで勉強する). までに pairs with an action COMPLETED by N (5時までに帰る). Compare: 5時まで勉強します (study until 5) / 5時までに帰ります (go home by 5)."},{"vs":"Vる前に","point":"Vる前に means before carrying out action V (ご飯を食べる前に). までに marks a DEADLINE (a time or event). Sometimes they can both work: 寝る前に本を読みます (read before bed) / 寝るまでに本を読みます (finish reading by bedtime). までに stresses the deadline."}]}},"n5-072||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate まで as “until” and use まで instead of までに everywhere, or attach までに to an ongoing verb. This completely changes the meaning of the sentence.","nuance":"These errors change the meaning: 5時まで勉強する ≠ 5時までに勉強する. The verb with までに should have a clear endpoint.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日曜日","r":"にちようび","m":"Sunday"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"返します","r":"かえします","m":"return"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will return the book by Sunday.","scene":"Returning a library book on time."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 5時までに勉強します。 → ✅ 5時まで勉強します。 (study until 5)","❌ 5時まで帰ります。 → ✅ 5時までに帰ります。 (go home by 5)","❌ あしたまでに働きます。 → ✅ あしたまで働きます。 or あしたまでにレポートを出します。"],"compare":null}},"n5-072||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"memory","definition":"Visual memory tip: まで is a straight line running to N; までに is a dot stopping before N. Imagine “submit the assignment before the deadline.”","nuance":"The particle に often marks a destination or point in time, so までに suggests aiming at a point before the deadline expires.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-072||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nまでに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nまでに","patternReading":"までに","coreMeaning":"By time/event N, no later than N","slug":"phrases","definition":"Phrases with までに appear often when discussing deadlines in conversation, work, and study.","nuance":"In conversation, いつまでに？ is a natural way to ask for a deadline. 時間までに／期限までに sound more formal.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"レポート","r":"れぽーと","m":"report"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"いつ","m":"when"},{"t":"までに","m":"by the deadline"},{"t":"出します","r":"だします","m":"submit"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"By when must the report be submitted?","scene":"Asking a coworker about the report deadline."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-073||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"core","definition":"〜Vることができます is an N5 pattern expressing the ability to do something or that something is possible because conditions allow it. The verb before こと is always in dictionary form (Vる), not ます-form. This is a common, polite, and safe way to say “can.”","nuance":"Unlike the shorter potential form, Vることができます treats “doing V” as a noun and often sounds polite or formal. Japanese speakers use it in both speech and writing. In replies, you can say briefly: はい、できます。／いいえ、できません。","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作る","r":"つくる","m":"make/cook"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother can cook delicious dishes.","scene":"Telling a friend about your mother"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"自転車","r":"じてんしゃ","m":"bicycle"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗る","r":"のる","m":"ride"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can ride a bicycle.","scene":"Introducing yourself"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮る","r":"とる","m":"take"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Can I take photos here?","scene":"Asking a museum staff member"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-073||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch shows how to connect a verb to the pattern and form its tense and negative versions. V always stays in dictionary form; only できます changes for tense and negation.","nuance":"Remember that V always stays in dictionary form. できます changes for tense and negation; こと never changes.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ことができます","note":"Present affirmative: can do V"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ことができません","note":"Present negative: cannot do V"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ことができますか","note":"Question: can you do V?"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"ことができました","note":"Past: could / managed to do V"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"トム","m":"Tom"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"泳ぐ","r":"およぐ","m":"swim"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tom can swim.","scene":"Introducing a classmate’s ability"},{"tokens":[{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"ことができません","r":"ことができません","m":"cannot"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I cannot go alone.","scene":"Replying to an invitation"},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"child"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピアノ","m":"piano"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"弾く","r":"ひく","m":"play"},{"t":"ことができました","r":"ことができました","m":"could"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I was a child, I could play the piano.","scene":"Talking about childhood"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → dictionary form (辞書形)"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段動詞 (godan)","Keep unchanged — ends in the う-row (む く ぐ す う つ ぬ ぶ る)","飲む（のむ） → `飲む（のむ）` (drink) ・ 書く（かく） → `書く（かく）` (write) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話す（はなす）` (talk) ・ 買う（かう） → `買う（かう）` (buy)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段動詞 (ichidan)","Keep unchanged — usually ends in いる or える","食べる（たべる） → `食べる（たべる）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見る（みる）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きる（おきる）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝る（ねる）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — する (suru) and compounds","Keep the 〜する form unchanged","する → `する` (do) ・ 勉強する（べんきょうする） → `勉強する（べんきょうする）` (study) ・ 運動する（うんどうする） → `運動する（うんどうする）` (exercise) ・ 結婚する（けっこんする） → `結婚する（けっこんする）` (get married)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 来る (kuru)","Keep unchanged: 来る (くる)","来る（くる） → `来る（くる）` (come)"]]}}},"n5-073||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vることができます is mainly used in two situations: (1) ability gained through practice or learning, and (2) possibility created by conditions, rules, or the environment.","nuance":"To politely ask whether someone can do something, use 〜ことができますか instead of the short form. With elders or strangers, this is natural and respectful.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"少し","r":"すこし","m":"a little"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"speak"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can speak a little Japanese.","scene":"Answering “Can you speak Japanese?”"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"走る","r":"はしる","m":"run"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can run in this park.","scene":"The park has a running path and allows it"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日曜日","r":"にちようび","m":"Sunday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On Sundays, I can go shopping by myself.","scene":"A child tells their mother they are more independent"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-073||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish similar patterns","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"compare","definition":"This branch compares Vることができます with easily confused patterns such as Vてもいいです and the potential form. The goal is to choose the right pattern for ability, permission, or conversational style.","nuance":"Although all may translate as “can,” each pattern emphasizes something different: ability (Vることができます), permission (Vてもいいです), or a shorter conversational form (potential form).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読む","r":"よむ","m":"read"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can read this kanji.","scene":"Contrasting it with permission: 読んでもいいです"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてもいいです","point":"Vてもいいです = permission to do something; Vることができます = ability or possibility. ここで泳ぐことができます → swimming is possible here; ここで泳いでもいいです → I allow you to swim here."},{"vs":"V可能形（Vえます／Vれます）","point":"Both mean “can,” but Vることができます is formal and longer, emphasizing “the act of V”; V可能形 is shorter, friendlier, and common in conversation: 日本語を話すことができます ＝ 日本語が話せます."}]}},"n5-073||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"These are common errors when using Vることができます. This branch shows typical incorrect expressions, explains that they come from using ます-form habits, and gives standard correct sentences to prevent repeated mistakes.","nuance":"Errors often come from using ます-form before こと or leaving out こと.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"一人","r":"ひとり","m":"alone"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can go alone.","scene":"Correct sentence after fixing the error"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 話しますことができます。 → ✅ 話すことができます。 （before こと, use dictionary-form V）","❌ 泳ぐができます。 → ✅ 泳ぐことができます。 （こと is needed to turn V into “the act of swimming”）","❌ 私はテニスをすることをできます。 → ✅ 私はテニスをすることができます。 （use が with こと, not を）"],"compare":null}},"n5-073||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips & imagery","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"memory","definition":"This branch uses imagery and kanji breakdowns to aid memory: Vる + こと forms “the act of V,” while できます suggests “being able to reach or achieve it,” helping you remember both the meaning and the form.","nuance":"Think of dictionary form + こと as the noun “the act of ...”; できます means “that act succeeds.” If you can “go out and get there,” it is possible.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-073||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases & replies","sub":"Vることができます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vることができます","patternReading":"ることができます","coreMeaning":"Can do V (ability/capability)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common connected phrases and short replies often seen on tests and in daily life.","nuance":"Japanese speakers often ask with 〜ことができますか and reply briefly with はい、できます／いいえ、できません. Common verbs include 話す、読む、書く、作る、行く.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読む","r":"よむ","m":"read"},{"t":"ことができます","r":"ことができます","m":"can"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I can read this kanji.","scene":"Pointing to kanji in class"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-074||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"core","definition":"This pattern nominalizes a verb with **こと**, then places it after the topic marker (は) to introduce a **hobby, job, dream, or favorite activity**. V must be in the **dictionary form** (Vる), not the ます form. Overall meaning: “The main topic is the act of ~.”","nuance":"Used in both **spoken and written Japanese**; polite and common at N5 level. It does not express strong emotion, only an **introduction or explanation**. It lightly emphasizes the nature of the topic, not an action happening now.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"趣味","r":"しゅみ","m":"hobby"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photos"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮る","r":"とる","m":"take"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My hobby is taking photos.","scene":"Answering 趣味は何ですか during a self-introduction"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住む","r":"すむ","m":"live"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My dream is to live in Japan.","scene":"Talking about a dream with a classmate"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-074||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is consistent: **N（趣味・夢・仕事・好きなこと）＋ は ＋ Vる ＋ こと ＋ です**. こと turns the entire verb phrase into a **noun**; は marks the topic; です ends the polite sentence.","nuance":"Do not use Vます or Vた before こと when talking about hobbies. For negation, Vないこと is possible, but it is uncommon in this hobby pattern.","structure":[{"lhs":"私の趣味","rhs":"は ＋ 写真を撮る ＋ こと ＋ です","note":"My hobby is taking photos"},{"lhs":"私の夢","rhs":"は ＋ 日本に住む ＋ こと ＋ です","note":"My dream is to live in Japan"},{"lhs":"仕事","rhs":"は ＋ 学生に日本語を教える ＋ こと ＋ です","note":"The job is teaching Japanese"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"students"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教える","r":"おしえる","m":"teach"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My job is teaching Japanese to students.","scene":"When asked about your job"},{"tokens":[{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"な"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What I like is traveling.","scene":"Telling a friend about a hobby"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-074||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern often appears in **self-introductions** and conversations about **hobbies and dreams**. Common topic nouns include 趣味, 夢, 仕事, 好きなこと, and 楽しみ.","nuance":"It is a natural answer to **趣味は何ですか** or **夢は何ですか**. Do not use it for temporary actions such as “What are you doing now?”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"趣味","r":"しゅみ","m":"hobby"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"cooking"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作る","r":"つくる","m":"make"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My hobby is cooking.","scene":"Answering when a new friend asks about hobbies"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"お店","r":"おみせ","m":"shop"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"働く","r":"はたらく","m":"work"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My job is working at a shop.","scene":"Introducing a part-time job"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-074||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"compare","definition":"This pattern is often confused with **NはNです** (a noun statement) and **Vることができます** (ability). Remember: **Vることです** connects an **action** to the topic; **NはNです** identifies or labels something; **Vることができます** expresses ability.","nuance":"To say “My hobby is swimming,” use **泳ぐことです**, not **泳ぐことができます**, which means “I can swim.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"my"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"趣味","r":"しゅみ","m":"hobby"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"泳ぐ","r":"およぐ","m":"swim"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My hobby is swimming.","scene":"Talking with a friend at a sports club"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"NはNです states a noun (私は先生です = I am a teacher). NはVることです states that the content is an action (仕事は教えることです = The job is teaching)."},{"vs":"Vることができます","point":"Vることができます = have the ability to do something (私は泳ぐことができます = I can swim). NはVることです = a hobby/job is doing something (私の趣味は泳ぐことです = My hobby is swimming)."},{"vs":"Vたことがあります","point":"Vたことがあります = have done something before (日本に行ったことがあります = I have been to Japan). NはVることです = a current hobby, not past experience."}]}},"n5-074||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes come from **forgetting こと** or keeping the **Vます form** before です. Always check that before です there is a **noun** or a **Vること** phrase.","nuance":"In casual speech, です may sometimes be omitted, but **こと cannot be omitted** because the nominalized meaning would be lost.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"楽しみ","r":"たのしみ","m":"pleasure"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"散歩","r":"さんぽ","m":"walk"},{"t":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My daily pleasure is walking the dog.","scene":"Talking about a pleasant daily habit"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私の趣味は写真を撮りますです。 → ✅ 私の趣味は写真を撮ることです。","❌ 私の趣味は写真を撮るです。 → ✅ 私の趣味は写真を撮ることです。","❌ 私の趣味は日本に行ったことがあります。 → ✅ 私の趣味は旅行することです。","❌ 私の趣味は泳ぐことができます。 → ✅ 私の趣味は泳ぐことです。"],"compare":null}},"n5-074||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tip","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of こと as “the act of.” 写真を撮ること = **the act of taking photos**. Then “My hobby is the act of taking photos” makes sense, and you remember to include こと.","nuance":"This tip helps you avoid writing “Vるです” and confusing the pattern with Vることができます.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-074||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NはVることです (sở thích) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NはVることです (sở thích)","patternReading":"は〜ことです","coreMeaning":"Hobbies / jobs / dreams = doing something","slug":"phrases","definition":"In JLPT N5 tests and everyday conversation, these phrases appear repeatedly: **趣味は～ことです**, **夢は～ことです**, **仕事は～ことです**, and **好きなことは～ことです**.","nuance":"When you hear **趣味は何ですか**, simply choose a verb for something you enjoy, put it in dictionary form, and add ことです to make a natural answer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"夢","r":"ゆめ","m":"dream"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"big"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"house"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住む","r":"すむ","m":"live"},{"t":"こと","m":"act"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My dream is to live in a big house.","scene":"Talking about a future dream"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-075||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜まえに expresses **an action performed before another action**. The action before まえに always uses the **dictionary form (Vる)**, regardless of the tense of the following clause. Example: 寝るまえに、歯を磨きます。 = Before going to bed, I brush my teeth.","nuance":"Neutral in tone; it simply states **the order of events** without emphasizing emotion or inference. It works in both spoken and written Japanese and is suitable with friends or superiors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"teeth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"磨きます","r":"みがきます","m":"brush"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going to bed, I brush my teeth.","scene":"Before bed in the evening"},{"tokens":[{"t":"出かける","r":"でかける","m":"go out"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"天気","r":"てんき","m":"weather"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認します","r":"かくにんします","m":"check"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going out, I check the weather.","scene":"Before leaving home in the morning"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-075||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern consists of **Vる + まえに**; V always stays in dictionary form. With a noun, use **N + の + まえに**. The following clause can use any tense, politeness level, or negative form.","nuance":"There is no tense conjugation for まえに. Vる means “before doing,” not “before having done.” This differs from English usage in some contexts.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"まえに","note":"Dictionary-form verb; no conjugation"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のまえに","note":"Noun marking an event/place"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食事する","r":"しょくじする","m":"eat"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hands"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗いましょう","r":"あらいましょう","m":"let’s wash"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before eating, let’s wash our hands.","scene":"A parent reminds a child before a meal"},{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"パスポート","m":"passport"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認","r":"かくにん","m":"check"},{"t":"してください","m":"please do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before traveling, please check your passport.","scene":"Staff give a reminder before a trip"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-075||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to say **one action happens before another**, especially for daily routines: before eating, sleeping, or going out. It is very common in daily life and on JLPT N5 questions.","nuance":"Its tone is **objective**, simply describing sequence. It can also give advice with ～てください or ～ましょう. The politeness level depends on the following clause.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"lights"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"消します","r":"けします","m":"turn off"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going to bed, I turn off the lights.","scene":"Before bed"},{"tokens":[{"t":"出かける","r":"でかける","m":"go out"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"鍵","r":"かぎ","m":"keys"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認します","r":"かくにんします","m":"check"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going out, I check my keys.","scene":"Preparing to leave home"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-075||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with Vてから","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare these two often-confused patterns: **Vるまえに** (“before”) and **Vてから** (“after”). Also distinguish them from **Nのまえに** when a noun comes before the pattern.","nuance":"Both express time sequence but in opposite directions. Learners may map “before” to まえに and “after” to あとで; remember that てから also means “after doing.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"teeth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"磨きます","r":"みがきます","m":"brush"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going to bed, I brush my teeth. (Compare: 歯を磨いてから寝ます – I go to bed after brushing my teeth.)","scene":"Seeing the reversed order"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてから","point":"Vるまえに: the following clause happens before V; Vてから: action Vて happens first. Example: 寝るまえに歯を磨く (brush teeth before sleeping) vs 歯を磨いてから寝る (sleep after brushing teeth)."},{"vs":"Nのまえに","point":"Vるまえに comes before an action (V); Nのまえに comes before a noun (event/place). Do not use Nのまえに to mean “before doing something.”"}]}},"n5-075||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common errors & traps","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common errors with ～まえに include **conjugating V before まえに**, **adding の after a verb**, and **confusing it with Vてから**. Remember: まえに always goes with dictionary form.","nuance":"These errors often come from mapping English “before” onto a past-looking verb; Japanese does not use that structure here.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べます","r":"たべます","m":"eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going to school, I eat breakfast.","scene":"Getting ready for school"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 寝ないまえに、歯を磨きます。 → ✅ 寝るまえに、歯を磨きます。 (Use Vる, not Vない.)","❌ 寝たまえに、歯を磨きます。 → ✅ 寝るまえに、歯を磨きます。 (Use Vる, not Vた.)","❌ 寝るのまえに、歯を磨きます。 → ✅ 寝るまえに、歯を磨きます。 (Only nouns use の.)","❌ 歯を磨くまえに、寝ます。(when you mean sleeping after brushing) → ✅ 歯を磨いてから、寝ます。"],"compare":null}},"n5-075||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: associate **まえ** with “front” or “before.” When saying “before doing something,” put the verb in dictionary form and attach **まえに**. If the verb is conjugated, check it again.","nuance":"A personal mnemonic to help you remember quickly.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-075||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vるまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vるまえに","patternReading":"まえに","coreMeaning":"Before doing V (one action happens before another)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These vocabulary phrases with まえに appear often in Minna no Nihongo textbooks and JLPT N5 questions. Learn them as ready-made “phrases.”","nuance":"They describe everyday routines and can be used immediately.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"まえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ストレッチ","m":"stretch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Before going to bed, I stretch.","scene":"An evening routine"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-076||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"core","definition":"「Nのまえに」expresses a time **before** a noun referring to an event or time point. The action in the later clause happens before that point. For example, **食事のまえに** means “before a meal,” and **試験のまえに** means “before an exam.”","nuance":"This is a neutral, polite expression usable in both speech and writing. It simply states the time order clearly, without emphasizing emotion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食事","r":"しょくじ","m":"meal"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hands"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗います","r":"あらいます","m":"wash"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wash my hands before a meal.","scene":"Before eating, Mom says to wash your hands"},{"tokens":[{"t":"試験","r":"しけん","m":"exam"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"よく","r":"よく","m":"thoroughly"},{"t":"勉強しました","r":"べんきょうしました","m":"studied"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I studied hard before the exam.","scene":"Describing studying before exam day"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-076||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & connection","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: **noun + の + まえに**. Do not omit **の**. The later clause describes an action that happens before that noun. The opposite is **Nのあとで** (after N).","nuance":"There is no conjugation and no difference between polite and plain usage. Any tense can be expressed by conjugating the verb in the later clause.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（しょくじ・しけん・かいぎ など）","rhs":"のまえに + ～","note":"N is a noun for an event or time"},{"lhs":"V（辞書形）","rhs":"まえに + ～","note":"With a verb, do not use の"},{"lhs":"Nのまえに、Vました","rhs":"～","note":"Used with the normal past tense"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"コピー","r":"こぴー","m":"copy"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"取ってください","r":"とってください","m":"please photocopy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please photocopy the materials before the meeting.","scene":"A boss asks an employee to prepare materials"},{"tokens":[{"t":"出発","r":"しゅっぱつ","m":"departure"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認しました","r":"かくにんしました","m":"checked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I checked my luggage before departing.","scene":"Describing trip preparations"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-076||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern for actions that happen before a familiar time or event, such as a meal, exam, meeting, or departure. It is very common in daily conversation and at work.","nuance":"Do not use it with ordinary nouns for people or objects, unless the noun represents a time. The later clause often contains a preparation or routine verb.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"確認します","r":"かくにんします","m":"check"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I check my money before shopping.","scene":"Talking about a habit before going shopping"},{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"trip"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"予約","r":"よやく","m":"reservation"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"make"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I make reservations before traveling.","scene":"Talking about trip preparations"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-076||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Easily confused patterns","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish **Nのまえに** from **Vるまえに** and **Nのあとで**. These three patterns are similar but are used differently.","nuance":"Nのまえに follows a noun, Vるまえに follows a verb, and Nのあとで has the opposite meaning to Nのまえに. To say “before doing something,” use Vるまえに, not の.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"会議","r":"かいぎ","m":"meeting"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"チェックします","r":"ちぇっくします","m":"check"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I check the materials before the meeting.","scene":"An employee discusses meeting preparation"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるまえに","point":"Nのまえに is used with a NOUN; Vるまえに is used with a VERB. Ex.: 食事のまえに (before a meal) vs 食べるまえに (before eating)."},{"vs":"Nのあとで","point":"まえに = BEFORE, あとで = AFTER. Ex.: 食事のまえに (before a meal) vs 食事のあとで (after a meal)."}]}},"n5-076||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with **のまえに** include forgetting の, adding の after a verb, or misreading the meaning with a place noun. Learners often make these errors.","nuance":"Always check whether N before の is a time or event noun. With a place noun, のまえに instead has the spatial meaning “in front of.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"顔","r":"かお","m":"face"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗います","r":"あらいます","m":"wash"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I wash my face before breakfast.","scene":"Talking about a morning routine"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食事まえに手を洗います。 → ✅ 食事のまえに手を洗います。","❌ 食べるのまえに手を洗います。 → ✅ 食べるまえに手を洗います。","❌ 試験のまえ勉強しました。 (missing に) → ✅ 試験のまえに勉強しました。","❌ 学校のまえに勉強します (if intended as a time expression) → ✅ 授業のまえに勉強します"],"compare":null}},"n5-076||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"memory","definition":"**前** (まえ) means “in front,” and **の** attaches the noun before it. Together, **Nのまえに** means “in front of N,” and for time it means “before N.” Imagine a time arrow standing before point N.","nuance":"Remember: when you see の, a noun must come before it. If you see a verb, leave の out.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-076||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Nのまえに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nのまえに","patternReading":"のまえに","coreMeaning":"Before N (a time/event noun)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These **noun + のまえに** phrases are very common in N5 textbooks, JLPT questions, and everyday conversation.","nuance":"Memorizing these phrases helps you respond quickly in multiple-choice questions and conversation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"のまえに","r":"のまえに","m":"before"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"コーヒー","r":"こーひー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みます","r":"のみます","m":"drink"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I drink coffee before work.","scene":"Talking about a morning habit at the office"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-077||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜たことがあります describes a past experience that has occurred at least once in one's life. The speaker states that they have done something, without specifying when. あります shows that this experience exists in their accumulated life experience.","nuance":"This is neutral and works with friends, elders, in speech, and in writing. Unlike 〜ました, which reports an event at a specific time, 〜たことがあります reviews an experience and sounds like sharing something from one's past.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行った","r":"いった","m":"went"},{"t":"ことがあります","r":"ことがあります","m":"have done before"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have been to Japan before.","scene":"Telling a friend about countries you have visited."},{"tokens":[{"t":"外国","r":"がいこく","m":"foreign country"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"住んだ","r":"すんだ","m":"lived"},{"t":"ことがあります","r":"ことがあります","m":"have done before"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have lived abroad before.","scene":"Introducing yourself in a job interview."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-077||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure has three parts: the verb in た-form + こと (a nominalizer) + あります. The た-form is conjugated like the て-form: Group 1 changes て→た/で→だ, while Group 2 and irregular verbs simply add た. Examples: 行く → 行った, 食べる → 食べた, する → した, 来る → 来た.","nuance":"There are two negative forms: ことがありません is the ordinary negative, while ことはありません adds は for stronger emphasis: 'as for that event, it does not exist.' To ask a question, add か at the end. In casual speech, が may be omitted: 行ったことある？","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ことがあります","note":"Affirmative: have done before"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ことがありますか","note":"Question: have you ever...?"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ことがありません","note":"Negative: have never done"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"ことはありません","note":"Strong negative"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"飛行機","r":"ひこうき","m":"airplane"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"乗った","r":"のった","m":"rode"},{"t":"ことがあります","r":"ことがあります","m":"have done before"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Have you ever been on an airplane?","scene":"Asking a friend who has never traveled."},{"tokens":[{"t":"おすし","r":"おすし","m":"sushi"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"ate"},{"t":"ことがありません","r":"ことがありません","m":"have never done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have never eaten sushi.","scene":"Answering about Japanese food."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → た形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — う · つ · る → った","Remove the final sound and add った","買う（かう） → `買った（かった）` (buy) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待った（まった）` (wait) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰った（かえった）` (return) ・ 会う（あう） → `会った（あった）` (meet)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — く → いた","Remove く and add いた","書く（かく） → `書いた（かいた）` (write) ・ 聞く（きく） → `聞いた（きいた）` (listen) ・ 歩く（あるく） → `歩いた（あるいた）` (walk)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — ぐ → いだ","Remove ぐ and add いだ (with ゛)","泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳いだ（およいだ）` (swim) ・ 急ぐ（いそぐ） → `急いだ（いそいだ）` (hurry) ・ 脱ぐ（ぬぐ） → `脱いだ（ぬいだ）` (take off)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — す → した","Remove す and add した","話す（はなす） → `話した（はなした）` (speak) ・ 貸す（かす） → `貸した（かした）` (lend) ・ 出す（だす） → `出した（だした）` (take out)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — ぬ · ぶ · む → んだ","Remove the final sound and add んだ","死ぬ（しぬ） → `死んだ（しんだ）` (die) ・ 遊ぶ（あそぶ） → `遊んだ（あそんだ）` (play) ・ 飲む（のむ） → `飲んだ（のんだ）` (drink) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読んだ（よんだ）` (read)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — ichidan: remove る + た","Remove る and add た","食べる（たべる） → `食べた（たべた）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見た（みた）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きた（おきた）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝た（ねた）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — Irregular","Memorize","する → `した` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来た（きた）` (come) ・ 行く（いく） → `行った（いった）` (go)"]]}}},"n5-077||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears in many situations: asking about experience ('have you ever...?'), sharing achievements ('I have...'), and strong negation ('I have never...'). It often occurs with 一度も (not even once) in negative sentences and 何度も (many times) in affirmative sentences.","nuance":"When asked 〜たことがありますか, short answers are usually はい、あります or いいえ、ありません. If the question includes 一度も, the answer normally uses the negative: いいえ、一度も…ありません. This is commonly tested in N5 exercises.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すき焼き","r":"すきやき","m":"sukiyaki"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"ate"},{"t":"ことがあります","r":"ことがあります","m":"have done before"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Have you ever eaten sukiyaki?","scene":"Asking a coworker at a party."},{"tokens":[{"t":"一度も","r":"いちども","m":"not even once"},{"t":"刺身","r":"さしみ","m":"sashimi"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"ate"},{"t":"ことがありません","r":"ことがありません","m":"have never done"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have never eaten sashimi, not even once.","scene":"Declining sashimi at a restaurant."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-077||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with Vました and Vることがあります, two forms that are easy to confuse. Vました describes an action that happened at a definite time (yesterday, last year, etc.). Vたことがあります only states that the experience exists. Vることがあります means something happens sometimes in the present, not that you have experienced it.","nuance":"Compared with 〜たことがあります, 〜ました sounds more objective because it simply reports an event. 〜たことがあります is more personal and highlights experience. 〜ることがあります is entirely different: it describes a present habit or occasional occurrence, not the past.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mount Fuji"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"登った","r":"のぼった","m":"climbed"},{"t":"ことがあります","r":"ことがあります","m":"have done before"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have climbed Mount Fuji before.","scene":"Telling a friend after a vacation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vました","point":"Vました reports an event at a specific time; Vたことがあります describes experience without needing a time. Example: 去年 富士山に登りました (I climbed it last year) vs 富士山に登ったことがあります (I have climbed it before)."},{"vs":"Vることがあります","point":"Vることがあります means 'sometimes do V' in the present; Vたことがあります means 'have done V before' as past experience. Example: 時々 刺身を食べることがあります (I sometimes eat sashimi) vs 刺身を食べたことがあります (I have eaten sashimi before)."}]}},"n5-077||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: using the wrong verb form (it must be た, not て or る), adding a specific time and losing the experience meaning, and confusing it with Vています (an action in progress).","nuance":"When you see a phrase like 'yesterday' or 'last week,' use Vました instead of Vたことがあります. For an ongoing action, use Vています. Vたことがあります is only for whether an experience exists.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行った","r":"いった","m":"went"},{"t":"こと","r":"こと","m":"event"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have been to Japan before.","scene":"A standard correct sentence for fixing an error."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 日本へ行きますことがあります。 → ✅ 日本へ行ったことがあります。 (Do not use the ます-form before こと)","❌ 昨日、富士山に登ったことがあります。 → ✅ 昨日、富士山に登りました。 (With a specific time, use Vました)","❌ 今、食べたことがあります。 → ✅ 今、食べています。 (For an ongoing action, use Vています)","❌ 一度もすしを食べたことがあります。 → ✅ 一度もすしを食べたことがありません。 (一度も requires a negative)"],"compare":null}},"n5-077||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember the structure as an image: Vた (done) + こと (event) + あります (exists). It means 'the event I did exists in my life.' When you want to say 'have done before,' ask yourself whether your experience store contains that event.","nuance":"This tip helps you remember the た-form: because the action is completed, the verb must be in the た-form, like recounting something that happened. It also explains why a specific time is not needed: something in your experience store does not require a date.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-077||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vたことがあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vたことがあります","patternReading":"たことがあります","coreMeaning":"Have done V before (life experience)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In JLPT N5 tests and everyday conversation, these phrases appear often: 〜たことがありますか (asking about experience), 一度も〜たことがありません (never, not even once), and 〜たことはありますが (have done it, but... — for contrast).","nuance":"The phrase 〜たことはありますが is useful for saying 'I have done it, but...' in a formal conversation. It can sound stiff with close friends; use やったことあるけど instead.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"富士山","r":"ふじさん","m":"Mount Fuji"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"登った","r":"のぼった","m":"climbed"},{"t":"ことは","r":"ことは","m":"as for the event"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"again"},{"t":"一度","r":"いちど","m":"one time"},{"t":"登りたくない","r":"のぼりたくない","m":"do not want to climb"},{"t":"です","r":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have climbed Mount Fuji, but I do not want to climb it again.","scene":"Talking with a coworker about a vacation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-078||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"core","definition":"The 〜たり〜たりします pattern **lists typical actions**, not a complete list. The speaker gives 2–3 examples, implying other actions as well. The closest meaning is “sometimes do this, sometimes do that.”","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. Its tone is casual and gentle, often describing habits or experiences. Do not use it for a single action. The actions may alternate and need not follow a fixed order.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読んだり","r":"よんだり","m":"read, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"聞いたり","r":"きいたり","m":"listen, for example"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On my days off, I read books, listen to music, and do other things.","scene":"Asked what you usually do on days off."},{"tokens":[{"t":"子ども","r":"こども","m":"children"},{"t":"たち"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"走ったり","r":"はしったり","m":"run, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遊んだり","r":"あそんだり","m":"play, for example"},{"t":"しています"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The children are running and playing in the park.","scene":"Watching children play in a park."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-078||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"forms","definition":"This is a **linking pattern**: change each verb to the た-form, then add り. The final verb connects to **します／しました／しています**. Tense, negation, and politeness are expressed by the final verb.","nuance":"In casual speech, します may be omitted at the end, but at N5 it is safest and most natural to use the full 〜たり〜たりします pattern.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"＋ り","note":"Example: 読んだ → 読んだり"},{"lhs":"Vたり + Vたり","rhs":"＋ します","note":"The final verb shows tense: します／しました／しています"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"パン","m":"bread"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べたり","r":"たべたり","m":"eat, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"サラダ","m":"salad"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べたり","r":"たべたり","m":"eat, for example"},{"t":"しました"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ate bread, salad, and other things.","scene":"Talking about a party you attended."},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"話したり","r":"はなしたり","m":"talk, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ゲーム","m":"game"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"したり","m":"play, for example"},{"t":"しました"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I talked with friends, played games, and did other things.","scene":"Texting a friend about yesterday."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-078||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"usage","definition":"This branch shows **when to use 〜たり〜たりします**: when listing activities as examples without listing everything. It often combines with **休みの日は**, **昨日**, or **旅行** to talk about habits or experiences.","nuance":"Use **しています** for ongoing actions and **しました** for the past. In questions, simply raise the intonation: 〜たり〜たりしますか. Japanese speakers often use this in daily conversation when describing what they did that day.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"掃除","r":"そうじ","m":"cleaning"},{"t":"したり","m":"do, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"洗濯","r":"せんたく","m":"laundry"},{"t":"したり","m":"do, for example"},{"t":"しました"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I cleaned, did laundry, and did other things at home.","scene":"Telling a friend by phone about your day off."},{"tokens":[{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"日","r":"ひ","m":"day"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行ったり","r":"いったり","m":"go, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見たり","r":"みたり","m":"watch, for example"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On my days off, I go shopping with friends, watch movies, and do other things.","scene":"Talking about weekend relaxation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-078||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"compare","definition":"**～たり～たりします** is often confused with **V1てV2** and **NやN**. The key difference is that ～たり～たり **lists typical activities** without implying order or completeness; V1てV2 emphasizes **sequence**; NやN lists **nouns**, not verbs. Understanding this prevents errors in conversation and on tests.","nuance":"This comparison helps distinguish three common beginner patterns. Remember: ～たり～たり means “list examples,” ～て means “connect in sequence,” and ～や means “list nouns.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"週末","r":"しゅうまつ","m":"weekend"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"走ったり","r":"はしったり","m":"run, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photos"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮ったり","r":"とったり","m":"take, for example"},{"t":"しました"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On the weekend, I went running, took photos, and did other things.","scene":"Answering “Did you do anything fun this weekend?”"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"V1てV2","point":"～て emphasizes a SEQUENCE of actions; ～たり～たり merely LISTS examples, so order is unimportant. 朝起きて顔を洗います vs 朝走ったり、本を読んだりします."},{"vs":"NやN（など）","point":"～や～ connects NOUNS; ～たり～たり connects VERBS/activities. パンや牛乳を買いました vs パンを買ったり、牛乳を買ったりしました."}]}},"n5-078||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make these mistakes: using **て-form** instead of **た-form** for linking, listing only **one action**, or forgetting **します** at the end. Remember: change each verb to the **た-form**, add り, and put **します／しました／しています** at the end. This makes the pattern accurate and natural.","nuance":"These errors often come from translating “while doing…” directly or listing only one action. Always check: are there at least two actions? Does the sentence end with a します verb?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"走ったり","r":"はしったり","m":"run, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遊んだり","r":"あそんだり","m":"play, for example"},{"t":"しました"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I ran, played, and did other things in the park.","scene":"Talking about an afternoon in the park."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 音楽を聞いて、本を読みます。 → ✅ 音楽を聞いたり、本を読んだりします。 (To list examples, use た-form + り, not て-form)","❌ 昨日テレビを見たりしました。 → ✅ 昨日テレビを見ました。 (Do not use ～たり for only one action)","❌ 昨日はテレビを見たり、本を読んだりでした。 → ✅ 昨日はテレビを見たり、本を読んだりしました。 (The sentence must end with します/しました)","❌ ひらがなを書いたり、読んだり。 → ✅ ひらがなを書いたり、読んだりします。 (A complete sentence needs します)"],"compare":null}},"n5-078||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"memory","definition":"A quick memory image: “tari” is the た-form “ta” plus “ri,” like an ellipsis … showing that the list remains open and incomplete.","nuance":"Remember the chant “ta... ri”: ta is the た-form, and ri is the “...” sign. When you see verb + たり, the speaker is listing examples, not everything.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-078||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V1たりV2たりします · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1たりV2たりします","patternReading":"たりたりします","coreMeaning":"Listing typical actions: sometimes this, sometimes that","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases used with 〜たり〜たりします, often seen on tests and in conversation.","nuance":"At N5, remember these phrase sets: 本を読んだり〜, 音楽を聞いたり〜, 遊んだり〜, 買い物をしたり〜. On tests, watch for た-form + り.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photos"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮ったり","r":"とったり","m":"take, for example"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"したり","m":"do, for example"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When traveling, I take photos, shop, and do many other things.","scene":"Sharing about a recent trip."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-079||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"core","definition":"〜くなる・〜になる describes a person or thing changing state and becoming a new state. なる means “to become.” The speaker observes the present result: the weather gets cold, one improves, or a tree grows.","nuance":"Used widely in both everyday conversation and writing. It emphasizes a process or result of change, rather than describing a static state. Compared with 〜です, it suggests “it was different before, but now it has become ~.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"上手","r":"じょうず","m":"good"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My Japanese has improved.","scene":"After studying for some time, you tell your friends."},{"tokens":[{"t":"秋","r":"あき","m":"autumn"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なって","r":"なって","m":"became"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"寒く","r":"さむく","m":"cold"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When autumn came, it became cold.","scene":"Commenting on the seasonal change."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-079||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"forms","definition":"These are the rules for connecting adjectives and nouns to なる. With an i-adjective, drop い and add く. With a na-adjective, drop な and add に. Nouns also connect with に.","nuance":"The pattern has polite present/future forms (〜くなります／〜になります) and polite past forms (〜くなりました／〜になりました). In casual speech, these are often shortened to 〜くなる／〜になる.","structure":[{"lhs":"Aい（〜い）","rhs":"〜く + なる","note":"寒い → 寒くなる"},{"lhs":"な-adj（〜な）","rhs":"〜に + なる","note":"静か → 静かになる"},{"lhs":"Danh từ","rhs":"〜に + なる","note":"先生 → 先生になる"},{"lhs":"Lịch sự","rhs":"〜くなります／〜になります","note":"寒い → 寒くなります"},{"lhs":"Quá khứ lịch sự","rhs":"〜くなりました／〜になりました","note":"上手 → 上手になりました"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"夏","r":"なつ","m":"summer"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"暑く","r":"あつく","m":"hot"},{"t":"なります","r":"なります","m":"becomes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In summer, it gets hot.","scene":"Hearing a seasonal weather forecast."},{"tokens":[{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大きく","r":"おおきく","m":"large"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My son has grown.","scene":"A mother proudly tells a neighbor."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-079||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when someone or something moves from an old state to a new one: the weather gets cold, someone grows up, skills improve, or an illness gets better.","nuance":"It can be used with degree adverbs such as だんだん (gradually) and とても (very). The subject need not be stated when it is clear from context. The pattern expresses an existing result, not an intentional action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"暖かく","r":"あたたかく","m":"warm"},{"t":"なります","r":"なります","m":"becomes"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In spring, it gets warm.","scene":"Describing a feature of spring."},{"tokens":[{"t":"娘","r":"むすめ","m":"daughter"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"優しく","r":"やさしく","m":"gentle"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My daughter has become gentler.","scene":"Talking with a colleague about your children."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-079||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions & common mix-ups","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with easily confused forms. The key point is that なる describes a change, する describes an intentional action, and です describes a static state. This helps learners choose the right pattern in conversation.","nuance":"Each comparison focuses only on the most important difference, keeping the distinction easy to remember.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寒く","r":"さむく","m":"cold"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"ね"}],"vi":"It’s getting cold, isn’t it?","scene":"Going outside and feeling the cold."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはAです","point":"〜です describes a current state; 〜くなる／になる reports a change. Example: 寒いです (it is cold) vs 寒くなりました (it became cold)."},{"vs":"Nになります","point":"Nouns and na-adjectives use になる, but i-adjectives change to く before なる. Example: 先生になる (become a teacher) vs 大きくなる (become large)."},{"vs":"Aくする／NAにする","point":"する expresses an intentional change, while なる is natural. Example: 部屋をきれいにする (I make the room clean) vs 部屋がきれいになる (the room becomes clean)."}]}},"n5-079||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse i-adjectives and na-adjectives when connecting them to なる. Rule: an i-adjective ending in い changes い to く, while a na-adjective uses に. Mastering this rule builds accurate habits.","nuance":"This mistake is common at beginner level, but correcting it early helps establish the right instinct.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"風邪","r":"かぜ","m":"cold"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"治って","r":"なおって","m":"recover"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"well"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My cold is gone, and I feel well again.","scene":"Telling a friend you have recovered."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 大きいになる → ✅ 大きくなる (大きい is an i-adjective, so drop い and add く)","❌ 静かくなる → ✅ 静かになる (静か is a na-adjective, so drop な and add に)","❌ きれいくなる → ✅ きれいになる (きれい is a na-adjective, even though it ends in い)"],"compare":null}},"n5-079||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember the changes, imagine removing い and attaching く to an i-adjective; with a na-adjective, remove な and replace it with に.","nuance":"This mnemonic helps prevent mix-ups and builds faster speaking reflexes.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-079||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Aくなります／NAになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aくなります／NAになります","patternReading":"くなります / になります","coreMeaning":"Become ~ (change of state)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These phrases are common in everyday conversation and on tests. Memorizing them helps you respond faster in speaking and writing.","nuance":"All of these phrases use なる to express a positive or natural change.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もっと","m":"more"},{"t":"元気","r":"げんき","m":"well"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なりました","r":"なりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I feel much better now.","scene":"Replying when friends ask how you are after an illness."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-080||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nになります","patternReading":"になります","coreMeaning":"Become N — a new role, job, season, or state","slug":"core","definition":"N + になります describes a new noun state that appears after a change: becoming a new role, job, season, or state. It emphasizes the RESULT of change, unlike NはNです, which only states identity.","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing. With people, 〜になりたい expresses a dream; 〜になりました shows completion. になります is neutral and polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"になりたい","r":"になりたい","m":"want to become"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the future, I want to become a doctor.","scene":"Asking about career dreams"},{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"になりました","r":"になりました","m":"has become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Spring has arrived.","scene":"Noticing warmer weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-080||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nになります","patternReading":"になります","coreMeaning":"Become N — a new role, job, season, or state","slug":"forms","definition":"Formula: **Noun + に + なる**. The group 1 verb なる becomes なります (polite), なりました (past), なりたい (want to), and なりません (negative).","nuance":"なります often indicates the future or politeness; なりました emphasizes completion; なりたい expresses a wish; なりません is negative.","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"になります","note":"will become N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"になりました","note":"became N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"になりたいです","note":"want to become N"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"になりません","note":"will not become N"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"来年","r":"らいねん","m":"next year"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"になります","r":"になります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Next year, I will become a teacher.","scene":"Telling a friend about future plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"息子","r":"むすこ","m":"son"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"大学生","r":"だいがくせい","m":"college student"},{"t":"になりました","r":"になりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My son has become a college student.","scene":"Proudly telling a colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-080||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nになります","patternReading":"になります","coreMeaning":"Become N — a new role, job, season, or state","slug":"usage","definition":"Commonly used for changes in jobs, positions, seasons, time, or life circumstances. At N5, remember these phrases: 医者になる, 先生になる, 春になる...","nuance":"Moderately formal and natural in everyday conversation. Do not use it for adjective changes; use Aくなる.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"休み","r":"やすみ","m":"day off"},{"t":"になります","r":"になります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting tomorrow, we will have a break.","scene":"A boss announces it at day's end"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来月","r":"らいげつ","m":"next month"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"job"},{"t":"になります","r":"になります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Starting next month, I will move to a new job.","scene":"Discussing an internal company change"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-080||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing from NはNです","sub":"Nになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nになります","patternReading":"になります","coreMeaning":"Become N — a new role, job, season, or state","slug":"compare","definition":"Quick comparison: **NはNです** confirms what something is; **Nになります** announces a change to a new state.","nuance":"です follows a noun that is already true; になります suggests a transition point.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"になりました","r":"になりました","m":"became"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He became a doctor.","scene":"Discussing a friend's career change"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはNです","point":"です describes the present or identity; になります describes the future or a change. Example: 彼は医者です (he is a doctor) vs 彼は医者になりました (he became a doctor)."},{"vs":"Aくなります","point":"Aくなる is for い-adjectives (become...); Nになります is for nouns (become...)."}]}},"n5-080||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nになります","patternReading":"になります","coreMeaning":"Become N — a new role, job, season, or state","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse になります with です, omit に, or use じゃありません for the future. Watch for these patterns.","nuance":"To say “become,” に is required. The negative of なる is なりません, not じゃありません.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"将来","r":"しょうらい","m":"future"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"になります","r":"になります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"In the future, I will become a doctor.","scene":"Stating a dream correctly"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 将来、医者です。 → ✅ 将来、医者になります。","❌ 医者なります。 → ✅ 医者になります。","❌ 医者になりではありません。 → ✅ 医者になりません。"],"compare":null}},"n5-080||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nになります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nになります","patternReading":"になります","coreMeaning":"Become N — a new role, job, season, or state","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine the noun as a NEW ROOM, に as an ARROW entering it, and なる as “become”—entering the room means becoming the person or thing there.","nuance":"This trick helps distinguish に from は (topic marker) and を (object): に always marks the destination of the change.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-081||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"core","definition":"The plain form is the **non-polite form** of verbs, adjectives, and nouns. It has four forms: present affirmative, present negative, past affirmative, and past negative. It is the **basis** for linking clauses and for casual speech.","nuance":"It is not formal; replace です/ます with it when speaking with friends or family. In subordinate clauses, the plain form is required even when the main clause is polite.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study every day.","scene":"Answering a friend who asks what you usually do"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒かった","r":"さむかった","m":"was cold"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It was cold yesterday.","scene":"Talking with a friend about yesterday's weather"},{"tokens":[{"t":"これ","r":"これ","m":"this"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"This is a book.","scene":"Introducing an object to a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-081||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"forms","definition":"A four-form conjugation table for **V**, **Aい**, **Aな**, and **N**. Each type has its own rules, but all use the same present/past × affirmative/negative framework.","nuance":"The negative of **Aな/N** uses じゃない (concise) or ではない (formal). With **Aい**, do not add だ in the negative.","structure":[{"lhs":"V","rhs":"Vる / Vない / Vた / Vなかった","note":"Conjugate by verb group; た/ない follow sound-change rules."},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"Aい / Aくない / Aかった / Aくなかった"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"Aだ / Aじゃない / Aだった / Aじゃなかった"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"Nだ / Nじゃない / Nだった / Nじゃなかった"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"am"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am a student.","scene":"Answering 'Who are you?' when meeting a new friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ない","r":"みない","m":"not watch"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I'm not watching TV.","scene":"Telling a younger sibling, 'I'm not watching TV tonight'"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → なかった形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 う・つ・る → わなかった・たなかった・らなかった","う→わ, つ→た, る→ら (あ-row) + なかった","買う（かう） → `買わなかった（かわなかった）` (buy) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待たなかった（またなかった）` (wait) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰らなかった（かえらなかった）` (return) ・ 会う（あう） → `会わなかった（あわなかった）` (meet)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 く・ぐ・す → かなかった・がなかった・さなかった","く→か, ぐ→が, す→さ (あ-row) + なかった","書く（かく） → `書かなかった（かかなかった）` (write) ・ 泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳がなかった（およがなかった）` (swim) ・ 話す（はなす） → `話さなかった（はなさなかった）` (speak) ・ 聞く（きく） → `聞かなかった（きかなかった）` (listen)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 ぬ・ぶ・む → ななかった・ばなかった・まなかった","ぬ→な, ぶ→ば, む→ま (あ-row) + なかった","死ぬ（しぬ） → `死ななかった（しななかった）` (die) ・ 遊ぶ（あそぶ） → `遊ばなかった（あそばなかった）` (play) ・ 飲む（のむ） → `飲まなかった（のまなかった）` (drink) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読まなかった（よまなかった）` (read)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 る → らなかった","る → ら (あ-row) + なかった","帰る（かえる） → `帰らなかった（かえらなかった）` (return) ・ 走る（はしる） → `走らなかった（はしらなかった）` (run) ・ 切る（きる） → `切らなかった（きらなかった）` (cut)"],["**Group II — Ichidan (一段)** — 一段 → なかった","Remove る and add なかった","食べる（たべる） → `食べなかった（たべなかった）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見なかった（みなかった）` (watch) ・ 起きる（おきる） → `起きなかった（おきなかった）` (wake up) ・ 寝る（ねる） → `寝なかった（ねなかった）` (sleep)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — Irregular + special","Memorize: する→しなかった · 来る→こなかった · ある→なかった","する → `しなかった` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来なかった（こなかった）` (come) ・ ある → `なかった` (exist (objects))"]]}}},"n5-081||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"usage","definition":"The plain form is used in **casual simple sentences**, **short casual questions**, and **subordinate clauses** before と/から/とき/と思います. It also appears in diary entries and short essays.","nuance":"Casual questions usually **drop か** and use rising intonation. In quotations, the clause must be in the plain form.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"What are you eating?","scene":"Asking a friend during lunch break"},{"tokens":[{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見る","r":"みる","m":"watch"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"思う","r":"おもう","m":"think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think I'll watch a movie.","scene":"Telling a friend about tonight's plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","r":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰る","r":"かえる","m":"go home"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"？"}],"vi":"Are you leaving already?","scene":"Seeing a friend prepare to leave"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-081||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Plain vs です/ます","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguishing the plain form from the polite forms です/ます is basic but essential Japanese grammar. The plain form is used with friends and family in casual speech, and in subordinate clauses such as と、から、とき. The polite form is used with older people, superiors, or new acquaintances. Understanding this difference helps you match politeness to the situation and sound natural.","nuance":"The plain form is not 'wrong'; it expresses a **different level of formality**. Use the polite form with superiors and the plain form with friends.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He is a student.","scene":"Talking with friends about another friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"です","point":"The meaning is the same, but the plain form **drops です** and uses forms such as Nだ, Aい, and Vる. Using it with the wrong person may sound rude."},{"vs":"ます","point":"Vます appears only at the end of a polite sentence; before と, とき, と思います… it must change to **Vる/Vた/Vない**."},{"vs":"ですか","point":"Plain-form questions drop か and use rising intonation; for example, 学生？ rather than 学生ですか."}]}},"n5-081||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes when changing from the polite to the plain form include keeping a polite ending, misforming the past negative, or adding だ where it does not belong. Learners often rebuild sentences from their English translations, which can lead to incorrect Japanese word order. Master the four-form table for each word type, check that subordinate clauses use the plain form, and never add だ to a negative い-adjective.","nuance":"Mistakes usually come from **keeping a polite ending** or **forming the past negative incorrectly**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"食べなかった","r":"たべなかった","m":"did not eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I didn't eat anything yesterday.","scene":"Answering a friend who asks what you ate yesterday"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は学生ですだ。 → ✅ 私は学生だ。 (do not combine です with だ)","❌ 毎日勉強しますと思う → ✅ 毎日勉強すると思う (use the plain form before と)","❌ 行きなかった → ✅ 行かなかった (do not form it as 行き+なかった)","❌ 寒くないだ → ✅ 寒くない (a negative い-adj takes no だ)"],"compare":null}},"n5-081||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tips for the plain-form conjugation table using a **2×2 table** and sound-change rules.","nuance":"Always think in pairs: **affirmative ↔ negative** and **present ↔ past**, rather than memorizing forms separately.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-081||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V/A/NAの普通形 · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/A/NAの普通形","patternReading":"ふつうけい","coreMeaning":"Plain form: 4 patterns, the basis for linking clauses","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common conversational phrases that use the plain form in everyday speech.","nuance":"All are **casual expressions** and should not be used with superiors.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大丈夫","r":"だいじょうぶ","m":"okay"},{"t":"だ","r":"だ","m":"is"},{"t":"よ"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"It's okay.","scene":"Reassuring someone who is worried"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-082||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"core","definition":"Vないと is the conversational short form of Vないといけない / Vないとだめ. The rest is often omitted, and the listener understands いけない/だめ. Core meaning: the speaker believes that they or someone else must do V, or something bad will happen.","nuance":"Used in everyday conversation with friends, family, and close colleagues. It is less formal than Vなければなりません. With a superior or stranger, use Vなければなりません. It can sound like self-reminding or a gentle reminder. Omitting the rest makes the sentence short and natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"早く","r":"はやく","m":"early"},{"t":"帰らないと","r":"かえらないと","m":"must go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to get home early.","scene":"Leaving work late; thinking of going home"},{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しないと","m":"must do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to do my homework.","scene":"Remembering unfinished homework"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"寝ないと","r":"ねないと","m":"must sleep"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to go to bed.","scene":"It is late; reminding oneself to sleep"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-082||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern consists of a **ない-form verb (Vない)** + the particle **と**. と expresses the condition 'if... then...'. The rest is often omitted, but the full forms are **Vないといけない** or **Vないとだめ**. Example: 行かないと = 行かないといけない.","nuance":"Vないと cannot directly express the past; for the past, use Vないといけなかった or Vないと（いけなかった）. This is a casual form and is not used in formal writing. For politeness, use Vなければなりません.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vない形","rhs":"と","note":"Example: 行かないと"},{"lhs":"Vないと","rhs":"いけない","note":"Full form: 行かないといけない"},{"lhs":"Vないと","rhs":"だめ","note":"Full, casual: 食べないとだめ"},{"lhs":"Vないと","rhs":"（省略）","note":"Implied: 早く帰らないと。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しないと","m":"must do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to study.","scene":"Thinking about the upcoming exam"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"から"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しないと","m":"must"},{"t":"いけない","m":"not OK"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a test tomorrow, so I have to study.","scene":"Explaining to a friend why one must study"},{"tokens":[{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲まないと","r":"のまないと","m":"must take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You have to take your medicine.","scene":"A mother reminds her child to take medicine"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-082||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"usage","definition":"Vないと is common in everyday conversation for **reminding yourself**, **gently reminding family or friends**, **announcing that you must leave**, and more. Add ね or なあ for a more casual feel. For a more polite version, use Vないといけません.","nuance":"Depending on intonation, Vないと can sound like a private thought or a gentle reminder. Do not use it directly with a superior because it is a casual short form. For politeness, use Vないといけません or Vなければなりません. It conveys urgency or necessity.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"行かないと","r":"いかないと","m":"must go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to get going.","scene":"At a friend's house; it is late, saying goodbye"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"急がないと","r":"いそがないと","m":"must hurry"},{"t":"！"}],"vi":"I have to hurry!","scene":"About to be late for work; speaking while running"},{"tokens":[{"t":"ご飯","r":"ごはん","m":"a meal"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べないと","r":"たべないと","m":"must eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"You have to eat.","scene":"A mother reminds her child to eat"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-082||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with similar patterns","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"compare","definition":"Vないと is often confused with other patterns. The key difference is that **Vないと is casual** and shortened from Vないといけない, while Vなければなりません is formal. Their meanings are similar, but their levels of politeness differ greatly.","nuance":"Use Vないと in casual conversation, and Vなければなりません in writing, speeches, or with superiors. Vないと is fine with friends, but use Vなければなりません with a boss.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"しないと","m":"must do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to study every day.","scene":"Telling a friend about studying Japanese"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vなければなりません","point":"Both mean 'must do', but Vなければなりません is formal, used in writing or with superiors; Vないと is casual and conversational."},{"vs":"Vてはいけません","point":"Vてはいけません means 'must not do' or 'doing is prohibited', the opposite of Vないと ('must do'). Both contain いけない/いけません, which can cause confusion."}]}},"n5-082||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common mistakes with Vないと include **omitting と**, **using it with a superior**, and **conjugating the shortened form for tense**. Remember: **と is required**, the form is casual, and past meaning uses the full past form.","nuance":"Although shortened, Vないと is still casual. With teachers, bosses, or customers, use Vなければなりません. For the past, use Vないといけなかった.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しないと","m":"must do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to do my homework.","scene":"Reminding oneself"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 宿題しない。 → ✅ 宿題しないと。 (Missing と; the meaning 'must do' is lost)","❌ 社長に「早く帰らないと」 → ✅ 社長に「早く帰らなければなりません」 (Do not use Vないと with a superior; it is not polite enough)","❌ 昨日、勉強しないと。 → ✅ 昨日、勉強しないといけなかった。 (For the past, add いけなかった)","❌ 薬を飲まない (implies must take it?) → ✅ 薬を飲まないと。 (と is needed to express the condition)"],"compare":null}},"n5-082||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"memory","definition":"To remember Vないと, think: Vない + と = **if I do not V, then...**. The rest is cut off, but we understand it as 'not OK'. Since 'if I do not V, it is not OK', the result is 'I must V'.","nuance":"This trick helps you remember both the form and the meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-082||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vないと (ngắn) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないと (ngắn)","patternReading":"ないと","coreMeaning":"Must do ~ (otherwise it won't work)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In everyday conversation, several phrases commonly use Vないと. Memorizing them helps you respond more quickly.","nuance":"These phrases often take ね、なあ, or よ for a more natural feel.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"そろそろ","m":"soon"},{"t":"行かないと","r":"いかないと","m":"must go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I should get going soon.","scene":"Preparing to leave where one is visiting"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"片付けないと","r":"かたづけないと","m":"must tidy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to tidy up.","scene":"Seeing a messy room; reminding oneself"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入らないと","r":"はいらないと","m":"must take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have to take a bath.","scene":"Coming home from work and wanting a bath"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-083||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **NがNにあります** expresses the **existence** of a nonliving thing (an object, plant, or abstract event) at a specific location. The particle **が** marks the existing thing, while **に** marks its location. If the subject is a person or animal, use **います** instead of **あります**.","nuance":"This is an **objective statement** that does not emphasize emotion. It works in both speech and writing, from casual to formal contexts. In speech, when asking for a specific location, people often use **〜はどこにありますか**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"机","r":"つくえ","m":"desk"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"上","r":"うえ","m":"on top"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a book on the desk.","scene":"You notice a book on a desk"},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"近く","r":"ちかく","m":"near"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"コンビニ","r":"コンビニ","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a convenience store near the station.","scene":"Telling someone about a store near the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-083||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"forms","definition":"The conjugated forms of **あります** are: negative **ありません**, past **ありました**, and past negative **ありませんでした**. Add **か** at the end to form a question. The location can be a place noun directly (がっこう, へや), or a noun + possessive particle **の** + location noun (上, 中, 近く, 前...).","nuance":"In negative sentences, speakers often replace **が** with **は** to create contrast or emphasis: **冷蔵庫にビールはありません** (there is no beer in the refrigerator). This is natural usage, not an error. When asking, besides **〜がありますか**, use **〜はどこにありますか** to ask for a specific location.","structure":[{"lhs":"N1 (vật)","rhs":"が N2 (nơi) に あります","note":"Present affirmative"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"が N2 に ありません","note":"Present negative"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"が N2 に ありました","note":"Past affirmative"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"が N2 に ありませんでした","note":"Past negative"},{"lhs":"N1","rhs":"が N2 に ありますか","note":"Question"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"椅子","r":"いす","m":"chair"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a chair in the room.","scene":"Entering a room and describing its furniture"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"椅子","r":"いす","m":"chair"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"does not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is no chair in the room.","scene":"Looking for a chair but finding none"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-083||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"usage","definition":"This pattern appears in many real-life situations: describing where objects are (books, desks, chairs, refrigerators), asking for and giving directions (restrooms, stores, hospitals), or talking about events and appointments (meetings, parties, things to do).","nuance":"When asking for directions politely, add **すみません** at the beginning: **すみません、この近くにトイレがありますか。** To emphasize the object, replace **が** with **は**: **本は机の上にあります。** (The book is on the desk.)","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"すみません","m":"excuse me"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"近く","r":"ちかく","m":"nearby"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"トイレ","r":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"か"}],"vi":"Excuse me, is there a restroom nearby?","scene":"A traveler asks a local for a restroom"},{"tokens":[{"t":"部屋","r":"へや","m":"room"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"中","r":"なか","m":"inside"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"机","r":"つくえ","m":"desk"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a desk in the room.","scene":"Introducing a new office to a colleague"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-083||compare":{"title":"⚖️ あります vs います vs です","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this with easily confused patterns: **あります** is for inanimate things; **います** is for people, animals, and insects. **です** identifies or classifies a subject rather than stating existence. Also, **に** marks the location of existence, while **で** marks where an action occurs and is not used for existence.","nuance":"In '本は机の上です', the listener understands 'the book is on the desk'—the location of a known object. In '机の上に本があります', the speaker simply announces that a book exists on the desk—introducing new information.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"公園","r":"こうえん","m":"park"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"ベンチ","r":"ベンチ","m":"bench"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a bench in the park.","scene":"Describing amenities in a park"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NがNにいます","point":"Use います for people, animals, and living things; use あります for objects and events. Examples: 公園に子供がいます / 公園にベンチがあります."},{"vs":"NはNです","point":"です identifies the subject or its location; it does not emphasize existence. Example: 本は机の上です (the book is on the desk—stating a known object's location) / 机の上に本があります (there is a book on the desk—introducing its existence)."},{"vs":"NでNがあります","point":"で marks where an action occurs (eating in a room, working at a company). に marks existence. Do not use で with あります: ❌ 部屋で机があります → ✅ 部屋に机があります."}]}},"n5-083||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes & traps","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make three mistakes: using **あります** for people or animals, using **で** instead of **に** for a location of existence, and reversing the sentence order. Remember that **は** can replace **が** in negative sentences while remaining grammatically correct.","nuance":"In a negative sentence, use **が** when there is no contrast: '部屋に椅子がありません' (there is no chair in the room). To emphasize the missing item, use **は**: '傘はありません' (there is no umbrella).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"冷蔵庫","r":"れいぞうこ","m":"refrigerator"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"卵","r":"たまご","m":"eggs"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"does not exist"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There are no eggs in the refrigerator.","scene":"Opening the refrigerator and finding no eggs"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 公園に子供があります。 → ✅ 公園に子供がいます。","❌ 机の上で本があります。 → ✅ 机の上に本があります。","❌ 本があります机の上に。 → ✅ 机の上に本があります。","❌ 冷蔵庫でビールはありません。 → ✅ 冷蔵庫にビールはありません。"],"compare":null}},"n5-083||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **A**ru starts with **A**, as in **A**rtifact, meaning an inanimate object. **I**ru starts with **I**, as in **I**ndividual, meaning a living individual.","nuance":"You can also picture it this way: 'Aru' sounds still, like an object lying motionless; 'Iru' sounds active, like a living creature moving. Remember: inanimate things use あります, while people and animals use います.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-083||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NがNにあります · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがNにあります","patternReading":"がにあります","coreMeaning":"There is an inanimate object somewhere","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases in N5 textbooks include '〜の上にあります' (on), '〜の中にあります' (inside), '〜の近くにあります' (near), and '〜の前/後ろ/左/右にあります'. Also, '〜があります' is used for events: '明日、会議があります' (there is a meeting tomorrow).","nuance":"When asking for directions, Japanese speakers often say 'すみません、〜はどこにありますか'—a very polite way to ask for a location.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"前","r":"まえ","m":"in front"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"自動販売機","r":"じどうはんばいき","m":"vending machine"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"exists"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"There is a vending machine in front of the station.","scene":"You see a vending machine by the station entrance"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-084||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"core","definition":"〜と思います is the **most common** beginner-level pattern for stating your own opinion, impression, or guess. It combines content (a clause in plain form) with the verb 思う (to think), connected by と.","nuance":"This pattern sounds **gentle and polite** and works in most situations. It is clearly subjective—the speaker gives their own thought without asserting it as certain fact. Adding か turns it into a question asking for the listener's opinion: 'Do you think...?'","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降る","r":"ふる","m":"fall"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think it will rain tomorrow.","scene":"Looking at the cloudy sky before school"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おもしろい","m":"interesting"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think this movie is interesting.","scene":"Giving your opinion after watching a movie with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-084||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is always **『plain-form clause』+ と + 思います**. Every word type before と must be in plain form (not polite form).","nuance":"This is the most common error: learners keep です/ます in the clause before と. Remember that と requires everything before it to be in plain form. With nouns and な-adjectives, add だ (or じゃない/だった for negative/past forms).","structure":[{"lhs":"V 普通形","rhs":"＋ と思います","note":"Verb: 行く / 行かない / 行った"},{"lhs":"いA 普通形","rhs":"＋ と思います","note":"寒い / 寒くない / 寒かった"},{"lhs":"なA 普通形","rhs":"＋ だ ＋ と思います","note":"きれいだ / きれいじゃない"},{"lhs":"N 普通形","rhs":"＋ だ ＋ と思います","note":"学生だ / 学生じゃない"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"もう","m":"already"},{"t":"帰った","r":"かえった","m":"went home"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think Mr. Tanaka has already gone home.","scene":"His shoe area is empty"},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"寒くない","r":"さむくない","m":"not cold"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't think it will be cold tomorrow.","scene":"Telling your mother after checking the forecast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"きれい","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think Japan is beautiful.","scene":"Looking at a friend's photos from Japan"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-084||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"usage","definition":"〜と思います is used when you want to **express a thought** about an issue, **comment** on something, or **predict** an uncertain event.","nuance":"In conversation, Japanese speakers often answer questions with 〜と思います instead of making a blunt assertion. For an intention or belief **held for a long time**, use 〜と思っています (a continuing state). 〜と思います leans toward 'here and now'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"ラーメン","m":"ramen"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい","m":"delicious"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think this restaurant's ramen is delicious.","scene":"Inviting a friend to your favorite restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"テスト","m":"test"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ある","m":"there is"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think there will be a test next week.","scene":"Telling a classmate based on the teacher's notice"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-084||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish similar patterns","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜と思います from related と patterns and other ways of expressing guesses.","nuance":"At N5, focus only on each core difference; deep comparison is not necessary yet.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think I like Japanese.","scene":"Answering when asked about your interests"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と言いました","point":"と follows **spoken words**, while 思います follows a **thought**. Example: 先生は「いい」と言いました (the teacher said 'good') vs 先生はいいと思います (I think the teacher is good)."},{"vs":"〜でしょう","point":"〜でしょう expresses a **fairly certain** prediction based on evidence, while 〜と思います is a **subjective opinion** with lower certainty. Example: あした雨が降るでしょう (it will probably rain, judging by the sky) vs あした雨が降ると思います (I think it will rain; I may be wrong)."},{"vs":"〜と思っています","point":"〜と思っています emphasizes a **thought held over time**, often an intention using the volitional form; 〜と思います is an immediate thought. Example: 日本で働こうと思っています (I intend to work in Japan)."}]}},"n5-084||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes learners make with 〜と思います. Most come from translating directly from English without remembering the fixed structure: plain-form clause + と. Learners often keep polite です/ます in the clause before と, forget だ after nouns or な-adjectives, add an unnecessary です after ます, or reorder the sentence. Think of と as quotation marks around the content to avoid these errors.","nuance":"These mistakes are common at N5, before learners are comfortable switching from polite to plain form. Once the pattern is clear, it becomes very natural.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"問題","r":"もんだい","m":"problem"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"むずかしい","m":"difficult"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think this problem is difficult.","scene":"Working on homework"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 寒いですと思います。 → ✅ 寒いと思います。 (Do not use です/ます before と)","❌ これは本だと思いますです。 → ✅ これは本だと思います。 (Do not add です after ます)","❌ 彼は学生と思います。 → ✅ 彼は学生だと思います。 (N/なA require だ)","❌ 私は思います、彼は来る。 → ✅ 彼は来ると思います。 (Do not reverse the order)"],"compare":null}},"n5-084||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine と as quotation marks or **'that'**: everything before と is the content placed 'in the mind' of 思います.","nuance":"It uses the same structure as 〜と言います (say that). If you learn 'say that' first, 'think that' is simply 言う replaced by 思う.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-084||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"〜と思います · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と思います","patternReading":"とおもいます","coreMeaning":"I think that ~ (stating an opinion or guess)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with 〜と思います that you will often see in textbooks, tests, and conversation.","nuance":"Memorizing these phrases will help you respond more quickly and naturally.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"も"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"と思います","r":"おもいます","m":"I think"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I think it's good too.","scene":"Agreeing with a friend's suggestion"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-085||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"〜と言いました · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と言いました","patternReading":"といいました","coreMeaning":"Said that ~ (reported speech)","slug":"core","definition":"〜と言いました is used to **report someone else's words**. The speaker tells someone else about a sentence or idea that was said earlier. The verb 言う is in its polite past form, 言いました, so this pattern works in classrooms, workplaces, and everyday conversation.","nuance":"This pattern emphasizes the **act of speaking**, not whether the content is currently true. When reporting it, the speaker **does not claim** that it is true or false—they are only passing on the information. It is therefore **objective**, unlike 〜と思います, which expresses a personal opinion.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あした","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tanaka said that he would come tomorrow.","scene":"After hearing Tanaka say he will come tomorrow, you tell a classmate."},{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"テスト","r":"てすと","m":"test"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"there is no"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher said there would be no test.","scene":"The teacher announces no test, and you tell your friends."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-085||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"〜と言いました · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と言いました","patternReading":"といいました","coreMeaning":"Said that ~ (reported speech)","slug":"forms","definition":"Connect 〜と言いました by placing the **entire sentence in plain form** before と. With nouns and na-adjectives, use **だ** (or だった for the past). For questions, add **か** before と. The verb 言う can be made negative as 言いませんでした.","nuance":"**Important:** Do **not use the polite form** (ます/です) before と. This is a common beginner error. When reporting a question, keep か and place it before と.","structure":[{"lhs":"Động từ thường (Vる/Vない/Vた)","rhs":"と言いました","note":"Example: 行く / 行かない / 行った"},{"lhs":"い-adjective (Aい)","rhs":"と言いました","note":"Example: 高い → 高いと言いました"},{"lhs":"な-adjective + だ","rhs":"と言いました","note":"Example: 静かだ → 静かだと言いました"},{"lhs":"Danh từ + だ","rhs":"と言いました","note":"Example: 学生だ → 学生だと言いました"},{"lhs":"Câu hỏi trực tiếp (〜か/ですか)","rhs":"と言いました","note":"Example: どこですか → どこですかと言いました"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"学生","r":"がくせい","m":"student"},{"t":"だ"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Tanaka said that he was a student.","scene":"You report Tanaka's self-introduction to a friend."},{"tokens":[{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"どこ"},{"t":"ですか","r":"ですか","m":"where is it"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said/asked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He asked where the station was.","scene":"A visitor asks for directions, and you report it."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-085||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"〜と言いました · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と言いました","patternReading":"といいました","coreMeaning":"Said that ~ (reported speech)","slug":"usage","definition":"In conversation, use 〜と言いました to report information received from someone else. You can **quote the exact words** (inside 「」) or **report the main idea**. To ask what someone said, use **何と言いましたか**.","nuance":"This pattern is used to **refer back to past speech**—the person spoke earlier. If the statement is still relevant now, Japanese often uses と言っています. At N5, however, と言いました is the key pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"あした","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"テスト","r":"てすと","m":"test"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"する","r":"する","m":"do"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher said that we would have a test tomorrow.","scene":"The teacher announces the test schedule, and you tell a classmate."},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"スーパー","r":"すーぱー","m":"supermarket"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother said that she was going to the supermarket.","scene":"You come home, find your mother away, and your father tells you."},{"tokens":[{"t":"あの"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"What did that person say?","scene":"You heard someone but could not make it out, so you ask nearby."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-085||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compared with 〜と思います","sub":"〜と言いました · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と言いました","patternReading":"といいました","coreMeaning":"Said that ~ (reported speech)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse 〜と言いました and 〜と思います because both use **と + a verb**. The key difference is the **speaker's action**: 言う means to say something aloud, while 思う means to think something internally.","nuance":"In conversation, と言いました usually passes on information from a third party; と思います expresses the speaker's personal view with less certainty.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He said that he would go.","scene":"You report a friend's decision."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜と思います","point":"Saying (言う) vs thinking (思う). と言いました reports someone else's words; と思います gives the speaker's opinion. Example: 彼は行くと言いました (He said he would go) vs 彼は行くと思います (I think he will go)."},{"vs":"NとVます (と = cùng với)","point":"The quotative と follows an entire sentence; と meaning “together with” follows a noun for a person or thing. Example: 彼は来ると言いました (He said he would come) vs 彼と行きます (I will go with him)."}]}},"n5-085||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"〜と言いました · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と言いました","patternReading":"といいました","coreMeaning":"Said that ~ (reported speech)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often omit と or use the wrong form before it. Always check: the clause before と **must be in plain form**.","nuance":"In casual speech, Japanese speakers may replace と with って (for example: 来るって言った). At N5, however, **do not omit と** in this polite pattern.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"来る","r":"くる","m":"come"},{"t":"と言いました","r":"といいました","m":"said that"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He said that he would come.","scene":"You report a friend's announcement."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 彼は来ますと言いました。 → ✅ 彼は来ると言いました。 (Do not use ます before と)","❌ 彼は来る言いました。 → ✅ 彼は来ると言いました。 (と is required)","❌ 私は友達と言いました。 (missing だ after the noun 友達) → ✅ 私は友達だと言いました。"],"compare":null}},"n5-085||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"〜と言いました · N5","v2":{"pattern":"〜と言いました","patternReading":"といいました","coreMeaning":"Said that ~ (reported speech)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine と as **two fingers making quotation marks** when repeating someone's words: “He said [content].” That content is the part before と.","nuance":"Unlike English, spoken Japanese **does not need quotation marks**; と connects the quoted speech. When you see と + 言う, immediately understand it as “say that.”","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-086||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"core","definition":"について follows a noun to mark the topic that the following action concerns. Sentences with について often answer “about what?”. For example, 音楽について話します means “talk about music.” Unlike を, which marks a direct object, について identifies the content referred to in communication or thought.","nuance":"This is neutral and works in both speech and writing; it is neither especially formal nor casual. In informal conversation, Japanese speakers sometimes use 〜のことを instead of について when talking about familiar people or things, but at N5, について is a safe choice.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"音楽","r":"おんがく","m":"music"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話しました","r":"はなしました","m":"spoke"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Yesterday, I talked about music with my friends.","scene":"After school, telling my mother about my day."},{"tokens":[{"t":"来週","r":"らいしゅう","m":"next week"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"レポート","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書きます","r":"かきます","m":"will write"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Next week, I will write a report about studying Japanese.","scene":"Asking a classmate what their report will be about."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-086||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"forms","definition":"Formally, について is a particle combined with a noun; it does not conjugate. The following verb conjugates normally for present, past, negative, polite, and other forms. You can also add の after について to modify a noun: についての本 = a book about a topic.","nuance":"This structure is neutral and not tied to politeness. It is perfectly normal with friends: 昨日の映画について話そう。 (Let’s talk about yesterday’s movie.)","structure":[{"lhs":"N","rhs":"について + V","note":"V expresses speaking/thinking/writing/research: 話す・書く・考える・調べる・教える…"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"についての + N2","note":"Modifies a noun: 日本語についての本 (a book about Japanese)."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"何","r":"なん","m":"what"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話します","r":"はなします","m":"will speak"},{"t":"か"}],"vi":"What topic will you talk about?","scene":"A teacher asks before a short presentation."},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"ください"}],"vi":"Please teach me about studying Japanese.","scene":"Asking a senpai to share their experience."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-086||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to introduce the topic of a conversation, piece of writing, or thought. In a sentence, について commonly goes with verbs such as 話す, 書く, 考える, 調べる, and 教える. Without について, the sentence may be incorrect or have a different meaning.","nuance":"Compare: 日本語を話します = speak Japanese (use Japanese as the language); 日本語について話します = talk about Japanese (Japanese is the topic). The meanings are clearly different.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"土曜日","r":"どようび","m":"Saturday"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movies"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話しました","r":"はなしました","m":"talked"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"On Saturday, we talked about Japanese movies.","scene":"Friends chat at the Japanese club."},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"studying"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"考えています","r":"かんがえています","m":"am thinking"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every day, I think about my studies.","scene":"Talking to myself while studying."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-086||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Nを vs. Nは","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"compare","definition":"Learners often confuse を and について because both come before a verb. The difference is: を marks the direct object of an action, while について marks the topic being discussed. Also distinguish は, the topic marker used to describe something, not the content of an activity.","nuance":"With 話す, を means “speak that language,” while について means “talk about that topic.” With 考える, を often means “think up something,” while について means “think about an issue.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話しています","r":"はなしています","m":"are talking"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"We are talking about Japanese.","scene":"Answering what we are doing in class."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Nを","point":"Nを = direct object: 日本語を話します (speak Japanese). Nについて = topic: 日本語について話します (talk about Japanese)."},{"vs":"Nは","point":"Nは = descriptive topic: 日本はきれいです. Nについて = activity content: 日本について話します."}]}},"n5-086||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Typical mistakes with について include using it with adjectives such as 好き and 上手, ending the sentence after について without a verb, and replacing について with を when expressing “talk about.”","nuance":"To express liking or skill in a field, Japanese uses が, not について: 音楽が好きです. This is much more natural than 音楽について好きです.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"話しましょう","r":"はなしましょう","m":"let’s talk"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s talk about the photo.","scene":"Looking through a photo album."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私は音楽について好きです。 → ✅ 私は音楽が好きです。","❌ 日本の文化を話しました。(meaning “talk about culture”) → ✅ 日本の文化について話しました。","❌ 昨日の旅行について。(missing a verb) → ✅ 昨日の旅行について話しました。"],"compare":null}},"n5-086||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that 就く (つく) means “follow” or “stay attached.” In its core image, について describes speech or thought that “follows” a topic. Imagine the topic leading the way while you follow behind with a flashlight to illuminate it.","nuance":"This memory aid helps you remember to use について with action verbs: there is always an action that “follows” or concerns something.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-086||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common collocations","sub":"Nについて · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Nについて","patternReading":"について","coreMeaning":"About N / concerning N (stating a topic)","slug":"phrases","definition":"In exams and everyday conversation, について often combines with the following verbs. Learn them in groups: language (話す・書く・聞く), thought (考える・知る), and research (調べる・習う・教える).","nuance":"When someone says 〜について, the listener understands it as “about something.” Use 何について to ask about the topic: 何について話しますか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"旅行","r":"りょこう","m":"travel"},{"t":"について","m":"about"},{"t":"調べています","r":"しらべています","m":"am researching"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am researching travel.","scene":"Answering a friend’s question about what I am doing."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-087||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern **Vる + 名詞** modifies abstract nouns such as **時間**, **約束**, and **機会**. The verb always uses the dictionary form (辞書形) and comes directly before the noun to clarify the action related to it. For example: **食べる時間** = time to eat, **会う約束** = an appointment to meet.","nuance":"This structure expresses the **content of the action**, not the time when it happens. It is natural and common in both everyday speech and writing. The listener immediately understands what action the noun is connected to.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time to eat lunch.","scene":"Busy at noon and unable to eat."},{"tokens":[{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"会う","r":"あう","m":"meet"},{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"appointment"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しました","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I made an appointment to meet tomorrow.","scene":"Telling a friend about tomorrow's plan."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-087||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is simple: place the **dictionary-form verb** (Vる) **before the noun**. Do not conjugate the verb or insert a particle between the two parts. The noun is often an abstract noun for **time, an appointment, an opportunity, or a thing**.","nuance":"Because the dictionary form is used, it does not express past or negative meaning. To say 'do not have time to do something,' put the negative at the end: **～時間がない**. The clause before the noun only states the action.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる（辞書形）","rhs":"+ 時間","note":"time to do something"},{"lhs":"Vる（辞書形）","rhs":"+ 約束","note":"an appointment to do something"},{"lhs":"Vる（辞書形）","rhs":"+ 機会","note":"an opportunity to do something"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"する"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have time to study Japanese.","scene":"Replying to a question about being free to study."},{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"と"},{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"約束","r":"やくそく","m":"appointment"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しました","m":"made"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I made an appointment to go shopping with my mother.","scene":"Telling a friend about your weekend plan."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-087||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when talking about a **period of time, an appointment, or an opportunity** connected to a specific action. It is very common for asking or answering about arranging time, plans, or wishes.","nuance":"It can combine with the **たい form** of a verb, such as **話したいこと**, meaning something one wants to say. It can also be used with nouns such as **予定** (schedule) and **用事** (an errand or task). The tone is natural and familiar, and it works with friends or older people when polite sentence endings are added.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日本","r":"にほん","m":"Japan"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"機会","r":"きかい","m":"opportunity"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"have"},{"t":"か"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Do you have an opportunity to go to Japan?","scene":"Asking a friend whether they plan to go to Japan."},{"tokens":[{"t":"話したい","r":"はなしたい","m":"want to talk"},{"t":"こと","m":"thing"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","m":"have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have something I want to talk to you about.","scene":"Opening a serious conversation."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-087||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare this pattern with the easily confused forms **Vること**, **Vます**, and **NのN** to understand why Vる is used to modify an abstract noun.","nuance":"Each structure has its own function: **Vること** turns a verb into a noun (the act of doing something), **Vます** cannot come before a noun, and **NのN** only expresses possession or a link between two nouns; it does not express an action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"話す","r":"はなす","m":"talk"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ありません","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time to talk.","scene":"Turning down a conversation because you are busy."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vること","point":"Vること = 'the act of doing ~' (nominalization), while Vる + noun modifies the noun. Example: 話すこと (the act of talking) / 話す時間 (time to talk)."},{"vs":"Vます","point":"Vます is used only at the end of a sentence and does NOT come before a noun. Say 食べる時間, not 食べます時間."},{"vs":"NのN","point":"NのN shows possession or a link between nouns, not an action. 会う約束 = an appointment to meet; 会いの約束 is incorrect."}]}},"n5-087||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by **using the wrong verb form before a noun** or **adding 'to' based on English phrasing**. Remember the rule: a verb modifying a noun always uses the **dictionary form**.","nuance":"The Vる + noun structure needs no particle or connecting word. Simply place Vる directly before the noun. When expressing 'to do something,' Vる + noun is often enough; ために is not always necessary.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"レポート","r":"れぽーと","m":"report"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書く","r":"かく","m":"write"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time to write the report.","scene":"Complaining to a friend about a deadline."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 昼ごはんを食べます時間がありません。 → ✅ 昼ごはんを食べる時間がありません。","❌ 日本に行っている機会がありますか。 → ✅ 日本に行く機会がありますか。","❌ 会うの約束をしました。 → ✅ 会う約束をしました。","❌ 昼ごはんを食べるの時間がありません。 → ✅ 昼ごはんを食べる時間がありません。"],"compare":null}},"n5-087||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: Imagine **Vる as a hat placed on a noun** to clarify what the noun is 'for.' For example, 会う約束 is an appointment 'wearing the hat' of meeting, and 食べる時間 is time 'wearing the hat' of eating.","nuance":"This image helps you avoid using the wrong verb form. When you see an abstract noun, ask yourself 'for what action?' and place Vる directly before it.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-087||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる + 時間／約束／機会など","patternReading":"じかん・やくそく・きかい など","coreMeaning":"Time / appointments / opportunities to do ~ (verb modifying a noun)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These are practical phrases commonly found on exams and in daily life. They help you quickly remember the Vる + abstract noun structure.","nuance":"These phrases can be used in many contexts: making plans with friends, discussing work, or asking about opportunities to study abroad.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"買い物","r":"かいもの","m":"shopping"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が","r":"が"},{"t":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I don't have time to go shopping.","scene":"Replying to an invitation to go shopping."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-088||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"core","definition":"A verb clause (Vる／Vた／Vない) comes **BEFORE** a noun and modifies it, like a “long adjective.” The speaker uses it to identify exactly which person or thing is meant. For example, **昨日買った本** (the book THAT I bought yesterday) answers “which book?”","nuance":"Common in both speech and writing. The particle after the noun determines the noun phrase's role in the sentence (は, を, に...). Remember: the clause comes **BEFORE the noun**, unlike English.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"これ"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"買った","r":"かった","m":"bought"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"This is the book I bought.","scene":"Introducing an object in front of someone"},{"tokens":[{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"話せる","r":"はなせる","m":"can speak"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"探しています","r":"さがしています","m":"am looking for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am looking for someone who can speak Japanese.","scene":"A company is hiring Japanese speakers"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-088||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"forms","definition":"There are three basic forms: **Vる** (present, future, or habitual), **Vた** (completed past), and **Vない** (negative or not yet happened). The clause always comes **before** the noun. If the noun is the action's subject, use **が** rather than **は** inside the clause (e.g., **私が買った本**).","nuance":"When the clause subject performs the action, は usually changes to が to avoid confusion with the sentence topic. However, は is possible for contrast or emphasis. Match the verb tense in the clause to the actual timing of the action.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"+ N","note":"Present/future action, habit, or ability (e.g., 飲む牛乳)"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"+ N","note":"Action completed in the past (e.g., 買った本)"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"+ N","note":"Negative or not-yet action (e.g., 食べない人)"},{"lhs":"Vている","rhs":"+ N","note":"Extension: ongoing state (e.g., 座っている人)"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"飲む","r":"のむ","m":"drink"},{"t":"牛乳","r":"ぎゅうにゅう","m":"milk"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"おいしい"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"The milk I drink every day is delicious.","scene":"Talking about a familiar food or drink"},{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"見た","r":"みた","m":"watched"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"面白かった","r":"おもしろかった","m":"was interesting"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"The movie I watched yesterday was very interesting.","scene":"Discussing a movie with a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"肉","r":"にく","m":"meat"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べない","r":"たべない","m":"do not eat"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"people"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"多い","r":"おおい","m":"many"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"Many people do not eat meat.","scene":"Talking about people's diets"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-088||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"usage","definition":"This structure **identifies or describes** a specific noun. It is often used to say “the person/thing that...”. It can introduce, narrate, explain, or ask about someone's identity. Example: **あそこに立っている人** — the person standing over there.","nuance":"Very natural in everyday conversation. Even with a long clause, Japanese places it before N and uses natural pauses. **この／その／あの** can add emphasis and make the reference clearer.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"あそこ"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"立って","r":"たって","m":"stand"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"田中","r":"たなか","m":"Tanaka"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"です"}],"vi":"The person standing over there is Tanaka.","scene":"Pointing out a colleague"},{"tokens":[{"t":"私","r":"わたし","m":"I"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"買った","r":"かった","m":"bought"},{"t":"靴","r":"くつ","m":"shoes"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"履きました","r":"はきました","m":"wore"}],"vi":"I wore the shoes I bought yesterday.","scene":"Trying on and showing off new shoes"},{"tokens":[{"t":"食べない","r":"たべない","m":"do not eat"},{"t":"もの"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"冷蔵庫","r":"れいぞうこ","m":"refrigerator"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入れました","r":"いれました","m":"put in"}],"vi":"I put the things I do not eat in the refrigerator.","scene":"Putting away food after a party"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-088||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinctions","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish this pattern from other N5 noun-modifying patterns to avoid confusion. Compare only **one core point** for each pattern.","nuance":"When a noun is modified by a verb phrase, the meaning focuses on an **action or state**. Adjectives and possession instead emphasize a **quality or origin**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"昨日","r":"きのう","m":"yesterday"},{"t":"貸した","r":"かした","m":"lent"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"返して","r":"かえして","m":"return"},{"t":"ください"}],"vi":"Please return the book I lent you yesterday.","scene":"Reminding a borrower to return a book"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Aい / NAな + N","point":"Adjectives modify N by its QUALITY (e.g., 高い本 – an expensive book); a V clause modifies N by an ACTION/STATE (e.g., 買った本 – a book that was bought). Same position, different meaning."},{"vs":"NのN","point":"NのN expresses POSSESSION/CONTENT/ORIGIN (e.g., 私の本 – my book); a V clause expresses an ACTION linked to N (e.g., 私が買った本 – the book I bought)."}]}},"n5-088||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"English-speaking learners often make mistakes by placing the modifying clause after the noun, as English does. Pay attention to clause position and particles.","nuance":"A modifying clause in Japanese always comes BEFORE the noun. The clause subject usually takes が (sometimes は for contrast). The verb tense must match the real timing of the action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"作った","r":"つくった","m":"made"},{"t":"料理","r":"りょうり","m":"dish"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べました","r":"たべました","m":"ate"}],"vi":"I ate the dish my mother made.","scene":"Talking about a home-cooked meal"}],"pitfalls":["❌ これは本昨日買ったです。 → ✅ これは昨日買った本です。","❌ 私は買った本はおもしろいです。 → ✅ 私が買った本はおもしろいです。","❌ ここで働く人 (when referring to someone currently working here) → ✅ ここで働いている人","❌ あの人と話す人は先生です。 (ambiguous) → ✅ あの人と話している人は先生です。"],"compare":null}},"n5-088||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine the V clause as a **jacket** worn by the noun. The jacket identifies which noun the listener should recognize. For example, '買った' is the “bought” jacket, while '食べる' is the “for eating” jacket.","nuance":"When a plain-form verb (る/た/ない) comes directly before a noun, mentally translate it as “that...” or “with...”. Do not insert a connector like “that/which” in Japanese.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-088||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vる／Vた／Vない + N","patternReading":"ふつうけいどうしとめいし","coreMeaning":"A V clause modifies a noun: N that/which + V","slug":"phrases","definition":"In JLPT N5 tests and everyday conversation, clauses attached to **人** (person), **もの** (thing), and **ところ** (place) appear very frequently.","nuance":"Abstract nouns such as 時間, 約束, and 機会 can also follow a V clause, but at this level, mastering these three basic combinations is enough.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"知って","r":"しって","m":"know"},{"t":"いる"},{"t":"人","r":"ひと","m":"person"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会いました","r":"あいました","m":"met"}],"vi":"I met someone I know.","scene":"Describing a chance meeting on the street"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-089||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Aい N / NAな N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aい N / NAな N","patternReading":"いけいようしとめいし","coreMeaning":"N with quality ~ (adjective modifying a noun)","slug":"core","definition":"This structure **modifies a noun**: the adjective comes directly before the noun and shows its quality. For example, 大きい荷物 means “large luggage,” and 静かなレストラン means “a quiet restaurant.” This is the most basic way to form a descriptive noun phrase in Japanese.","nuance":"The tone is **neutral** and works in any situation, with friends or elders. When modifying a noun, an い-adj keeps its ending **い**, while a な-adj must add **な**. Forgetting な is a very common beginner error.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"大きい","r":"おおきい","m":"large"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carrying"},{"t":"います","m":"present state"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I am carrying large luggage.","scene":"At the airport, talking about your suitcase"},{"tokens":[{"t":"静かな","r":"しずかな","m":"quiet"},{"t":"レストラン","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"たい","m":"want to"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to eat at a quiet restaurant.","scene":"Choosing a quiet place to eat with a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-089||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"Aい N / NAな N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aい N / NAな N","patternReading":"いけいようしとめいし","coreMeaning":"N with quality ~ (adjective modifying a noun)","slug":"forms","definition":"The formula is simple: **Aい + N** and **NAな + N**. An い-adj stays unchanged; a な-adj adds **な** before the noun. For negative or past forms, place the entire **conjugated form** before the noun: 高くない山 (a mountain that is not high), 静かじゃない部屋 (a room that is not quiet).","nuance":"There is no politeness difference between the two forms; the choice depends only on the adjective’s **word class**. With a な-adj, **な is required** before a noun, but not at the end of a sentence: この部屋は静かだ。","structure":[{"lhs":"い-adj","rhs":"N","note":"Keep the い ending. E.g.: 高い + 山 → 高い山"},{"lhs":"な-adj","rhs":"な + N","note":"Add な before the noun. E.g.: 元気 + 子 → 元気な子"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"新しい","r":"あたらしい","m":"new"},{"t":"スマホ","m":"phone"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"欲しい","r":"ほしい","m":"want"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want a new smartphone.","scene":"Telling your mother what you want to buy"},{"tokens":[{"t":"有名な","r":"ゆうめいな","m":"famous"},{"t":"俳優","r":"はいゆう","m":"actor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"会い","r":"あい","m":"meet"},{"t":"たい","m":"want to"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I want to meet a famous actor.","scene":"Telling a friend about your favorite celebrity"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-089||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"Aい N / NAな N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aい N / NAな N","patternReading":"いけいようしとめいし","coreMeaning":"N with quality ~ (adjective modifying a noun)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when you want to **specify what kind of noun something is or what qualities it has**. It often appears in answers to **どんなNですか** (What is N like?). Example: どんな店ですか。→ 静かな店です。","nuance":"The expression is **descriptive** and does not strongly emphasize emotion. To intensify the adjective, place とても or ちょっと before it: とても静かなレストラン. When changed to NはAです, the sentence emphasizes the property rather than modifying a noun.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"小さい","r":"ちいさい","m":"small"},{"t":"犬","r":"いぬ","m":"dog"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飼って","r":"かって","m":"raise"},{"t":"います","m":"currently"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I have a small dog.","scene":"Showing off your pet to a friend"},{"tokens":[{"t":"安い","r":"やすい","m":"cheap"},{"t":"レストラン","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"昼ご飯","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べ","r":"たべ","m":"eat"},{"t":"ましょう","m":"let’s"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Let’s have lunch at an inexpensive restaurant.","scene":"Inviting a coworker to lunch"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-089||compare":{"title":"⚖️ AいN vs NはAです","sub":"Aい N / NAな N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aい N / NAな N","patternReading":"いけいようしとめいし","coreMeaning":"N with quality ~ (adjective modifying a noun)","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish these two roles: **Aい N / NAな N** creates a **noun phrase** that can serve as a subject or object, while **NはAです** is a **complete sentence** with the adjective as its predicate. Compare: 高い山が好きです (I like high mountains) vs この山は高いです (This mountain is high).","nuance":"A noun phrase usually **adds information** within a sentence, while NはAです **asserts a property**. この/その/あの + N is demonstrative—it identifies “this/that/that over there,” but does not describe the thing’s qualities.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"高い","r":"たかい","m":"high"},{"t":"山","r":"やま","m":"mountain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"好き","r":"すき","m":"like"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I like high mountains.","scene":"Answering what kind of mountains you like"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"NはAです","point":"AいN/NAなN modifies N and needs another predicate; NはAです is a complete sentence. E.g.: 古い映画を見ました (I watched an old film) vs この映画は古いです (This film is old)."},{"vs":"この/その/あのN","point":"These are demonstratives, not descriptions. あの建物 (that building over there) does not say whether it is big or small; 大きい建物 says it is a large building."}]}},"n5-089||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Aい N / NAな N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aい N / NAな N","patternReading":"いけいようしとめいし","coreMeaning":"N with quality ~ (adjective modifying a noun)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget **な** after な-adj or attach **な** to い-adj. There is one rule: **い-adj does not add な**, while **な-adj must have な** before a noun. Also remember the order: the adjective MUST come BEFORE the noun.","nuance":"These errors make a sentence sound unnatural and clearly ungrammatical. Japanese speakers may still understand it, but it sounds like the speaker has not mastered the basics.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"きれいな","r":"きれいな","m":"beautiful"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flower"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買い","r":"かい","m":"buy"},{"t":"ました","m":"did"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I bought beautiful flowers.","scene":"Telling a friend you bought flowers at the market"}],"pitfalls":["❌ きれい花 → ✅ きれいな花 (きれい is a な-adj, so it needs な before the noun)","❌ 大きいな荷物 → ✅ 大きい荷物 (大きい is an い-adj, so do not add な)","❌ 有名です俳優 → ✅ 有名な俳優 (drop です and add な when modifying a noun)","❌ 山高いが好きです → ✅ 高い山が好きです (the adjective must come before the noun)"],"compare":null}},"n5-089||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Aい N / NAな N · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Aい N / NAな N","patternReading":"いけいようしとめいし","coreMeaning":"N with quality ~ (adjective modifying a noun)","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture an **い-adj** with **い** as a strong child standing alone, needing no support. A **な-adj** is weaker and needs **な** as a bridge to the noun. When you see a noun, ask yourself: “Is this an い-adj or a な-adj?”","nuance":"This trick helps prevent the most common error: identify the word class first. い-adj often ends in the sound **い**, such as 高い and 安い; な-adj may not, such as きれい and 静か.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-090||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V/Aとき · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aとき","patternReading":"とき","coreMeaning":"When ~ / at the time of an action","slug":"core","definition":"〜とき means **when / at the time**, linking an action or state to a time reference. With verbs, **Vる** or **Vた** determines whether it means before or after: Vる-とき = before doing V, Vた-とき = after doing V. With nouns and adjectives, とき describes the situation or state at that time.","nuance":"Neutral in formality, so it works in both speech and writing. It does not express a cause or reason; it only says 'when'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"食べる","r":"たべる","m":"eat"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手","r":"て","m":"hand"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"洗います","r":"あらいます","m":"wash"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I am about to eat, I wash my hands.","scene":"Mother reminds me to wash before a meal"},{"tokens":[{"t":"食べた","r":"たべた","m":"ate"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲みました","r":"のみました","m":"took"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"After I finished eating, I took my medicine.","scene":"Taking medicine after a meal as the doctor advised"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-090||forms":{"title":"🧩 Forms & conjugation","sub":"V/Aとき · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aとき","patternReading":"とき","coreMeaning":"When ~ / at the time of an action","slug":"forms","definition":"Before とき, always use the **plain form**, not ます/です. The five main forms are Vる・Vた・Nの・Aい・NAな, with Vない added to say 'when not doing'.","nuance":"Politeness appears at the end of the sentence; the とき-clause stays in plain form.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる","rhs":"とき","note":"Before V (V has not happened)"},{"lhs":"Vた","rhs":"とき","note":"After V has happened"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"のとき","note":"Time/situation of N"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"とき","note":"When state A applies"},{"lhs":"NA","rhs":"なとき","note":"When state NA applies"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"とき","note":"When not doing V"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しい","r":"いそがしい","m":"busy"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べません","r":"たべません","m":"do not eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I am busy, I do not eat breakfast.","scene":"Rushing to work in the morning"},{"tokens":[{"t":"暇な","r":"ひまな","m":"free"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"映画","r":"えいが","m":"movie"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ます","r":"みます","m":"watch"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I have free time, I watch movies.","scene":"Relaxing at home on a free weekend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-090||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V/Aとき · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aとき","patternReading":"とき","coreMeaning":"When ~ / at the time of an action","slug":"usage","definition":"The とき-clause provides a **time frame** for the main clause. It can describe habits, repeated events, or one specific action. In conversation, 'どんなとき…?' asks about the situation.","nuance":"Do not use とき to give a reason. To say 'because', use から. とき answers 'when?', not 'why?'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"疲れた","r":"つかれた","m":"tired"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"お風呂","r":"おふろ","m":"bath"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"入ります","r":"はいります","m":"take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I am tired, I take a bath.","scene":"After a tiring day at work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ない","r":"ない","m":"do not have"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"バス","m":"bus"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I do not have time, I take the bus.","scene":"Taking the bus instead of walking when late"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-090||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V/Aとき · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aとき","patternReading":"とき","coreMeaning":"When ~ / at the time of an action","slug":"compare","definition":"とき is easily confused with time-order patterns such as **Vるまえに** (before), **Vてから** (after), and **Vているとき** (while). Knowing the differences helps you choose the right pattern: とき sets a time point, while the others emphasize order or an ongoing process.","nuance":"Key idea: とき = time point; まえに/てから = sequence; ているとき = action in progress.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"teeth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"磨きます","r":"みがきます","m":"brush"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I go to bed, I brush my teeth.","scene":"Bedtime habit before sleeping"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vるまえに","point":"Vるまえに = 'before V', emphasizing sequence; Vる-とき = 'when preparing to do V', without clearly saying before or during."},{"vs":"Vてから","point":"Vてから = 'after finishing V, then...', emphasizing sequence and allowing a time gap; Vた-とき simply marks the point after V is complete."},{"vs":"Vているとき","point":"Vている-とき = 'while doing V', describing the middle of an action; Vる-とき = before starting V."}]}},"n5-090||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V/Aとき · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aとき","patternReading":"とき","coreMeaning":"When ~ / at the time of an action","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often translate 'when' mechanically, leading to the wrong verb form before とき, keeping ます/です in the subordinate clause, or forgetting の/な with nouns and な-adjectives. Mastering these traps prevents common grammar errors.","nuance":"When writing a とき sentence, check: Is the form before とき plain? Is Vた or Vる correct for after or before?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"寝る","r":"ねる","m":"sleep"},{"t":"とき","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歯","r":"は","m":"teeth"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"磨きます","r":"みがきます","m":"brush"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I go to bed, I brush my teeth.","scene":"Evening habit"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 食べますとき、手を洗います。 → ✅ 食べるとき、手を洗います。","❌ 暇のとき、映画を見ます。 → ✅ 暇なとき、映画を見ます。","❌ 病気とき、学校を休みます。 → ✅ 病気のとき、学校を休みます。","❌ 寝たとき、歯を磨きます。(means before sleeping) → ✅ 寝るとき、歯を磨きます。"],"compare":null}},"n5-091||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"core","definition":"Vないで means **not doing V and instead doing another action**, or **skipping action V**. The speaker emphasizes that V **is not performed**, then moves on to V2. This pattern is very common in daily life: for example, going to school without eating breakfast or going out without taking an umbrella.","nuance":"Used in both spoken and written Japanese; it is neither especially formal nor casual. To be polite, add ください: Vないでください. Used alone with an older person or boss, it may sound like a command.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べないで","r":"たべないで","m":"without eating"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to school without eating breakfast.","scene":"A child explains why they went to school late."},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"何も","r":"なにも","m":"anything"},{"t":"言わないで","r":"いわないで","m":"without saying"},{"t":"帰りました","r":"かえりました","m":"went home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He went home without saying anything.","scene":"Friends are surprised that someone left silently."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-091||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"forms","definition":"The pattern is **Vない form + で**. First, remember how to make the ない form by verb group: Group 1 changes う → あ (買う→買わない), Group 2 drops る and adds ない (食べる→食べない), and Group 3 is する→しない, 来る→こない. Then add で and the main clause.","nuance":"This structure works in statements and requests. With friends, omit ください for a casual tone; with someone above you, add ください for politeness.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（ない形）","rhs":"＋ で ＋ V2","note":"V2 is the main action: do V2 without doing V."},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"＋ でください","note":"A polite request not to do V; ください may be omitted casually."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"辞書","r":"じしょ","m":"dictionary"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使わないでください","r":"つかわないでください","m":"please don't use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't use a dictionary.","scene":"A teacher instructs students during a test."},{"tokens":[{"t":"英語","r":"えいご","m":"English"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"使わないで","r":"つかわないで","m":"don't use"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Don't use English.","scene":"A Japanese teacher addresses students in class."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-091||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"usage","definition":"Vないで links two actions: action V **is not performed**, followed by the main action V2. When it appears at the end of a sentence or before a verb such as ください, it becomes a mild request.","nuance":"The tone is neutral and common in speech. To emphasize deliberately not doing something, Japanese speakers often use ないで rather than なくて.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"テレビ","m":"TV"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"見ないで","r":"みないで","m":"without watching"},{"t":"勉強します","r":"べんきょうします","m":"study"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I study without watching TV.","scene":"A student describes how they study."},{"tokens":[{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持たないで","r":"もたないで","m":"without taking"},{"t":"出かけました","r":"でかけました","m":"went out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went out without taking an umbrella.","scene":"It was raining, but I hurried to work."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-091||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Vないで vs. Vなくて","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare **Vなくて**, which also means 'not V' but links a reason or feeling. **Vないで** emphasizes choosing not to do V and doing another action. If the following clause expresses a reason or feeling, use なくて.","nuance":"Many N5 textbooks call ないで an adverbial form of the negative verb. Use ないで when an action clause follows; use なくて when the following clause gives a reason or emotional state.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べないで","r":"たべないで","m":"without eating"},{"t":"来ました","r":"きました","m":"came"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I came without eating breakfast.","scene":"Explaining why I arrived at work hungry."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vなくて","point":"Vないで → replacement action: 朝ごはんを食べないで学校へ行く = go to school without eating breakfast. Vなくて → reason/state: 朝ごはんを食べなくておなかがすいた = I am hungry because I did not eat breakfast."},{"vs":"Vないでください","point":"Vないで + ください = a polite request, 'please don't do V'; bare Vないで can also be a casual request, 'don't do V.'"}]}},"n5-091||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"The biggest mistake is using **Vないで** to link a reason. Remember: ないで goes with an action, while なくて goes with a reason or feeling. Also, some learners forget to change the verb to the ない form before adding で, or use a bare command that sounds impolite.","nuance":"With strangers or older people, do not use bare Vないで as a request; add ください. In formal writing, Vないで can still be used descriptively.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しないで","m":"without doing"},{"t":"学校","r":"がっこう","m":"school"},{"t":"へ"},{"t":"行きました","r":"いきました","m":"went"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I went to school without doing my homework.","scene":"A student forgot to do the homework."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 朝ごはんを食べなくて学校へ行きました。 → ✅ 朝ごはんを食べないで学校へ行きました。 (Use なくて only if you mean “I was hungry because I did not eat”.)","❌ 何も言わなくて出て行きました。 → ✅ 何も言わないで出て行きました。","❌ ここで写真を撮らないください。 → ✅ ここで写真を撮らないでください。"],"compare":null}},"n5-091||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"memory","definition":"Think of **ない** as 'not having' and **で** as 'with/in a condition.' Together: 'in the state of not doing V' → 'without doing V.' A second action always follows to complete the meaning.","nuance":"For requests, remember to add ください for politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-091||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"Vないで · N5","v2":{"pattern":"Vないで","patternReading":"ないで","coreMeaning":"Do V2 without doing V; don't do V","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with ないで include **〜ないでください** (please don't), **〜ないで出かける** (go out without...), **〜ないでいる** (continue not doing...), and **〜ないで済む** (manage without having to...). Conversation also uses **ご飯を食べないで…**, **連絡しないで…**, and more; use each in the right context.","nuance":"These phrases often appear on N5 tests and in daily conversation. 〜ないでください is the most polite phrase in this group.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photos"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮らないでください","r":"とらないでください","m":"please don't take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Please don't take photos.","scene":"A museum staff member reminds visitors."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-092||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"core","definition":"～と links two events with a natural cause-and-effect relationship: when V1 occurs, V2 happens automatically, like a rule. The speaker states an objective fact, not a personal opinion or prediction.","nuance":"This pattern has a definite, inevitable nuance and is used in both speech and writing. In conversation, it is often used for directions and instructions. V2 must not express volition, a command, or an invitation.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"桜","r":"さくら","m":"cherry blossoms"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"咲きます","r":"さきます","m":"bloom"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When spring arrives, the cherry blossoms bloom.","scene":"Describing a familiar natural law in Japan"},{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"ボタン","r":"ぼたん","m":"button"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"押す","r":"おす","m":"press"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ドア","r":"どあ","m":"door"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"開きます","r":"あきます","m":"open"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When you press this button, the door opens.","scene":"Explaining how to use an automatic door"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-092||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **dictionary-form V** + と + V2. V1 is the condition, and V2 is the natural result. If V1 is **Vない**, the meaning is “if you do not do V1, V2.”","nuance":"V1 is always in dictionary form; it never takes ます, た, or ている. V2 may be a verb or an adjective describing the result, but it must not express volition, a request, or a command.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vる（辞書形）","rhs":"と、Vます","note":"V1 is the condition and V2 is the natural result. Example: 押すと、開きます。"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"と、Vます","note":"If you do not do V1, V2 happens. Example: 勉強しないと、合格しません。"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"右","r":"みぎ","m":"right"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"曲がる","r":"まがる","m":"turn"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"コンビニ","r":"こんびに","m":"convenience store"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you turn right, there is a convenience store.","scene":"Giving directions to a tourist"},{"tokens":[{"t":"お湯","r":"おゆ","m":"hot water"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"入れる","r":"いれる","m":"add"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"3分","r":"さんぷん","m":"3 minutes"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"食べられます","r":"たべられます","m":"ready to eat"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Add hot water, and it is ready to eat in three minutes.","scene":"Explaining how to cook instant noodles"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-092||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"usage","definition":"～と appears in these situations: **a)** natural laws, such as seasons and weather—flowers bloom or snow falls; **b)** repeated habits; **c)** directions and machine instructions; **d)** cooking and drink recipes.","nuance":"This pattern is **neutral and objective**, without strong emotion. Japanese speakers often use it when **giving directions** and **explaining how to operate equipment**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"エアコン","r":"えあこん","m":"air conditioner"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"つける","r":"つける","m":"turn on"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"涼しく","r":"すずしく","m":"cool"},{"t":"なります","r":"なります","m":"become"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When you turn on the air conditioner, it gets cool.","scene":"Explaining what an air conditioner does"},{"tokens":[{"t":"まっすぐ","m":"straight"},{"t":"行く","r":"いく","m":"go"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you go straight, there is a station.","scene":"Giving directions to a passerby"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-092||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with たら/ば","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare ～と with two other conditional patterns: ～と expresses a natural condition that is **always true**; ～たら expresses a specific condition that may or may not occur; ～ば expresses a general condition with exceptions.","nuance":"～と cannot be used with a volitional V2, while ～たら and ～ば can be used in more situations, including hypothetical ones.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"ここ","m":"here"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"花","r":"はな","m":"flowers"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"咲きます","r":"さきます","m":"bloom"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When spring arrives here, the flowers bloom.","scene":"Introducing a flower-viewing spot"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たら","point":"と: natural result, always true; たら: a specific condition or hypothesis. Example: 春になると、桜が咲きます / 日本に行ったら、富士山を見ます。"},{"vs":"〜ば","point":"と: obvious natural law; ば: general condition with exceptions. Example: 春になると、桜が咲きます / 春になれば、桜が咲きます。"}]}},"n5-092||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by using a volitional, imperative, or desire-expressing V2, or by putting V1 in ます form. Remember the strict rule: **V1 is always in dictionary form**, and **V2 cannot express volition**.","nuance":"押すと、ドアが開きます is correct; 押すと、ドアを開けます is wrong because 開けます expresses the speaker's intentional action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この","m":"this"},{"t":"スイッチ","r":"すいっち","m":"switch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"押す","r":"おす","m":"press"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電気","r":"でんき","m":"light"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"つきます","r":"つきます","m":"turn on"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When you press this switch, the light turns on.","scene":"Explaining how to turn on a light"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 春になると、花見に行きます。 → ✅ 春になると、桜が咲きます。 (V2 must be a natural result, not volition)","❌ このボタンを押すと、ドアを開けます。 → ✅ このボタンを押すと、ドアが開きます。 (Use an intransitive V2, not a wrongly transitive one)","❌ 春になりますと、桜が咲きます。 → ✅ 春になると、桜が咲きます。 (V1 does not take ます form)"],"compare":null}},"n5-092||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine と as a **time arrow**: whenever V1 occurs, it **immediately** leads to V2. For example, press the button and the door opens; spring arrives and the flowers bloom.","nuance":"If V2 contains たい or ましょう, do not use と. Remember: と is for certain, natural results.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-092||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên) · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V1とV2 (điều kiện tự nhiên)","patternReading":"と","coreMeaning":"When V1 happens, V2 occurs naturally (an inevitable condition)","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common fixed phrases found in textbooks and tests include: ～になると、～が咲きます; ～を押すと、～が開きます; 右に曲がると、～があります.","nuance":"Memorizing these phrases helps you respond quickly in listening and reading comprehension.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"冬","r":"ふゆ","m":"winter"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なる","r":"なる","m":"become"},{"t":"と","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"雪","r":"ゆき","m":"snow"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ります","r":"ふります","m":"fall"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When winter arrives, snow falls.","scene":"Describing winter weather"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-093||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"core","definition":"〜てあげます describes **the doer (usually the speaker) performing a beneficial action for someone else**. The recipient is marked by the particle **に**. This is a basic pattern in Japanese giving-and-receiving expressions.","nuance":"This pattern is neutral in politeness and works in both speech and writing. However, when the recipient is a superior (teacher, boss, older person), use **てさしあげます** to avoid sounding as if you are doing them a favor. In modern conversation, Japanese speakers also often avoid てあげます when describing their own actions for others because it can sound condescending.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ってあげました","r":"もってあげました","m":"carried for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I carried my friend's luggage for them.","scene":"Describing helping a friend carrying many things"},{"tokens":[{"t":"弟","r":"おとうと","m":"younger brother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"宿題","r":"しゅくだい","m":"homework"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えてあげます","r":"おしえてあげます","m":"teach for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will help my younger brother with his homework.","scene":"Telling a friend about helping your brother study"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-093||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **て-form verb** + **あげます**. あげます changes for tense, negation, volition, and so on. The recipient comes before it and is marked by **に**.","nuance":"With a superior, use **さしあげます** instead of あげます. In casual conversation, あげます becomes **あげる**. There is no special passive form; simply conjugate あげます.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげます","note":"Present/future — polite"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげました","note":"Past — polite"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげません／あげませんでした","note":"Present/past negative"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげましょう","note":"Suggest doing something for someone"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"あげる","note":"Casual form (spoken)"},{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"さしあげます","note":"Formal — for superiors"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お母さん","r":"おかあさん","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手紙","r":"てがみ","m":"letter"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"読んであげました","r":"よんであげました","m":"read for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I read the letter for my mother.","scene":"Mother asks you to read because her eyes are tired"},{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"コーヒー","r":"コーヒー","m":"coffee"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買ってあげます","r":"かってあげます","m":"buy for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I will buy coffee for my father.","scene":"Father is busy, so you plan to get coffee"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-093||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern to **describe yourself or someone else doing something for another person**. Common situations include helping friends, family members, or strangers in difficulty.","nuance":"It can sound **self-important** or as if you are doing someone a favor when used with a stranger or superior. In polite communication, use **さしあげます** or another polite expression such as **〜ましょうか** instead of てあげましょうか.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"席","r":"せき","m":"seat"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"ゆずってあげました","r":"ゆずってあげました","m":"gave up for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I gave up my seat on the train.","scene":"Describing giving your seat to an elderly person"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸してあげました","r":"かしてあげました","m":"lent to"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I lent my friend a book.","scene":"Telling your family a friend borrowed a textbook"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-093||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Contrast with related patterns","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"compare","definition":"Distinguish 〜てあげます from 〜てもらいます and 〜てくれます, the three Japanese giving-and-receiving patterns for actions. The key difference is direction: てあげます points from the speaker (or subject) to someone else and describes doing something for them. てもらいます points from someone else to the speaker and describes receiving a favor, while てくれます also points inward and adds a grateful nuance.","nuance":"Keep the direction diagram clear: てあげます = give outward, てもらいます = receive, てくれます = receive an action performed by someone else. Reversing the direction changes the sentence’s meaning.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"弟","r":"おとうと","m":"younger brother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"算数","r":"さんすう","m":"math"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えてあげました","r":"おしえてあげました","m":"taught"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I taught my younger brother math.","scene":"Telling a friend you tutored your brother"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"てもらいます","point":"てあげます: the subject does something for someone else. てもらいます: the subject receives an action from someone else. E.g. 私は弟に算数を教えてあげました (I teach my brother) ↔ 私は弟に算数を教えてもらいました (My brother teaches me)."},{"vs":"てくれます","point":"てあげます: the speaker performs the action. てくれます: someone else performs it for the speaker. E.g. 私は弟に算数を教えてあげました ↔ 弟が私に算数を教えてくれました (My brother taught me)."}]}},"n5-093||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common basic mistakes: reversing the direction, omitting に, and using てあげます for a superior.","nuance":"Reversing the direction completely changes the meaning and can make Japanese speakers misunderstand who receives the action. Memorize the direction of the three giving-and-receiving patterns.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"資料","r":"しりょう","m":"materials"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"コピーしてさしあげました","r":"こぴーしてさしあげました","m":"copied for"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I copied the materials for my teacher.","scene":"Describing copying materials for a teacher — さしあげます is respectful"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 友だちは私に本を貸してあげました。 → ✅ 友だちは私に本を貸してくれました。(The friend acts, and I receive it, so use てくれます)","❌ 先生に資料をコピーしてあげました。 → ✅ 先生に資料をコピーしてさしあげました。(Use さしあげます with a teacher)","❌ 私にコーヒーを買ってあげます。 → ✅ 私はコーヒーを買います。(あげます is not used for yourself)","❌ 友だち荷物を持ってあげました。 → ✅ 友だちに荷物を持ってあげました。(に is missing)"],"compare":null}},"n5-093||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"memory","definition":"Memory tip: **上げる** basically means “raise” or “lift up.” Imagine extending your hand and giving an action to someone else — in other words, **giving outward**.","nuance":"Quick reminder: “A-げます = give out.” Combine it with もらいます (receive) and くれます (give to me) to form the giving-and-receiving triangle.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-093||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"VてあげますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてあげますNに","patternReading":"てあげます","coreMeaning":"Do something for someone else — help, directed outward","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common phrases with てあげます in conversation and exams include 手伝ってあげる, 教えてあげる, 買ってあげる, and 送ってあげる. Learning them helps you respond quickly when connecting a verb’s て-form with あげます.","nuance":"These phrases are very common, especially when offering help or describing something you did for someone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"この"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"手伝ってあげましょうか","r":"てつだってあげましょうか","m":"shall I help?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Shall I help with this task?","scene":"Offering to help a busy colleague with part of the work"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-094||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"core","definition":"The pattern 〜てもらいます expresses **someone else doing an action for or on behalf of me**, with the speaker as the **beneficiary**. Unlike あげます (giving), this pattern emphasizes **receiving help or benefiting from the result**. The person performing the action always takes に.","nuance":"This is a **normally polite** expression used in daily conversation at school, work, and home. When saying てもらいます, the speaker often feels **grateful or indebted**, so it is not used to boast about making someone obey. With superiors or customers, use the more formal 〜ていただきます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"朝ごはん","r":"あさごはん","m":"breakfast"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"もらいます","m":"have someone do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Every day, my mother makes breakfast for me.","scene":"Telling a friend that your mother often makes breakfast"},{"tokens":[{"t":"わからない","m":"not understand"},{"t":"時","r":"とき","m":"when"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"もらいます","m":"ask someone to do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When I do not understand, I ask my friend to explain it.","scene":"Talking about your study-group habit"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-094||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"forms","definition":"General structure: **Doer + に + Vて + もらいます**. Conjugate V into the て-form, then connect it to もらう. **もらう** changes for tense and polite form: もらいます / もらいました / もらいません. This giving-and-receiving pattern is for **actions**.","nuance":"The particle に marks the **person performing the action**, not the object. The main subject is the person **receiving the benefit** (often 私), and it can be omitted.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（người làm）","rhs":"に Vて もらいます","note":"Present / future: ask or have someone do"},{"lhs":"N（người làm）","rhs":"に Vて もらいました","note":"Past: asked or had someone do"},{"lhs":"N（người làm）","rhs":"に Vて もらいません","note":"Polite negative: do not ask / have done"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"もらっています","m":"am having taught"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My teacher is teaching me Japanese.","scene":"Talking about your Japanese class"},{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持って","r":"もって","m":"carry"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"had someone do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked Mr. Tanaka to carry my luggage.","scene":"Telling how a coworker carried your suitcase"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-094||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses & situations","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this when **the speaker asks or has another person do something**, and the result benefits the speaker. There are three main situations: ① actively asking for help, ② simply benefiting from someone’s action, and ③ asking an expert or superior to handle something difficult.","nuance":"With someone junior or a close friend, a request can sound forceful if the intonation is wrong; てもらいます usually carries gratitude. With a superior, use 〜ていただきます for greater politeness.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"写真","r":"しゃしん","m":"photo"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"撮って","r":"とって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"asked someone to do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked my friend to take a photo for me.","scene":"Asking a friend to take a photo while out"},{"tokens":[{"t":"医者","r":"いしゃ","m":"doctor"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"薬","r":"くすり","m":"medicine"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"出して","r":"だして","m":"prescribe"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"had someone do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The doctor prescribed medicine for me.","scene":"After seeing a doctor at the hospital"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"had someone do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend drove me to the station.","scene":"Telling how a friend gave you a ride"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-094||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with あげる / くれる","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"compare","definition":"This pattern is easily confused with てあげます and てくれます because all three describe giving and receiving actions. The difference lies in the **direction of the action** and **who benefits**.","nuance":"All three describe someone doing something for someone. てあげます can sound like doing someone a favor; てくれます resembles てもらいます but makes the doer the subject; てもらいます emphasizes that the speaker asks for and receives the action.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"本","r":"ほん","m":"book"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸して","r":"かして","m":"lend"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"asked someone to do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked my friend to lend me a book.","scene":"Borrowing a book from a friend"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてあげます","point":"The action goes FROM me → to someone else. Example: 私は友だちに本を貸してあげました (I lent my friend a book)."},{"vs":"Vてくれます","point":"The action is done by SOMEONE ELSE, who is the subject. Example: 友だちが私に本を貸してくれました (My friend lent me a book)."},{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"I am the subject, and the doer takes に. Example: 私は友だちに本を貸してもらいました (I asked my friend to lend me a book)."}]}},"n5-094||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse the **particles** and **direction** in giving-and-receiving patterns. Remember: the **person performing the action in てもらいます always takes に**, and the sentence emphasizes **the speaker receiving**.","nuance":"When speaking politely to a superior, use ていただきます instead of てもらいます. At N5 level, てもらいます is acceptable with friends and teachers.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"had someone help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"I asked my friend to help me.","scene":"Correct pattern after fixing the mistake"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 先生を教えてもらいました。 → ✅ 先生に教えてもらいました。 (the doer takes に, not を)","❌ 私は先生に教えてあげました。 (when the teacher teaches me) → ✅ 私は先生に教えてもらいました。 (I was taught by the teacher)","❌ 友だちが手伝ってもらいました。 → ✅ 友だちに手伝ってもらいました。 (the doer follows に)","❌ 友だちに写真を撮るもらいました。 → ✅ 友だちに写真を撮ってもらいました。 (V must be in the て-form)"],"compare":null}},"n5-094||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember with an **arrow direction**: the action starts with the person after に and moves toward the speaker. もらう basically means **receive**, so the 'receiver' is the subject and the 'doer' takes に.","nuance":"You do not need to memorize the whole formula. Just remember: あげる = 'give', くれる = 'give me', もらう = 'receive'. When you mean 'ask / have done for me,' choose もらう.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-094||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"VてもらいますNに · N5","v2":{"pattern":"VてもらいますNに","patternReading":"てもらいます","coreMeaning":"Have / ask someone to do something for me","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions include **〜を教えてもらう** (be taught / get an explanation), **手伝ってもらう** (receive help), **直してもらう** (have something fixed), and **送ってもらう** (be taken / seen off). In tests, these phrases often use に.","nuance":"These are common collocations, so do not translate them word by word; learning them as chunks helps you speak faster.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"take"},{"t":"もらいました","m":"had someone take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend took me to the station.","scene":"Thanking a friend after getting a ride to the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-095||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"core","definition":"**〜てくれます** describes **someone else voluntarily doing something for me**. Compared with てもらいます, the key point is **no request is needed** — they do it on their own for my sake. The sentence often conveys **gratitude and appreciation**.","nuance":"Usable in both spoken and written Japanese at the polite -ます level. The recipient is **the speaker (私)** or someone in the speaker's group. The feeling is often **warm and appreciative**; if the action is undesirable, てくれる may sound sarcastic.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"お弁当","r":"おべんとう","m":"boxed lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作ってくれます","r":"つくってくれます","m":"makes for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother makes me a boxed lunch every day.","scene":"You see the lunch your mother prepared early in the morning"},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"迎え","r":"むかえ","m":"pick up"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"来てくれました","r":"きてくれました","m":"came for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend came all the way to the station to pick me up.","scene":"Telling your mother that your friend came to the station"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-095||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and forms","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"forms","definition":"The structure is **Vて + くれます**. The doer comes before it and takes **が**. The recipient, usually **私**, is often omitted. It has affirmative, negative, past, and question forms.","nuance":"The verb before くれます must be in the **te-form**, not the polite form. くれます is the polite form of くれる; in casual conversation, Japanese speakers often use **〜てくれる**.","structure":[{"lhs":"N（人）が","rhs":"Vてくれます","note":"Present/future: someone does it for me"},{"lhs":"N（人）が","rhs":"Vてくれました","note":"Past: someone did it for me"},{"lhs":"N（人）が","rhs":"Vてくれません","note":"Negative: someone does not do it for me"},{"lhs":"N（人）が","rhs":"Vてくれますか","note":"Question/request: could you do it for me?"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"佐藤さん","r":"さとうさん","m":"Mr. Sato"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えてくれました","r":"おしえてくれました","m":"taught me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Sato taught me Japanese.","scene":"A coworker corrects your work"},{"tokens":[{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"洗濯","r":"せんたく","m":"laundry"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"してくれます","r":"してくれます","m":"does for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother does the laundry for me every day.","scene":"Talking about how your mother still looks after you"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-095||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"usage","definition":"Use this pattern when you **want to thank someone or talk about something they did for you**. The forms **Vてくれますか** and **Vてくれませんか** are also polite but friendly ways to make a request.","nuance":"**Vてくれますか** is used with friends, family, and close coworkers. With superiors, use 〜ていただけませんか for greater politeness. When telling a story, add 本当に／ありがとう to emphasize gratitude.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"誰か","r":"だれか","m":"someone"},{"t":"ドア","r":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けてくれますか","r":"あけてくれますか","m":"could you open"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could someone open the door for me?","scene":"Your hands are full, so you need someone in the room to open the door"},{"tokens":[{"t":"彼","r":"かれ","m":"he"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"荷物","r":"にもつ","m":"luggage"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"持ってくれました","r":"もってくれました","m":"carried for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"He carried my luggage for me.","scene":"Telling a friend after a coworker helped carry your things"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-095||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with もらいます・あげます","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"compare","definition":"These three patterns express giving and receiving actions: **〜てあげます** (I/someone else → another person), **〜てもらいます** (someone does something for me, usually after a request), and **〜てくれます** (someone voluntarily does something for me, with gratitude).","nuance":"The easiest confusion is between **もらいます** and **くれます**: もらいます focuses on the result I receive and may involve a request; くれます focuses on the other person's kind initiative.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"傘","r":"かさ","m":"umbrella"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"貸してくれました","r":"かしてくれました","m":"lent me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend lent me an umbrella.","scene":"It suddenly rains, and your friend gives you an umbrella"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜てもらいます","point":"もらいます: 'receive an action' — usually after a REQUEST; くれます: 'they do it THEMSELVES' — emphasizes kindness."},{"vs":"〜てあげます","point":"あげます points OUTWARD (I do something for someone); くれます points INWARD (someone does it for me). Example: 友達に傘を貸してあげました ≠ 友達が傘を貸してくれました."}]}},"n5-095||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by **translating 'for me' or 'receive' directly into あげる/もらう** and forgetting くれる's direction. Check **who is the が subject** and whether the action points toward you.","nuance":"When someone does something for you, **never use あげます**. When using もらいます, the sense of 'voluntary action' is less explicit than with くれます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"田中さん","r":"たなかさん","m":"Mr. Tanaka"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"説明","r":"せつめい","m":"explanation"},{"t":"してくれました","r":"してくれました","m":"explained for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Mr. Tanaka explained it to me.","scene":"After the meeting, Mr. Tanaka explains the unclear part again"}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私が母に弁当を作ってくれます。 → ✅ 母が（私に）弁当を作ってくれます。","❌ 友だちを手伝ってくれました。 → ✅ 友だちが手伝ってくれました。","❌ 弟は私に宿題をしてあげました。（if my younger brother did it for me） → ✅ 弟が（私に）宿題をしてくれました。"],"compare":null}},"n5-095||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"memory","definition":"Picture くれます as an **arrow pointing toward you**: someone does something and 'sends it into' your sphere. When someone does something kind, tell yourself: **'They did it for me on their own' → くれる**.","nuance":"Memory phrase: **'They do it for me freely — てくれる'**. If you had to ask, use もらう; if they acted on their own, use くれる.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-095||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common expressions","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me → gratitude","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions with 〜てくれます include: **教えてくれます** (teach or tell me), **作ってくれます** (make for me), **迎えに来てくれます** (come pick me up), and **開けてくれます** (open it for me).","nuance":"In daily conversation, you will often hear shortened forms like **〜てくれる？** with friends. **持ってきてくれる** (bring it for me) is also extremely common.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いい","m":"good"},{"t":"店","r":"みせ","m":"restaurant"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えてくれました","r":"おしえてくれました","m":"recommended"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend recommended a good restaurant to me.","scene":"Your friend recommends a newly opened restaurant"},{"tokens":[{"t":"また","r":"また","m":"again"},{"t":"会いに","r":"あいに","m":"to meet"},{"t":"来てくれますか","r":"きてくれますか","m":"will you come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Will you come visit me again?","scene":"Saying goodbye after a trip and hoping to meet again"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-096||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"core","definition":"〜たら expresses a **condition** or **time**. Meaning “if,” it describes an unrealized assumption. Meaning “when,” it says that after the first action is complete, the second action occurs. It is the **easiest condition pattern** to use because it can often replace と, ば, or なら (except for natural laws).","nuance":"Used in both speech and writing; casual but still natural at work. Neutral in tone. Compared with と, たら emphasizes a **specific situation** rather than an obvious rule.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ったら","r":"ふったら","m":"if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行きません","r":"いきません","m":"will not go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it rains, I won't go.","scene":"Cloudy weather before going out."},{"tokens":[{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"帰ったら","r":"かえったら","m":"when you get home"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone call"},{"t":"して","r":"して","m":"make"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When you get home, please call me.","scene":"Telling a friend to call after getting home."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-096||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure & conjugation","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"forms","definition":"Conjugation is simple: change to the **た form (past)**, then add ら. Specifically, change Vます to Vた (remove ます), then attach ら. For い-adjectives, remove い and add かったら. For な-adjectives and nouns, add だったら. Negative forms: Vない → なかったら, Aい → くなかったら, NA/N → じゃなかったら.","nuance":"Do not use the masu form in this pattern. Use the **short (plain) form**. The first clause is always the condition for the action or quality.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（た-form）","rhs":"Vた＋ら","note":"Example: 行く → 行ったら, 食べる → 食べたら"},{"lhs":"いadj","rhs":"語幹＋かったら","note":"安い → 安かったら"},{"lhs":"なadj / N","rhs":"＋だったら","note":"暇 → 暇だったら, 雨 → 雨だったら"},{"lhs":"Vない","rhs":"Vなかったら","note":"行かない → 行かなかったら"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"春","r":"はる","m":"spring"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"なったら","r":"なったら","m":"when it becomes"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"花見","r":"はなみ","m":"flower viewing"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"しましょう","r":"しましょう","m":"let's do"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"When spring comes, let's go see the flowers.","scene":"Inviting a friend to see cherry blossoms after winter."},{"tokens":[{"t":"安かったら","r":"やすかったら","m":"if it is cheap"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"will buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it's cheap, I'll buy it.","scene":"Considering an item's price."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null,"conjugationTable":{"headers":["Verb group","Conjugation rule","Example: dictionary form (辞書形) → たら形"],"rows":[["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 う・つ・る → ったら","Remove the final sound and add ったら","買う（かう） → `買ったら（かったら）` (buy) ・ 待つ（まつ） → `待ったら（まったら）` (wait) ・ 帰る（かえる） → `帰ったら（かえったら）` (return) ・ 会う（あう） → `会ったら（あったら）` (meet)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 く → いたら","Remove く and add いたら","書く（かく） → `書いたら（かいたら）` (write) ・ 聞く（きく） → `聞いたら（きいたら）` (listen) ・ 歩く（あるく） → `歩いたら（あるいたら）` (walk)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 ぐ → いだら","Remove ぐ and add いだら","泳ぐ（およぐ） → `泳いだら（およいだら）` (swim) ・ 急ぐ（いそぐ） → `急いだら（いそいだら）` (hurry)"],["**Group I — Godan (五段)** — 五段 ぬ・ぶ・む → んだら","Remove the final sound and add んだら","飲む（のむ） → `飲んだら（のんだら）` (drink) ・ 遊ぶ（あそぶ） → `遊んだら（あそんだら）` (play) ・ 死ぬ（しぬ） → `死んだら（しんだら）` (die) ・ 読む（よむ） → `読んだら（よんだら）` (read)"],["**Group III — Irregular** — 一段・する・来る","一段: remove る + たら · する→したら · 来る→来たら(きたら)","食べる（たべる） → `食べたら（たべたら）` (eat) ・ 見る（みる） → `見たら（みたら）` (watch) ・ する → `したら` (do) ・ 来る（くる） → `来たら（きたら）` (come)"]]}}},"n5-096||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage & situations","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"usage","definition":"The first clause is the condition; the second is the result or action. If the first clause is an **unrealized assumption** → “if.” If the first clause is a **completed action** followed by another action → “when.” It also gives **polite suggestions** with 〜たらどうですか or makes conditional invitations/requests with 〜たら〜てください.","nuance":"Very common in daily conversation. Note: when the second clause is **past tense**, the sentence usually means “when,” not “if” (for “if,” use もし and make the context clear).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あったら","r":"あったら","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have time, please help me.","scene":"Asking a coworker for help when free."},{"tokens":[{"t":"国","r":"くに","m":"country"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"帰ったら","r":"かえったら","m":"when you return"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"何","r":"なに","m":"what"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"do"},{"t":"か","m":"?"}],"vi":"When you return to your country, what will you do?","scene":"Asking a foreign friend about future plans."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-096||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguish from と・てから","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare three easily confused patterns: と, てから, and たら. All can translate as “when ~ then ~,” but their nuances differ. と expresses an obvious rule (it happens every time). てから emphasizes the order “after completing,” without a conditional meaning. たら is the most flexible, covering both conditions and timing.","nuance":"If the second clause is an invitation or suggestion, たら is more natural; と does not pair with commands or invitations. てから cannot express “if.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"お酒","r":"おさけ","m":"alcohol"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"飲んだら","r":"のんだら","m":"if you drink"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"運転","r":"うんてん","m":"drive"},{"t":"しないで","r":"しないで","m":"don't"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you drink alcohol, please don't drive.","scene":"Warning a friend after they drink beer."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vと","point":"と expresses a natural rule or “it happens every time.” たら describes a specific situation. Example: 春になると、花が咲く (flowers bloom every spring—obvious rule) vs 春になったら、花見に行く (when spring comes, I’ll go flower viewing)."},{"vs":"Vてから","point":"てから only means “after doing V1, do V2” (order); it does not imply a condition. たら can replace it when “after” marks a step before the next action. But たら can also mean “if,” which てから cannot."}]}},"n5-096||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes (traps)","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often make mistakes by adding ら to the dictionary form. Remember: verbs require the **た form** before ら; い-adjectives change to **かった**; な-adjectives and nouns use **だった**.","nuance":"English says “if I go,” which can make the form 行くたら feel tempting. Always check: did you change it to the past form (た)? If the second clause expresses a plan or future action, たら can still mean “if.” A past second clause usually means “when.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"電車","r":"でんしゃ","m":"train"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"止まったら","r":"とまったら","m":"if it stops"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"歩いて","r":"あるいて","m":"walk"},{"t":"帰ります","r":"かえります","m":"go home"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If the train stops, I’ll walk home.","scene":"Discussing what to do if the train breaks down."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 時間があったたら → ✅ 時間があったら (only one た)","❌ 安いだったら → ✅ 安かったら (an い-adj does not take だった)","❌ 雨が降ったら、出かけませんでした → ✅ 雨が降ったから、出かけませんでした (with a past result and clear reason, use から)","❌ 春になると、花見をしましょう → ✅ 春になったら、花見をしましょう (と does not pair with invitations)"],"compare":null}},"n5-096||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"memory","definition":"Imagine たら as a **turning point**: action 1 happens first, then action 2. Think of た as a gateway and ら as “oh.” Mnemonic: *“AFTER IT’S DONE…”*.","nuance":"No need to memorize anything complicated. When you have just finished one action and want to talk about the next, use たら.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-096||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"V/Aたら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aたら","patternReading":"たら","coreMeaning":"If / when ~ (condition or time)","slug":"phrases","definition":"These fixed phrases appear often in exams and conversation. Memorize them as units.","nuance":"〜たらどうですか is an invitation or suggestion framed as a condition. 〜たらいいですか asks for advice. もし〜たら highlights a less likely or more hypothetical condition.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あったら","r":"あったら","m":"if there is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"遊びに","r":"あそびに","m":"to play"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"ください","r":"ください","m":"please"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have time, please come visit.","scene":"Inviting friends over when free"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-097||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"core","definition":"もし〜たら means **if, supposing** something happens. The speaker uses もし at the beginning to emphasize the hypothetical nature, while たら connects the condition to its result. This pattern is common in both everyday situations and wishes, especially in conversation.","nuance":"Usable in both casual and polite speech (with ます/です). It has a clearer “suppose that” nuance than たら alone. Do not use it for obvious facts or scientific results.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"お金","r":"おかね","m":"money"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"なかったら","r":"なかったら","m":"if there is none"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"借ります","r":"かります","m":"borrow"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I had no money, I would borrow some.","scene":"Friends discuss what to do if they run out of money"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"明日","r":"あした","m":"tomorrow"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"だったら","r":"だったら","m":"if it is"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ピクニック","r":"ピクニック","m":"picnic"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"やめます","r":"やめます","m":"cancel"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic.","scene":"A weekend outing may be canceled because of rain"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-097||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"forms","definition":"This branch summarizes **how to form the 〜たら clause**. The general rule is to take the **past form** and add ら. For verbs: 行った → 行ったら; for い-adjectives: 安かった → 安かったら; for nouns / な-adjectives: 先生だった → 先生だったら. もし is simply added at the beginning to clarify the hypothetical meaning.","nuance":"たら itself is not polite or impolite; add です in the following sentence for polite speech. The form before たら **must be past tense**, even when the hypothetical meaning refers to the future. This is an easy point to get wrong.","structure":[{"lhs":"V（た形）","rhs":"Vたら","note":"Example: 行った → 行ったら、食べた → 食べたら"},{"lhs":"Aい（た形）","rhs":"Aかったら","note":"Example: 安い → 安かったら"},{"lhs":"N／Aな（だ → だった）","rhs":"N／Aな だったら","note":"Example: 先生 → 先生だったら、暇 → 暇だったら"},{"lhs":"もし ＋ 上記の〜たら","rhs":"もし〜たら","note":"もし adds emphasis and may be omitted"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"行ったら","r":"いったら","m":"if I go"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"電話","r":"でんわ","m":"phone"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"call"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I go, I will call.","scene":"Telling your mother you will call when you arrive"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"安かったら","r":"やすかったら","m":"if it is cheap"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"buy"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it is cheap, I will buy it.","scene":"Comparing prices at a supermarket"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"暇","r":"ひま","m":"free"},{"t":"だったら","r":"だったら","m":"if you are"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"来てください","r":"きてください","m":"please come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you are free, please come over.","scene":"Inviting a friend over"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-097||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"usage","definition":"〜たら is used when the speaker **sets a hypothetical condition** and then gives its result. In もし〜たら, the result is often a **wish, plan, invitation, or request**. It is one of the most natural ways to say “if” in conversation.","nuance":"It is well suited to spoken language and has a moderately polite tone. もし makes the situation sound more distant from reality, so it works well for dreams or gentle invitations such as もしよかったら.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"宝くじ","r":"たからくじ","m":"lottery ticket"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"当たったら","r":"あたったら","m":"if I win"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"世界旅行","r":"せかいりょこう","m":"world travel"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"します","r":"します","m":"take"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I won the lottery, I would travel around the world.","scene":"Talking about dreams with friends"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if, supposing"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あったら","r":"あったら","m":"if I have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"手伝います","r":"てつだいます","m":"help"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If I have time, I will help.","scene":"Replying to a friend who asks for help"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if"},{"t":"よかったら","r":"よかったら","m":"if convenient"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"いっしょに","r":"いっしょに","m":"together"},{"t":"昼ごはん","r":"ひるごはん","m":"lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"食べませんか","r":"たべませんか","m":"shall we eat?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it is convenient, shall we have lunch together?","scene":"Inviting a coworker to lunch"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-097||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Comparing たら・と・ても","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"compare","definition":"This section distinguishes もし〜たら from commonly confused conditional patterns: 〜たら, 〜と, and 〜ても. Each pattern has a **different focus**: hypothesis, regularity, or concession.","nuance":"〜たら alone already means “if/when”; もし only adds a “suppose that” nuance. 〜と is rarely used for wishes, while 〜ても means “even if …, still …”.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ったら","r":"ふったら","m":"if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"家","r":"いえ","m":"home"},{"t":"に"},{"t":"います","r":"います","m":"stay"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it rains, I will stay home.","scene":"The forecast says it may rain"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜たら","point":"〜たら alone is enough for “if/when”; もし only adds a hypothetical nuance. Example: 時間があったら、行きます。（もし is not needed）"},{"vs":"〜と","point":"〜と expresses an obvious rule (春になると暖かい); もし〜たら expresses a personal hypothesis or wish."},{"vs":"〜ても","point":"〜ても means “even if ~, still ~,” with a result contrary to the condition; もし〜たら gives a result based on the condition."}]}},"n5-097||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"This section highlights typical mistakes learners make with もし〜たら. Study the incorrect and corrected examples to avoid losing points.","nuance":"These errors often come from translating word for word. Remember: たら attaches to the **past form**, and the following clause does **not use the past** when expressing a hypothetical result.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if"},{"t":"時間","r":"じかん","m":"time"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あったら","r":"あったら","m":"if I have"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"来てください","r":"きてください","m":"please come"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you have time, please come over.","scene":"Texting a friend to come by"}],"pitfalls":["❌ もし時間があるたら → ✅ もし時間があったら (Vた + ら)","❌ もし宝くじが当たったら、世界旅行をしました → ✅ もし宝くじが当たったら、世界旅行をします (The following clause is not past tense)","❌ 宝くじを当たったら → ✅ 宝くじが当たったら (当たる does not take を)"],"compare":null}},"n5-097||memory":{"title":"🎯 Quick memory tips","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"memory","definition":"Quick memory tip: **もし** is like “if” at the start of a sentence; **たら** is like “after that happens.” Imagine opening the “if” door (もし) and then seeing the result (たら).","nuance":"This tip helps you remember that the form before たら must be the **past form**, as if the hypothetical event has already been completed.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-097||phrases":{"title":"💬 Useful phrases","sub":"もし〜たら · N5","v2":{"pattern":"もし〜たら","patternReading":"もし〜たら","coreMeaning":"If, supposing ~","slug":"phrases","definition":"These もし〜たら phrases appear frequently in JLPT N5 questions and everyday conversation. Memorizing them will help you respond more quickly.","nuance":"These are everyday phrases usable in both casual and polite speech. もしよかったら is especially useful for inviting someone.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降ったら","r":"ふったら","m":"if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"出かけません","r":"でかけません","m":"will not go out"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If it rains, I will not go out.","scene":"Calling a friend to change plans"},{"tokens":[{"t":"もし","r":"もし","m":"if"},{"t":"ほしかったら","r":"ほしかったら","m":"if you want it"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"あげます","r":"あげます","m":"give"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"If you want it, I’ll give it to you.","scene":"Offering a friend something you no longer use"}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-098||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"V/Aても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aても","patternReading":"ても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, ~ still happens (concession)","slug":"core","definition":"〜ても connects a condition with a result, expressing that **even if ~ happens, the result does not change**. The speaker uses it to **stress that there are no exceptions**—no matter what happens, the action or decision remains the same.","nuance":"This neutral pattern works in both speech and writing. Unlike 〜てから (after) or 〜たら (if), 〜ても has a **decisive, regardless-of-the-condition** nuance. The emotion is often strong and may show opposition or determination.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降っても","r":"ふっても","m":"even if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"I will go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, I will still go.","scene":"A planned outing continues despite the rain."},{"tokens":[{"t":"忙しくても","r":"いそがしくても","m":"even if busy"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"運動します","r":"うんどうします","m":"exercise"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even when busy, I exercise every day.","scene":"Describing a workout habit despite a packed schedule."},{"tokens":[{"t":"何度","r":"なんど","m":"how many times"},{"t":"聞いても","r":"きいても","m":"even if I ask"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"わかりません","r":"わかりません","m":"I do not understand"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how many times I ask, I still do not understand.","scene":"Explaining math, but the younger sibling still doesn't understand."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-098||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"V/Aても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aても","patternReading":"ても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, ~ still happens (concession)","slug":"forms","definition":"For **verbs**, use the て-form and add も (行く→行っても). For **い-adjectives**, replace い with く and add ても (高い→高くても). For **な-adjectives and nouns**, simply add でも (静か→静かでも, 学生→学生でも). The **negative** uses Vなくても (行かなくても = even if one does not go).","nuance":"The form does not change with tense or politeness; tense and politeness appear at the end of the sentence. Distinguish でも (な-adjectives/nouns) from ても (verbs/い-adjectives) to avoid mistakes.","structure":[{"lhs":"Vて","rhs":"も","note":"Verb: 行く→行っても"},{"lhs":"Vなくて","rhs":"も","note":"negative: 行かなくても"},{"lhs":"Aい","rhs":"くても","note":"高 い→高 くても"},{"lhs":"Aな","rhs":"でも","note":"静か→静かでも"},{"lhs":"N","rhs":"でも","note":"学生→学生でも"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"日曜日でも","r":"にちようびでも","m":"even on Sunday"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"仕事","r":"しごと","m":"work"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"あります","r":"あります","m":"there is"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even on Sunday, I still have work.","scene":"Telling a friend about weekend overtime."},{"tokens":[{"t":"高くても","r":"たかくても","m":"even if expensive"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"I will buy it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it is expensive, I will still buy it.","scene":"Choosing a favorite item despite its high price."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-098||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"V/Aても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aても","patternReading":"ても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, ~ still happens (concession)","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜ても to say **“even if ~ happens, the result remains exactly the same.”** It often appears with 何度 (how many times) or いくら (how much/no matter how) to stress that nothing changes. Adding たとえ (even if) makes the “regardless of everything” nuance even clearer.","nuance":"いくら + ても has a strong nuance and may sound slightly **frustrated** in negative sentences (いくら言っても聞かない = no matter how much I say it, they will not listen). たとえ + ても is more formal and is common in writing or speeches.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"いくら","r":"いくら","m":"how much"},{"t":"食べても","r":"たべても","m":"even if I eat"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"太りません","r":"ふとりません","m":"do not gain weight"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"No matter how much I eat, I do not gain weight.","scene":"Surprised that a friend eats a lot but stays thin."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","r":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"雨でも","r":"あめでも","m":"even if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"I will go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, I will still go.","scene":"Showing determination to attend an important event."},{"tokens":[{"t":"国","r":"くに","m":"country"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"違っても","r":"ちがっても","m":"even if different"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"友達","r":"ともだち","m":"friends"},{"t":"です","m":"are"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even though we are from different countries, we are still friends.","scene":"Talking about friendship across two countries."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-098||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Commonly confused patterns","sub":"V/Aても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aても","patternReading":"ても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, ~ still happens (concession)","slug":"compare","definition":"The three patterns most often confused with 〜ても are **Vたら** (if ~), **Vてもいい** (it is permitted), and **Vてから** (after ~). Each expresses a different relationship between the clauses.","nuance":"〜ても stresses a result **despite the condition**; 〜たら stresses that the **condition determines the result**; 〜てもいい expresses **permission**; 〜てから expresses **time order**.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降っても","r":"ふっても","m":"even if it rains"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行きます","r":"いきます","m":"I will go"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, I will still go.","scene":"Emphasizing a decision despite the weather."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vたら","point":"Vたら = “if ~, then” (the result occurs when the condition is met). 〜ても = “even if ~, the result stays the same.” Example: 雨が降ったら行く (if it rains, I will go—the trip depends on the rain) vs 雨が降っても行く (even if it rains, I will go—the rain will not stop me)."},{"vs":"Vてもいい","point":"Vてもいい = “it is okay/permitted to do ~” (いい means “good,” so doing it is acceptable). 〜ても omits いい and only means “even if one does ~.” Example: 行ってもいい (I may go) vs 雨が降っても行く (I will go even if it rains)."},{"vs":"Vてから","point":"Vてから = “after doing ~ (and then...),” showing time order. 〜ても = “even if doing ~,” showing concession. Example: 食べてから出かける (eat, then go out) vs 食べても出かける (go out even after eating? This needs special context)."}]}},"n5-098||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"V/Aても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aても","patternReading":"ても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, ~ still happens (concession)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often forget **も**, conjugate い-adjectives incorrectly, insert です before でも, or confuse たら with ても. Remember: ても must contain **も**; い-adjectives change to **く**; nouns and な-adjectives take **でも** directly after the stem; たら does not express concession.","nuance":"These errors often come from translating “even if” too directly and leaving out も. Always ask: have I included も?","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"高くても","r":"たかくても","m":"even if expensive"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買います","r":"かいます","m":"I will buy it"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it is expensive, I will still buy it.","scene":"Determined to buy a favorite item despite its price."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 雨が降って、行きます。 → ✅ 雨が降っても、行きます。 (Without も, this means “I go after it rains.”)","❌ 忙しいでも、行きます。 → ✅ 忙しくても、行きます。 (An い-adjective must use 〜く + ても.)","❌ 学生ですでも、アルバイトできます。 → ✅ 学生でも、アルバイトできます。 (Do not put です before でも.)","❌ 雨が降ったら、行きます。 → ✅ 雨が降っても、行きます。 (Use ても instead of たら for “even if... still...”.)"],"compare":null}},"n5-098||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"V/Aても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"V/Aても","patternReading":"ても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~, ~ still happens (concession)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember **ても** as て (clause linker) + も (also). When you see も, think **“even”**: add “even if” before the て-clause and “still” before the following clause.","nuance":"This trick helps you parse long sentences quickly. When 〜ても appears in the middle, translate the first clause as “even if...,” while keeping the original affirmative or negative meaning of the second clause.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-099||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"たとえ〜ても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"たとえ〜ても","patternReading":"たとえても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~ (strong concession)","slug":"core","definition":"**たとえ〜ても** means that **no matter how harsh or extreme the situation is, the event in the second clause still happens or remains unchanged**. The second clause often expresses will, determination, or an unchanging fact.","nuance":"The nuance is **formal, literary, or emphatic**. In everyday speech, Japanese speakers often use **〜ても** because it is shorter; adding **たとえ** emphasizes **“no matter what.”**","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"難しく","r":"むずかしく","m":"difficult"},{"t":"ても","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"諦め","r":"あきらめ","m":"give up"},{"t":"ません","m":"not"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it is difficult, I will not give up.","scene":"Telling a friend this while studying for an exam."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降って","r":"ふって","m":"fall"},{"t":"も","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"試合","r":"しあい","m":"match"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"続き","r":"つづき","m":"continue"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, the match will continue.","scene":"The captain assures the team after hearing the forecast."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"失敗","r":"しっぱい","m":"failure"},{"t":"して","m":"do"},{"t":"も","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"また","m":"again"},{"t":"挑戦","r":"ちょうせん","m":"challenge"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I fail, I will try again.","scene":"Encouraging yourself after a failure."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-099||forms":{"title":"🧩 Form and conjugation","sub":"たとえ〜ても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"たとえ〜ても","patternReading":"たとえても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~ (strong concession)","slug":"forms","definition":"Basic structure: **たとえ + [て-form] + も**. With verbs: **Vて + も**; with い-adjectives: **Aくて + も**; with nouns and な-adjectives: **N / NA + でも**. For negative verbs, use **Vなくても**.","nuance":"Do NOT use **たとえ** with **たら** or **ば**. The second clause can be in the present, past, or future.","structure":[{"lhs":"たとえ + Vて","rhs":"も + 文","note":"Example: たとえ雨が降っても"},{"lhs":"たとえ + Aくて","rhs":"も + 文","note":"Example: たとえ高くても"},{"lhs":"たとえ + N／NA","rhs":"でも + 文","note":"Example: たとえ子どもでも"},{"lhs":"たとえ + Vなくて","rhs":"も + 文","note":"Example: たとえ行かなくても"}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"高く","r":"たかく","m":"expensive"},{"t":"ても","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"買い","r":"かい","m":"buy"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it is expensive, I will still buy it.","scene":"Telling your mother you want to buy something pricey."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"行か","r":"いか","m":"go"},{"t":"なくて","m":"not"},{"t":"も","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"大丈夫","r":"だいじょうぶ","m":"okay"},{"t":"です"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if you do not go, it is okay.","scene":"Reassuring a friend unsure about attending the meeting."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-099||usage":{"title":"🎬 Usage and situations","sub":"たとえ〜ても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"たとえ〜ても","patternReading":"たとえても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~ (strong concession)","slug":"usage","definition":"**たとえ〜ても** emphasizes that no matter how bad the condition is, the result or action in the second clause remains unchanged. It often appears in statements expressing determination, belief, or an obvious fact.","nuance":"It has a **formal** tone, like a promise or slogan. With the **ます-form**, it sounds forceful: 諦めません、続けます.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"忙しく","r":"いそがしく","m":"busy"},{"t":"ても","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"日本語","r":"にほんご","m":"Japanese"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"勉強","r":"べんきょう","m":"study"},{"t":"します"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if I am busy, I will study Japanese every day.","scene":"Promising yourself this during a busy study schedule."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"寒く","r":"さむく","m":"cold"},{"t":"ても","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"毎朝","r":"まいあさ","m":"every morning"},{"t":"走り","r":"はしり","m":"run"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it is cold, I still run every morning.","scene":"Telling a friend about your exercise routine."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-099||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Distinguishing it from 〜ても","sub":"たとえ〜ても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"たとえ〜ても","patternReading":"たとえても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~ (strong concession)","slug":"compare","definition":"Compared with ordinary **〜ても**, **たとえ〜ても** adds **emphasis** to an extreme condition or strong assumption. It is often used when the speaker wants to stress the idea “no matter what.”","nuance":"**〜ても** can express a mild condition: 雨でも行きます. **たとえ〜ても** sounds stronger and more formal, so it may not fit very casual contexts.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"でも","m":"even"},{"t":"行き","r":"いき","m":"go"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, I will go. (ordinary)","scene":"Saying this when the rain is light."},{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降って","r":"ふって","m":"fall"},{"t":"も","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行き","r":"いき","m":"go"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, I will still go. (emphatic)","scene":"Insisting that you will go even in heavy rain."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"〜ても","point":"〜ても simply states a concessive condition; たとえ〜ても emphasizes “even under the most extreme circumstances.”"},{"vs":"もし〜たら","point":"もし〜たら expresses the hypothetical “if”; たとえ〜ても expresses “even if,” with the second clause still holding true."}]}},"n5-099||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"たとえ〜ても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"たとえ〜ても","patternReading":"たとえても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~ (strong concession)","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Learners often confuse **たとえ〜ても** with **もし〜たら** because both can involve “if.” In fact, **たとえ** only goes with **〜ても**; do not use it with **たら** or **ば**.","nuance":"**たとえ〜ても** emphasizes concession, not a hypothetical condition. Therefore, do NOT use it in a sentence like “If I have money, I will buy it.”","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"たとえ","m":"even if"},{"t":"雨","r":"あめ","m":"rain"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"降って","r":"ふって","m":"fall"},{"t":"も","m":"even if"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"行き","r":"いき","m":"go"},{"t":"ます"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Even if it rains, I will still go.","scene":"A correct standard example."}],"pitfalls":["❌ たとえ雨が降ったら、行きます。 → ✅ たとえ雨が降っても、行きます。","❌ たとえ高ければ、買います。 → ✅ たとえ高くても、買います。","❌ たとえ失敗しても、後悔します。 (the second clause is illogical) → ✅ たとえ失敗しても、挑戦します。"],"compare":null}},"n5-099||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tips","sub":"たとえ〜ても · N5","v2":{"pattern":"たとえ〜ても","patternReading":"たとえても","coreMeaning":"Even if ~ (strong concession)","slug":"memory","definition":"Remember that **たとえ** means “even if” and combines with **ても** to create **even if... still...**. Picture someone standing firm in a storm—no matter how hard the rain and wind are, they do not retreat.","nuance":"Quick mnemonic: **たとえ** introduces an extreme hypothetical situation; **ても** gives the unchanged result.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-100||core":{"title":"📖 Core meaning","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"core","definition":"〜てくれます is used when **someone else willingly does something for me** (or for someone in my family or group), and I feel **grateful**. The focus is not simply on the action, but on **the doer's kindness**.","nuance":"This is the polite Vます form, used in everyday conversation with friends, family, and coworkers. It feels warm and familiar. When someone above you does something for you, use **〜てくださいます** for greater politeness. 'くれます' always directs the action toward **the speaker** or someone in the speaker's group.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"母","r":"はは","m":"mother"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"毎日","r":"まいにち","m":"every day"},{"t":"弁当","r":"べんとう","m":"boxed lunch"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"作って","r":"つくって","m":"make"},{"t":"くれます","r":"くれます","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My mother makes a boxed lunch for me every day.","scene":"Watching my mother prepare my lunch."},{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"駅","r":"えき","m":"station"},{"t":"まで","m":"as far as"},{"t":"迎えに","r":"むかえに","m":"pick up"},{"t":"来て","r":"きて","m":"come"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend came all the way to the station to pick me up.","scene":"After work, I saw my friend waiting at the station."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-100||forms":{"title":"🧩 Structure and conjugation","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"forms","definition":"Conjugate the verb into the **て-form**, then connect it to **くれます**. The subject is **the person doing the action** (Nが); the recipient is usually **私に** and is often omitted. Example: 母が弁当を作ってくれます = My mother makes a boxed lunch for me.","nuance":"This is the polite form (丁寧). In casual speech, use **くれる / くれた**. Past: **くれました**; negative: **くれません**. **くれますか** is both a question and a gentle request. With a superior, use **くださいます** for greater politeness.","structure":[{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vて + くれます","note":"Present / future: someone does something for me."},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vて + くれました","note":"Past: did something for me."},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vて + くれません","note":"Negative: does not do something for me."},{"lhs":"Nが","rhs":"Vて + くれますか","note":"A polite question or request."}],"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"先生","r":"せんせい","m":"teacher"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"漢字","r":"かんじ","m":"kanji"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"teach"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"The teacher taught me kanji.","scene":"When I asked about kanji, the teacher gladly explained it."},{"tokens":[{"t":"父","r":"ちち","m":"father"},{"t":"は"},{"t":"車","r":"くるま","m":"car"},{"t":"で"},{"t":"会社","r":"かいしゃ","m":"company"},{"t":"まで"},{"t":"送って","r":"おくって","m":"drive"},{"t":"くれます","r":"くれます","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My father drives me to work.","scene":"My father drives me to work every morning."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-100||usage":{"title":"🎬 Uses and situations","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"usage","definition":"Use 〜てくれます to say that **someone does something useful for me**, especially when they **volunteer** without being asked. Use it to describe a favor, express thanks, or make a gentle request with **Vてくれますか**.","nuance":"This pattern suits everyday conversation and often conveys gratitude or happiness. Do not use it for **unwanted actions** or actions by **inanimate things**, except playfully through personification.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"姉","r":"あね","m":"older sister"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"ケーキ","m":"cake"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"買って","r":"かって","m":"buy"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My older sister bought me a cake.","scene":"It is my birthday, so my sister bought a cake."},{"tokens":[{"t":"だれか","m":"someone"},{"t":"、"},{"t":"ドア","m":"door"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"開けて","r":"あけて","m":"open"},{"t":"くれますか","r":"くれますか","m":"could you?"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Could someone open the door for me?","scene":"My hands are full, so I need help opening the door."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-100||compare":{"title":"⚖️ Compare with てもらいます","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"compare","definition":"Compare **てくれます** and **てもらいます**: both mean that someone else does something for me, but **てくれます** emphasizes that the doer **volunteers**, while **てもらいます** emphasizes that I **ask for or receive** the action.","nuance":"Both are polite, but てくれます feels grateful because of the doer's initiative. てもらいます can be used with a superior, but when making a request, the benefit may feel more one-sided.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"林","r":"はやし","m":"Hayashi"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"地図","r":"ちず","m":"map"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"書いて","r":"かいて","m":"draw"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Hayashi drew a map for me.","scene":"When I got lost, Hayashi drew me a map."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":[{"vs":"Vてもらいます","point":"てもらいます = the subject is the RECIPIENT (I receive/ask for the action); てくれます = the subject is the DOER (someone else does it for me)."},{"vs":"Vてあげます","point":"てあげます = I **do something for** someone else (outward direction); てくれます = someone else does it **for me** (inward direction)."}]}},"n5-100||pitfalls":{"title":"🚨 Common mistakes","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"pitfalls","definition":"Common learner errors with てくれます include using the wrong particle, using it for bad actions, or forgetting that the action must come from someone else toward the speaker.","nuance":"Remember that the subject of a てくれます sentence is always **the doer**, not the recipient. The correct particle is **が** (or **は**).","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"友だち","r":"ともだち","m":"friend"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"トイレ","m":"restroom"},{"t":"の"},{"t":"場所","r":"ばしょ","m":"location"},{"t":"を"},{"t":"教えて","r":"おしえて","m":"show"},{"t":"くれました","r":"くれました","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"My friend showed me where the restroom was.","scene":"At the station, my friend showed me the restroom."}],"pitfalls":["❌ 私が友だちに本を貸してくれました。 → ✅ 友だちが私に本を貸してくれました。","❌ 犬が私の宿題を食べてくれました。 → ✅ 犬が私の宿題を食べました。","❌ 雨が降ってくれます。 → ✅ 雨が降っています。"],"compare":null}},"n5-100||memory":{"title":"🎯 Memory tip","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"memory","definition":"A memory tip for てくれます: **くれ** carries the sense of 'give to me', because when Japanese people say 'くれる', it means someone gives something to them. Combine it with the verb's **て** form to mean 'do something for me'.","nuance":"Picture an arrow pointing from the other person toward you. 'て' is like a hand offering you an action.","structure":null,"examples":[],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}},"n5-100||phrases":{"title":"💬 Common phrases","sub":"NがVてくれます · N5","v2":{"pattern":"NがVてくれます","patternReading":"てくれます","coreMeaning":"Someone does something for me (gratefully); a favor from others → me","slug":"phrases","definition":"Common expressions with てくれます in conversation and on tests include '手伝ってくれる', '教えてくれる', '助けてくれる', and '迎えに来てくれる'.","nuance":"When making a request, '〜てくれますか' is polite; with family or close friends, use '〜てくれる？'.","structure":null,"examples":[{"tokens":[{"t":"佐藤","r":"さとう","m":"Sato"},{"t":"さん"},{"t":"が"},{"t":"いつも","m":"always"},{"t":"手伝って","r":"てつだって","m":"help"},{"t":"くれます","r":"くれます","m":"for me"},{"t":"。"}],"vi":"Sato always helps me.","scene":"Talking about a kind coworker."}],"pitfalls":null,"compare":null}}}}